BEGIN;

UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Agriculture', description_en='Agriculture, livestock farming, agricultural machinery, viticulture', editorial_mdx_en='The French agricultural sector boasts a trade show calendar as varied as its industries: from arable farming to animal production, taking in viticulture, machinery and organic farming along the way. The trade shows cover the entire chain, from the livestock breeder to the equipment manufacturer, and from the regional production basin to European agritech.

The key question for an exhibitor: is my market national or regional, and am I selling to the farmer or to the cooperative? The answer entirely determines the choice of trade show. For a visitor keeping an eye on the sector''s innovations, the question is different: should one attend the general trade show or the specialised event for one''s own industry?

## The leading trade shows in France

The [Salon International de l''Agriculture (SIA)](/salons/sia-paris-2026) is the largest agricultural event open to the general public in Europe, organised every February at [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles) by Comexposium on behalf of CENECA. It brings together around 1,000 exhibitors across 9 pavilions and more than 600,000 visitors over nine days. Its purpose is as much political as commercial: it is the event for French food sovereignty, attended by every government minister. The 2026 edition, held under the theme "Générations, Solutions !", ran from 21 February to 1 March. For professional exhibitors, the SIA''PRO, a 100% professional trade show for agricultural technologies and solutions, takes place simultaneously (23-25 February 2026) at the heart of the SIA.

The [SPACE](/salons/space-rennes) (Salon des Productions Animales Carrefour Européen, Rennes, annual in September) is the world''s leading event for livestock farming. The 2025 edition recorded 102,528 visitors (a record), including 14,011 international attendees from 125 countries, and 1,230 exhibitors from 40 countries. It covers every animal sector (cattle, pig, poultry, sheep, goat, rabbit, aquaculture) across 11 sector-specific halls and 16 hectares of open-air space. Next edition: 15-17 September 2026.

The [Sommet de l''Élevage](/salons/sommet-elevage-clermont) (Clermont-Ferrand, annual in October, at the [Grande Halle d''Auvergne](/lieux/grande-halle-d-auvergne)) is another major event for the livestock farming sectors, complementing SPACE in its themes. The 35th edition will be held from 6 to 9 October 2026, with pastoralism in the spotlight. The Sommets d''Or awards there reward machinery innovations.

[Innov''Agri](/salons/innov-agri-ondes) is the leading open-field event, organised every two years (alternating between several regions). The 33rd edition in 2026 will take place in Haute-Garonne, at Ondes, on 9 and 10 September. Its aim is to bring farmers closer to suppliers under real operating conditions: open-field demonstrations, with techniques and equipment in action.

The [SIVAL](/salons/sival-angers) (Angers, annual in January, [Parc des Expositions d''Angers](/lieux/parc-expo-angers)) opens the agricultural calendar each year. It brings together more than 700 exhibitors from 55 nationalities and 25,000 professional visitors around specialised plant production: arboriculture, vegetable crops, viticulture and winemaking, horticulture, aromatic plants and cider production. Its programme features 155 experts and speakers.

[SITEVI](/salons/sitevi-montpellier) (Montpellier, every two years in November) is the international trade show for equipment and techniques for vines, olives and fruit. It covers the wine-growing and winemaking industry as a whole, from cellar equipment to precision technologies. It attracts exhibitors and visitors from many wine-producing countries.

[Vinitech-Sifel](/salons/vinitech-sifel-bordeaux) (Bordeaux, every two years in December) is the leading international trade show for the wine-growing, arboriculture and market-gardening industries, with 800 exhibitors, 40,000 professionals and visitors from 70 countries. Next edition: 1-3 December 2026.

## Regional and specialised trade shows

The richness of the French agricultural calendar also stems from its specialised regional formats. [Tech Ovin](/salons/tech-ovin-bellac) is the leading trade show for the French sheep-farming industry. MedFEL (Perpignan, annual, 28-29 April 2026) is the only French trade show dedicated entirely to fruit and vegetables, with 250 exhibitors and 5,000 decision-maker visitors. The Salon de l''herbe et des fourrages (Poussay in the Vosges, biennial, 20-21 May 2026) offers a forage showcase of 600 micro-plots across 30 hectares, with 150 exhibiting brands. [Les Terres de Jim](/salons/terres-de-jim) (organised by the Jeunes Agriculteurs, Metz-Magny 11-13 September 2026) is the largest open-air agricultural festival in Europe.

In the organic segment, Millésime BIO (Montpellier, 26-28 January 2026, Sudvinbio, 33rd edition) is the world fair for organic wine, with an exclusively professional visitor base. La Terre est Notre Métier (FNAB network, Brittany, September) covers organic farming with 24 conferences. BIO N''Days (Alixan, 4 June 2026) is a business convention dedicated to organic products.

## The major European trade shows

Agritechnica (Hanover, every two years in November, organised by the DLG) is the world''s largest agricultural machinery trade show, with more than 470,000 visitors and 2,800 exhibitors. It is the absolute benchmark for equipment manufacturers and large cooperatives.

EuroTier (Hanover, every two years, DLG) is the counterpart to Agritechnica for the animal sectors: it brings together the players in global livestock farming around techniques, genetics and production technologies. It alternates with Agritechnica in the biennial Hanover calendar.

EIMA International (Bologna, every two years, organised by FederUnacoma) is the leading international agricultural machinery trade show for the Mediterranean market and emerging economies. Bologna is also the world capital of wine-growing and winemaking machinery.

Potato Europe is the leading event for the European potato industry, rotating between different producer countries. It brings together professionals in the production, storage, processing and equipment of potato cultivation.

Anuga (Cologne, every two years in October, Koelnmesse) extends into the food sector but remains a benchmark for the players in global agricultural distribution.

## Beyond the trade shows

The agricultural federations (FNSEA, Jeunes Agriculteurs, chambers of agriculture) organise technical days and local agricultural fairs throughout the year. The Concours Général Agricole (CGA), integrated into the SIA, is a major quality showcase for producers. For the players in agritech, the French AgriTech programmes (Bpifrance) and the regional agripoles are complementary acceleration vectors. The specialised press (Reussir.fr, La France Agricole) publishes comprehensive calendars of trade shows by industry every year.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='agriculture';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Food & Beverage', description_en='Food industry, beverages, institutional catering', editorial_mdx_en='The French food and beverage industry, the country''s largest industrial sector by turnover, has a trade show calendar built around a worldwide biennial flagship (SIAL) and a constellation of events specialised by industry segment or by market. The challenge for an exhibitor: deciding whether they are looking to win new export markets, to get their products listed in French large-scale retail, or to position their offering with the HoReCa channel (cafes, hotels, restaurants). Commercial logic dictates the choice of trade show.

## The leading trade shows in France

[SIAL Paris](/salons/sial-paris) is the world''s largest food trade show, held every two years in October at [Paris Nord Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte) by Comexposium. The 2024 edition brought together 7,500 exhibitors from 127 countries and 285,000 visitors (79% of them international, from 205 countries), with 400,000 products on display. Its vocation is resolutely international: buyers from large-scale retail, foodservice and specialist distribution worldwide come here to source. For a French manufacturer, it is the main window onto export. SIAL Summits offers premium conference programmes on global trends. Next edition: October 2026.

[SIRHA Lyon](/salons/sirha-lyon) is the world trade show for the food trades and foodservice solutions, held every two years in January at Lyon [Eurexpo](/lieux/eurexpo-lyon) by GL events. It targets chefs, restaurateurs, caterers and players in institutional catering, and is the setting for the Bocuse d''Or and the World Pastry Championship.

[Wine Paris](/salons/wine-paris) is the trade show for vines and wine, held every year in February at [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles). The 2026 edition ran from 9 to 11 February. It brings together winegrowers, merchants, importers and buyers from the global wine industry around master classes, innovations and meetings with producers from around the world.

## Specialist and regional trade shows

The [Salon du Chocolat](/salons/salon-du-chocolat-paris) (Paris, annual) is both a trade and consumer event. [CFIA Rennes](/salons/cfia-rennes) (March, annual) covers food industry technologies; its [CFIA Toulouse](/salons/cfia-toulouse) spin-off completes the regional coverage. The Salon du Fromage et des Produits Laitiers (Paris) is the leading sector event for dairy. [Vinitech-Sifel](/salons/vinitech-sifel-bordeaux) (Bordeaux, biennial) covers the wine-growing, arboriculture and market-gardening industries (see also the Agriculture sector). For the food packaging sector, [All4Pack Paris](/salons/all4pack-paris) (Paris, biennial) is the leading European trade show, covering packaging, materials, processes and logistics.

## The major European and international trade shows

Anuga (Cologne, every two years in October, Koelnmesse) is the world''s largest food trade show after SIAL, with more than 7,500 exhibitors and 170,000 visitors from 201 countries. More than 90% of exhibitors and 70% of visitors come from outside Germany. It is a global purchasing platform: the whole world buys here. Next edition: Cologne 2027.

Alimentaria (Barcelona, every two years, Fira Barcelona) is the leading food trade show for the Ibero-American market. It brings together players in food, beverages and gastronomy in a mixed trade and institutional format. It is the preferred gateway for French companies targeting Spain, Portugal and Latin America.

IFE London (International Food & Drink Event, London, every two years) is the leading food platform for the British market and English-speaking distribution channels. It is particularly relevant for French exporters targeting UK retailers and distributors.

TUTTOFOOD Milan (every two years, alternating with Alimentaria) is the food and gastronomy trade show for the Italian and Mediterranean market. Organised by Fiera Milano, it provides access to Italian distributors and buyers, a strategic market for the French food and beverage industry.

Fruit Logistica (Berlin, annual in February, [Messe Berlin](/lieux/messe-berlin)) is the global platform for the international fresh fruit and vegetable trade, with nearly 3,000 exhibitors from 90 countries and more than 70,000 trade visitors. It is the must-attend event for the entire fresh produce industry (fresh, export, logistics).

Seafood Expo Global (Barcelona, annual in April, organised by Informa Markets) is the world''s largest seafood trade show, with more than 2,000 exhibitors from 90 countries and 40,000 trade buyers. It is the trading platform for the entire fishing, aquaculture and fish-processing industry.

Prowein (Düsseldorf, annual, Messe Düsseldorf) is the European reference for the wine sector on export. The American market is addressed via the Fancy Food Show (New York and San Francisco, Specialty Food Association).

## Beyond the trade shows

Food and beverage is a sector where product listings are often decided away from the trade shows, during buyer meetings organised directly by the retail chains. Events such as the Trophées de l''Innovation, the FCD awards or international competitions (Sofi Awards at SIAL) are important complementary visibility levers.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='agroalimentaire';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Art and Culture', description_en='Trade shows and fairs dedicated to art, photography, publishing, antiques and craftsmanship.', editorial_mdx_en='The art and culture market rests on an economy of transactions that is often invisible to the general public: the essential business plays out between galleries, dealers, publishers, institutional collectors and professional buyers. For an exhibitor, an art fair is not an exhibition: it is a marketplace where sales are closed, relationships with collectors are forged and the standing of the artists represented is built. The logic is commercial before it is aesthetic.

The choice of event depends on the segment and the positioning. A contemporary art gallery, a book publisher looking to sell rights, a photographer represented by a gallery and a craft workshop do not target the same buyers or the same channels of influence. In recent years, Paris has established itself as a capital of the art market, which has made its calendar of professional fairs all the more dense.

## The leading fairs in France

[Paris Photo](/salons/paris-photo) is the foremost international fair dedicated to photography, organised each November at the [Grand Palais](/lieux/grand-palais) by RX France. It brings together galleries, specialist publishers and dealers from around the world around fine-art photography, collectible prints and the photographic book. For a gallery or a photography publisher, it is the major prescriptive event, attended by collectors and institutions.

[Art Paris](/salons/art-paris) is the capital''s modern and contemporary art fair, organised each April at the [Grand Palais](/lieux/grand-palais) by France Conventions. It stands out for a positioning that is at once international and attentive to the French and regional scene, making it a gateway for emerging galleries as much as an established fixture for collectors.

[Festival du Livre de Paris](/salons/festival-livre-paris) is the major event for the publishing sector, organised each April at the [Grand Palais](/lieux/grand-palais) by the Syndicat national de l''édition (via Paris Livres Événements). Beyond its public dimension, it serves as a professional working venue: rights deals, co-editions, meetings between publishers, agents and booksellers. For a player in the publishing world, it is the prime point of contact with the French book ecosystem.

The [Salon Photo & Vidéo](/salons/salon-photo-paris), organised each October at the [Grande Halle de la Villette](/lieux/grande-halle-villette) by Comexposium, rounds out this picture on the equipment and imaging-technology side: it addresses manufacturers, distributors and image-capture professionals rather than the collectible photography market.

## The major international events

Paris now hosts Art Basel Paris (Grand Palais, in October), which succeeded Paris+ par Art Basel. Backed by the Swiss benchmark brand of the art market, it is the fair that draws the world''s leading galleries and international collectors to the capital. Art Basel also stages its editions in Basel, Miami Beach, Hong Kong and Qatar, forming the most structuring network of the global art market.

Frieze London (Regent''s Park, in October) is the other major European hub for contemporary art, paired with Frieze Masters for ancient and modern art. For a gallery aiming at the English-speaking market and the international clientele present in London, it is a decisive port of call. On the photography side, Photo London (London, in May) is the benchmark British fair for the medium.

## Beyond the trade shows

The art and culture market is also structured outside the fairs. Craftsmanship has its own professional showcase with the Révélations biennial, organised by Ateliers d''Art de France at the Grand Palais, which brings together creators, workshops and galleries specialising in the contemporary creation of art objects. The professional music sector, for its part, has its business event with MaMA Music & Convention in Paris, which combines a convention (labels, booking, publishers, distribution, rights) and a discovery programme: it is the meeting point of the recorded music and live performance markets. More broadly, professional bodies (Comité professionnel des galeries d''art, Syndicat national de l''édition) and public institutions sustain a network of meetings and committees that extend, outside the fair calendar, the exchanges between the sector''s players.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='art-culture';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Automotive', description_en='Automotive and professional mobility trade shows: vehicles, equipment manufacturers, aftermarket, industrial and commercial vehicles.', editorial_mdx_en='The French automotive industry is undergoing a profound transformation: the electrification of model ranges, the rise of connectivity, pressure on aftermarket costs and the shift towards new forms of mobility. For an exhibitor, the most common mistake is to confuse the automotive sector with [logistics and transport](/secteurs/logistique-transport). The former covers the design, manufacture and maintenance of vehicles (equipment manufacturers, tier 1 and tier 2 suppliers, repairers, parts distributors); the latter covers the movement of goods (supply chain, warehousing, parcel delivery). A spare parts supplier and a route-optimisation software publisher are not addressing the same buyers.

The choice of trade show depends first and foremost on what you sell and to whom. Four segments structure the industry: equipment manufacturers and original equipment (selling to vehicle manufacturers), the aftermarket and replacement parts (selling to repairers and distributors), industrial and commercial vehicles (heavy goods vehicles, buses, urban vehicles), and sustainable mobility (charging, micromobility, services). The major showcase trade fair and the strictly professional trade shows do not play the same commercial role.

## The showcase trade fair in France

The [Mondial de l''Auto Paris](/salons/mondial-auto-paris) is the historic automotive gathering in France, organised every two years in October (even years) at [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles) by the PFA (Plateforme Automobile). Next edition: 12 to 18 October 2026. It is above all a consumer showcase, dedicated to model launches and the brand-consumer relationship. For a B2B exhibitor, it retains genuine but targeted value: press presence, relations with corporate fleets, visibility among equipment manufacturers and principals. It is not a sourcing trade show for replacement parts or tooling: its logic is image and launch, not the workshop transaction.

## The B2B pillars

[Equip Auto Paris](/salons/equip-auto-paris) is the benchmark trade show for the aftermarket and mobility services, organised every two years (odd years) at [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles) by Comexposium. Next edition: 12 to 16 October 2027. It covers the entire aftermarket chain: spare parts, workshop equipment, tooling, diagnostics, tyres, bodywork and paint, washing and mobility services. It is the gathering point for distributors, repairers, auto centres and parts platforms. For a replacement parts or garage equipment supplier targeting the French market, it is the priority sourcing trade show.

[Solutrans](/salons/solutrans) is the international trade show for industrial and urban vehicles and the mobility of tomorrow, organised every two years (odd years) in November at [Eurexpo Lyon](/lieux/eurexpo-lyon) by Comexposium. Next edition: 16 to 20 November 2027. It addresses the heavy goods vehicle, commercial vehicle, industrial bodywork and road haulage of goods and passengers sectors. It is the meeting point for HGV manufacturers, body builders, equipment manufacturers and hauliers. For an industrial vehicle player, it has no equivalent in France.

Sustainable urban mobility (electric charging, micromobility, shared mobility services) constitutes a separate segment, younger and still in flux. An exhibitor positioned in these new forms of mobility must weigh up the dedicated spaces of the major generalist trade shows against the emerging specialised events.

## The major European trade shows

Germany remains the industrial heart of European automotive and concentrates two structuring international gatherings.

IAA Mobility (Munich, biennial in September, organised by the VDA, the German automotive federation) has succeeded the Frankfurt show as the major continental showcase, now centred on mobility in the broad sense: vehicles, charging, cycling, urban services. The 2025 edition brought together more than 700 exhibitors and an audience numbering in the hundreds of thousands of visitors. The next edition is announced for 7 to 12 September 2027. It is the European benchmark for anyone wanting to take the pulse of the industry''s transition.

Automechanika Frankfurt (biennial in September, Messe Frankfurt) is the largest automotive aftermarket trade show in the world, the international counterpart to Equip Auto. It covers the entire chain of replacement parts, repair and workshop equipment, and attracts thousands of exhibitors from more than 170 countries. Next edition announced for 8 to 12 September 2026. For a replacement parts supplier with export ambitions, it is the benchmark marketplace.

## Beyond the trade shows

Several organisations structure the industry''s exchanges outside the trade show calendar. The PFA (Plateforme Automobile) brings together manufacturers and equipment suppliers around industry-wide issues. The FIEV (Fédération des Industries des Équipements pour Véhicules) represents equipment manufacturers, while the FEDA carries the voice of spare parts distribution. On the industrial vehicle and transport side, the FNTR and the OTRE lead the haulage profession. These federations organise technical days, committees and conferences that often anticipate regulatory developments (emissions standards, the right to repair, battery recycling) before they are reflected on the stands.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='automobile';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Construction & Civil Engineering', description_en='Building, civil engineering, materials, architecture', editorial_mdx_en='French construction is going through a decade of profound change: decarbonisation imposed by regulation, the rise of low-carbon building and energy retrofitting, pressure on skills, and the digitalisation of building sites. The trade show landscape reflects these shifts, and some long-standing events are seeing their centre of gravity move while new formats emerge.

For a decision-maker in the sector, whether a sales director looking to exhibit or a buyer keeping an eye on technology, choosing where to invest time and budget is no longer straightforward. Is Batimat still the national must-attend it once was? Should you favour Artibat to reach tradespeople, Interclima for HVAC engineering, [Eurobois](/salons/eurobois-lyon) for the timber industry now undergoing a major reconfiguration? The answer depends on your commercial target, your budget and your maturity in the market.

This page brings together our analysis of the leading construction and civil engineering trade shows on the French calendar, with a look at regional and European events. For trade shows labelled "Agoris Certified", you will find a reasoned verdict, an exhibitor budget range, an analysis of the ecosystem of brands present and our verification of the promises made by the organisers. For the others, the essential factual information, pending their progress through our full editorial cycle.

Our conviction: in a sector where exhibiting easily costs more than 40,000 euros for a stand with decent visibility, it is better to invest an hour in genuinely understanding the trade show before signing the order form. And for a visitor, it is better to know which door to knock on before blocking out two days in the diary.

## The benchmark trade shows in France

Five trade shows structure the national calendar of French construction. They do not address the same audience and do not play the same role: confusing them is the first mistake in trade show strategy.

**[Batimat](/salons/batimat)** is the benchmark generalist trade show. Held every two years at Paris Expo Porte de Versailles by RX France (Reed Expositions), it is the cornerstone of the Mondial du Bâtiment, bringing together Batimat, Interclima and Idéobain under one roof. The 2024 edition gathered 1,473 exhibitors (including 559 new ones, +15% compared with 2022) and more than 135,000 visitors over four days, along with 350 conferences and 230 award-winning innovations. Next edition: 28 September to 1 October 2026. Trend: focused on energy retrofitting and decarbonisation, which take up a growing share at each edition.

**[Artibat](/salons/artibat-rennes)** is the largest regional construction trade show in France, but its reputation extends well beyond the west: it attracts exhibitors and visitors from across the country. Held every two years in Rennes (Parc des Expositions) by CAPEB Pays de la Loire since 1988, its 2025 edition brought together more than 1,100 exhibitors and 45,000 trade visitors across 65,000 m². It covers five poles: structural work, finishing work, technical trades, equipment and heavy civil engineering machinery. Next edition: October 2027. Trend: a growing emphasis on innovation and robotics (the 400 m² "Collab''Zone" dedicated to AI and robotics was one of the flagship novelties of 2025).

**[Interclima](/salons/interclima)**, the trade show for HVAC engineering, heating and ventilation, takes place jointly with Batimat in Paris. Co-organised by RX France, it targets installers, design offices and manufacturers of thermal and air-handling solutions. In the context of the energy transition and the obligation to retrofit heating systems, Interclima gains strategic centrality at each edition.

**[Intermat](/salons/intermat-paris)** is the international trade show for construction and infrastructure equipment and techniques. Held every four years in Paris (Parc des Expositions de Villepinte), it stands out for its strong focus on construction machinery, civil engineering and infrastructure, with a high level of international representation. It brings together nearly 1,200 exhibitors and visitors from 160 countries.

**[BePositive](/salons/bepositive-lyon)** has established itself as the national trade show dedicated to the energy transition and building performance, held in Lyon (Eurexpo) every two years. It brings together professionals committed to energy retrofitting, low-carbon building, systems efficiency and renewable energy applied to buildings. Next edition: 23 to 25 March 2027.

## Regional trade shows: proximity and grassroots presence

Regional trade shows are not watered-down versions of the major national events. They follow a different logic: geographical proximity, easier access for SMEs and tradespeople, more clearly defined commercial targets. For a manufacturer whose market is regional, a trade show like Nordbat or Batinov can offer a better return on investment than a stand at Batimat.

**[Nordbat](/salons/nordbat-lille)** (Lille, every two years) is the benchmark construction trade show in the Hauts-de-France. Its 2026 edition welcomed more than 400 exhibitors and 18,140 trade visitors: a 7% increase on the previous one. It is reputed for its friendly atmosphere, with an integrated programme of conferences and workshops. Next edition: 15 to 17 March 2028, Lille Grand Palais.

**[Batinov](/salons/batinov-lyon)** (Lyon, every two years) positions itself on digital technologies and new technologies applied to construction: BIM, site management, digitalisation. It is a more specialised trade show, useful for players keeping an eye on technology in the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes region.

Other regional events round out the calendar according to geographical area: Artibat covers the west, the CAPEB''s Rencontres des Métiers du Bâtiment rotate between several cities (the next edition will be held in Marseille), and Architect@Work runs in several French cities (Paris, Bordeaux, Nantes) to connect architects and suppliers in a more intimate format. For players in landscaping and outdoor design, [Paysalia](/salons/paysalia-lyon) (Lyon, biennial) is the benchmark sector event.

## The major European construction trade shows

For French companies developing internationally, or for procurement departments tracking major global trends, three European trade shows are unmissable.

**[Bauma](/salons/bauma-munich)** (Munich, every three years) is quite simply the largest construction trade show in the world. Organised by Messe München since 1954, its 2025 edition brought together nearly 600,000 visitors from more than 200 countries and 3,601 exhibitors from 57 nations, across more than 550,000 m² of exhibition space. Its speciality: construction machinery, building equipment and civil engineering machinery. A must for all players in heavy equipment. Next edition: April 2028.

**[BAU](/salons/bau-munich)** (Munich, every two years in January) is the world''s leading trade show for architecture, materials and building systems. Also organised by Messe München, it brings together around 2,250 exhibitors and 250,000 visitors. Its profile is more oriented towards building and materials innovation than Bauma, with an audience of architects, engineers and project developers. Next edition: 11 to 15 January 2027.

**[ISH](/salons/ish-francfort)** (Frankfurt, every two years) is the world''s leading trade show for building services: plumbing, heating, air conditioning, sanitary equipment. Organised by Messe Frankfurt, it is the European benchmark for HVAC engineering players, complementing Interclima for the French market.

Worth noting for France: these major German trade shows are often covered by delegations organised by the professional federations (FFB, CAPEB, UNICLIMA), which can make participation easier for SMEs that would rather not manage the organisation on their own.

## Beyond the trade show: formats, events and communities

Construction trade shows are no longer limited to aisles of exhibitors. Recent editions have massively developed parallel formats that radically change the nature of the event.

**Conferences and forums.** Batimat 2024 offered nearly 350 speaking slots across fourteen themed stages. The Renodays programme occupied a dedicated space on the energy retrofitting of housing, with workshops and conferences on thermal performance, financial aid and new regulations. Artibat, for its part, offers a dense, hands-on technical programme, with live demonstrations in real conditions that are part of the trade show''s identity.

**Competitions and awards.** The Innovation Awards of the Mondial du Bâtiment reward the most remarkable solutions at each edition: 230 award-winning innovations in 2024 from 229 entries. For exhibitors, this accolade has become a communication tool in its own right before, during and after the trade show.

**Immersive themed spaces.** Batimat 2024 notably offered Climate Sense, an experience simulating site conditions at +50 °C in the context of global warming. Artibat 2025 launched the Collab''Zone, entirely dedicated to AI and robotics in construction. These spaces are becoming visitor magnets and strong signals of the sector''s priorities.

**Complementary formats outside trade shows.** Some organisers have developed standalone events that no longer wait for the major biennial edition. This is the case for RX France, which launched the Renodays forum (7 and 8 October 2025) as an independent event between two editions of Batimat. [BIM World Paris](/salons/bim-world-paris) is another example: 100% dedicated to digital technology and the digital transformation of building, it has established itself as an unmissable annual event for players in digital modelling and site data management.

**Professional communities outside trade shows.** The major federations (FFB, CAPEB, UNICLIMA, SYNAMOME) organise congresses, technical days and regional events throughout the year that structure the sector''s professional networks. For an exhibitor or a visitor, these events are often more useful for local business development than the major national trade shows.

## What Agoris covers on construction

Our construction catalogue covers the main French trade shows with two levels of qualification. Agoris Certified entries offer a reasoned verdict, an analysis of the exhibitor ecosystem, a verification of promises and a realistic stand budget range. Standard entries list the essential factual information pending their full editorial treatment.

Our conviction remains intact: in a sector where a poor choice of trade show can cost 50,000 euros and six months of team mobilisation for zero return, quality information is not a luxury. It is a prerequisite.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='btp-construction';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Cosmetics and Beauty', description_en='Cosmetics, perfumery, beauty, wellness', editorial_mdx_en='France is the world''s leading cosmetics powerhouse, and its trade show calendar reflects this. But the sector is fragmented across several commercial logics: the professional market (hair salons, beauty institutes), pharmacy distribution, the perfumery channel, luxury and the export market. Each channel has its own dedicated events.

For an exhibitor, the first question is: am I looking to win over distributors or beauty professionals? The second trade-off: Paris or the international market? The two are not interchangeable.

## The benchmark trade shows in France

[Cosmoprof Worldwide Bologna](/salons/cosmoprof-bologna) (Bologna, annual in March, [BolognaFiere](/lieux/bolognafiere)) is the world''s leading professional beauty trade show, with more than 2,300 exhibitors from 70 countries and over 170,000 visitors. [Cosmetic 360](/salons/cosmetic-360) (Paris, annual in October, [Carrousel du Louvre](/lieux/carrousel-du-louvre)) targets the decision-makers and buyers of the major brands with 300 to 400 specialist exhibitors. [Pharmagora Plus](/salons/pharmagora-plus-paris) (Paris, annual in March, CloserStill Media) features a significant cosmetics and dermo-cosmetics strand through the Victoires de la Pharmacie.

## Specialist trade shows

The Salon International de la Coiffure (Paris, biennial) is the gathering point for hairdressers and hair-care suppliers. Interbeauté covers the aesthetics and beauty-institute market. [Natexpo](/salons/natexpo-paris) (Lyon, biennial) has a sizeable natural cosmetics space for organic-sector players.

## The major European and global trade shows

Beautyworld Middle East (Dubai, annual in October, Messe Frankfurt) has become the world''s third-largest beauty trade show. The 2025 edition brought together 2,000 exhibitors and 71,000 visitors from 160 countries, with a 70% rise in French visitors. Beautyworld Frankfurt and [in-cosmetics](/salons/in-cosmetics-global-paris-2026) round out the European calendar for raw-material manufacturers.

## Beyond the trade shows

The Cosmetic Valley runs its own B2B meetings and its annual congress. The Trophées de la Beauté and the beauty-tech influencer communities play a growing role in the discovery of new products.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='cosmetique-beaute';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Decoration and Home Living', description_en='Design, interior decoration, furniture, home living', editorial_mdx_en='Decoration and interior home living make up a sector in which France plays a leading role on the world stage. The calendar is structured around Maison & Objet, the biannual benchmark, and several events covering professional decoration, contract, interior textiles and furnishing.

## The benchmark trade show

[Maison & Objet Paris](/salons/maison-et-objet-paris-2026) (biannual, January and September, [Paris Nord Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte), SAFI/RX France) brings together 2,300 to 2,500 brands, 60% of them international, and more than 70,000 trade visitors per edition. The September edition coincides with Paris Design Week.

## Specialised trade shows

Paris Design Week and Paris Déco Off complement Maison & Objet. Domotex (Hanover, Messe) is the European benchmark for floor coverings. IMM Cologne (Koelnmesse) covers European furniture. [Equipbaie Metalexpo](/salons/equipbaie-metalexpo-paris) (Paris, [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles)) remains a key event for joinery and closures.

## The European benchmark trade show

Salone del Mobile (Milan, annual, April, FederlegnoArredo) is the world''s trade show for furniture and design, with more than 300,000 visitors. For French brands targeting high-quality international distribution, Milan is essential.

## Beyond the trade shows

The decoration sector is rooted in the culture of the permanent showroom. Networks of specifiers (interior architects, decorators) are often more effective than stands for high-end brands.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='decoration-habitat';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Defence and Security', description_en='Defence, security, aerospace, space', editorial_mdx_en='France is one of the world''s leading powers in the defence and security industry. Its calendar is structured around Eurosatory for land defence, Milipol for homeland security and FIC for cybersecurity.

## The flagship trade shows in France

[Eurosatory](/salons/eurosatory-paris) (Paris, every two years in June, GICAT) is the world''s leading trade show for land defence and security, with more than 2,000 exhibitors and 76,000 participants. Next edition: 15-19 June 2026.

[Milipol Paris](/salons/milipol-paris) (Paris, every two years, under the aegis of the Ministry of the Interior/CIVIPOL) welcomed 1,171 exhibitors and 32,000 visitors in 2025. 2025 theme: "Homeland security and artificial intelligence".

[FIC](/salons/fic-lille) (Forum International de la Cybersécurité, [Lille](/lieux/lille-grand-palais), annually in January) brings together more than 15,000 participants, 600 exhibitors and 400 speakers on digital security.

## The major European trade shows

DSEI (London, every two years) covers all military domains. [Euronaval](/salons/euronaval-paris) (Paris, every two years, GICAN) specialises in naval defence. IDEX (Abu Dhabi) is the strategic gathering for exports to the Middle East.

## Beyond the trade shows

Strategic conferences (IHEDN, Cyberdefense forum) play an essential complementary role in a sector where purchasing decisions are influenced by institutional networks.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='defense-securite';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Energy and Environment', description_en='Renewable energy, environment, sustainable development', editorial_mdx_en='No other sector is seeing such a flurry of activity on the trade show market. Energy transition, hydrogen, biogas, circular economy, solar, wind, water management, CSR: every sub-segment now has its own event, and some are emerging in step with European regulatory shifts.

This density is an opportunity, but it is also a trap. Pollutec remains the cross-cutting heavyweight, but it now sits alongside specialised trade shows such as Hyvolution for hydrogen, Expobiogaz for biogas or Produrable for CSR initiatives, which capture a sharper and often more qualified audience for a targeted message. An industrial player spending 80,000 euros at Pollutec is not making the same bet as one splitting the same sum across three vertical trade shows.

This page brings together our analysis of the leading energy and environment trade shows on the French calendar, with an eye on the formats that are emerging and on the major European events. For trade shows labelled "Agoris Certified", you will find our reading of recent developments, an analysis of the exhibitor profile and our verdict on relevance for different types of company. For the others, the essential factual information, pending their passage through our full editorial cycle.

Our conviction: in this sector undergoing a major reshaping, the value of a trade show is measured less by its visitor figures than by the quality of its ecosystem and its ability to anticipate the topics that will shape the market over the next two years. Whether you are an exhibitor seeking the best return on investment or a visitor on strategic watch, choosing the right trade show is already a decision in itself.

## The leading trade shows in France

The French market for energy and environment trade shows has become considerably denser over ten years. Today two categories can be distinguished: cross-cutting generalists and specialised vertical trade shows. Both have their relevance, depending on the message you are carrying and the target you want to reach.

**[Pollutec](/salons/pollutec-lyon)** is the leading trade show in Europe for environmental solutions and the ecological transition. Organised by RX France (Reed Expositions) at Lyon Eurexpo, it brings together each year around 2,000 exhibitors and more than 51,000 professional participants from over 100 countries. Its coverage is very broad: 11 sectors represented, from water and waste management to industrial decarbonisation, taking in the bioeconomy and environmental health. Its programme systematically features nearly 400 conferences across 16 stages, innovation awards and themed villages. Next edition: 12 to 15 October 2027, Lyon Eurexpo.

**[Hyvolution](/salons/hyvolution-paris)** has become, in just a few years, the leading trade show in Europe for hydrogen. Organised each January in Paris at Porte de Versailles by GL events, in partnership with France Hydrogène, its 2025 edition brought together more than 550 exhibitors and 15,000 visitors from 65 countries, with 15 national hydrogen associations represented. Its growth is rapid: the 2024 edition had already been described as a record, with 572 exhibitors from 28 countries and more than 11,500 visitors. The trade show includes a Hyvolution Summit, high-level conferences and workshops dedicated to industry, mobility and energy. Next edition: 27 to 29 January 2026, Paris, then 19 to 21 January 2027.

**[Expobiogaz](/salons/expobiogaz-bordeaux)** is the leading renewable gas trade show in France, devoted to methanisation, power-to-gas and bio-CNG. Organised by GL events at Eurexpo Lyon (within its GreenTech+ division), it is held every two years and brings together the players of the biogas industry, from farmers to industrial firms and local authorities. Its sector focus makes it a highly targeted business development tool for specialised players. Next edition: 10 and 11 March 2026, Eurexpo Lyon.

**[Produrable](/salons/produrable-paris)** is the leading trade show and congress on CSR and corporate sustainable development, organised in Paris. It is aimed primarily at CSR departments, general management and procurement functions of large companies and mid-sized firms. Its positioning is more strategic and corporate than technical: people come less to find a supplier than to understand regulatory trajectories and benchmark market practices.

**[EnerGaïa](/salons/energaia-montpellier)** is the renewable energy trade show for the greater south of France, organised in Montpellier each December by GL events. It mainly targets regional players in photovoltaics, wind and the local energy transition, with a marked Mediterranean anchoring and conferences on regional energy policies.

## Regional and specialised formats not to be overlooked

The structuring of the sector by sub-segments has fostered the emergence of specialised formats that usefully complement the major national events.

**[EnerJ-meeting](/salons/enerj-meeting-paris)** is a conference-exhibition format, organised by the Batiactu group, which brings together decision-makers each year on the themes of energy moderation, energy efficiency and the decarbonisation of buildings. It runs in several regional editions (Paris, Lyon, Bordeaux). The format is denser and more concentrated than a traditional trade show: a few dozen exhibitors, but a highly developed conference programme with 100 speakers and five themed rooms for the major editions.

**[Pollutec Paris](/salons/pollutec-paris-2026)**, launched by RX France as a complementary edition to the Lyon one, is held in Paris in even-numbered years with a more agile format (two days, congress-exhibition format). It targets primarily public and corporate decision-makers on regulatory and transition financing topics.

Other formats are emerging around specific themes: the **[SIBCA](/salons/sibca-paris)** on the decarbonisation of buildings (launched in 2022, already more than 200 exhibitors and 12,500 visitors expected in 2026), or **[Open Energies](/salons/open-energies-lyon)** (GL events, Lyon), devoted to solutions for cities and territories in energy transition.

## The major European trade shows in the sector

For companies developing internationally or keeping watch on trends that will reach France in two to three years, three European events are not to be missed.

**[The smarter E Europe](/salons/smarter-e-europe-munich)** (Munich, annual in May or June) is the largest alliance of energy trade shows in Europe. It brings together under one roof four simultaneous events: Intersolar Europe (solar), ees Europe (energy storage), Power2Drive Europe (e-mobility) and EM-Power Europe (intelligent energy management). The whole is organised at Messe München and constitutes the meeting point for the entire energy transition value chain, from the solar panel to the charging point. Intersolar is recognised as the world''s leading solar sector trade show.

**[Hannover Messe](/salons/hannover-messe)** (Hanover, annual in April) is the largest industrial fair in the world, organised by Deutsche Messe AG. While it covers industry in the broad sense, its energy component is massive: industrial hydrogen, electricity grids, energy efficiency of processes, and the digitalisation of production. For French industrial players positioned on process decarbonisation, it is often more relevant than purely environmental trade shows.

**[E-world energy & water](/salons/e-world-essen)** (Essen, annual in February) is the principal European trade show dedicated to energy suppliers, grid operators and players in the water sector. It is particularly relevant for companies positioned on regulation, energy markets and energy infrastructure.

## Beyond trade shows: formats, events and communities

The energy and environment sector has developed a particularly rich event culture, to the point where the trade shows themselves are often only part of the programme.

**Integrated conferences and summits.** Pollutec 2025 offered 400 conferences across 16 themed stages, with debates led by the sector''s leading figures. Hyvolution holds its Hyvolution Summit each year, with strategic round tables on European policies, financing and the industrial challenges of decarbonised hydrogen. These hybrid trade show-congress formats have become the norm in the sector.

**Innovation awards and labels.** The Pollutec Innovation Awards, organised jointly with the Pexe (Les éco-entreprises de France), reward each year the innovations with the strongest market potential. Hyvolution awards its own distinctions. These labels have become communication tools in their own right for winning exhibitors, used well beyond the duration of the trade show.

**Immersive and scenario-based spaces.** Pollutec 2025 introduced for the first time in Lyon the "Down to Earth, The Expo" exhibition, an immersive zone of more than 200 m² dedicated to setting environmental challenges in context. This type of arrangement, first tested in Paris, illustrates an underlying trend: the major trade shows are seeking to create a visitor experience beyond the simple product catalogue.

**Professional communities and federations.** France Hydrogène, the Pexe, France Renouvelables, the ATEE (Association Technique Énergie Environnement) or the SER (Syndicat des énergies renouvelables) organise technical days, regulatory workshops and business meetings throughout the year that structure the sector''s networks independently of the major events. For an exhibitor, these proximity formats often offer more qualified access to sector decision-makers than the aisles of a large trade show.

## What Agoris covers on energy and the environment

We document this diversity with particular attention to trajectories: which trade shows are rising, which are plateauing, which are suffering from the inflation of supply. For Agoris Certified listings, you will find our reading of recent developments, an analysis of the exhibitor profile (proportion of newcomers, recurring brands, dominant sectors) and our verdict on relevance for different types of company.

The energy and environment sector is the one where the quality of the trade show choice matters most: supply has fragmented so much that the risk of preaching to the converted, or conversely of getting lost in too broad an audience, is very real. It is precisely to avoid this pitfall that we treat this sector with a specific reading framework.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='energie-environnement';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Finance and Insurance', description_en='Banking, insurance, fintech, investment, wealth management', editorial_mdx_en='The finance and insurance sector has a dense events calendar, but one that is fragmented across several professional communities that rarely overlap: wealth managers, traditional bankers and insurers, fintech and insurtech players, and capital markets. Each segment has its own gatherings and its own commercial logic.

The essential distinction: wealth management trade shows (Patrimonia) connect producers of financial products with prescriber advisers. Fintech conferences (Money 20/20, Paris Fintech Forum) connect start-ups with banks, investors and regulators. These two worlds coexist but only intersect at the margins. For an exhibitor, the trade-off is first and foremost a question of target client.

## The leading trade shows in France

**Patrimonia** is the benchmark convention for wealth management professionals, held every year in September in Lyon (Cité Internationale) by Infopro Digital, under the patronage of the Ministry of the Economy. The 31st edition in 2024 brought together 9,054 professionals and 380 exhibitors over two days. Its audience is highly targeted: wealth management advisers (CGP), CGPi, family offices, private bankers, notaries, tax lawyers, brokers and chartered accountants. The 100 speaking slots cover regulation (MiFID II, DDA), ESG, investment products (SCPI, private equity, life insurance) and the digital transformation of advisory firms. For asset management companies, SCPIs and insurers, it is the direct access point to prescribers. Next edition: 30 September - 1 October 2026, Lyon.

**Paris Fintech Forum** is the European fintech gathering in Paris, held every year in October in a high-end conference-exhibition format. It brings together the leaders of major European banks, insurers, regulators and fintechs around the challenges of digital financial transformation. Its format is more exclusive and less stand-oriented than Patrimonia: it is an event for strategic positioning and visibility.

**Les Journées du Courtage** (Paris, annual, two days in September) is the benchmark trade show for insurance brokers and financial intermediaries. Each year it brings together several thousand brokers with insurance companies and financial product operators. It is the counterpart to Patrimonia for the insurance distribution market.

### Specialist trade shows

**Finance Innovation** organises themed days in Paris each year on fintech, insurtech and regtech. The **Assises de l''Assurance** and the **Congrès de la Mutualité Française** are major political and professional events in the insurance sector. The wealth property market is covered both by Patrimonia and by MIPIM (see the Property section). For those involved in business creation and venture capital, the conferences of the France Invest and France Fintech federations structure exchanges.

## The major European and international trade shows

**Money 20/20 Europe** (Amsterdam, annual, organised by Ascential) is the world''s leading event for fintech and financial services. The 2025 edition brought together thousands of participants from more than 2,200 companies across nearly 100 countries, with 450 speakers from 40 countries. Several major announcements were made there by Revolut, Klarna, Mastercard, Deutsche Bank and the ECB. It is where the strategic partnerships between European banks, fintechs and regulators are forged. 2025 themes: stablecoins, AI and open banking.

**Sibos** (rotating city, annual, organised by SWIFT) is the global event for the institutional financial and banking community. It brings together more than 10,000 professionals around payments, compliance, financial infrastructure and international regulation. Its profile is less start-up than Money 20/20 and more geared towards large institutions, central banks and market operators.

**InsurTech Insights Europe** (London, annual) is the leading European conference dedicated to the technological transformation of the insurance sector. It brings together insurers, reinsurers, brokers and insurtech start-ups around the challenges of personalisation, AI and the digital distribution of insurance.

## Beyond the trade shows

The finance and insurance sector is one in which investment decisions are least often made at a trade show. Commercial relationships are built over the long term, through networks of trust. The conferences of the AFG (Association Française de Gestion), AMAFI, FBF (Fédération Bancaire Française) and France Assureurs structure professional exchanges throughout the year. For fintechs, the acceleration programmes of the Banque de France (innovation lab), the ACPR and the AMF offer privileged routes to regulators and major institutions.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='finance-assurance';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Franchise & Retail', description_en='Franchise, retail, trade, distribution, e-commerce', editorial_mdx_en='Franchising and organised retail make up a market in which trade shows play a structuring role: it is mainly at trade shows that prospective franchisees meet network heads and compare brands.

## The benchmark trade show

[Franchise Expo Paris](/salons/franchise-expo-paris) (Paris, annual in March, FFF/Infopro Digital) drew 32,282 participants, 595 exhibitors and 472 brands in 2025. 60% of visitors are attending for the first time. Breakdown: retail (42%), food (41%), services (17%). Next edition: 14-16 March 2026.

## Complementary formats

[Salon SME](/salons/salon-sme-paris) is aimed at founders and the self-employed. Regional franchise trade shows ([Forum Franchise Lyon](/salons/franchise-expo-lyon), Bordeaux, Rennes) reach local candidates. For catering brands, [SIRHA Lyon](/salons/sirha-lyon) is also a recruitment channel.

## Beyond the trade shows

The Trophées de la Franchise, the Franchise Magazine rankings and the FFF''s conferences shape brand awareness. The information days organised by network heads are often the first point of contact before visiting the trade show.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='franchise-commerce';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Real Estate', description_en='Commercial real estate, residential, investment, development', editorial_mdx_en='The real estate sector has a handful of structuring events with very different rationales. MIPIM is a platform for international transactions and investment; SIMI is geared towards the domestic market; RENT targets proptech.

## The leading trade shows in France

**MIPIM** (Cannes, annually in March, RX France) is the international market for real estate professionals, regarded as the largest real estate trade show in the world. More than 20,000 participants from over 90 countries in 2025, including 70% of the world''s top 100 asset managers.

[SIMI](/salons/simi) (Paris, annually in December, FSIF) is the trade show for French commercial real estate and investment, highly qualified on the French market.

[Salon RENT](/salons/rent) (Paris, annual) is the trade show for proptech and real estate digital technology.

## The major European and international trade shows

Expo Real (Munich, annual, Messe München) is the second-largest European real estate trade show, strongly oriented towards Central and Eastern Europe.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='immobilier';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Industry and Packaging', description_en='Manufacturing industry, automation, robotics, packaging and packing', editorial_mdx_en='French industry, in the midst of a reindustrialisation drive, benefits from a very dense trade show calendar covering machinery, automation, robotics, chemicals, aerospace, maintenance and packaging. The latter two, often treated separately, in fact share a partially common audience: the technical, procurement and R&D directors of process industries attend generalist industrial trade shows just as much as the shows dedicated to packaging.

The challenge of the industrial sector lies in its breadth: a manufacturer of automation solutions, a precision mechanical engineering subcontractor and a supplier of primary packaging do not share the same commercial targets. Choosing the right trade show begins by clarifying who you are selling to, and in which client industry.

## The benchmark trade show in France

[Global Industrie](/salons/global-industrie-lyon) is the largest French industrial trade show, covering the full range of technologies and expertise of the manufacturing industry. Organised by GL events and RX France every two years, alternating between Lyon and Paris, it brought together 2,500 exhibitors and 45,000 visitors in Lyon in 2025, a 20% increase compared with 2023, held under the high patronage of the President of the Republic. It covers 14 major industrial sectors: sheet metalwork, 3D printing, robotics, industrial IoT, maintenance, machining, welding, plastics processing, surface treatments and more. Next edition: [Paris Nord Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte), 30 March-2 April 2026.

## Specialised industry trade shows

Maintenance Expo (Paris, annual) is the benchmark trade show for industrial maintenance, covering predictive maintenance solutions, tooling, spare parts and monitoring technologies. [SEPEM Industries](/salons/sepem-toulouse) is a touring format organised by GL events that covers equipment, processes and services for industry. It is staged across several regional editions (Rouen, Toulouse, Grenoble, Angers, Colmar and others) that allow suppliers to reach local manufacturers who are often little present at the major Parisian trade shows.

[JEC World](/salons/jec-world-paris-2026) (Paris, annual, organised by JEC Group) is the world''s benchmark trade show for composite materials, with more than 1,500 exhibitors and 45,000 visitors from 110 countries. Paris is the world capital of composites, and JEC is its showcase. [Midest](/salons/midest) (Paris, annual, RX France) is the international trade show for industrial subcontracting, the meeting point between contractors and subcontractors across all manufacturing trades.

## Packaging and packing trade shows

Packaging is a cross-cutting sector: its buyers are found in the food, cosmetics, pharmaceutical and industrial sectors. Its suppliers (packaging designers, materials manufacturers, packing line equipment makers) need dedicated trade shows to reach all of these client industries in a single location.

[All4Pack Paris](/salons/all4pack-paris) (Paris Nord Villepinte, every two years in November, RX France) is the European benchmark trade show for packaging, packing, handling and logistics. It covers the entire packaging value chain: materials (cardboard, plastic, glass, metal, paper), packing machines, labelling, printing, eco-design and end-of-life solutions. Its audience is very broad, ranging from food and cosmetics to pharmaceuticals and industry. It is the natural starting point for any packaging supplier targeting the French and European market.

Emballage (Paris, every two years, alternating with All4Pack) is the other major French event for industrial packaging, more focused on processes and production line equipment. The two trade shows alternate within the biennial Parisian calendar and address slightly different segments of the sector.

## The major European trade shows

[Hannover Messe](/salons/hannover-messe) (Hanover, annual in April, Deutsche Messe AG) is the world''s largest industrial fair, with 130,000 visitors from 150 countries and more than 4,000 exhibitors. It covers automation, robotics, industrial energy, process decarbonisation and the digital transformation of industry. It is the global benchmark for manufacturers keeping track of technology.

Interpack (Düsseldorf, every three years, Messe Düsseldorf) is the world''s largest packaging and packing trade show, with more than 2,800 exhibitors from 60 countries and around 170,000 visitors. It covers the full range of packaging materials, packing machines, printing and eco-design. The next edition takes place from 7 to 13 May 2026 in Düsseldorf: ideal timing for packaging players who want to assess the European market before deciding on their trade show strategy.

Formnext (Frankfurt, annual, Messe Frankfurt) is the world benchmark for additive manufacturing and industrial 3D printing, with more than 35,000 visitors and 900 exhibitors. For players in rapid prototyping, tooling and 3D-printed series parts, it is the defining event.

FachPack (Nuremberg, every two years, Messe Nürnberg) is the European trade show specialising in packaging, packing and display, with a more SME-oriented positioning than Interpack. It attracts nearly 40,000 visitors and 1,500 exhibitors, mainly European.

## Beyond the trade shows

The technical days organised by the UIMM (Union des Industries et Métiers de la Métallurgie), France Industrie and the competitiveness clusters (Aerospace Valley, Nuclear Valley, Systematic, Elastopole for plastics processing) structure exchanges between manufacturers outside the trade show calendar. For packaging specifically, ELIPSO (the plastic packaging trade body), FEFCO (the European corrugated board body) and ADEME (the ecological transition of packaging) run working groups and conferences that often anticipate regulatory trends before they appear at the trade shows.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='industrie';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Logistics and Transport', description_en='Logistics, supply chain, transport, mobility', editorial_mdx_en='Logistics and transport make up a sector undergoing profound change: warehouse automation, last-mile logistics, fleet decarbonisation, digital supply chain.

## The leading trade show in France

[SITL](/salons/sitl) ([Paris Nord Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte), annual in March-April, RX France) is the leading European supply chain trade show: more than 30,000 visitors and around 800 exhibitors. Next edition: 31 March-2 April 2026.

## Specialised trade shows

[Intralogistics Europe](/salons/intralogistics-france) (co-located with SITL, warehouse automation) and [Solutrans](/salons/solutrans) (Lyon, biennial, industrial vehicles) round out the calendar.

## The major European trade shows

Transport Logistic (Munich, biennial, Messe München) is the world''s largest freight platform, with more than 2,500 exhibitors and 70,000 visitors. The Paris Air Show (Le Bourget, biennial) is the world''s aerospace trade show.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='logistique-transport';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Fashion and Textiles', description_en='Fashion, ready-to-wear, textiles, accessories, jewellery and watchmaking, luxury', editorial_mdx_en='France is one of the world''s fashion capitals. The Paris textile trade shows shape the sourcing decisions of international brands two to three seasons in advance.

## The leading trade shows in France

[Première Vision Paris](/salons/premiere-vision-paris) (twice yearly in February and September, [Paris Nord Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte)) is the world''s leading trade show for fashion raw materials. [Who''s Next](/salons/who-s-next-paris-2026) (Paris, twice yearly, WSN) targets buyers from multi-brand boutiques and concept stores. [Texworld Paris](/salons/texworld-paris) and Apparel Sourcing Paris (twice yearly, Messe Frankfurt) are the major international textile sourcing trade shows.

## The major European trade shows

Munich Fabric Start (three editions per year) is the European platform for fabric sourcing. Pitti Uomo (Florence) is the world''s benchmark for menswear.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='mode-textile';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='HR & Training', description_en='Human resources, professional training, employment', editorial_mdx_en='The market for HR and professional training trade shows has been transformed by the rise of HR Tech. Generalist trade shows coexist with specialised formats focused on digital training, recruitment or management.

## The leading trade shows in France

HR Technologies France (Paris, annual in January, CloserStill Media/Parlons RH) brought together 410 exhibitors and 350 conferences in 2026. Co-located with [Learning Technologies France](/salons/learning-technologies-paris), the combined event represents 16,000 HR and L&D decision-makers with a free shared badge. Next edition: 20-21 January 2027.

[Salon Solutions RH](/salons/solutions-rh-paris-2026) (Paris, annual) welcomes 300 exhibitors and 9,000 visitors across the full range of HR and management solutions.

## Across Europe

HR Technologies UK (London, CloserStill) is the British twin format. Zukunft Personal (Cologne, Messe) is the largest HR trade show in Europe, with nearly 20,000 visitors and 700 exhibitors.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='rh-formation';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Health & Pharma', description_en='Healthcare, pharmacy, medical devices, biotechnologies', editorial_mdx_en='The healthcare sector in France does not fit into a single trade show. It takes around ten of them to cover all audiences: Santexpo for hospital buyers, Pharmagora Plus for retail pharmacy, MedFIT for medtech and innovation partnerships, Paris Healthcare Week for health policy, plus a series of specialised events on imaging, dentistry, laboratories or digital health.

This fragmentation is rational: buyers and prescribers are not the same across segments. But it demands precise choices. A medical device manufacturer will not derive the same benefit from a presence at Santexpo as from one at MedFIT, even though the budgets committed may be comparable. The logic of attendance is as much a matter of commercial strategy as of event marketing.

This page covers the main healthcare trade shows on the French calendar, the regional and specialised formats, and the major European events that French players cannot afford to ignore. For the Agoris Certified profiles, you will find the actual make-up of the visitor base, the average investment ticket observed for various attendance formats, and the recent trend in footfall since the end of the Covid period.

Our conviction: in a regulated sector where every purchasing decision runs through long cycles, the quality of a trade show is measured by its ability to bring the right stakeholders together in the same place at the right point in the buying cycle. The raw visitor figure is a very imperfect indicator. Whether you are a supplier, a startup or a decision-making attendee, knowing which event to choose is already a strategic decision.

## The leading trade shows in France

The French healthcare trade show market is structured around a few major events that do not really compete with one another: they address different audiences, at different points in the buying cycle and with distinct commercial logics.

**[Santexpo](/salons/paris-healthcare-week-2026)** is the leading trade show for healthcare and the medico-social sector in France, organised each year in May at Paris Expo Porte de Versailles by the Fédération Hospitalière de France (FHF), with logistics handled by GL events. The 2025 edition broke all records with 35,150 participants, a 17% increase compared with 2024, and 695 exhibitors. Its audience is highly institutional: hospital directors, public decision-makers, hospital buyers, public authorities and ministries. The 232 speaking sessions with 530 speakers also make it as much a health policy congress as a commercial trade show. Next edition: 19 to 21 May 2026, Paris.

**[Pharmagora Plus](/salons/pharmagora-plus-paris)** is the largest retail pharmacy trade show in France, organised each year in March at Paris Porte de Versailles by CloserStill Media. With 40 years of existence behind it, it brings together more than 12,000 healthcare professionals and nearly 400 exhibitors over two days. Its audience is highly targeted: pharmacy owners, locums and dispensary teams. Registration is free for healthcare professionals. The 2026 themes centre on the digital transformation of the pharmacy and the practical applications of AI. Next edition: 14 and 15 March 2026, Paris.

**[MedFIT](/salons/medfit-marseille)** is the leading European business convention dedicated to innovation partnerships in medical technologies and diagnostics. Organised by Eurasanté with Medicaps, Clubster NHL and Bio Valley France, it rotates each year between different French cities (Strasbourg, Lille, Grenoble, Marseille). Its format differs from a conventional trade show: the event is structured around pre-arranged BtoB meetings, pitch sessions and conferences. It brings together nearly 900 international players for its 10th edition, scheduled for December 2026 in Marseille. Its purpose: to connect large medtech firms, SMEs, startups, academics and investors. It is not the right place to sell a finished product; it is the right place to launch or accelerate a partnership.

**[Paris Healthcare Week](/salons/paris-healthcare-week-2026)** brings together each year in Paris the decision-makers across the entire French healthcare system: public and private hospitals, the national health insurance scheme, mutual insurers, local authorities and industry. It is a format that combines congress and exhibition, with a strong political and institutional dimension. It is particularly relevant for players selling to healthcare establishments and payers.

## Specialised and regional formats

The specialisation of the healthcare sector has fostered the emergence of segmented events that complement the major generalist trade shows.

**[Medi''Nov Connection](/salons/medi-nov-connection-lyon)** (Lyon, annually in March) is a business convention dedicated to medical devices and medtech, organised by the Lyonbiopole competitiveness cluster. Conferences, workshops and BtoB meetings structure the exchanges around the Lyon and Rhône-Alpes ecosystem, one of the densest in France.

**[Le Congrès ADF](/salons/congres-adf-paris)** (Paris, annually in November) is the leading dental surgery congress, organised by the Association Dentaire Française. It is one of the most heavily attended by practitioners and suppliers in the oral and dental sector, with a very active exhibition component.

**[Préventica](/salons/preventica-paris)** (Lyon, biennial) covers occupational health, safety and quality of working life. It addresses HR directors, occupational physicians and HSEQ managers, across a spectrum that extends beyond healthcare in the strict sense but concerns many occupational health players.

Other sector-specific formats round out the calendar: **[Rééduca](/salons/reeduca-paris)** (rehabilitation and physical medicine, Paris), **[SILMO](/salons/silmo-paris)** (optics and vision, Paris), and the **[Salon Infirmier](/salons/salon-infirmier-paris)** (Paris, every year). All offer free admission to healthcare professionals upon registration.

## The major European healthcare trade shows

For medical device manufacturers, healthcare software vendors and diagnostics players, three European trade shows structure the international market.

**[MEDICA](/salons/medica-dusseldorf)** (Düsseldorf, annually in November) is the world''s leading medical technology trade show. Organised by Messe Düsseldorf, its 2025 edition brought together more than 5,000 exhibitors from 72 countries and 83,000 trade visitors from 165 countries. It covers the entire chain: medical imaging, laboratory technology, diagnostics, digital health, medical AI, cybersecurity and hospital infrastructure. It is held alongside COMPAMED, the trade show for suppliers of components for medical device manufacturing. For a hospital supplier or a medical device manufacturer seeking access to the global market, MEDICA is unmissable.

**[Arab Health](/salons/arab-health-dubai)** (Dubai, annually in January) is the leading medical trade show in the Middle East and one of the largest in the world, with more than 3,450 exhibitors and 110,000 visitors representing over 180 countries. The Emirates serve as a re-export platform towards neighbouring markets, which makes it a strategic entry point for French players looking to position themselves in South-West Asia and Africa.

**[MedtecLIVE](/salons/medteclive-stuttgart)** (Stuttgart, annual) is a European trade show that has gained momentum in recent years, focused on medical device manufacturing and production innovations. It is particularly relevant for subcontractors and industrial players positioned on the manufacturing chain rather than on downstream commercialisation.

## Beyond trade shows: congresses, conventions and communities

The healthcare sector is one in which the boundary between trade show and medical congress is at its most porous. Understanding this distinction is helpful in choosing where to invest.

**Trade shows versus medical congresses.** Specialised medical congresses (cardiology, oncology, orthopaedics) bring practitioners together around scientific exchange and CPD (Continuing Professional Development). They often include an exhibition area, but their logic is primarily scientific. For an industry player, exhibiting there provides access to specialised prescribers: a different audience from that of a generalist trade show such as Santexpo, where hospital buyers and managers predominate.

**Expert trails and immersive formats within trade shows.** Santexpo has developed thematic "expert trails" organised by partner institutions (APSSIS for cybersecurity, Numeum for digital, Smart Buildings Alliance for the smart hospital). These guided formats within the trade show allow visitors to navigate the exhibitor offering with a business logic rather than a geographical one.

**Regional communities and clusters.** Lyonbiopole, Eurobiomed (Montpellier), Medicen (Paris), MEDICALPS (Alpine arc), Alsace BioValley: these competitiveness clusters organise, throughout the year, business days, regulatory seminars and investor meetings that fall outside the trade show calendar but deeply structure local ecosystems. For a medtech startup, these local networks are often more effective for finding industrial partners than an isolated presence at a major trade show.

## What Agoris covers in healthcare

Our Agoris Certified profiles for the healthcare sector focus on three elements often absent from conventional directories: the actual make-up of the visitor base (buyers, prescribers, operational staff, executives), the average investment ticket observed for various attendance formats, and the recent trend in footfall since the end of the Covid period, which has profoundly reshaped the landscape.

The raw visitor figure remains a very imperfect indicator in this sector. What matters is knowing who is in the room, at what point in their buying cycle, and whether your offering is aligned with their regulatory and budgetary constraints of the moment. That is what our verdicts seek to clarify.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='sante-pharma';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Sport and Outdoor', description_en='Sports equipment, outdoor gear, watersports, leisure, fitness, running kit', editorial_mdx_en='The sport and outdoor sector covers very different realities: consumer sporting goods, technical clothing, outdoor equipment (hiking, climbing, skiing, trail running), connected sports technologies, nutrition, fitness and wellbeing. This diversity is reflected in the trade show calendar, which draws a clear distinction between B2B sourcing events and consumer trade shows.

The key question for an exhibitor: am I looking to open accounts with specialist distributors and retailers, or am I targeting buyers from sports retail chains? These two approaches lead to different trade shows. For a professional visitor, the question is similar: am I coming to source products or to keep up with the latest trends in the sector?

## The leading trade shows in France

**Sport-Achat** is the largest French event dedicated to outdoor sports professionals, organised each January at [Alpexpo Grenoble](/lieux/alpexpo-grenoble). It is the ordering event where brands present their winter season collections: specialist retailers, resellers and purchasing managers from sports chains come here to place their seasonal orders. Its Grenoble base is no accident: the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes region accounts for a major share of the French outdoor industry.

**Le Salon des Sports et Parasports** ([Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles), held annually in November) is the event for sport professionals: elected officials, sports facility managers, clubs and federations, and suppliers of solutions and equipment. The next edition will take place from 24 to 26 November 2026. Its Forum Sports & Territoires brings together local authorities and public-sector sport stakeholders. It is the right place for suppliers selling to public sports facilities and professional clubs, rather than to retailers.

**Sportem** (Paris, Roland-Garros, annual) is the trade show for sports marketing and the spectator experience. It brings together around fifty exhibitors and the main French and European sports rights holders around ticketing, branded content, sponsorship and fan management. A more intimate format, with a highly qualified audience.

### Regional and specialist trade shows

The French outdoor sector has active regional clusters. Outdoor Sports Valley (Annecy) and Sporaltec (Grenoble) organise showrooms, business days and collective initiatives at international trade shows, notably the Camp de Base France at the Outdoor Show. For snow sports, ISPO Munich remains the benchmark for setting the order of winter collections, even though its attendance has shifted (see below). Specialist sector trade shows are multiplying: Bike Festival, running events, triathlon events, golf and watersports each have their own B2B gatherings.

## The major European and international trade shows

**ISPO Munich** (Munich, held annually in November-December, Messe München) has historically been the largest trade show in the world for the sport and sportswear industry, founded in 1970. The 2024 edition brought together more than 2,300 exhibitors from 50 countries and 55,000 trade visitors from 113 countries, with 65% international exhibitors and 72% international visitors. It is worth noting that attendance has fallen significantly compared with the pre-Covid period (80,000 visitors in 2019, 3,300 exhibitors in 2018). One point to watch: in 2025 Messe München announced the creation of a joint venture with the British group Raccoon Media Group to run ISPO, with a possible move to Amsterdam. Its trajectory is worth monitoring before committing a stand budget.

**Outdoor Show Friedrichshafen** (Friedrichshafen, Germany, annual, Messe Friedrichshafen) is the European benchmark for pure outdoor: hiking, climbing, camping, technical clothing. It brings together close to 1,000 exhibitors and 20,000 visitors, with a visitor base heavily concentrated in the German-speaking arc (Germany, Austria, Switzerland). For French brands, it is the entry trade show into the German outdoor market, the largest in Europe.

**FIBO** (Cologne, held annually in April, Reed Exhibitions) is the largest trade show in the world for fitness, wellbeing and health, with more than 1,100 exhibitors from 50 countries and 130,000 visitors. It covers gym equipment, sports nutrition, connected fitness technologies and solutions for club operators.

## Beyond the trade shows

The sport sector is structured around the sporting federations (FFF, FFR, FFN, FFA, etc.) and the CNOSF, which run their own professional events. The French sporttech ecosystem is coordinated by Sport numérique and the regional clusters (Outdoor Sports Valley, Sporaltec, Sportlab, etc.). The Assises du Sport, organised by the Ministry of Sport, are the sector''s annual political and strategic event. For equipment manufacturers and infrastructure suppliers, local authority tenders remain the main channel for awarding contracts, independently of the trade shows.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='sport-outdoor';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Tech & Digital', description_en='Technology, startups, AI, cloud, cybersecurity', editorial_mdx_en='The French tech ecosystem gave rise to Vivatech, which in less than ten years has established itself as the showcase event for European innovation. But Vivatech does not sum up the sector: for a B2B SaaS vendor, a cloud integrator, a data scale-up or a cybersecurity player, the relevant calendar looks very different from that of a consumer startup seeking investors.

This is precisely the challenge of the tech sector: the diversity of company profiles and commercial targets makes choosing trade shows far more strategic than elsewhere. IT Partners to reach resellers and integrators, Big Data & AI Paris for specialisation, All4Customer for the customer relationship ecosystem, E-commerce Paris for digital retail: each event has its own audience, its own entry ticket and its own logic of returns.

This page covers the main tech trade shows on the French calendar, the specialised formats by segment, and the major European and global references that French players cannot afford to ignore. For the Agoris Certified profiles, we specify who attends (decision-makers, operational staff, suppliers, investors), with what level of seniority, and what type of business is actually signed on site or in the weeks that follow.

Our conviction: the ROI of a tech trade show depends far more on the quality of the visitor than on their volume. A profile that gives you the visitor figure without qualifying its real composition does not help you decide.

## The benchmark trade shows in France

The French market for tech trade shows is one of the most fragmented. La French Tech created the conditions for a very dense events offering, where a large-format generalist event coexists with a multitude of vertical events, each with its own audience.

**[Vivatech](/salons/vivatech-paris-2026)** is the largest tech trade show in Europe, organised every year in June at Paris Expo Porte de Versailles by the Les Echos group and Publicis. Its 2025 edition set a new record with 180,000 visitors, 14,000 startups from 171 countries, more than 3,000 investors and 450 speakers, including several heads of state and CEOs of the world''s largest tech companies. More than 300 major announcements were made at this edition. Vivatech is at once a showcase for corporates exhibiting their innovation initiatives, a fundraising platform for startups and an event of international media visibility. It is less a classic commercial trade show than an event for positioning and visibility. Next edition: 17 to 20 June 2026, Paris.

**[IT Partners](/salons/it-partners-paris)** is the benchmark trade show for the IT channel in France, organised every year in February by RX France. It brings together brands, wholesalers, resellers, integrators and IT service companies around a format explicitly designed to generate business in IT distribution. Its visitors are made up of IT directors, sales managers and buyers from retail, local authorities, industry and services. In 2025 it moved to Paris La Défense Arena, with around 220 exhibitors per edition. It is the preferred tool for vendors and manufacturers wanting to activate their distribution networks in France.

**[Big Data & AI Paris](/salons/big-data-paris-2026)** is the French benchmark trade show on data and artificial intelligence in business, organised every year in September in Paris. It brings together the major international data and AI players, the pure players and scale-ups, as well as the most innovative startups. Its 2026 edition marks a deliberate refocus on large-account decision-makers: the aim is to double the share of visitors from large organisations and to qualitatively filter profiles in order to maximise the value of the encounters. It is the relevant place for data/AI solution providers selling to CIOs and business divisions, not for those addressing developers or startups.

**[All4Customer](/salons/all4customer-paris)** is the trade show dedicated to customer relationship management, digital marketing, data and e-commerce, organised every year at Paris Porte de Versailles. It is aimed at the marketing, CX and digital departments of large companies and mid-caps. Its positioning covers the entire CRM chain, from customer data collection through to marketing automation and conversational AI. It is the natural entry point for vendors of CRM, CDP, CCaaS and marketing automation solutions targeting buyers in large organisations.

## Specialised formats by segment

The growing specialisation of the tech ecosystem has given rise to segmented events that usefully complement the calendar of the major trade shows.

**[BIM World Paris](/salons/bim-world-paris)** (annual, Paris) is the benchmark event on digital technology and the digital transformation of building and infrastructure: digital modelling, construction-site data management, smart building. It is aimed at a very specific profile, at the intersection of tech and construction.

**E-commerce Paris** (annual, Paris) is the benchmark trade show for French digital commerce, bringing together online retail players, technical and logistics service providers, and marketing solutions for e-commerce. It is aimed at the e-commerce, digital and IT departments of retailers and pure players. It is where platform and tooling investment decisions are prepared.

The **French Tech community events** also structure the calendar outside the classic trade shows: scale-up lunches, themed meetings by vertical (fintech, healthtech, greentech, deeptech), investor meetups in major cities. These informal formats are often more effective for local business development than exhibition stands.

## The major European and global tech trade shows

For French tech players developing internationally, three events stand out as must-attends depending on their positioning.

**[CES](/salons/ces-las-vegas)** (Las Vegas, annual in January) is the world''s largest trade show for consumer and professional technological innovation, organised by the Consumer Technology Association. It brings together more than 4,000 exhibitors and 130,000 professional visitors from 160 countries. For French startups, it has become an essential international springboard: la French Tech has a strong presence there with regional delegations organised by the CCIs and competitiveness clusters. Its global media reach also makes it an unrivalled visibility tool for product launch years.

**[MWC Barcelona](/salons/mwc-barcelona)** is the world''s largest mobile connectivity event, organised every year in February or March by the GSMA at Fira Barcelona. Its 2025 edition brought together 109,000 participants from 205 countries and more than 2,900 exhibitors, with 1,200 speakers. It covers mobile telephony, 5G, AI applied to networks, industrial connectivity and smart mobility. The satellite event 4YFN (4 Years From Now) hosts more than 1,000 startup exhibitors and 900 investors in parallel. For players in telecoms, IoT and digital infrastructure, MWC is the structuring event of the year.

**[Web Summit](/salons/web-summit-lisbonne)** (Lisbon, annual in November) is one of the largest tech events in Europe, distinguished by its strongly startup- and investor-oriented format. It brings together more than 70,000 participants from 160 countries around conferences, pitches and encounters between founders and funds. Its visitor profile is quite different from that of Vivatech or MWC: younger, more early-stage and venture-capital oriented, less corporate and large-account. It is the right place for a startup looking for its next funding round or its first international partners.

## Beyond the trade shows: formats, communities and parallel events

Events culture in the tech sector is particularly dense and innovative. The major trade shows are often only the visible tip of an iceberg of parallel activities.

**Side events and after-parties.** Vivatech 2025 offered around fifty parallel events on the fringes of the trade show to extend conversations outside opening hours. This phenomenon is even more pronounced at MWC and CES, where some major deals are closed at private events organised by large groups on the fringes of the official trade show. For an exhibitor, understanding this calendar of side events is part of strategic preparation.

**Competitions and pitch nights.** Vivatech organises a Grand Pitch Night and a Demo Day every year, giving selected startups access to media and investor visibility far greater than that of a classic stand. MWC runs an innovation competition through the GSMA Ecosystem Accelerator. These awards have become important signals of credibility for startups in their negotiations with large accounts.

**Conferences and keynotes.** Vivatech 2025 featured 450 speakers across more than 300 talks. These conferences are part of the trade show''s value for visitors keeping an eye on strategic trends: many attend as much for the sessions as for the stands. For a vendor or consultant wanting to position themselves as an expert in their field, the conference circuit is often more profitable than the exhibition stand.

**Communities and accelerators.** Outside the trade shows, the French tech ecosystem comes to life around Station F, the French Tech Next40/120 programmes, the Bpifrance accelerators and regional hubs such as EuraTechnologies (Lille), Unitec (Bordeaux) and Minalogic (Grenoble). These structures organise meetings, demos and corporate events throughout the year that shape local partnerships well ahead of the major trade shows.

## What Agoris covers on tech

Our Agoris Certified analyses take the sector''s segmentation seriously. For each documented trade show, we specify who attends and with what level of seniority, what type of business is actually signed on site or in the weeks that follow, and what the difference is between the organiser''s messaging and the public feedback from exhibitors at past editions.

In a sector where hype is structural and where visitor figures often mix students, the curious and decision-makers, the composition of the audience is the most valuable piece of information. That is what our verdicts seek to clarify.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='tech-numerique';
UPDATE sectors SET name_en='Tourism and Hospitality', description_en='Tourism, hospitality, catering, leisure', editorial_mdx_en='Tourism is one of France''s leading economic sectors. Its calendar distinguishes professional B2B events (IFTM) from consumer-facing ones (Salon Mondial du Tourisme), two fundamentally different approaches.

## The benchmark trade shows in France

[IFTM Top Resa](/salons/iftm-top-resa) (Paris, annual in September, RX France) is the benchmark trade show for tourism professionals, bringing together the entire travel chain. Its Everywhere platform facilitates pre-scheduled business meetings. Next edition: 15-17 September 2026.

[Equip''Hôtel](/salons/equip-hotel-paris) (Paris, biennial in November, RX France) is the benchmark trade show for hospitality and catering equipment and technologies.

## The major European and global trade shows

[ITB Berlin](/salons/itb-berlin) (Berlin, annual in March, [Messe Berlin](/lieux/messe-berlin)) is the world''s largest tourism trade show, with more than 100,000 visitors and 5,000 exhibitors from 183 countries. WTM London (annual in November, RX France) brings together more than 50,000 participants. FITUR (Madrid, annual in January) is the Ibero-American tourism trade show.

The [Salon Mondial du Tourisme](/salons/salon-mondial-du-tourisme-paris) (Paris, [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles), annual in March) remains the benchmark consumer event for inbound travel agencies and tourist offices.

## Beyond the trade shows

The federations (Atout France, SNAV, UMIH) organise commercial workshops and roadshows that are often more effective than the major trade shows for direct business development.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='tourisme-hotellerie';

UPDATE venues SET description_en='Exhibition and conference centre in Grenoble with around 42,000 sqm of covered floor space. Alpexpo is a key venue for the technology and industrial trade shows of the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes region.', editorial_mdx_en='## Overview

Alpexpo, officially the **Parc Événementiel de Grenoble**, is the leading events venue of the Grenoble metropolitan area. Located on **avenue d''Innsbruck**, on the footprint of the former Grenoble-Mermoz aerodrome to the south-west of the city (Mistral / Eaux-Claires districts, close to the Grand''Place shopping centre), it offers around **42,000 sqm of covered exhibition space**, complemented by a conference centre (Alpes Congrès) and the Le Summum concert hall on the same site.

The venue was **inaugurated on 6 February 1968**, in the wake of the Grenoble Winter Olympic Games held that same year, and designed by the architect **Jean Prouvé**. The complex was extended in the late 1980s, and Alpes Congrès, the conference centre, was created in 1974.

Operations are today managed by **SPL Alpexpo** (a local public company, since 2019), whose shareholding is split between the **Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes Region (51%)**, **Grenoble-Alpes Métropole (35%)**, the **Isère Department (5%)** and the **City of Grenoble (9%)**. This regional governance makes the venue a structuring asset for the area''s attractiveness strategy.

Alpexpo is known as the stage for two major events of the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes region: **Mountain Planet**, the biennial international trade show for mountain development, and the **Foire de Grenoble**, the historic general-interest fair held each autumn.

## Halls

The venue has **3 modular exhibition halls**, totalling around **42,000 sqm** of covered floor space, complemented by the Alpes Congrès conference centre and the Le Summum concert hall.

- **Hall Jean Marandjian**: around **32,540 sqm**, the main exhibition hall, configurable with full partitioning or as a single open floor for high-attendance consumer trade shows.
- **Hall 89**: around **8,726 sqm**, a complementary hall for medium-sized trade shows or extensions.
- **Hall Mermoz**: complementary floor space, named in tribute to the former aerodrome that occupied the site.
- **Alpes Congrès conference centre**: 12 modular rooms. The main **Dauphine** room offers up to **970 seats** in plenary configuration, complemented by the Oisans (240), Stendhal (180), Écrins (up to 650 combined), Bayard, Berlioz and Lesdiguières rooms for workshops and parallel sessions.
- **Le Summum**: a concert hall inaugurated in 1988, with a capacity of up to **5,000 seats**, used for major events and concerts.

## Access & transport

- **Address**: Avenue d''Innsbruck, 38100 Grenoble.
- **Tram**: line **A**, **Pôle Sud - Alpexpo** stop, connecting with the TAG metropolitan network.
- **Bus**: Chrono C3 (Alpexpo stop); Chrono C6, C8 and lines 12, 65, 67 (Grand''Place stop).
- **Motorway**: from the A48 (Lyon), A49 (Valence), A41 (Chambéry) or A51 (Sisteron), join the **Rocade Sud**, **exit 6 Alpexpo**.
- **Grenoble TGV station**: around **15 minutes** from Alpexpo by tram, served by direct TGV trains from Paris, Lyon and Marseille.
- **Lyon-Saint-Exupéry Airport**: around **1 hour** by road, the main international gateway for the Grenoble area.
- **Grenoble-Alpes-Isère Airport** (Saint-Étienne-de-Saint-Geoirs): around 45 minutes away, open mainly during the winter season.
- **Parking**: **free parking** on site, several thousand spaces, complemented by the car parks of the neighbouring Grand''Place shopping centre. Exact capacity not publicly released by the operator.
- **Accommodation**: hotel options are concentrated in Grenoble city centre and around the station, 10-15 minutes away by tram. Detailed hotel capacity not publicly released by SPL Alpexpo.

## Services

- **Catering**: event catering provided on site, for both exhibitors and visitors, with an offer sized for peak attendance (Mountain Planet, Foire de Grenoble).
- **Business centre and meeting rooms**: facilities for exhibitor meetings, press points and organising committees, housed in Alpes Congrès (12 modular rooms) and in the Espace 1968 (a 456-seat amphitheatre, rooms for 20 to 500 people).
- **Accommodation**: no hotel on site. Organisers negotiate hotel agreements in central Grenoble (10-15 minutes away by tram).
- **WiFi and connectivity**: coverage announced across the whole site, with exhibitor terms to be confirmed in the technical manual of each trade show.

2026-2027 pricing details (hall hire, technical options, exhibitor services): not publicly released, quotes available on request from SPL Alpexpo.

## News

**French Alps 2030 bid**: the French bid for the 2030 Winter Olympic and Paralympic Games, led in particular by the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes and Provence-Alpes-Côte d''Azur regions, was **selected by the IOC on 24 July 2024** at its 142nd Session. Grenoble and its events ecosystem, including Alpexpo, are positioned within the orbit of the metropolitan arrangements around the 2030 deadline, although no precise Olympic hosting programme (event, ceremony or media hub) has been published to date. Data on the final decision not publicly available.

This prospect is fuelling the local debate on **modernising the venue**: refurbishment of the halls, upgrading of the conference centre, and integration into the metropolitan transport scheme to be strengthened by 2030. A firm timetable and budget envelope have not been publicly released by SPL Alpexpo or Grenoble-Alpes Métropole to date.

## Flagship trade shows

Alpexpo hosts several structuring events for the mountain, energy, industry and consumer sectors. Selection listed on Agoris:

- [Mountain Planet](/salons/mountain-planet) (mountain development and equipment, 21-23 April 2026): a biennial international trade show, a global showcase for resorts and ski-lift manufacturers, organised in partnership with **Domaines Skiables de France**.
- [Bois Énergie](/salons/salon-bois-energie) (wood-energy sector, 17-19 March 2026): the benchmark trade show for wood-fired heating, pellets and energy forestry.
- [SEPEM Industries Sud-Est](/salons/sepem-grenoble) (industry, subcontracting, industrial services, 24-26 November 2026): the regional edition of the SEPEM circuit, anchored in the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes industrial area.

The venue also historically hosts the **Foire de Grenoble** (the annual general-interest fair in November) and the Grenoble **Salon Habitat**, neither of which is covered by Agoris to date.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='alpexpo-grenoble';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Trade fair district in Emilia-Romagna (around 375,000 sqm), operated by BolognaFiere S.p.A. (born from the Ente Autonomo created in 1947, with the grounds inaugurated in 1965). Host to Cosmoprof Worldwide Bologna, Cersaie, Lineapelle, Marca, SAIE, EIMA International and the Bologna Children''s Book Fair.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

BolognaFiere is one of Italy''s leading exhibition grounds, located in the northern district of Bologna (Fiera District) in Emilia-Romagna. The site totals **around 375,000 sqm of surface area**, including a substantial covered portion and outdoor spaces, and is organised around a business district marked by the **seven towers designed by Kenzō Tange** (visible from the A14 motorway, delivered progressively from the early 1980s onwards, following the Tange project initiated in 1967).

The Fiera District''s **urban master plan** stems from a competition won by the Italian architects **Leonardo Benevolo, Tommaso Giura Longo and Carlo Melograni** ([Wikipedia IT, Fiera di Bologna](https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fiera_di_Bologna)). Kenzō Tange is the author of the **seven-towers project** integrated into the plan, as well as of a Northern master plan first presented in 1970 for the city''s northern district.

The trade fair district is operated by **BolognaFiere S.p.A.**, a joint-stock company formed in **2002** from the transformation of the **Ente Autonomo delle Fiere di Bologna** (created in **1947** at the initiative of the Bologna Chamber of Commerce). The first block of the modern grounds was **inaugurated in 1965** for the 29th edition of the Bologna Fair.

The current principal shareholders are the **Comune di Bologna** (~14.7%), the **Camera di Commercio di Bologna** (~14.7%), the **Emilia-Romagna Region** (~11.6%), the **Città Metropolitana di Bologna** (~11.3%), the **Fondazione Cassa di Risparmio in Bologna** and **GL events Italia** (~7.7%), alongside other investors. Since **December 2023**, the ordinary shares of BolognaFiere S.p.A. have been admitted to trading on **Euronext Growth Milan, Professional Segment**.

Beyond the Bologna grounds, BolognaFiere holds international events activities, notably through the **Cosmoprof** network (Cosmoprof North America in **Las Vegas**, Cosmoprof Asia in **Hong Kong**, Cosmoprof India in **Mumbai**) and numerous specialised trade shows run in partnership across more than 10 countries.

## Halls

The grounds comprise **18 padiglioni** (halls), all cabled and air-conditioned ([AEFI, BolognaFiere](https://www.aefi.it/it/quartiere/bologna-fiere-s-p-a/), [Wikipedia IT](https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fiera_di_Bologna)). The numbering follows the site''s expansion history:

- **Historic core inaugurated in 1965**: padiglioni 21, 22, 25, 26, 31, 32
- **2000s extensions**: padiglioni 16-18 (2004), 14-15 (2008)
- **Recent extensions**: padiglioni 29-30 (2018)

The halls are linked by covered galleries that serve the entire site from several independent entrances, including the **Costituzione** entrance (city side) and the **Michelino** entrance (motorway side). In total, the site has **5 independent entrances** for visitors and exhibitors ([BolognaFiere, Location](https://bolognafiere.it/en/location-2/bolognafiere/)).

The site also includes several **service centres** (Centro Servizi, Palazzo dei Congressi with its adjoining Europauditorium auditorium) as well as a set of meeting rooms for conferences and side events.

## Access & transport

**Train**: **Bologna Centrale** station, served by the Italian high-speed trains **Frecciarossa** and **Italo** and the entire regional network, is located around **6 km** from the grounds. Bologna is one of northern Italy''s main railway hubs, with direct connections to Milan, Rome, Florence, Venice and Naples.

**Dedicated shuttle**: during major trade shows, a **direct shuttle bus** links Bologna Centrale to the Costituzione entrance of the grounds.

**Car**: the grounds are accessible from the **A14 motorway** via the dedicated **Bologna Fiera** exit, as well as via the **Bologna tangenziale** (ring road). The site has around **14,500 parking spaces** ([BolognaFiere, Location](https://bolognafiere.it/en/location-2/bolognafiere/)), split between visitor car parks, exhibitor car parks and heavy-goods logistics areas.

**Airport**: the international airport **Bologna-Guglielmo Marconi** is around 6 km from the grounds. An automated **People Mover** link connects the airport to Bologna Centrale in around 7 minutes.

**Urban bus**: several lines of the **TPER** network serve the entrances to the grounds from Bologna city centre, in addition to the event shuttle.

## Services

**Catering**: seated dining and quick-service food points, cafés and catering corners distributed across the halls and galleries during trade shows. An on-stand catering offer is available to exhibitors through the service providers listed by BolognaFiere.

**Exhibitor services**: business centre, equipped meeting rooms, technical services (electricity, water, telecoms), furniture hire and turnkey stands, plus logistics and customs assistance for international exhibitors.

**Connectivity**: **WiFi** available across the halls and reception areas, sized to suit major international trade shows.

**Nearby accommodation**: several hotels are located immediately next to the grounds, including the **Savoia Hotel Regency** and the **Sheraton Bologna Hotel & Conference Center**. Bologna''s historic centre, around 15 minutes away by taxi, concentrates most of the 4-5 star hotel offer.

## News

Since **2018**, BolognaFiere has been running a multi-year programme to **modernise and expand the grounds** worth around **100 million euros**, including the progressive renovation of the historic padiglioni and the development of a **new western hub (West Hall)** intended to increase usable exhibition space and improve visitor flow during major events ([BolognaFiere, Location](https://bolognafiere.it/en/location-2/bolognafiere/)).

Internationally, BolognaFiere is consolidating its position as a **global cosmetics platform**: the company directly operates **Cosmoprof North America** in Las Vegas, **Cosmoprof Asia** in Hong Kong and **Cosmoprof India** in Mumbai, in partnership with local players in the sector.

## Flagship trade shows

BolognaFiere hosts several of the largest international trade shows held in Italy, a significant share of them **world leaders** in their respective sectors:

- **B2B cosmetics**: [Cosmoprof Worldwide Bologna](/salons/cosmoprof-bologna), the world''s leading B2B trade show for the cosmetics and beauty industry.
- **Ceramics & materials**: **Cersaie**, the international trade show for ceramic tiles for architecture and bathroom furnishings.
- **Retail & private label**: **Marca by BolognaFiere**, Europe''s benchmark trade show for retailer brands (private label).
- **Construction & building**: **SAIE**, the Italian trade show for construction, infrastructure and urban renewal.
- **Leather & leather goods**: **Lineapelle**, the world''s trade show for the leather, tanning and accessories supply chain for fashion and interiors.
- **Children''s publishing**: **Bologna Children''s Book Fair**, the international benchmark event for children''s publishing and adaptation rights.
- **Agricultural machinery**: **EIMA International**, the biennial trade show for agricultural machinery and equipment, among the world''s largest in the sector.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='bolognafiere';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='A prestigious events venue located beneath the Louvre Pyramid. With 8,800 sqm of modular floor space, the Carrousel du Louvre hosts art and design trade shows alongside high-end events.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The Carrousel du Louvre is an underground events venue located beneath the Carrousel esplanade, between the Louvre''s Cour Napoléon and the Tuileries Garden. It was inaugurated in **1993** as part of the second phase of the **Grand Louvre**, complementing the Pyramid project by **Ieoh Ming Pei** launched in 1989. The layout combines the **inverted pyramid** designed by Pei (a monolithic glass roof pointing towards the ground at the heart of the underground junction) and the **shopping arcade** designed by the architect **Michel Macary**, connected by a direct passageway to the entrance hall of the Musée du Louvre.

The site is operated by **Viparis**, a joint venture owned in equal shares by the **CCI Paris Île-de-France** and **Unibail-Rodamco-Westfield**, which brings together 10 events sites across the Paris region, including Paris Expo [Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles), [Paris Nord Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte) and the [Palais des Congrès de Paris](/lieux/palais-des-congres-paris). The Carrousel du Louvre plays a specific role within this portfolio: a **prestigious small-format venue in central Paris**, positioned for sector trade shows with a strong image component (luxury, cosmetics, art, design, high-end gastronomy), fashion shows and corporate conventions with limited headcounts.

Attendance and the floor space marketed are not published by Viparis as a consolidated annual figure for this site specifically. *Data not available for annual volumes.*

## Halls

According to Viparis, **Les Salles du Carrousel** offer approximately **6,635 sqm** of modular indoor spaces, organised around a central foyer and four main spaces. The site is smaller in volume than the other Viparis venues: its value proposition rests on **modularity**, the **prestige of the address** and the **direct connection to the Musée du Louvre**.

Spaces published by Viparis:

- **Espace Le Nôtre**: **1,900 sqm**, ceiling height up to 6.27 m, fitted with **retractable tiered seating** (500 to 1,600 seats) for plenary, conference or fashion-show configurations.
- **Espace Delorme**: **1,600 sqm**, height up to 6.33 m, also fitted with **retractable tiered seating** (500 to 1,200 seats). Can be combined with Gabriel to reach 2,500 sqm.
- **Foyer**: **1,310 sqm**, height up to 5.30 m, a divisible reception and welcome space structuring the flow between the halls.
- **Espace Soufflot**: **925 sqm**, height up to 6.20 m, fitted with a catering pantry and dedicated service circulation.
- **Espace Gabriel**: **750 sqm**, height up to 6.33 m, including tiered seating for 520.

The venue as a whole accommodates **500 to 4,000 people** depending on the configuration (seated conference, dinner or cocktail reception). Detailed technical plans and capacity grids by configuration are made available to organisers on request from Viparis.

## Access & transport

The site benefits from a dense central-Paris transport network, unusual for an events venue of this nature.

- **Address**: 99 rue de Rivoli, 75001 Paris. Main entrance via rue de Rivoli, additional access via 1, place du Palais Royal.
- **Métro**: **Palais Royal - Musée du Louvre** station on lines **1** and **7**, with direct underground access to the Carrousel without coming up to street level.
- **RATP bus**: lines **21, 24, 27, 39, 68, 72, 76, 85, 95**, with several stops in the immediate vicinity (Musée du Louvre, Palais Royal, Rivoli - Pont Neuf).
- **RER A**: **Châtelet - Les Halles** station roughly 5 minutes'' walk away via the Tuileries or rue de Rivoli.
- **Car**: the **Carrousel - Louvre car park** with around **670 spaces**, managed by Indigo, with direct access from avenue du Général Lemonnier and an underground connection to the site.
- **Taxis and private-hire vehicles**: drop-off recommended at place du Palais Royal or rue de Rivoli.
- **Museum connection**: the entrance hall of the **Musée du Louvre** is directly accessible from the Carrousel shopping arcade, a distinctive feature for programmes combining a professional event with a cultural visit.

**Accessible (PRM)** access is provided by lifts from rue de Rivoli and the car park. Goods access conditions, set-up times and HGV traffic rules are available on request from Viparis.

## Services

The services offering combines Viparis facilities integrated into the site with the services of the Carrousel **shopping arcade**, which is open to the public throughout the day.

- **Catering**: the Carrousel shopping arcade houses a **food court** (Restaurants du Monde) and several fast-food outlets open throughout the arcade''s opening hours. For event formats, Viparis partner caterers handle cocktail receptions, gala dinners and VIP room service.
- **Shopping arcade**: around forty shops (fashion, accessories, art bookshop, jewellery, design), accessible to participants outside programme hours.
- **Business centre**: modular meeting rooms within the complex, by reservation through Viparis.
- **Technical services**: audiovisual control, security, hostess reception, cloakroom, signage and utilities, provided or coordinated by Viparis.
- **Luxury hotels**: a high concentration of 4- and 5-star hotels in the immediate vicinity (Tuileries district, place Vendôme, faubourg Saint-Honoré), well suited to international delegations and the site''s luxury positioning.

Pricing terms and exhibitor service grids are available on commercial request from Viparis. *Data not publicly available.*

## News

The Carrousel du Louvre is part of the multi-year **Viparis 2030** investment plan, but the details of works specific to the site are not communicated publicly to date. No extension works have been announced, as the land is constrained by its interlocking with the Musée du Louvre and the Carrousel esplanade.

The site''s commercial trajectory has consolidated since the mid-2010s around its **beauty and cosmetics** and **art, design and luxury** positioning. On the cosmetics side, the Carrousel hosts several structuring events for the French sector (B2B sourcing, packaging, formulation), driven in particular by Cosmetic Valley and Reed Expositions.

*Data not publicly available for the details of Fashion Week editions held at the Carrousel: the official catwalk programme of the Fédération de la Haute Couture et de la Mode calendar is spread across multiple venues and changes each season.*

## Flagship trade shows

The Carrousel du Louvre hosts a programme oriented towards the **beauty and cosmetics sector** and **art, design and luxury**, consistent with its prestigious central-Paris positioning. A selection listed on Agoris:

- [MakeUp in Paris](/salons/makeup-in-paris) (make-up, cosmetics subcontracting and suppliers, 17-18 June 2026): an annual B2B beauty sourcing trade show.
- [Beyond Beauty Paris](/salons/beyond-beauty-paris) (cosmetics and skincare, 8-9 September 2026): an annual B2B trade show positioned on product innovation and supplier sourcing.
- [Cosmetic 360](/salons/cosmetic-360) (cosmetics innovation, 14-15 October 2026): a B2B convention organised by **Cosmetic Valley**, dedicated to the R&D and innovation ecosystem of the French sector.
- [Les Thermalies](/salons/les-thermalies-paris) (water, wellness, spa and thermal treatments, 21-24 January 2027): an annual trade show for the general public and professionals, a reference for the thalassotherapy and thermal sector.

The site has also historically hosted art and design trade shows (Art Paris held some editions at the Carrousel before moving to the [Grand Palais](/lieux/grand-palais-paris)), corporate conventions and shows from the Paris fashion calendar. This programming is not covered by Agoris to date.

For planning a professional event in the heart of Paris that does not require the volume of an exhibition park, the main central-Paris alternative is the [Palais des Congrès de Paris](/lieux/palais-des-congres-paris) (40,000 sqm, Porte Maillot, with greater plenary capacity). For higher-volume formats: [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles) (15th arrondissement) or [Paris Nord Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte) (Seine-Saint-Denis, with access to Roissy).', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='carrousel-du-louvre';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Located in the Cité Internationale district, the Centre de Congrès de Lyon offers 26,000 sqm of modular space on the banks of the Rhône. A sought-after venue for medical and scientific congresses.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The **Centre de Congrès de Lyon** sits within the **Cité Internationale**, in the **6th arrondissement**, between a bend of the **Rhône** and the **Parc de la Tête d''Or**. The site is part of the Cité Internationale development project designed by architect **Renzo Piano** (RPBW), delivered in several phases between **1996 and 2006**. The Centre is operated by **GL Events Venues** under a public service delegation awarded by the **Métropole de Lyon**, which owns the site.

The site spans roughly **24,000 sqm** of modular space, combining **auditoriums**, **breakout rooms** and **exhibition areas**. Its positioning is clearly geared towards **medical and scientific congresses**, **corporate conventions** and **cultural events** (concerts, screenings), with a residual share of **trade shows**. The riverside location on the Rhône, the proximity of the Parc de la Tête d''Or and the presence of an integrated hotel complex make it one of the most recognisable urban congress venues in France.

## Halls

The site is built around a large auditorium, two further auditoriums and a set of modular rooms, complemented by substantial exhibition space.

- **Amphithéâtre 3000** (Salle 3000): around **3,000 seats** in theatre configuration, the site''s main plenary hall and one of the largest congress auditoriums in France.
- **Auditorium Lumière**: around **900 seats**, the site''s second plenary hall.
- **Auditorium Pasteur**: around **300 seats**, an intermediate conference format.
- **35 modular breakout rooms**, with capacities from 50 to 450 seats.
- **Exhibition areas** of around **8,400 sqm** dedicated to trade shows and exhibitions attached to congresses.

The spaces are connected by a central hall and allow several events to be staged simultaneously, with a total capacity of up to 19,000 people.

## Access and transport

The site benefits from multimodal connections provided by the Métropole de Lyon''s TCL networks.

- **Tram**: line **T1**, stop **Cité Internationale - Centre de Congrès**. Line **T4** extended to the Cité Internationale, also accessible via **Charpennes**.
- **Bus**: lines **C1**, **C2**, **C5** and **70** serve the Cité Internationale directly.
- **Car**: access from the **A42** and **A43**, Cité Internationale exit; northern ring road.
- **Parking**: underground car park of the **Cité Internationale**, with direct access to the Centre de Congrès.
- **Airport**: **Lyon-Saint-Exupéry** around 30 minutes away by car, with the **Rhônexpress** shuttle from **Lyon Part-Dieu** station.
- **Cycling and soft mobility**: Vélo''v station and landscaped banks of the Rhône.

The official address is **50 Quai Charles de Gaulle, 69006 Lyon**.

## Services

The Centre de Congrès operates as an integrated events complex within the Cité Internationale.

- **Adjacent accommodation**: the **Marriott Lyon Cité Internationale** (formerly Hilton Lyon) within the Cité Internationale, along with the **Crowne Plaza Lyon Cité Internationale**, both within walking distance of the Centre.
- **Catering**: restaurants and bars within the Cité Internationale, plus a wider offering across the 6th arrondissement (the banks of the Rhône, the Parc de la Tête d''Or).
- **Business centre** and congress services: equipped meeting rooms, audiovisual services, dedicated **GL Events Venues** teams.
- **WiFi** throughout the site, fibre-optic connectivity and dynamic signage.
- **GL Events event services**: concierge desk, technical teams, integrated management of audiovisual and logistics service providers.

## News

The Centre de Congrès is part of a strategy of continuous modernisation led by **GL Events Venues** in conjunction with the **Métropole de Lyon**.

- **Strong scientific programme**: the site regularly hosts the annual congresses of French and European learned societies in **cardiology**, **oncology** and other medical specialities.
- **GL Events CSR approach**: an environmental policy aligned with the standard of the GL Events Venues group, which operates several major congress venues in France.

## Flagship trade shows

The Centre de Congrès de Lyon is positioned above all around **professional and medical congresses**, **corporate conventions** and **cultural events**, rather than trade shows with a large exhibition component. Among the site''s key fixtures:

- **Medical and scientific congresses**: annual gatherings of learned societies in **cardiology**, **oncology**, **ophthalmology** and other specialities, including several European congresses held on a rotating basis.
- [Innorobo by SIDO](/salons/innorobo-by-sido): the meeting point for professional robotics and industrial technology players, organised by **Infopro Digital Trade Shows**.
- **Corporate conventions** and international symposia in the Lumière and Pasteur auditoriums.
- **Concerts, screenings and corporate evenings**: the modularity of the Amphithéâtre 3000 allows for a complementary cultural programme, notably as part of the Festival Lumière.

Worth noting for B2B exhibitors: **Pollutec**, France''s leading environmental trade show, is held at **Eurexpo Lyon** and not at the Centre de Congrès. The site remains geared above all towards **congresses and conventions** rather than trade shows with a large exhibition component.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='centre-de-congres-de-lyon';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Urban convention centre in Reims, around 15,000 m² across three levels, a 10-minute walk from Reims Centre station. Managed by Reims Events (GL events).', editorial_mdx_en='The Centre des Congrès de Reims is an events venue located right in the heart of the city, around a ten-minute walk from Reims Centre station. Designed by the architect Claude Vasconi and inaugurated in 1994, the building offers around 15,000 m² across three levels and hosts congresses, conventions, seminars and trade shows. Its largely glazed façade looks out over the Patte d''Oie park. It is now managed by Reims Events (the GL events group).

## Halls and surface areas

The Centre des Congrès spans around 15,000 m² spread over three levels. It comprises two auditoriums (356 and 720 seats), around ten committee and meeting rooms, as well as an exhibition space of around 2,500 m². Its modularity makes it suitable for hosting plenary sessions as well as mid-sized trade shows.

## Access and transport

The centre is located at 12 boulevard du Général Leclerc, around a 10-minute walk from Reims Centre station. The Champagne-Ardenne TGV station, in Bezannes (around 5 km away), is connected to the city centre by tram and TER. By car, access is via the A4 and A26 motorways. An underground car park with 232 spaces is available, supplemented by an exhibitor car park with around 75 spaces.

## Services

The centre has catering areas and offers the standard services of an urban convention centre (reception, audiovisual equipment, modular rooms). Its central location provides immediate access to a range of hotels, cafés and restaurants, as well as to the historic centre of Reims. To discover the city''s other events, see the [Reims](/villes/reims) page.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='centre-des-congres-reims';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='The leading congress centre of France''s Grand Ouest region, opened in 1992 in the centre of Nantes, opposite the TGV station. Around 6,000 sq m of modular exhibition space arranged around the Grande Halle, three auditoriums and some thirty meeting rooms.', editorial_mdx_en='La Cité, the Centre des Congrès de Nantes (formerly the Cité des Congrès), is the leading congress centre of the Grand Ouest region. Opened in 1992 and located right in the city centre, in the Euronantes business district, opposite the TGV station, it hosts congresses, conventions and trade shows. La Cité is arranged around the Grande Halle and offers around 6,000 sq m of modular exhibition space, complemented by three auditoriums and some thirty meeting rooms.

## Halls and surface areas

La Cité offers around 6,000 sq m of modular exhibition space arranged around the Grande Halle. The venue has three auditoriums, the largest of which seats up to 2,000 people, as well as some thirty committee and meeting rooms (from 15 to 300 seats).

## Access and transport

Address: 5 rue de Valmy, 44000 Nantes, in the Euronantes district.

La Cité is located opposite the Nantes TGV station (north exit). Tram line 1, "Duchesse Anne - Château des Ducs de Bretagne" stop. Busway line 4, "Cité Internationale des Congrès" stop. The airport shuttle serves the "Lieu Unique" stop, in the immediate vicinity.

By car, the site has an underground car park with 420 spaces, complemented by more than 2,000 spaces in the surrounding car parks.

## Services

In addition to its exhibition and meeting spaces, La Cité offers on-site catering. Its city-centre location, in the immediate vicinity of the station and numerous hotels, makes accommodation and travel easier for attendees. The district offers many services and shops within walking distance.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='cite-des-congres-nantes';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Exhibition and events venue located at Porte de Champerret in the 17th arrondissement of Paris, operated by Viparis. Three modular ground-level halls host trade shows, fairs and congresses in the heart of Paris.', editorial_mdx_en='Espace Champerret is an exhibition and events venue located in the 17th arrondissement of Paris, at Porte de Champerret. Operated by Viparis, it hosts trade shows, fairs, congresses and conventions in ground-level halls that are modular and independent. Its location within the city limits, alongside the ring road and directly connected to public transport, makes it a sought-after site for medium-sized events in the heart of Paris.

## Halls and surface areas

The site features three ground-level exhibition halls, independent and complementary. The total exhibition surface area is estimated at approximately 8,450 m². The venue also offers conference rooms and a dedicated reception area.

## Access and transport

Address: 6 rue Jean Oestreicher, 75017 Paris. The entrance is located at square Auguste Balagny.

- Underground: line 3, Porte de Champerret station (exit 1)
- Tram: line T3b, Porte de Champerret station
- RER: line C, Pereire-Levallois station
- Bus: lines 84, 92, 39, 163, 164, 165 and PC, Porte de Champerret stop
- Car: direct access from the Porte de Champerret exit of the ring road

## Services

The venue offers modular spaces for catering and visitor reception. Numerous hotels and dining options are available in the immediate vicinity, in the 17th arrondissement and in Levallois-Perret.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='espace-champerret';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Exhibition and convention centre in Laval, opened in 2021: 5,000 sqm indoors, 10,000 sqm outdoors, modular halls (Mayenne 4,500 seats, Pégase 1,500 seats). Notably hosts Laval Virtual every spring.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

Espace Mayenne is a versatile events venue opened in 2021 in Laval (Mayenne), owned by the **Département de la Mayenne**. The architectural design is by **Hérault Arnod Architectures**. The construction cost amounted to **42 million euros**.

The site combines two large multi-purpose halls (concerts, sport, trade shows), a convention hall, modular meeting rooms and **10,000 sqm of outdoor space**. Its remit is regional: professional and consumer trade shows, concerts and live shows, and sporting competitions. The first event hosted was a Tour de France stage finish on 30 June 2021; the official opening took place on 23 October 2021.

## Halls

Espace Mayenne is laid out across several modular spaces that can be combined to suit the needs of the event.

- **Salle Mayenne**: main modular hall with **1,000 to 5,000 seats**, standing/seated configurations and sports formats.
- **Salle Pégase**: secondary hall accommodating **up to 1,900 seats** depending on configuration.
- **Convention hall**: a **493-seat** auditorium for conventions and seminars.
- **3 modular meeting rooms**: from **75 to 225 sqm** each, dedicated to workshops, training sessions and break-out sessions.
- **Reception spaces**: from 70 to 1,800 people.
- **Outdoor spaces**: **10,000 sqm** complemented by a velodrome and sports pitches.

## Getting there and transport

- **Car**: A81 motorway exit, 5 minutes from the site.
- **Train**: Laval station on the Paris-Brest high-speed line, 10 minutes from the site by bus. Paris-Laval in around 1h15 by TGV.
- **Plane**: Laval-Entrammes airport, 15 minutes away.
- **Bus**: TUL network line serving the area.

The official address is **2 rue Joséphine Baker, 53000 Laval**.

## Services

Espace Mayenne combines the standard events functions of a regional venue:

- Event catering and catering offering.
- Modular rooms for break-outs, workshops and working meetings running alongside a trade show or convention.
- Multi-event configuration for simultaneous events (main trade show + convention + meetings).
- An integrated velodrome and sports pitches for the sporting competitions hosted.

## News

The site hosts an annual volume of around **200 to 220 event days**, split between professional events (~160 days), concerts and live shows (30 to 35) and sporting events (20 to 25). Among the recurring fixtures identified for 2026: the **Salon de l''Habitat et du Jardin** (11-13 September 2026), the **French Table Tennis Championships** (12-14 June 2026), the **European Climbing Championships** (28-30 August 2026), the Geek Life Festival, and a regular cultural programme (concerts, comedy, live shows).

## Flagship trade shows

- [Laval Virtual](/salons/laval-virtual): the largest European event devoted to extended reality (XR), three days in April, with around 180 exhibitors and 5,000 attendees announced for the 2027 edition (7-9 April).

The **Salon de l''Habitat et du Jardin** (mid-September) and the **Geek Life Festival** are also among the recurring fixtures hosted at the site, with no dedicated Agoris listing to date.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='espace-mayenne';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='France''s second-largest exhibition centre with 125,000 m² of exhibition space, located on the outskirts of Lyon. Eurexpo hosts more than 50 trade and consumer shows every year.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

According to GL Events Venues, Eurexpo Lyon is **France''s second-largest exhibition centre** after Paris-Nord Villepinte, with around **140,000 m² of covered space** (following the delivery of Hall 7 in 2018) and **51,000 m² of outdoor areas** on a **110-hectare** site in Chassieu, to the east of the Lyon metropolitan area.

The centre was inaugurated on **29 September 1984** by S.E.P.E.L. (Société d''Exploitation du Parc des Expositions de Lyon) to replace the former Palais de la Foire near the Parc de la Tête d''Or, which had become unsuited to the volume of Lyon''s trade shows. The land is owned by the **Métropole de Lyon** (through the public body COFIL); operations are run by **SEPEL** (Société d''Exploitation du Parc des Expositions de Lyon), **53% held by the CCI Lyon Métropole** and **47% by GL Events**, the latter having taken operational control since **2006-2007** within the **GL Events Venues** division, which also organises several of the site''s flagship shows (Global Industrie, Sirha, Solutrans, Paysalia, BePOSITIVE, Rocalia, Eurobois).

Eurexpo hosts **more than 50 trade and consumer shows a year** along with some sixty corporate events, and ranks among Europe''s top 15 exhibition centres according to the CCI Lyon Métropole.

## Halls

The centre comprises **7 modular halls interconnected** by covered galleries, ranging from **3,208 m² to 14,824 m²** depending on configuration, with **3 independent entrances** allowing several distinct events to be hosted simultaneously.

Reference surface areas published by GL Events Lyon:

- **Hall 1**: around 6,393 m².
- **Hall 2** (sub-spaces 2.1 and 2.2): sub-space 2.2 of around 6,246 m², 2.1 delivered in 2015.
- **Hall 4** (sub-spaces 4.1, 4.2, 4.3): Hall 4.2 of 12,000 m² column-free, favoured by organisers for configurations with no structural constraints.
- **Hall 7**: around 9,500 m², inaugurated in October 2018, linked to Hall 6 by two footbridges.
- **Espace Paul Bocuse**: 10,000 m² coupled with an auditorium seating 4,200, delivered in 2011.
- **Espace Confluence**: 7,000 m² dedicated to the conference format.

The per-hall surface breakdown (Halls 3, 5, 6) is not published by GL Events Venues in open access: quotations and technical plans are available on request from the organiser. **26 meeting rooms** from 43 to 654 m² complete the offering, with capacity for 50 to 600 people per room.

## Access & transport

- **Address**: Boulevard de l''Europe, 69680 Chassieu.
- **Tram T5**: *Eurexpo* terminus at the end of the line, from the *Grange-Blanche* station (Lyon 8th arrondissement, interchange with metro line D). The line runs at increased frequency during show attendance peaks.
- **Bus**: line C200 and the 100 Direct Eurexpo line from *Carré de Soie* (metro A) and Lyon Part-Dieu station during consumer shows.
- **Event shuttles**: arranged by certain organisers (Sirha, Pollutec, Global Industrie) from Part-Dieu and the airport during show days; arrangements to be checked in each edition''s programme.
- **Lyon-Saint-Exupéry Airport**: around **20 minutes** away by car (≈ 10 km); the Saint-Exupéry TGV station is served by TGV and OuiGo, then taxi or shuttle.
- **TGV stations**: Lyon Part-Dieu and Lyon-Perrache around 20 minutes away by public transport or taxi.
- **Motorways**: A43 *Eurexpo* exit, A46 eastern Lyon bypass, direct connection to the Lyon ring road.
- **Car parks**: **13,000 spaces** on site according to GL Events Venues (≈ 8,000 visitors + 5,000 exhibitors), spread across 3 independent entrances.
- **Accommodation**: 2,200 rooms of all categories within an 8 km radius of the centre (East Lyon, Saint-Priest, airport).

The CCI Lyon Métropole regularly points to **transport links** as the site''s main area for improvement, compared with Paris-Nord Villepinte (RER B) or the Hanover Fair. Extending the tram and a project for a direct station-to-airport link are recurring topics in the metropolitan debate. No firm timetable is available to date.

## Services

- **On-site catering**: according to GL Events Venues, **7 restaurants** and **8 bars** spread across the halls and galleries, sized for show footfall (Sirha, Pollutec, Global Industrie can exceed 40,000 visitors/day).
- **Business centre**: 26 modular meeting rooms (43 to 654 m²) for exhibitor meetings, press points and organiser committees.
- **WiFi and 4G**: coverage advertised across the whole site.
- **Espace Paul Bocuse auditorium**: 4,200 seats, up to 6,000 standing places for keynote or show formats.
- **Certifications**: **ISO 20121** (responsible event management) since 2019. *Great Place to Work* accreditation obtained by GL Events Venues.

Concierge, cloakroom and premium exhibitor services: arrangements provided by organisers on a case-by-case basis. No publicly available data on detailed 2026-2027 pricing.

## News

**Hall 4.3 delivered in 2026**: GL Events Venues has launched construction of a new hall offering **6,700 m²** of exhibition space, with a 10.5-metre clear height, adjoining Hall 4.2 with an independent entrance and a modular separation system. The roof is to be fitted with **500 kWc** of solar panels as announced, contributing to the site''s energy autonomy. Announced investment of **€16m excluding tax**, funded by SEPEL''s shareholders (GL Events and CCI Lyon Métropole Saint-Étienne Roanne).

With this extension, Eurexpo Lyon is targeting an announced total capacity of **150,000 m²** of event space by 2026-2027.

**Extension history**:

- **2011**: delivery of the Espace Paul Bocuse (10,000 m² + 4,200-seat auditorium).
- **2015**: delivery of Hall 2.1.
- **October 2018**: delivery of Hall 7 (9,500 m², investment of **€16.5m excluding tax** according to GL Events Lyon), bringing the communicated covered surface to 140,000 m².
- **2026**: Hall 4.3 (6,700 m²).

Eurexpo is part of the **GreenTech+** trajectory led by GL Events Group, which also brings together other venues (Paris, Brussels, Budapest, Battersea) and operates the conference and exhibition division of the Métropole de Lyon. According to Tour Hebdo, the GL Events group in Lyon totalled close to 500 events and **2.5 million visitors in 2025**.

## Flagship shows

Eurexpo hosts the structuring trade events of several French and European sectors. A selection listed on Agoris:

- [SIRHA Lyon](/salons/sirha-lyon) (hospitality, catering, food trades, 21-25 January 2027): a biennial international show, owned by GL Events.
- [Pollutec](/salons/pollutec-lyon) (environment and ecological transition, 12-15 October 2027): a European benchmark for environmental solutions.
- [BePOSITIVE](/salons/bepositive-lyon) (building energy performance, March 2027): paired with Eurobois and Rocalia in certain editions.
- [Paysalia](/salons/paysalia-lyon) (landscaping, gardens, sport, November 2027): a benchmark show for landscapers.
- [Piscine Global Europe](/salons/piscine-global-lyon) (pools, spas, aquatic wellness, 17-20 November 2026): the sector''s global biennial show.
- [Solutrans](/salons/solutrans) (road and urban transport, 30 Nov-2 Dec 2027): biennial, organised by the FFC.
- [Global Industrie](/salons/global-industrie-lyon) (industry, subcontracting, robotics, 15-18 March 2027): alternates between Lyon (odd years) and Paris-Nord Villepinte (even years).
- [Eurobois](/salons/eurobois-lyon) (timber sector, February 2028): biennial.
- [Midest](/salons/midest) (industrial subcontracting, integrated into Global Industrie).
- [Prod&Pack](/salons/europack-euromanut-lyon) (packaging, materials handling, November 2027).
- [Rocalia](/salons/rocalia-lyon) (natural stone, November 2027).
- [FIP - Forum International de la Plasturgie](/salons/plastic-expo-lyon) (plastics processing, June 2026).
- [Preventica Lyon](/salons/preventica-lyon) (workplace health and safety, October 2026).
- [Salon Habitat et Design](/salons/salon-habitat-design-lyon) (March 2026).
- [3D Print Congress & Exhibition](/salons/3d-print-lyon) (additive manufacturing, June 2026).
- [Solscope](/salons/solscope) (drilling, boring, geotechnics, June 2027).
- [Flame Expo](/salons/flame-expo-lyon) (fireplaces and stoves, February 2026).
- [EMM](/salons/emm-lyon) (equipment, machinery, materials, March 2026).

The site has also historically hosted *Equita* (the horse show), the *Mondial des Métiers Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes*, *Mountain Planet* (mountain development, biennial) and the *Foire de Lyon* (annual general-interest edition), none of which are covered by Agoris to date.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='eurexpo-lyon';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Europe''s largest exhibition centre, with 345,000 sqm of covered space, located in Rho near Milan. Hosts CPhI Worldwide, Salone del Mobile, Host and numerous international trade shows.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

Fiera Milano Rho-Pero is one of the largest exhibition districts in Europe, with **345,000 sqm of covered space** and **60,000 sqm of outdoor areas** on a site straddling the municipalities of **Rho** and **Pero**, roughly 12 km north-west of central Milan. The complex was **inaugurated in March 2005** to designs by the Italian architect **Massimiliano Fuksas**, whose practice is notably responsible for the *Vela*, the serpentine glass canopy linking all the pavilions together.

The site is owned by the **Fondazione Fiera Milano** and is operated by **Fiera Milano S.p.A.**, a company listed on Borsa Italiana (STAR segment) since December 2002. The centre''s former historic site, **Fiera Milano City** (near the MiCo congress centre), is still used as a complement for consumer trade shows such as Milan Fashion Week, while the major international professional trade shows have moved to Rho-Pero.

## Halls

According to Fiera Milano, the district comprises **20 exhibition pavilions spread across 8 large buildings**, two of which are double-storey, lined up on either side of a **central pedestrian street of roughly 1 km** set beneath Fuksas''s *Vela*. The odd-numbered pavilions occupy one side of the central axis, the even-numbered pavilions the other.

- **Vela Fuksas**: serpentine glass canopy of around **38,000 sqm** made up of **40,000 glass pieces**, undulating along the 1 km of the pedestrian street. It houses the circulation areas, cafeterias and service points, and forms the architectural signature of the site.
- **Milano Ice Park**: four pavilions of the district (13, 15, 22, 24) have been temporarily reconverted for Milano-Cortina 2026. Pavilions 13 and 15 form the **Milano Speed Skating Stadium** (400 m oval track, around 35,000 sqm covered); pavilions 22 and 24 house the **Milano Rho Ice Hockey Arena**. Outside the Olympic period, these pavilions revert to their exhibition use.
- **Stella Polare conference centre**: 10 modular rooms, integrated into the district.
- **Meeting rooms**: **74 rooms** in total across the site according to Fiera Milano.

The detailed individual floor areas of pavilions 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 9, 22 are not published openly by Fiera Milano S.p.A.; technical plans and configurations are provided on request to organisers via *exhibitors.fieramilano.it*.

## Access & transport

The site is positioned to the north-west of Milan, on the Rho/Pero municipal boundary, alongside the major Lombard motorway routes.

- **Metro M1**: **Rho Fieramilano** station, the terminus of the red line, directly integrated into the eastern entrance of the district. Journey ≈ 25 minutes from central Milan (Duomo).
- **Train (Trenord and Trenitalia)**: **Rho Fiera Milano** station, served by the suburban lines **S5 and S6** from Milano Cadorna / Milano Centrale, as well as by certain high-speed trains (Frecciarossa, Italo) linking the district to the rest of Italy.
- **Milan-Malpensa Airport**: around **20 minutes** via the *Malpensa Express* (changing at Bovisa or Saronno depending on the service) or by car on the A8.
- **Milan-Linate Airport**: around **30 minutes** by car depending on traffic.
- **Roads**: *Fiera Milano* exit on the A8 / A4 and access from the A50 (Milan''s western ring road); the district is designed to absorb peak traffic during major trade shows.
- **Parking**: around **14,000 visitor spaces** and **5,000 exhibitor spaces**, distributed around the district.

## Services

- **Catering**: food courts integrated into the *Vela* and restaurants scattered throughout the pavilions. The offering is sized for the attendance peaks of the major international trade shows (Salone del Mobile, EICMA and HOST can exceed 200,000 visitors per edition).
- **Business centre**: 74 modular meeting rooms across the entire site, including 10 in the **Stella Polare** conference centre.
- **On-site hotel accommodation**: an **NH Hotel Milano Fiera** is located immediately alongside the district, a few minutes'' walk from the main entrances.
- **Exhibitor services**: build-up, handling, utilities, security and logistics coordination operated by Fiera Milano S.p.A. via the *exhibitors.fieramilano.it* portal. WiFi advertised across the entire site.
- **Expo 2015 connectivity**: the district was directly connected to the Expo 2015 site by a pedestrian footbridge, an infrastructure that has been retained and reused for various events.

## News

**Milano-Cortina 2026**: Fiera Milano is an official partner of the Milano-Cortina Olympic and Paralympic Winter Games (announcement January 2025, Fiera Milano press release). Four pavilions of the district (13, 15, 22, 24) have been temporarily reconfigured into the **Milano Ice Park**: **pavilions 13 and 15** form the *Milano Speed Skating Stadium* (400 m oval track) dedicated to speed skating, and **pavilions 22 and 24** house the *Milano Rho Ice Hockey Arena*, which hosts part of the Olympic ice hockey tournament (men''s preliminary matches and the majority of the women''s tournament, with the finals held at the Milano Santagiulia Ice Hockey Arena). The temporary investment for these facilities was announced at around **€15m** and financed through private funds. The complex also houses the **IBC/MBC** (International Broadcast Centre / Main Broadcast Centre) for the broadcasting of the Games.

The district also hosted, in 2015, the **Expo 2015** Universal Exposition on the adjacent site (« Nelle vicinanze ed all''interno del complesso »), and the post-Olympic programming of pavilions 13-15 will revert to a standard exhibition use.

## Flagship trade shows

Rho-Pero hosts most of the major Milanese trade shows that structure the design, fashion, beauty, motorcycle, hospitality-catering and tourism sectors:

- **Salone del Mobile.Milano**: the global benchmark for design and furniture, an annual edition in April, mobilising the whole district.
- **EICMA**: Esposizione Internazionale Ciclo e Motociclo, the international motorcycle trade show, an annual edition in November.
- **HOST Milano**: a biennial trade show for professional hospitality, catering and coffee (odd-numbered years).
- **MIDO**: the global optics and eyewear trade show, an annual edition in February-March.
- **HOMI Milano**: a trade show for the worlds of the home (lifestyle, decoration), two editions per year.
- **MICAM Milano**: the international footwear trade show, two editions per year (February and September).
- **MIPEL**: the international leather goods trade show, co-located with MICAM.
- **MADE expo**: an architecture and construction trade show, a biennial edition.
- **BIT**: Borsa Internazionale del Turismo, the tourism trade show.
- **TUTTOFOOD**: a B2B food and beverage trade show, biennial.

For planning professional events in Northern Italy, Rho-Pero is the benchmark alternative in terms of scale to **BolognaFiere** (Cosmoprof, SAIE) and **Fiera di Vicenza**; its volume positions it at European level alongside **Messe Frankfurt**, **Messe Düsseldorf** and **Hannover Messe**.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='fiera-milano';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Exhibition centre and events venue in Douai (Nord), managed by the Douaisis agglomeration. Several ground-level halls hosting fairs, trade shows and concerts, with modular meeting rooms.', editorial_mdx_en='Gayant Expo is the exhibition centre and events venue of Douai, in the Nord. Managed by the Communauté d''agglomération du Douaisis, the site hosts fairs, trade shows, concerts and sporting events, and is one of the largest events facilities north of Paris.

## Halls and surface areas

The centre has several ground-level halls, among them the Hall 5000 (around 5,500 m²) and the Hall 7000 (around 7,500 m²). Total surface areas vary depending on the sources consulted, between 14,000 and 15,000 m² of covered space. The site also offers modular meeting rooms, including the Espace Europe (600 m², up to 500 people), the Salon Marceline (300 m², up to 250 people) and the Salon Corot (150 m², up to 100 people). In a performance configuration, the main hall can accommodate up to 13,000 people.

## Access and transport

The centre is located on Route de Tournai, in Douai, close to the Douai SNCF railway station. Road access is via the A1 and A23 motorways, in a central position between Lille, Paris and Brussels. The site has a large free car park with around 3,200 spaces. To discover the other trade shows in the city, see the [Douai](/villes/douai) page.

## Services

The site offers technical services (audiovisual, furniture, signage, security) and an events reception. Catering is not imposed on organisers. The centre does not provide on-site accommodation, but the hotel offering of Douai and the surrounding agglomeration is accessible nearby.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='gayant-expo-douai';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Iconic landmark on the Champs-Élysées, built for the 1900 Exposition Universelle. Renovated and reopened in 2024, it hosts Paris Photo, Art Paris, the Festival du Livre, Art Basel Paris and many major art exhibitions and fairs.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The Grand Palais is a listed historic monument located in the very heart of Paris, immediately adjacent to the Champs-Élysées and the Pont Alexandre III. Inaugurated on **1 May 1900** for the Paris Exposition Universelle and designed by architects **Henri Deglane, Albert Louvet, Albert Thomas and Charles Girault**, it is owned by the **French State** and operated by the **RMN-Grand Palais** (Réunion des Musées Nationaux), a public operator under the supervision of the Ministry of Culture. The building covers around **77,000 sqm** in total and is distinguished by its **central Nave**, an immense space covered by a **glass roof** whose main vessel reaches nearly **240 metres** in length and **45 metres** beneath the dome, one of the largest glass and steel structures in the world.

The building underwent a **major heritage renovation between 2021 and 2024**, commissioned by the RMN-Grand Palais under the project management of **Chatillon Architectes** (lead, François Chatillon, chief architect of historic monuments), for an announced budget of around **466 million euros**. The Grand Palais hosted the fencing and taekwondo events of the **Paris 2024 Olympic Games** (July-August 2024), then reopened to the public for its first cultural exhibition, **Chiharu Shiota - The Soul Trembles**, on **11 December 2024**. The full reopening of all the galleries took place in **June 2025**. The site is now organised around three poles: the **monumental Nave** for major events, the **Galeries Nationales** dedicated to museum exhibitions, and the **Palais de la Découverte** (science museum), whose reopening is now announced for **early 2027**.

## Halls

The Grand Palais offers several complementary spaces, designed for very different event formats.

- **Central Nave**: **13,500 sqm** beneath the great glass roof, with a clear height of 45 m under the dome. The heart of the site and the building''s visual signature. ERP capacity of **5,220 people**, with cocktail / gala configurations from **2,000 to 6,000 people** depending on the staging. A benchmark venue for art fairs, fashion shows, gala dinners and prestige sporting competitions.
- **Salon d''Honneur**: **1,200 sqm** in the heritage wing, a reception space beneath period décor, seated dinners for up to 450 people, cocktails for up to 700 people. Suited to press conferences, launches and VIP dinners.
- **Galeries Nationales**: a set of modular rooms within the exhibitions pole, configurable as a museum route or as event sub-spaces according to requirements.
- **VIP and reception spaces**: ancillary rooms for event back-office operations, partner hospitality and press areas.

This versatility allows the site to host a museum exhibition in the Galeries and a private event in the Nave simultaneously, without operational interference.

## Access and transport

The Grand Palais enjoys dense Parisian connections, atypical for an exhibition venue of this scale.

- **Métro**: **Champs-Élysées Clemenceau** station (lines **1 and 13**) about **200 metres** away. Franklin D. Roosevelt (lines 1 and 9) and Invalides (lines 8 and 13) stations are in the immediate vicinity.
- **RER**: **RER C, Invalides station**, about **600 metres** away.
- **Bus**: RATP lines 28, 42, 72, 73, 80, 83, 93 serving the Champs-Élysées and the Pont Alexandre III.
- **Vélib''**: dedicated stations on Avenue Winston Churchill and Place Clemenceau.
- **Car**: **Concorde public car park** nearby. Parking is very constrained within central Paris, so access by public transport remains recommended.

The official address is **3 avenue du Général Eisenhower, 75008 Paris**. The 2021-2024 renovation restored the building''s **historic permeability**, with numerous circulations and openings reopened compared with the pre-works configuration, and unified public access increased by around 140% according to Chatillon Architectes.

## Services

The Grand Palais operates as a premium event venue with an integrated offering managed by the RMN-Grand Palais.

- **In-house catering**: cafés and a **brasserie** deployed throughout the building since the reopening, operated in keeping with the heritage setting.
- **Business centre**: meeting and back-office spaces available for organisers of premium events, on reservation with the RMN-GP teams.
- **RMN-GP exhibitor services**: integrated event support (production, logistics, security, ticketing), VIP services, partner hospitality.
- **Accessibility for people with reduced mobility**: lifts and adapted routes throughout the public circuit, with historic circulations restored.
- **RMN bookshop-boutique** and cultural offerings associated with the Galeries Nationales exhibitions.

## News

The 2021-2024 renovation profoundly reconfigured the use of the site, and the 2025-2026 calendar marks the structured return of the major Parisian events to the Nave.

- **2021-2024 renovation**: heritage programme led by **Chatillon Architectes** (lead), for an announced budget of around **466 million euros**. Objectives: architectural restoration, compliance upgrades (safety, accessibility, energy performance), and the reopening of historically closed circulations.
- **Paris 2024 Olympics**: the Nave hosted the fencing and taekwondo events (July-August 2024).
- **Cultural reopening to the public**: **11 December 2024** with the Chiharu Shiota - The Soul Trembles exhibition, the first major exhibition in the renovated Nave.
- **Full reopening**: **June 2025**, with the opening of all the galleries and the full resumption of the museum programme.
- **Restoration of urban permeability**: historic circulations restored (north-south Champs-Élysées / Seine axis, east-west axis), smoothing visitor flows between the Nave, the Galeries and the future opening of the Palais de la Découverte.
- **Palais de la Découverte**: reopening now announced for the **first quarter of 2027** (after successive delays), the site''s third structuring pole.
- **Return of the major Parisian trade fairs**: Paris Photo, Art Paris and the Festival du Livre de Paris have returned to the Nave after their temporary exile to the Grand Palais Éphémère (Champ-de-Mars) during the renovation.

## Flagship trade shows

The Grand Palais hosts a dense calendar of art, fashion and culture fairs, organised around the monumental Nave and the Galeries Nationales.

- [Paris Photo](/salons/paris-photo): the leading international art photography fair, traditionally organised in **November** in the Nave.
- [Art Paris](/salons/art-paris): a modern and contemporary art fair, a **springtime (April)** fixture beneath the glass roof.
- [Festival du Livre de Paris](/salons/festival-livre-paris): the major Parisian celebration of books and publishing, scheduled **in April** in the Nave since its return to the Grand Palais.

The events programme also includes recurring fixtures not covered by the B2B directory: the **Saut Hermès** (international show-jumping competition, spring), the **Bal des Vampires** (private gala), haute couture and ready-to-wear fashion shows, and institutional gala dinners. The site simultaneously hosts the temporary exhibitions of the Galeries Nationales and the permanent programme of the Palais de la Découverte once reopened.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='grand-palais';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Exhibition and convention centre of the Puy-de-Dôme located in Cournon-d''Auvergne, operated by Clermont Auvergne Events (GL events). Hall 1 (12,600 m²) and Hall 2 (10,440 m², 2021), 2,400 m² of modular function rooms, a convention centre and outdoor grounds. Notably hosts the Sommet de l''Elevage, Europe''s leading trade show for animal production.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The Grande Halle d''Auvergne (GHA) is the exhibition and convention centre serving the Clermont-Ferrand metropolitan area, located in **Cournon-d''Auvergne** (Puy-de-Dôme), on the Sarliève plain. The main building was **completed on 28 November 2003** (a second exhibition hall was added in 2021). The site has been **operated since 2022 by Clermont Auvergne Events**, a subsidiary of the **GL events** group (Venues division), under the trading name *Parc Expo - Grande Halle d''Auvergne*.

The site covers **around 23,000 m² of covered exhibition halls** (Hall 1: 12,600 m²; Hall 2: 10,440 m²), supplemented by **2,400 m² of modular function rooms**, a **convention centre** with a 614-seat auditorium and 13 meeting rooms (40-200 m²), as well as **two outdoor grounds of around 39,500 m² each**. The whole complex spans nearly 100 developed hectares.

Versatile by design, it hosts three families of events: trade and consumer shows, congresses and conventions, and large gatherings (livestock competitions, sporting events, exhibitions).

Its national reputation rests mainly on one landmark event: the [Sommet de l''Elevage](/salons/sommet-elevage-clermont), established on the site since 2004 and now **Europe''s leading trade show for animal production** (1,789 exhibitors from 34 countries in 2025).

## Halls

The centre is organised around **two main exhibition halls**:

- **Hall 1**: 12,600 m² (capacity of around 12,000 people standing in a concert configuration)
- **Hall 2**: 10,440 m² (added in 2021)

Adjoining these are a **modular function room** space of 2,400 m² divisible into four independent rooms of 600 m², a **convention centre** (614-seat auditorium, 13 rooms of 40-200 m²) and the **outdoor grounds** dedicated notably to livestock competitions and machinery demonstrations.

In the immediate vicinity, the **Zénith d''Auvergne** (a separate site, shared within the same perimeter) hosts concerts and shows for up to **9,600 attendees**. The precise surface areas and configurations of each hall, as well as the modular capacities, are provided to organisers on request via the official website.

## Access & transport

- **Tram / bus**: **tram-bus line C** of Clermont-Ferrand''s **T2C** network, stops at **Grande Halle - Zénith** or **Cournon Allier** (direct access to the site).
- **Motorway**: **A75** (Clermont-Béziers route) with **junction 1** (north) via Aubière and **junction 3** (south) via Cournon; connections to the **A89** (Bordeaux-Lyon route) and **A71** (Paris-Clermont route) via the Clermont-Ferrand bypass.
- **Rail**: **Clermont-Ferrand** station around 12 km away, served by the Intercités Paris-Austerlitz - Clermont-Ferrand services (around 3h30).
- **Airport**: **Clermont-Ferrand Auvergne** (CFE) around 10 minutes from the site, domestic flights and seasonal international routes.
- **Parking**: **6,500 free parking spaces** on site, accessible to cars, buses and coaches, spread across four entrances (north, east, south, west).

## Services

- **Catering**: food halls and catering points deployed at organisers'' request for major events (Sommet de l''Elevage, Foire Internationale de Clermont-Cournon, concerts). No permanent offering outside events.
- **Business centre and meeting rooms**: 13 modular rooms adjoining the halls plus a 614-seat auditorium for conferences, press briefings and exhibitor committees.
- **Dedicated services for major events**: technical management, security, utilities, VIP reception, livestock competition coordination (outdoor grounds, animal washing facilities) within the Sommet de l''Elevage perimeter.

The site is **ISO 20121 certified** (responsible event management). Full pricing details and the exhaustive list of services are provided by the operator via the official website clermontauvergne-events.com.

## News

A modernisation programme has continued since the early 2020s: the **opening of Hall 2** in 2021 (an additional 10,440 m²), and the **takeover of operations by Clermont Auvergne Events (GL events)** in 2022. The site welcomed **229,000 visitors in 2024** (across all events). The schedule of upcoming investments is not published on the operator''s institutional page as of 1 June 2026.

On the programming side, the most defining trajectory is the **Sommet de l''Elevage**: a record in 2024 with **120,000 visitors**, falling back to **104,000 in 2025** (-13.4%) due to the absence of cattle following outbreaks of lumpy skin disease (LSD) in France, while reaching a record number of exhibitors at **1,789** from **34 countries** with **6,000 international visitors from 104 countries**. The return of cattle in 2026 (subject to the lifting of health restrictions) will determine the centre''s ability to reclaim the 120,000-visitor threshold.

## Flagship trade shows

- [Sommet de l''Elevage](/salons/sommet-elevage-clermont) (October, **annual**): **Europe''s leading trade show for animal production**, organised by Sommet de l''Elevage SAS. 1,789 exhibitors from 34 countries and 104,000 visitors in 2025. Mobilises all the covered halls, the outdoor grounds and the machinery demonstration zones. Next edition: **6-9 October 2026**.
- **Foire Internationale de Clermont-Cournon**: an annual general consumer trade fair, with consumer-facing programming over around ten days in September.
- **Salon Habitat Clermont**: a home, renovation and décor show, held annually.
- **Concerts and shows**: regular programming at the **Zénith d''Auvergne** (up to 9,600 seats) located on the same shared site.

For organisers seeking a venue suited to mixed programming combining exhibitions, livestock competitions and large gatherings in the southern half of France, the Grande Halle d''Auvergne positions itself as the regional alternative to the major Paris and Lyon sites in this specific segment.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='grande-halle-d-auvergne';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Former 1867 cattle hall, listed as a historic monument, converted into an events venue of around 20,000 sq m at the heart of the Parc de la Villette, in the 19th arrondissement of Paris.', editorial_mdx_en='The Grande Halle de la Villette is a Parisian events venue housed in a former cattle hall built between 1865 and 1867 by the architects Jules de Mérindol and Louis-Adolphe Janvier. A masterpiece of 19th-century iron-and-glass architecture, the building was added to the supplementary inventory of historic monuments in 1979 and later converted into a cultural and professional venue. Located at the heart of the Parc de la Villette, in the 19th arrondissement, it hosts consumer and trade shows, conventions and cultural events.

## Halls and surface areas

The Grande Halle offers a surface area of around 20,000 sq m, with ground dimensions in the order of 250 metres by 85 metres. The main volume can be partitioned into several spaces thanks to modular systems (tympanum, gantries, mobile platforms, side rooms), making it possible to configure the Nave and complementary spaces. The building also houses an auditorium equipped for conferences and screenings.

## Access and transport

The venue is located at 211 avenue Jean Jaurès, 75019 Paris. The nearest metro station is Porte de Pantin (line 5), less than 200 metres away. The site is also served by the Corentin Cariou and Porte de la Villette stations (line 7), by the T3b tramway (Porte de Pantin stop) and by bus routes 75, 139, 150, 151, 152, PC2 and PC3. Car parking is available in the Parc de la Villette car parks, on the Porte de Pantin side and the northern side, which provide spaces reserved for people with reduced mobility.

Other Parisian trade shows are listed on the [trade shows in Paris](/villes/paris) page.

## Services

The site has a restaurant, a bookshop and reception areas. The Grande Halle is part of the wider Parc de la Villette (55 hectares), which brings together numerous cultural facilities in the immediate vicinity. There is a wide range of accommodation and dining options in the neighbourhood and the north-east of Paris, which is well connected to the rest of the capital by public transport.', seo_title_en='Grande Halle de la Villette: trade shows and events venue in Paris', seo_description_en='Grande Halle de la Villette, a listed 1867 venue of around 20,000 sq m in the Parc de la Villette, Paris. Halls, access and trade shows hosted on site.' WHERE slug='grande-halle-villette';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Monaco''s congress and exhibition centre, opened in 2000 on Avenue Princesse Grace. 41,000 sq m of modular indoor space, two exhibition halls and several auditoriums.', editorial_mdx_en='The Grimaldi Forum Monaco is the Principality''s congress and exhibition centre, opened in 2000 on Avenue Princesse Grace, on the seafront in the Larvotto district. Designed as a modular venue hosting trade shows as well as congresses, performances and cultural exhibitions, it welcomes around 100 events and 300,000 visitors a year. In January 2025, the site inaugurated an extension bringing its total capacity to 41,000 sq m of modular indoor space.

## Halls and surface areas

The Grimaldi Forum offers 41,000 sq m of modular indoor space, complemented by outdoor areas. Its exhibition offering is built around two main halls: the Espace Ravel and the Espace Diaghilev, both accessible from the Promenade Princesse Grace. The venue also includes several auditoriums, among them the Salle des Princes (1,864 seats), as well as numerous conference rooms.

## Access and transport

The Grimaldi Forum is located at 10 Avenue Princesse Grace, 98000 Monaco. The nearest airport is Nice Côte d''Azur, around thirty kilometres away, connected to Monaco by road and by helicopter. The Monaco Monte-Carlo station serves the centre via the regional rail network. On-site parking is available.

## Services

The site offers catering areas, a wifi connection and services dedicated to event organisers. Numerous hotels and accommodation options are available in the immediate vicinity, within Monaco and the Larvotto district.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='grimaldi-forum';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Lille''s congress and exhibition centre, with 77,000 sqm at the heart of the Euroregion. Lille Grand Palais hosts international congresses, trade shows and cultural events.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

Lille Grand Palais is a singular events complex in France thanks to its **combination of exhibition centre, Zénith arena and congress centre** brought together within a single building, in the very centre of Lille. It was **inaugurated in June 1994** as part of the **Euralille** project, designed to accompany the arrival of the TGV at Lille-Europe (the station opened on 6 May 1994). The oval-shaped building, often nicknamed "the egg", was designed by the Dutch architect **Rem Koolhaas** with the engineer **Cecil Balmond**.

The venue is operated by the **SAEM Lille Grand Palais - Zénith de Lille**, a semi-public company in which the city of Lille is the majority shareholder. According to the shareholding structure published by the SAEM in 2025, the **Métropole Européenne de Lille (MEL)** holds 30% of the capital and **GL Events** appears at 5.63%, in keeping with the public-private operating model of France''s major exhibition centres.

The site totals **77,000 sqm** of usable space (exhibition centre, Zénith Arena, congress spaces and theatres, meeting-room levels), a **5-minute walk from the Lille-Flandres and Lille-Europe stations**. Its urban centrality, rare in France for an exhibition centre of this scale, and its dual Eurostar/TGV accessibility make it an anchor point for the major events of the Hauts-de-France region and the Lille-Brussels-London euroregion.

## Halls

The building combines **4 modular exhibition halls**, **2 complementary modular spaces**, a **Zénith Arena** and **3 theatres**.

Exhibition halls (surfaces published by Lille Grand Palais):

- **Hall Paris**: 6,219 sqm.
- **Hall Bruxelles**: 6,030 sqm.
- **Hall Londres**: 4,636 sqm.
- **Hall Lille**: 1,900 sqm.

Total announced exhibition surface: **18,000 sqm**.

Modular spaces:

- **Les Beffrois**: 1,354 sqm.
- **Le Square**: 1,350 sqm.

Plenary halls and theatres:

- **Zénith Arena**: capacity of **4,500 seated places** and up to **7,000 places in a seated/standing configuration** according to the SAEM Lille Grand Palais - Zénith de Lille.
- **Grand Théâtre**: 1,474 seats.
- **Théâtre Louis Pasteur**: 477 seats.
- **Théâtre Marie Curie**: 433 seats.

Meeting rooms: Lille Grand Palais lists **28 committee rooms** spread across 4 levels, adaptable into **39 configurations** as required. The surface breakdown per room is not published openly: technical plans are provided on request to the organiser.

## Access & transport

- **Address**: 1 boulevard des Cités Unies, 59777 Lille. Additional pedestrian access via boulevard Émile Dubuisson.
- **Lille-Europe station**: a **5-minute walk** away. Served by **Eurostar** (towards London St Pancras and Brussels-Midi/Amsterdam following the 2023 Thalys-Eurostar merger) and **TGV inOui** (Paris-Nord in around 1h).
- **Lille-Flandres station**: a **5-minute walk** away. TGV inOui, TER Hauts-de-France, and TERGV shuttles to the Côte d''Opale.
- **Metro line 2**: **Lille Grand Palais** stop 400 m from the site, **Mairie de Lille** stop also 400 m away. The Lille-Flandres and Lille-Europe stations are 650 m away (lines 1 and 2).
- **Tram**: 2 stops 650 m away.
- **Bus**: Citadine line, "Lille Grand Palais" stop; line 18, "Grand Palais" stop.
- **V''Lille**: **1 station in front of the site** and 2 stations less than 300 m away (Cité Administrative and Hôtel de Région).
- **Motorway**: from the **A1** (Paris, Tournai, Brussels, Valenciennes) take exit 2 then route 2A; from the **A25** (Dunkirk, Calais, Boulogne) take exit 1 then route 2A.
- **Car park**: according to Wikipedia, the building has around **1,250 integrated parking spaces**. The exact number per level is not published on the official website.

## Services

- **Catering**: Brasserie and bars integrated into the building, with on-stand catering provided through service providers approved by Lille Grand Palais.
- **Business centre**: equipped committee and meeting rooms, sized for exhibitor meetings, press briefings and organiser committees.
- **Connectivity**: WiFi across the whole site, with infrastructure sized for major international congresses (Forum InCyber Europe, Séries Mania).
- **Reception and signage**: teams dedicated to delegates and exhibitors, cloakrooms.
- **Virtual tour**: a virtual walkthrough of the spaces is published by the operator.

Data not publicly available to date on CSR certifications (ISO 20121, the *Destination Innovante Durable* label) and on the detail of premium exhibitor services.

## News

- **Forum InCyber Europe contract**: according to communications from the trade show and its organiser Forward Global, the exclusivity contract between the [Forum InCyber Europe (formerly FIC)](/salons/fic-lille) and Lille Grand Palais runs until **2029**, lastingly anchoring Europe''s cybersecurity gathering in Lille.
- **Extension project**: an extension project is mentioned in the Hauts-de-France regional press and is jointly led by the MEL and the SAEM. Timeline, scale and amount: *Data not available for this edition.*
- **Zénith renovation**: a renovation of the Zénith Arena is mentioned in the operator''s communications. Precise timeline: *Data not available for this edition.*

## Flagship trade shows

Lille Grand Palais structures several professional sectors in the Hauts-de-France region and hosts a major European cybersecurity event.

- [Forum InCyber Europe](/salons/fic-lille) (formerly FIC, European cybersecurity): 18th edition from 31 March to 2 April 2026, exclusivity contract until 2029.
- [BioFIT](/salons/biofit-lille) (life sciences, research-industry partnerships): December 2026 edition.
- [Nordbat](/salons/nordbat-lille) (regional Hauts-de-France construction): March 2028 edition.
- [Salon des Vacances de Lille](/salons/salon-tourisme-voyages-lille) (tourism and travel): January 2026 edition.

The site also hosts **Séries Mania** (international TV series festival, city of Lille), **Art Up!** (contemporary art fair), **Lille Horse Event** and the **International Lille Tattoo Convention** according to the list of recurring events published by Wikipedia, none of which are currently covered by Agoris.', seo_title_en='Lille Grand Palais: Congress & Exhibition Centre', seo_description_en='Lille Grand Palais, the congress and exhibition centre in the heart of Lille: 77,000 sqm, 4 halls, Zénith Arena and trade shows, a 5-minute walk from the stations.' WHERE slug='lille-grand-palais';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Opened in 2020, the MEETT is the new exhibition centre and convention centre of Toulouse Métropole. With 155,000 sqm, it is one of the largest event complexes in France.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The MEETT (Meet Toulouse Events, Exhibitions, Conventions) is the exhibition centre and convention centre of the Toulouse metropolitan area. Straddling the municipalities of **Aussonne** (postal address) and **Beauzelle**, to the north-west of the conurbation, it is owned by **Toulouse Métropole** and operated by **GL Events** under the commercial brand Toulouse Events. The site was designed by the architecture practice **OMA** (Office for Metropolitan Architecture), selected through a competitive tender in 2010.

Construction began in October 2016. The MEETT entered operation in September 2020 and was officially inaugurated on **4 September 2021**, having been delayed by the health crisis. It replaces the former Île du Ramier exhibition centre and the Pierre Baudis Convention Centre, both located in the heart of Toulouse. The total cost of the programme, including road and tram access, is estimated at **310 million euros**, of which 90 million was devoted to access infrastructure.

The MEETT covers a total footprint of **155,000 sqm**, including **40,000 sqm of fully modular exhibition halls**, a **14,000 sqm central nave**, **25,000 sqm of outdoor exhibition space** and **15,000 sqm of convention centre**. It is the largest event venue in the south-west of France.

## Halls

The venue extends across two main sections linked by the central nave, OMA''s architectural signature.

- **Exhibition halls**: **40,000 sqm in a single span, fully modular**, extended by **two mezzanines** for ancillary uses (cloakrooms, conference rooms). The configuration accommodates both major B2B trade shows (Aeromart, SIANE) and public events.
- **Central nave**: **14,000 sqm** of covered circulation space, the signature backbone of the OMA project. It serves as a reception area, an event platform and links the halls to the convention centre.
- **Outdoor esplanade**: **25,000 sqm** of open-air exhibition space, available for heavy equipment, vehicle demonstrations and outdoor events.
- **Convention & Congress Centre**: **15,000 sqm, fully modular**, across two levels. Capacity of **100 to 10,000 people**, with plenary sessions and seminars running simultaneously. Level 1 offers private seminar spaces available from 100 people.

## Access and transport

The site is served by a dedicated multimodal infrastructure, delivered at the same time as the venue.

- **Tram**: line **T1** (Palais de Justice ↔ MEETT), terminus stop **MEETT** (in the municipality of Beauzelle). The line was extended by 700 metres to the site and brought into service on 31 August 2020.
- **Bus**: Tisséo line **30** to MEETT, MEETT stop (no. 27460).
- **Airport**: **Toulouse-Blagnac, 7 minutes** away by car.
- **Station**: Toulouse Matabiau, **21 minutes** away by tram.
- **Car**: access via the **A621 motorway**, extended by the **RM902 (Voie Lactée)** over 4 km, created to serve the venue, and via the redeveloped **RN224**.
- **Parking**: **5,000 spaces on site** (3,000 for visitors, 2,000 for exhibitors), with mandatory card payment at the machines. Disabled and reduced-mobility spaces near the lift blocks.
- **Cycling**: **400 sheltered cycle racks** near the entrances.

The official address is **Concorde Avenue, 31840 Aussonne**.

## Services

The MEETT operates as an integrated event venue, designed to limit movement between halls, conventions and services.

- **Catering**: **Le Central by Maison Pergo** restaurant, open during certain events, with event capacity from 50 people and a modular terrace and hall. No telephone booking, on-site only.
- **Accommodation nearby**: more than **4,300 rooms** within a 20-minute radius of the site, according to the operator.
- **Accessibility**: only guide dogs and assistance dogs are admitted. Dedicated disabled and reduced-mobility parking.
- **Exhibitor services**: quotes and contact via Toulouse Events (contact@toulouse-events.fr, 05 62 25 45 45).
- **No ATM** on site.

## News

The MEETT moved into full operation after two years disrupted by the health crisis. According to GL Events, the site hosted **85 events in 2022** and **more than 700,000 visitors in 2023**, reflecting the gradual ramp-up of the complex following its opening in September 2020.

In terms of operating policy, the MEETT has been **certified ISO 20121 since July 2022** according to the official website, attesting to a responsible management system applied to events (waste management, energy, mobility, inclusion). 70% of the waste generated on site is recycled according to the operator.

## Flagship trade shows

The MEETT hosts B2B trade shows that are structural for the south-west, mainly in the aerospace, industry, food-processing and process sectors.

- [Aeromart Toulouse](/salons/aeromart-toulouse): international business convention for the aerospace sector, held biennially, organised by BCI Aerospace (ABE group).
- [CFIA Toulouse](/salons/cfia-toulouse): trade show for suppliers to the food-processing industry, the Toulouse edition of CFIA Rennes (GL Events).
- [SEPEM Industries Sud-Ouest](/salons/sepem-toulouse): trade show for industrial services, equipment, processes and maintenance.
- [SIANE](/salons/siane-toulouse): trade show for industry partners across the Grand Sud.
- [REGAL](/salons/regal-toulouse): trade show for food and catering in Occitanie.

The site also hosts the Foire Internationale de Toulouse, a regional public event that inaugurated the MEETT in September 2021, as well as various sector conventions (health, technology, training).', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='meett-toulouse';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Berlin exhibition centre with 27 halls and around 160,000 m² of covered space (190,000 m² in total with the recent extensions), located at Berlin ExpoCenter City. Hosts ITB Berlin, IFA, InnoTrans, Grüne Woche and Fruit Logistica.', editorial_mdx_en='
## Description

The **Messegelände Berlin** is the historic exhibition centre of the German capital, set in the west of the city in the **Westend district (borough of Charlottenburg-Wilmersdorf)**. The land belongs to the **Land of Berlin**, while operations are run by **Messe Berlin GmbH**, a public company of which the Land is the sole shareholder. The site covers around **160,000 m² of covered exhibition space** within its historic perimeter, rising to around **190,000 m²** with the more recent halls (CityCube, hub27), supplemented by significant outdoor areas and an adjoining congress centre.

The centre as we know it today is the result of a development begun in **1922** and structured in the mid-1920s, under the direction of the architect Heinrich Straumer. Its silhouette is dominated by the **Funkturm**, a steel broadcasting tower inaugurated on **3 September 1926**, standing 146.7 m tall, which has become the visual landmark and emblem of the site. At the foot of the tower, the **Sommergarten** (summer garden) serves as an outdoor extension to the halls during major events. The centre has remained on this site without interruption for nearly a century, and remains the principal exhibition venue of the Berlin-Brandenburg region.

Alongside it, the **ICC Berlin (Internationales Congress Centrum)**, completed in 1979, rounds out the ecosystem as a major congress centre. Listed as a historic monument in September 2019, the ICC has been closed since 2014 for asbestos removal and technical obsolescence. Renovation works are under way but no firm reopening date has been confirmed to date.

## Halls

The Messegelände unfolds across **27 exhibition halls** arranged around the Sommergarten, plus the CityCube Berlin and the ICC as complements.

- **Historic halls**: the numbering runs from 1.1 to 27, in several blocks (halls 1 to 12 on the Sommergarten side, halls 18 to 26 on the Funkturm side). Surfaces range from a few thousand m² to more than 10,000 m² per hall, in bare-floor or partitioned configuration depending on requirements.
- **Sommergarten**: a central outdoor esplanade of around 10,000 m², usable as an extension of the halls (demonstrations, event catering, activations).
- **CityCube Berlin**: a multipurpose pavilion of **12,000 m²**, completed in **May 2014** on the site of the former Deutschlandhalle, designed as a modular volume combining congresses, conferences and exhibitions. Used standalone or in conjunction with the neighbouring halls depending on the event format.
- **hub27**: the most recent hall, opened in **August 2019**, offering **10,000 m² column-free** (configurable into up to 10 rooms via mobile partitions), supplemented by 5,000 m² of usable space across 3 levels (foyer, 20 meeting rooms, rooftop terrace). It is hall number 27, which closes the numbering.
- **ICC Berlin**: the adjoining congress centre, closed since 2014 and listed as a historic monument in 2019. A reopening is envisaged over the longer term, with no firm timetable to date.

The whole is interconnected by pedestrian galleries and an internal shuttle system during major editions.

## Access & transport

The centre benefits from a dense multimodal service, rare for a site of this scale within Berlin.

- **S-Bahn**: **Messe Nord/ICC** station (formerly Messe Nord/ZOB since late 2024), served by lines **S41 and S42** (Ringbahn) and **S46**, on the Funkturm side. **Messe Süd** station, served by lines **S5 and S75**, on the Eichkamp side / access to the southern halls.
- **U-Bahn**: line **U2**, **Theodor-Heuss-Platz** station a 10-minute walk from the south entrance, and **Kaiserdamm** station on the Funkturm side.
- **BVG buses**: lines 104, 139, 218, 349 and X34 serve the centre''s entrances directly.
- **Airport**: **Berlin Brandenburg Airport (BER)** around **22 km** to the south, accessible by S-Bahn (lines FEX and S9) alongside the regional metro. Direct connection in around 45 minutes.
- **Road**: **A100 (Stadtring)** exit "Messedamm", with direct access to the main car park.
- **Parking**: around **5,000 spaces** on site, car parks P1 to P5 distributed around the site, with dedicated exhibitor and visitor access.

The official address is **Messedamm 22, 14055 Berlin**.

## Services

The centre operates as a mixed-use event complex, with a service offering calibrated for large-scale international editions.

- **Catering**: catering points distributed throughout the halls (food courts, cafés, VIP areas) operated by the Messe Berlin Catering partners. Premium catering in the dedicated halls and on the CityCube rooftop.
- **Accommodation**: no hotel is integrated into the centre itself. The **Hotel Palace Berlin** and several 4- and 5-star establishments cover the Charlottenburg area; the offering is rounded out by hotels in the city centre and the Kurfürstendamm district, accessible by U-Bahn in under 15 minutes.
- **Congresses and conferences**: integrated capacity via the CityCube Berlin (plenaries and modular rooms) and hub27 (20 meeting rooms supplementing the main hall). Meeting rooms distributed throughout the halls for B2B formats.
- **Exhibitor services**: technical management, utilities, handling, customs and logistics managed by Messe Berlin; online exhibitor portal for pre-ordering services.
- **Connectivity**: WiFi across the whole site, with a network infrastructure dedicated to major tech editions (IFA, InnoTrans).
- **Accessibility for people with reduced mobility**: adapted entrances and lifts, with dedicated assistance available through the welcome centre.

## News

The site has entered a multi-speed modernisation phase, structured around three projects.

- **Renovation of the ICC Berlin**: a programme undertaken after the building''s closure in 2014 (asbestos removal, bringing up to standard). The building was listed as a historic monument in September 2019. To date, no firm reopening date has been confirmed by Messe Berlin or by the Land of Berlin; the cost of restoration far exceeds the initial budget.
- **hub27**: the opening of the new hall no. 27 in August 2019 (10,000 m² column-free, €75 million of investment largely borne by Messe Berlin), now the largest volume on the site and one of its principal event halls.
- **Sustainability strategy**: Messe Berlin has put in place a formalised environmental policy and operates under environmental and energy management certification for the relevant editions.

The whole forms part of the Land of Berlin''s overall strategy to reposition the Westend area as a "congress, exhibition, sport and culture" cluster centred on the Messegelände and the neighbouring Olympic stadium.

## Flagship trade shows

The Messegelände hosts a dense calendar of B2B and consumer trade shows, with several world leaders in their category.

- [ITB Berlin](/salons/itb-berlin): the world reference for professional tourism, held annually in spring. The sector''s historic gathering since 1966, operated by Messe Berlin.
- **IFA Berlin**: the international consumer electronics and home appliances trade show, one of the oldest trade shows in Germany (1924). Held annually in September, open to the general public and to professionals.
- **Internationale Grüne Woche**: the consumer food and agriculture trade show, held annually in January. One of the most important consumer food events in Europe.
- **InnoTrans**: the world trade show for rail and rail-based mobility, held biennially (even years) in September. Mobilises the entire halls and the site''s railway tracks for rolling-stock demonstrations.
- **Fruit Logistica**: the international professional trade show for fresh fruit and vegetables, held annually in February. The world reference for the sector, operated by Messe Berlin.

The centre also hosts a broad spectrum of other sector-specific trade shows (materials, gaming, B2B conventions), as well as international medical and scientific congresses as part of the ICC + CityCube + hub27 strategy.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='messe-berlin';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Munich exhibition centre operated by Messe München GmbH, located in Messestadt Riem to the east of the city. 18 halls and 200,000 m² of covered space, one of the largest sites in Europe. Hosts trade fairs including Bauma, BAU and The smarter E Europe.', editorial_mdx_en='The Trade Fair Center Messe München is one of Europe''s leading exhibition centres, operated by Messe München GmbH in the Messestadt Riem district to the east of Munich, on the site of the former Munich-Riem airport. Opened in 1998, it hosts major international trade shows such as Bauma, BAU and The smarter E Europe.

## Halls and floor space

The site comprises 18 single-storey exhibition halls, for a total covered area of 200,000 m². It also offers more than 400,000 m² of outdoor space used for exhibitions and parking, making it one of the largest exhibition centres in Europe.

## Access and transport

The centre is directly served by the underground (U-Bahn) line U2, via the two stations Messestadt West and Messestadt Ost. Line U2 connects the site to Munich''s central station (München Hauptbahnhof) without changing. By car, access is via the A94 motorway (München-Riem and Feldkirchen-West exits). Munich Airport is reachable from the city centre on the S1 and S8 lines.

## Services

The centre offers car parks, including the Parkhaus West fitted with charging points for electric vehicles, and a dynamic traffic guidance system. Numerous hotels are accessible within Munich and close to the site, which is linked to the city centre by line U2. For the trade shows held at this venue, see the [Munich](/villes/munich) page.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='messe-munchen';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Exhibition centre and convention centre in Besançon, opened in 1987 on a site of around 11 hectares, bringing together modular exhibition halls, a convention centre and an auditorium.', editorial_mdx_en='Micropolis is the exhibition centre and convention centre of Besançon, in the Bourgogne-Franche-Comté region. Opened in 1987 and set on a site of around 11 hectares to the south-west of the city, it brings together modular exhibition halls, a convention centre and a large auditorium under a single address, making it one of the region''s principal events venues.

## Halls and floor space

The site extends across a complex of around 11 hectares. It comprises several modular exhibition halls, a convention centre and an auditorium able to seat up to 2,150 people, or accommodate more than 7,000 in a standing configuration. The covered exhibition area varies according to the configuration chosen: refer to the official website for full details of the spaces.

## Access and transport

The address is 3 Boulevard Ouest, CS 82019, 25050 Besançon cedex. The site is served by the tram (lines T1 and T2, Micropolis stop around 300 m from the main entrance and Malcombe stop around 200 m from the Malcombe entrance) and by several bus lines (7, 51-59, 84 at the Micropolis stop; 7 and 53 at the Parc des Expositions stop). Besançon Viotte station is connected to the site via the tram. By air, Dole-Tavaux airport is around 50 km away, Lyon-Saint Exupéry 240 km, Mulhouse-Bâle 150 km and Genève-Cointrin 160 km. On-site parking is available.

## Services

Micropolis also operates as a park-and-ride facility for reaching the city centre by tram. For precise details (catering, technical equipment, capacity by space), refer to the venue''s official website.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='micropolis-besancon';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='A Parisian events venue on Place de la Bourse (2nd arrondissement), the former Palais de la Bourse converted into a conference and events centre, operated by GL Events.', editorial_mdx_en='The Palais Brongniart is a Parisian events venue located on Place de la Bourse, in the 2nd arrondissement. Built in the early 19th century to house the Paris stock exchange, this neoclassical landmark designed by the architect Alexandre-Théodore Brongniart was converted into a conference and events centre after the stock exchange activities moved out. Now operated by GL Events, it hosts trade shows, congresses, conventions and corporate evenings within a heritage setting at the heart of the business district.

## Spaces and rooms

The Palais Brongniart offers several modular spaces spread across different levels (garden level, ground floor, upper levels and the central Nave), comprising two auditoriums (99 and 594 seats) as well as around ten meeting rooms. The site has 4,000 m² of event space.

## Access and transport

The venue is located at 16 Place de la Bourse, 75002 Paris. Access by metro via Bourse station (line 3) or Grands Boulevards (lines 8 and 9). Several bus routes serve the area: check the RATP website for connections at the Bourse stop. The central location makes access easy from the main Parisian railway stations.

## Services

The site offers on-site catering (a restaurant and a reception area provided by partner service providers). Numerous hotels and dining establishments are available in the immediate vicinity, in the Bourse and Grands Boulevards district. For logistical needs and the booking of its spaces, the venue is managed by GL Events.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='palais-brongniart';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='A 22,000 sqm contemporary art centre at 13 avenue du Président Wilson in the 16th arrondissement of Paris, opposite the Trocadéro. Modular bare-concrete spaces, reached via metro line 9 (Iéna, Alma Marceau).', editorial_mdx_en='The Palais de Tokyo is a contemporary art centre located in the 16th arrondissement of Paris, at 13 avenue du Président Wilson, opposite the Trocadéro and a stone''s throw from the Seine and the Eiffel Tower. Inaugurated as part of the 1937 International Exhibition, the building offers a vast industrial shell of bare concrete that makes it one of the largest sites devoted to contemporary creation in Europe. Beyond its artistic programme, its generous volumes and distinctive architecture make it a venue for professional events, particularly in fashion and design.

## Halls and surface areas

The Palais de Tokyo now spans roughly 22,000 sqm following the extension of its spaces, compared with 8,000 sqm previously. The whole presents itself as one large, modular volume rather than a succession of numbered halls, allowing bespoke scenography for the exhibitions, performances and events it hosts.

## Access and transport

The entrance is at 13 avenue du Président Wilson, 75116 Paris. By metro, line 9 serves the Iéna and Alma Marceau stations, just a few minutes'' walk away. The RER C stops at Pont de l''Alma. Several bus routes pass nearby (32, 42, 63, 72, 80, 82, 92), and a Vélib'' station (Marceau Président Wilson) is located in front of the venue. Car parks can be found close to the site.

## Services

The venue offers on-site catering and a detailed accessibility provision for visitors with disabilities. Its central location, in a dense hotel district between the Trocadéro and the Champs-Elysées, makes it easy for exhibitors and visitors to find accommodation in the immediate vicinity.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='palais-de-tokyo';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Iconic Paris convention centre located at Porte Maillot. With its 4 auditoriums and 32,000 sqm of space, it hosts congresses, conventions and trade shows.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The Palais des Congrès de Paris is an iconic convention centre located at **Porte Maillot, in the 17th arrondissement**. Inaugurated on **28 February 1974** and designed by architects **Guillaume Gillet, Serge Maloletenkov, Yves Betin and Henri Guibout**, it has been operated since **2008** by **Viparis**, a joint subsidiary of the **CCI Paris Île-de-France** and **Unibail-Rodamco-Westfield**. The site covers **32,000 sqm** in total, with a combination that is unique for an urban centre: a **Grand Amphithéâtre seating 3,723**, several secondary auditoriums, **95 modular rooms** and **19,000 sqm dedicated to trade shows and conventions**.

The original building, delivered in 1974, underwent a **major extension between 1997 and 1999** led by architect **Christian de Portzamparc**, comprising the construction of a tower adjoining the Palais and the extension of the **Hyatt Regency Paris Étoile** hotel complex (which first opened in 1974). **Successive renovations in the 2010s** modernised the auditoriums and the exhibition spaces. Today the Palais hosts a structuring mix of **international medical congresses**, **corporate conventions**, **performances** (the Grand Amphithéâtre also operating as a performance venue) and targeted **trade shows**.

## Halls

The site is organised around **four main auditoriums** and a very dense set of modular rooms spread across several levels.

- **Grand Amphithéâtre**: **3,723 seats**, the site''s main plenary hall and one of the largest seated capacities in central Paris. Used both as a congress plenary hall and as a performance venue.
- **Amphithéâtre Bleu**: **826 seats**, level 2, the site''s second plenary hall.
- **Amphithéâtre Bordeaux**: **650 seats**, level 3, dedicated to mid-capacity sessions.
- **Amphithéâtre Havane**: **373 seats**, level 3, a conference format dedicated to more intimate sessions.
- **95 modular rooms** spread across levels 1 to 5, with capacities ranging from a few dozen to more than a thousand participants.
- **19,000 sqm of trade show and convention space**, dedicated to exhibitions held alongside congresses.

## Access and transport

The site benefits from dense multimodal connections, among the most comprehensive in Paris for a convention centre.

- **Metro**: line **1**, **Porte Maillot** station, with direct access to the Palais. Line **3**, **Pereire** station in the immediate vicinity.
- **RER**: line **C**, **Neuilly-Porte Maillot** station.
- **Tram**: line **T3b**, **Porte Maillot** stop.
- **Bus**: several RATP routes serve Porte Maillot.
- **Car**: direct access from the **boulevard périphérique** (Porte Maillot exit) and the avenue de la Grande-Armée.
- **Parking**: around **1,800 spaces** in the Indigo Porte Maillot underground car park, with direct access to the levels of the Palais.
- **Airports**: direct **Roissybus** shuttle from Porte Maillot to **Paris-Charles-de-Gaulle** (CDG).

The official address is **2 Place de la Porte Maillot, 75017 Paris**.

## Services

The Palais operates as an integrated events complex, attached to a hotel and retail ensemble.

- **On-site accommodation**: the **Hyatt Regency Paris Étoile**, adjacent to the Palais, is one of the largest hotels in Paris by number of rooms, with direct access from the levels of the Palais.
- **Catering**: multiple restaurants and catering points within the complex, complemented by the offering of the **Les Boutiques du Palais** shopping centre (15,300 sqm) integrated into the site.
- **Business centre** and congress services: meeting rooms, audiovisual equipment, dedicated Viparis teams.
- **WiFi** throughout the site.
- **Viparis services**: events concierge, dedicated technical teams for major events, integrated management of service providers.

## News

The Palais des Congrès is part of an **ongoing modernisation** programme led by Viparis and Unibail-Rodamco-Westfield from **2025**, in connection with the development of the Porte Maillot district.

- **2025 and beyond renovation project**: works undertaken around the Porte Maillot complex, incorporating changes to the Palais''s reception and circulation areas.
- **ISO 20121 certification approach**: responsible event management, in line with the standard applied across the entire Viparis portfolio.

## Flagship trade shows

The Palais des Congrès is positioned on a very specific mix: **professional and medical congresses**, **corporate conventions** and **performances** rather than consumer trade shows with a large exhibition component. Among the site''s structuring events:

- **Medical congresses**: annual events in **cardiology**, **oncology**, **gastroenterology** and other specialities, with several learned societies holding their annual congress here.
- [Produrable](/salons/produrable-paris): the major gathering of players and solutions in the sustainable economy, held over two days in late September.
- **International symposia** and corporate conventions in the main auditoriums.
- **Concerts and performances**: regular programming in the 3,723-seat Grand Amphithéâtre.

Of note for B2B exhibitors: **the Salon des Entrepreneurs**, long hosted at the Palais des Congrès, was renamed **GO Entrepreneurs** in 2021 and has moved to **Paris La Défense Arena** since 2022. The site remains primarily oriented towards **congresses and conventions** rather than trade shows with a large exhibition component.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='palais-des-congres-paris';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='The Palais des Festivals et des Congrès de Cannes, located on the Croisette, hosts major international professional events with exhibition halls, the Auditorium Lumière (2,309 seats) and numerous meeting rooms.', editorial_mdx_en='The Palais des Festivals et des Congrès de Cannes is one of the leading venues for professional events on the Côte d''Azur. Located at 1, boulevard de la Croisette, by the sea, it combines exhibition spaces, auditoriums and meeting rooms, and hosts major international gatherings in tourism, real estate and events every year.

## Halls and surface areas

The Palais des Festivals is organised across several levels bringing together exhibition halls, auditoriums and numerous meeting and conference rooms. The Auditorium Lumière, the site''s largest room, seats up to 2,309 people. The official website does not publish a consolidated total surface area.

## Access and transport

Cannes station, served by high-speed trains (Inoui, Ouigo) and regional services, is around a six-minute walk from the Palais. Nice Côte d''Azur airport is 30 km away (a shuttle of around 45 minutes to the centre of Cannes), and the Cannes-Mandelieu business airport is about fifteen minutes away. An underground car park with 924 spaces spread over 3 levels is located beneath the Palais, with accessible (PRM) spaces and charging points for electric vehicles.

## Services

The site offers the services expected of a major congress centre: catering, meeting spaces and fully equipped auditoriums. Its central location, on the Croisette and right next to the station, places many hotels and restaurants just a few minutes'' walk away. To discover the city''s other venues and events, see the [Cannes](/villes/cannes) page.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='palais-des-festivals-cannes';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Congress centre of the City of Marseille, set in the former imperial palace of Napoleon III at the entrance to the Old Port (7th arrondissement). A 900-seat auditorium, an exhibition hall and historic reception rooms with a panoramic terrace overlooking the bay.', editorial_mdx_en='The Palais du Pharo is the congress centre of the City of Marseille, set in the former imperial palace commissioned by Napoleon III, at the entrance to the Old Port in the 7th arrondissement. The site combines a historic building, including its imperial reception rooms and their panoramic terrace overlooking the bay, with a contemporary extension dedicated to professional events: an auditorium, an exhibition hall and modular reception spaces. Its seafront location and heritage setting make it a prime venue for the congresses, conventions and B2B trade shows held in Marseille.

## Halls and floor space

The centrepiece is a 900-seat auditorium, complemented by an exhibition hall of around 917 m². The Palais du Pharo brings together some twenty event spaces split between the historic building and the contemporary extension. It is a congress centre rather than a pavilion-based exhibition park: the floor space is divided into reception rooms and halls rather than large exhibition pavilions.

## Access and transport

The Palais du Pharo is located at 58 boulevard Charles Livon, 13007 Marseille. Marseille-Saint-Charles station is around 3.5 km away. Several bus routes serve the Pharo area. Marseille Provence airport is around 25 km away: a shuttle links the airport to Saint-Charles station, from which you can reach the city centre and the Old Port. Parking is available at the Q-Park Marseille Pharo car park, located right next to the entrance.

## Services

The site offers on-site catering with a dedicated space and catering services. Wi-Fi is available throughout the spaces. The panoramic terrace of the historic reception rooms offers a 360-degree view over the bay and the monuments of Marseille. Numerous hotels are accessible nearby, in the city centre and around the Old Port.

To discover all the professional events organised in Marseille, see the [trade shows in Marseille](/villes/marseille) page.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='palais-du-pharo';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Strasbourg convention centre located in the Wacken district, near the European Parliament. Renovated in 2016, it brings together a 3,000 sqm exhibition hall, three auditoriums and multi-purpose spaces, across roughly 50,000 sqm in total.', editorial_mdx_en='The Palais de la Musique et des Congrès (PMC), whose full official name has been Palais des congrès Pierre-Pflimlin since the 2016 renovation, is Strasbourg''s convention centre, located in the Wacken district, in the immediate vicinity of the European Parliament and the institutions of the European capital. Fully renovated in 2016, it combines convention spaces, auditoriums and exhibition areas within a single facility managed by Strasbourg Events. It hosts professional conventions as well as cultural events and concerts.

## Halls and floor space

The facility offers roughly 50,000 sqm of space in total. It comprises a 3,000 sqm pillar-free exhibition hall, with an 11 m ceiling height, complemented by 10,000 sqm of modular multi-purpose spaces. The PMC also features three auditoriums (1,800, 1,200 and 500 seats) and around twenty configurable committee rooms.

## Access and transport

The PMC is served by the tram, lines B and E, Wacken stop, a few minutes'' walk from the building. From Strasbourg-Ville station (TGV), the tram provides a direct connection to the Wacken stop. The site has on-site parking.

## Services

The Palais offers a catering area that can seat up to 1,400 guests. Its location in the Wacken district, Strasbourg''s business and institutional quarter, provides a wide choice of hotel accommodation nearby. For the practical details of each edition, please refer to the website of the organiser of the relevant event.

To discover all the trade shows held in the city, see the [trade shows in Strasbourg](/villes/strasbourg) page.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='palais-musique-congres-strasbourg';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Exhibition and convention centre of Marseille with 38,000 sqm of space. Located close to the Stade Vélodrome, the Parc Chanot hosts trade shows, congresses and Mediterranean cultural events.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The Parc Chanot is the historic exhibition and convention centre of Marseille, located on the **Rond-Point du Prado, in the 8th arrondissement**, in the immediate vicinity of the Stade Vélodrome. The site covers **17 hectares** in the heart of Marseille and offers **more than 40,000 sqm of covered space** dedicated to events, complemented by outdoor areas and landscaped zones.

The park was created in **1906** to host the **first Colonial Exhibition of Marseille**, on the former parade ground of the Rouet. It bears the name of **Jean-Baptiste Amable Chanot**, mayor of Marseille (1902-1908 and 1912-1914), who initiated the project. The site hosted a second Colonial Exhibition in 1922, which left significant infrastructure that was reused as early as **1925** for the first edition of the **Foire de Marseille**, an annual commercial event still running today (its centenary was celebrated in 2025). The park has since established itself as the benchmark facility for hosting trade shows, congresses and consumer events in the **Provence-Alpes-Côte d''Azur** region.

The site has been operated since **1 January 2025** by **Marseille events**, a dedicated company owned **75% by GL events** and **25% by CCIAMP Infrastructures** (a subsidiary of the CCI Aix-Marseille-Provence), under a public service delegation awarded by the City of Marseille. The park was historically managed by the **SAFIM** (Société Anonyme des Foires Internationales de Marseille) from 1985 to the end of 2024. The park combines several dedicated buildings (exhibition palace, congress palace, arts palace, events palace) and outdoor areas that can be configured as required.

## Halls

The Parc Chanot extends across several interconnected palaces, sized for trade show, congress and show formats. Surface areas provided by the operator:

- **Palais Phocéen**: around **13,400 sqm**, the largest single-span covered volume, suited to large-scale consumer and B2B trade shows.
- **Palais des Congrès**: a dedicated complex of around **15,000 sqm** over three levels, including two modular auditoriums (from 200 to 1,200 seats), **15 committee rooms** and around **3,800 sqm** of flexible exhibition and reception space.
- **Grand Palais**: around **6,900 sqm** with a ceiling height of 14 m, Art Deco architecture, suited to high-impact set designs.
- **Palais de la Méditerranée**: around **6,900 sqm**, divisible into two spaces, with natural light.
- **Palais des Événements**: around **6,500 sqm** column-free, accommodating up to **3,200 people** in plenary configuration (1,600 to 3,200 seats).
- **Palais de l''Europe**: around **4,800 sqm** column-free, divisible.
- **Palais des Arts**: a historic palace (a remnant of the 1922 Colonial Exhibition) used for receptions and heritage events, with an adjoining garden.

The **main Auditorium** of the Palais des Congrès has a capacity of **400 to 1,200 seats**, complemented by the **Amphithéâtre Callelongue** (130 to 426 seats, divisible).

## Access and transport

The site benefits from multimodal links in the heart of the city, uncommon for an exhibition centre of this scale.

- **Metro**: line **M2**, **Rond-Point du Prado** station, direct access to the park''s main entrance.
- **Tram**: line **T3**, stops in the immediate vicinity.
- **Bus**: RTM services around the Rond-Point du Prado, complemented by reinforced event shuttles during major trade shows.
- **Car**: motorway exits **A50** (from Aubagne / Toulon) and **A55** (from Martigues / Aix-en-Provence). The **L2** ring road connects the two axes (progressive opening completed in 2018).
- **Marseille-Provence airport** (Marignane): around **25 km** to the west of the park.
- **Gare Saint-Charles** (TGV): around 4 km to the north of the site, with M2 metro connection to the Rond-Point du Prado.
- **Parking**: around **1,800 parking spaces** on site, available depending on event configurations.

The official address is **Rond-Point du Prado, 13008 Marseille**.

## Services

The range of services follows the event rhythm of the site, with logistics operated directly by Marseille events in conjunction with its partner service providers.

- **Catering**: restaurants integrated into the park and food truck areas deployed during major events. The offer is sized for trade show footfall.
- **Business centre**: meeting rooms and modular committee spaces (15 dedicated rooms within the Palais des Congrès), available to complement the palaces for press conferences, exhibitor meetings and organiser committees.
- **Operator services**: technical management, handling, utilities, security, ticketing and logistics coordination provided directly by Marseille events.
- **Accommodation**: no hotel integrated into the park. The offer is concentrated along the Prado, Vieux-Port and Castellane axes, in the immediate vicinity of the park (notably the **Pullman Marseille Palm Beach** and **Mercure Marseille Centre Prado Vélodrome**), complemented by the wider hotel ecosystem of Marseille city centre.

## News

The Parc Chanot entered a new phase in early 2025, under the governance of Marseille events (GL events / CCIAMP Infrastructures), with a roadmap built around the modernisation of facilities, the opening of the park to local residents and the strengthening of business tourism in the Mediterranean.

- **New public service delegation 2025-2027**: Marseille events took over management on 1 January 2025, after being awarded the contract by the City of Marseille on 12 December 2024.
- **Urban transformation**: since September 2025, the outdoor areas have been redeveloped (gardens, refreshment area, a secure cycle path linking the Rond-Point du Prado to the rue Raymond Teisseire) to open the park up further to the people of Marseille.
- **Adjoining Stade Vélodrome**: the overhaul of the Stade Vélodrome (delivered in 2014) and the redevelopment of the Prado-Borély district reinforce the events ecosystem of the 8th arrondissement, with a hosting capacity combining park, stadium and hotels.

Data is not publicly available on the consolidated schedule of upcoming infrastructure deliveries (palaces, halls).

## Flagship trade shows

The Parc Chanot hosts the structuring event programme of the Marseille metropolitan area, covering both historic consumer trade shows and sector-specific B2B events.

- **Foire Internationale de Marseille**: a major general-interest consumer event anchored in the park since **1925** (its centenary was celebrated in 2025). Annual format, late September to early October. One of the oldest commercial fairs in France still running.
- **Salon Maison & Habitat**: a consumer trade show dedicated to housing, decoration and interior design, held annually at the Parc Chanot.
- **SAVIM**: the wine and gastronomy trade show of Marseille, annual format in November.
- **Sirha Méditerranée**: a professional event for catering / hospitality / food trades for the Mediterranean basin, with its first edition in March 2026 (Palais des Événements).
- **Euromaritime**: a trade show for the maritime and river industries, with its 2026 edition at the Parc Chanot (February 2026).
- **SIAC Marseille**: the international contemporary art show, annual format.
- **HeroFestival**: an annual pop culture / geek convention.
- **Marseille Tattoo Convention**: an international tattoo convention held annually.
- [MAP Pro](/salons/map-pro-marseille): a trade show for hospitality, catering and food trades in the PACA region.

For planning a B2B event in PACA, the Parc Chanot is the benchmark option in the Marseille metropolitan area. Organisers targeting a complementary format in the region can also refer to the facilities of the Nice-Côte d''Azur metropolis (Palais des Expositions, Palais des Congrès Acropolis) or the Aix-Marseille metropolis (dedicated convention centres).
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-chanot-marseille';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Parc des Expositions de Bordeaux Lac, located to the north of the city with 80,000 sqm of floor space. It hosts the Vinexpo trade show and numerous wine-industry and food and beverage events.', editorial_mdx_en='## Overview

The **Parc des Expositions de Bordeaux** is located in the **Bordeaux Lac** district, to the north of the city, on an estate of around thirty hectares on the edge of the lake. The site offers approximately **70,000 sqm of flexible indoor space** spread across three halls (Hall 1, Hall 3, Hall 4), to which are added the **15,500 sqm** of the adjoining **Palais 2 l''Atlantique**, and more than **120,000 sqm of outdoor space**. It provides **7,000 free parking spaces** on site and, together with the Palais 2 l''Atlantique, forms one of the leading B2B events hubs in south-west France.

The venue and all of Bordeaux''s events sites are operated by [BEAM (Bordeaux Events And More)](https://www.beam.fr), a simplified joint-stock company (SAS) created through the **renaming**, on **4 October 2022**, of *Congrès et Expositions de Bordeaux (CEB)*. BEAM manages **5 complementary sites** across the metropolitan area: the Parc des Expositions, the **Palais 2 l''Atlantique** (15,500 sqm delivered on **6 May 2019**, adjoining the venue), the **Palais des Congrès**, the **Hangar 14** (on the quays) and the **Centre de Congrès Cité Mondiale** (in the historic heart, near the Quais des Chartrons). The operator reports **more than 270 events per year** and over 700,000 cumulative visitors across its entire portfolio.

The venue hosts international B2B trade shows, public exhibitions, conferences, concerts and sporting competitions. It is notably the long-standing host of [Vinitech-Sifel](/salons/vinitech-sifel-bordeaux), a biennial international trade fair for equipment and services dedicated to viticulture, fruit growing and market gardening.

## Halls

The venue is today organised around **3 flexible halls** numbered **Hall 1, Hall 3 and Hall 4** under BEAM''s current nomenclature, complemented by the adjoining Palais 2 l''Atlantique and by vast configurable outdoor spaces. The former Hall 2, historically dedicated to trade shows and conferences, has had its functions taken over by the Palais 2 l''Atlantique delivered in 2019. All the halls are modular and can be combined or partitioned to accommodate a very wide range of event configurations.

- **Hall 1**: approximately **50,140 sqm** in a single span, the largest on the site, delivered in **1969** with exceptional interior dimensions (840 m long by 60 m wide). It hosts the very largest trade shows and makes it possible to organise several events simultaneously thanks to movable partitions.
- **Hall 3**: approximately **12,000 sqm** column-free, with several meeting rooms with movable partitions and a dedicated private car park. It is particularly suited to trade shows with a conference component or to events requiring a mix of exhibition and sessions.
- **Hall 4**: approximately **7,500 to 8,000 sqm** of covered space, complemented by organiser offices and a meeting room. It hosts human-scale trade shows and mid-format professional events.
- **Palais 2 l''Atlantique**: **15,500 sqm** adjoining the venue, including a main "Agora" hall of **7,140 sqm** divisible into three parts, a **1,000 sqm** reception hall and **12 committee rooms**. Able to accommodate up to **6,000 people** in a plenary configuration.

The whole is rounded out with configurable **outdoor spaces** (more than 120,000 sqm) used for demonstrations, exhibitor car parks, public attractions and equipment trials (drones, agricultural machinery, heavy equipment).

## Access & transport

**Tram**: **line C** serves the venue at the **Parc des Expositions** stop (formerly *Parc des Expositions - Stade*), immediately adjacent to the site. The journey from the centre of Bordeaux takes around 25 minutes.

**Car**: the venue is accessible via the **A630 ring road**, exit **4** (or 4a / 4b depending on the direction of travel). Allow around **15 minutes** from Bordeaux city centre by car, outside peak hours.

**Bus**: several TBM network lines serve the district (notably lines **37** and **75**).

**Airport**: **Bordeaux-Mérignac** is around **20 minutes by car** and accessible by public transport via the airport link, with a tram connection.

**Parking**: **7,000 free spaces** on site, distributed around the halls.

**Accommodation**: around **1,400 hotel rooms** are available less than 5 minutes'' walk from the venue, notably in the Cité Mondiale complex and the hotels of the Lac district.

## Services

**Catering**: catering services provided in-house by the BEAM teams, with a kitchen integrated into the site and several catering points open during events (food courts, seating areas, on-stand kiosks). Exhibitors can order on-stand catering services from the official service providers.

**Exhibitor services**: business centre, equipped meeting rooms, signage, reception, security and guarding, furniture hire and technical installations (electricity, water, utilities), logistical assistance before and during the event.

**Connectivity**: WiFi coverage deployed across the halls, network infrastructure sized for major international trade shows.

**Nearby accommodation**: the **Cité Mondiale** and the hotels of the Bordeaux Lac district concentrate the bulk of the hotel offer close to the venue.

## News

On **6 May 2019** the venue gained the **Palais 2 l''Atlantique**, a new 15,500 sqm exhibition and conference hall, whose construction began in January 2018 for an investment of around **34.5 million euros**. This facility, adjoining the Parc des Expositions, can accommodate up to 6,000 people and is intended to take over the functions of the former Hall 2 for events with a conference component.

On **4 October 2022**, *Congrès et Expositions de Bordeaux (CEB)* changed its name to become **BEAM (Bordeaux Events And More)**, retaining its legal structure (SAS) and its portfolio of sites. This rebranding aimed to broaden the operator''s image beyond the sole "congresses and exhibitions" label and to structure a coherent events offer across the Bordeaux metropolitan area (5 complementary sites).

The venue and the Bordeaux destination are committed to an **ISO 20121** approach (a responsible management system applied to events activity), led by BEAM and the Bordeaux Convention Bureau as part of the destination''s "eco-partners" programme.

## Flagship trade shows

The venue hosts a mix of international B2B trade shows, regional sector fairs and public events:

- [**Vinitech-Sifel**](/salons/vinitech-sifel-bordeaux): a biennial international trade fair for equipment, technologies and services for viticulture, oenology, fruit growing, market gardening and horticulture. The event, taken over by BEAM in October 2022, is one of the world''s benchmark gatherings for the vine and fruit-growing sectors.
- [**Salon de l''Agriculture Nouvelle-Aquitaine**](/salons/salon-agriculture-nouvelle-aquitaine): a major regional event dedicated to south-west agriculture, combining livestock exhibitions, technical demonstrations and public attractions.
- [**UAV Show (Air Drone)**](/salons/air-drone-bordeaux): an international professional trade show devoted to civil drones and the industrial applications of unmanned aerial systems.
- [**Salon de l''Habitat Bordeaux**](/salons/salon-habitat-bordeaux): a public exhibition dedicated to construction, renovation, decoration and home improvement, held each year in spring.
- [**Exp''Hotel Bordeaux**](/salons/exphotel-bordeaux): a biennial professional trade show dedicated to the hospitality, catering and tourism trades across the wider south-west.
- [**BLC Bordeaux Lac**](/salons/blc-bordeaux): a professional event held early in the year at the site.

The venue also hosts editions of **Conforexpo** (a trade fair created in 1960), the **International Show Jumping Competition** (Jumping International de Bordeaux, organised in parallel in the Palais 2 l''Atlantique) and numerous concerts and public events.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-des-expositions-de-bordeaux';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Exponantes offers 48,000 sqm of exhibition space on the outskirts of Nantes. It hosts trade and consumer shows, notably in the housing, industry and agriculture sectors.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The **Parc des Expositions de la Beaujoire** is located in the north-east of Nantes, in the Beaujoire district, along the banks of the **Erdre**, immediately adjacent to the Beaujoire stadium and the terminus of tram line 1. The site covers around **20 hectares** in total, including the **6 main covered halls** and the **Grand Palais**, complemented by extensive outdoor areas that can be fitted out. Together with the Cité des Congrès, it forms the main B2B and consumer events hub of the Nantes metropolitan area.

The venue was created in the **late 1960s**, with the construction of the **Grand Palais** designed by the Nantes-based architect **Yves Liberge** and inaugurated in **1969**. The site expanded gradually through successive halls, up to the inauguration of the **Hall XXL** in **August 2013**, built on the site of the former halls 5 and 6.

The venue is operated by **[Exponantes](https://www.exponantes.com/)** (a local public company in which the **CCI Nantes Saint-Nazaire** is the reference shareholder alongside Nantes Métropole), on behalf of **Nantes Métropole**, which owns the facilities.

Since **1 January 2026**, the SPL Exponantes and the SPL Cité des Congrès de Nantes have joined **a single local public company** (Société Publique Locale) in order to jointly manage the two events venues of the metropolitan area, under the joint governance of **Nantes Métropole** and the **CCI Nantes Saint-Nazaire**. The definitive legal form (merged SPL or SEM) is still being structured. This combination aims to organise a coordinated events offering across the Loire-Atlantique area, articulating major B2B trade shows at the Parc des Expositions and congresses / conferences at the Cité des Congrès.

The venue hosts national B2B trade shows (Serbotel, IPA), regional consumer fairs (Habitat, Vinipro, Salon de l''Étudiant) and numerous sector-specific professional events.

## Halls

The venue is organised around **6 main halls** and the **Grand Palais**, complemented by extensive outdoor areas that can be fitted out. All the halls are modular and can be combined or partitioned to accommodate a range of event configurations.

- **Halls 1 to 4**: modular covered halls that host B2B trade shows, sector fairs and major consumer fairs depending on the edition.
- **Hall XXL**: a large hall of **15,501 sqm** (SHON) inaugurated in **August 2013**, built on the site of the former halls 5 and 6. It is dedicated to configurations requiring a very large floor footprint (trade shows with a demonstration component, conventions, sporting or cultural formats), with a capacity that can reach several thousand people in event configuration.
- **Grand Palais**: the historic building of the site (1969) over three levels, incorporating exhibition spaces, meeting rooms and catering areas, connected to the other halls by covered walkways.
- **Outdoor areas**: outdoor surfaces that can be fitted out for demonstrations, exhibitor car parks, consumer activities and equipment trials.

## Access & transport

**Tram**: **line 1** serves the venue at its **Beaujoire** terminus, immediately on the edge of the site. The **Ranzay** station (line 1) provides a second access point. This is the preferred mode of access from Nantes city centre (around 20 minutes from the centre).

**Bus**: the **C6 line** of the TAN network also serves the Beaujoire district.

**Car**: the venue is accessible via the **A11 motorway**, **Beaujoire** exit. From the Nantes ring road, allow a few minutes outside peak hours.

**Airport**: **Nantes-Atlantique** is around **25 minutes by car** to the south of the metropolitan area.

**Parking**: several thousand parking spaces on site, complemented by the stadium and Beaujoire district car parks on major event days.

## Services

**Catering**: on-site catering services during events (catering points, seated areas). Exhibitors can order on-stand catering services from the site''s official service providers.

**Exhibitor services**: business centre, meeting rooms, signage, reception, security and guarding, furniture hire and technical installations (electricity, utilities), logistics assistance before and during the event.

**Connectivity**: **WiFi** coverage deployed across the halls.

**Accommodation**: a dense hotel offering in Nantes city centre (15 to 20 minutes by tram), with a few hotels in the immediate vicinity of the Beaujoire district.

## News

Since **1 January 2026**, the SPL Exponantes and the SPL Cité des Congrès de Nantes have joined **a single local public company** (Société Publique Locale) responsible for operating the two Nantes events venues (Parc des Expositions de la Beaujoire + Cité des Congrès). Governance is jointly held by **Nantes Métropole** (owner of the sites) and the **CCI Nantes Saint-Nazaire**, with the definitive legal form (merged SPL or SEM) still being structured.

This strategic project aims to organise a coordinated events offering across the Loire-Atlantique area: major trade shows and fairs at the Parc des Expositions on one side, congresses, conventions and conferences at the Cité des Congrès on the other. The new entity brings together around 130 staff, manages nearly 400 events per year and represents a combined turnover of around 27 million euros. For organisers and exhibitors, this merger ultimately means a single point of contact for the Nantes destination, strengthened coordination of calendars and a pooling of exhibitor services between the two sites.

## Flagship trade shows

The venue hosts a mix of national B2B trade shows, regional sector fairs and consumer events:

- [**Serbotel**](/salons/serbotel-nantes): a biennial trade show (odd years) dedicated to the **out-of-home catering (CHR)** trades, hospitality, bakery-patisserie and the food trades of the Grand Ouest. It is one of the venue''s long-standing B2B fixtures.
- [**IPA Nantes**](/salons/ipa-nantes): a biennial trade show for **food ingredients and processes** (Ingredients & Process Alimentaires), organised by **GL Events**.
- [**Batibig Nantes**](/salons/batibig-nantes): an annual trade show dedicated to **building and construction** in the Grand Ouest.
- **Salon Habitat Nantes**: an annual consumer fair dedicated to construction, renovation, decoration and home improvement.
- **Salon Vinipro**: a trade show dedicated to wine and spirits buyers of the Grand Ouest (CHR, wine merchants, distribution).
- **Salon de l''Étudiant Nantes**: an annual careers and education guidance fair for post-baccalauréat options, aimed at sixth-formers and students of Loire-Atlantique.

The venue also hosts large-scale regional consumer events (the Nantes fair, seasonal specialist trade shows) and numerous congresses, conventions and sporting competitions.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-des-expositions-de-nantes';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='The Parc des Expositions de Strasbourg, in the heart of the Wacken district, offers 24,000 m² of exhibition space across four modular halls (architect Kengo Kuma, opened 2022), within an events complex that includes the Palais de la Musique et des Congrès (PMC).', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The Parc des Expositions de Strasbourg stands in the heart of the **Wacken district**, the diplomatic quarter on the north bank of central Strasbourg. The new Parc Expo, inaugurated on **2 September 2022** to designs by the Japanese architect **Kengo Kuma**, offers **24,000 m² of exhibition space** spread across four modular halls, within an overall events complex of around 74,000 m² that includes the adjoining **Palais de la Musique et des Congrès (PMC)**.

The site is operated by **Strasbourg Événements (SEM)**, a semi-public company whose capital is shared between the **Ville de Strasbourg** and the **Eurométropole de Strasbourg** (holding a 51% majority) and **GL Events**, which acquired a **47%** stake in 2014. GL Events sits on the SEM as an industrial shareholder: the local authority retains the majority and sets the strategic direction.

The Parc Expo + PMC complex forms the leading events hub of the Grand Est region, positioned around regional trade shows with a cross-border dimension (Germany, Switzerland) and around European gatherings driven by local diplomacy.

## Halls

The new Parc Expo comprises **four modular halls**, sized for both trade and public show formats. Halls 2, 3 and 4 are interconnected to allow continuous circulation. Indicative areas provided by the operator:

- **Hall 1**: around **6,100 m²**, the largest single-span hall.
- **Hall 2**: around **4,460 m²** (bridge hall).
- **Hall 3**: around **4,430 m²**.
- **Hall 4**: around **4,430 m²**, which can be coupled with the neighbouring halls for large-format operations.

The site also includes **La Nef**, a multi-purpose space of **2,480 m²** with 17-metre glazed bays opening onto the canal and the surrounding green spaces. The PMC adds an auditorium and conference rooms to the exhibitor configurations, allowing trade shows to incorporate a conference programme without moving off site.

## Access and transport

The site enjoys fast multimodal connections from central Strasbourg and the borders.

- **Tram**: lines **B and E**, **Wacken** stop, a 3-minute walk from the site.
- **Bus**: lines **H** (direct link from the station) and **2**.
- **Train**: from **Strasbourg station**, around **12 minutes by tram** on line B.
- **Car**: **A35**, Strasbourg centre exit. Paid visitor parking on rue Fritz Kieffer.
- **Air**: **Strasbourg-Entzheim airport**, around **15 minutes** by shuttle or car.

The official address of the Parc Expo is **7 Place Adrien Zeller, 67000 Strasbourg**, in the heart of the Wacken district. The adjoining PMC has a separate entrance on Place de Bordeaux.

## Services

The Strasbourg Événements operation shares services between the Parc Expo and the PMC.

- **Catering** on site: an offer integrated into the halls and the PMC, supplemented by the brasseries of the Wacken district.
- **Business centre** at the PMC: meeting rooms and workspaces for exhibitors and delegations.
- **WiFi** deployed across the entire site.
- **Events services** from Strasbourg Événements: technical management, reception, ticketing and organiser support.

## News

The Wacken district is the subject of the **Wacken Europe** development project, an urban programme led by the Eurométropole de Strasbourg to reposition the area around its dual events and European vocation. The project brings together housing, offices, facilities and a reconfiguration of access routes around the Parc Expo and the PMC.

The site benefits from its immediate proximity to the **European institutions** based in Strasbourg: the **European Parliament**, the **Council of Europe** and the **European Court of Human Rights** are all less than **500 metres** from the Parc Expo. This concentration fuels the site''s appeal for institutional congresses and events with a diplomatic component.

## Flagship trade shows

The Parc des Expositions de Strasbourg hosts a range of trade and public shows with a Grand Est and cross-border reach.

- [EGAST](/salons/egast-strasbourg): the Grand Est trade show for food and catering trades (HoReCa), held biennially in even-numbered years. The 2026 edition runs **from 15 to 18 March 2026** (20th edition, the show''s 40th anniversary).
- **Foire européenne de Strasbourg**: the benchmark annual trade fair in Strasbourg, created in **1932** and held every September.
- **Salon Maison Bois Énergie**: a regional event dedicated to timber construction and renewable energy for buildings.
- **Mondial des Vins Blancs Strasbourg** and **European Red & Rosé Wine Contest**: international wine competitions organised by Strasbourg Événements at the PMC. The 2026 edition was held on **14 and 15 March 2026** (28th Mondial des Vins Blancs, 1st edition of the European Red & Rosé Wine Contest).', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-des-expositions-de-strasbourg';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Parc des Expositions d''Angers operated by Destination Angers (SPL ALTEC). ISO 20121-certified site. Hosts SIVAL, the trade show for specialised plant production, among others.', editorial_mdx_en='## Overview

The Parc des Expositions d''Angers is located in Verrières-en-Anjou, a 10-minute drive from the city centre. Opened on 24 May 1985 on a 40-hectare site, it offers 27,000 sqm of covered exhibition space (34,000 sqm in total with the Amphitéa and the Galerie de Liaison). The site is operated by Destination Angers, a local public company that also manages the Centre de Congrès Jean-Monnier in the city centre. Together they host around 600,000 visitors and more than 300 events each year.

## Halls

The venue comprises six main modular, interconnected spaces:

- **Amphitéa**: 4,000 sqm of modular space, up to 7,500 seats in a concert configuration. Also serves as an exhibition hall for large trade shows.
- **Grand Palais**: 11,400 sqm, the main exhibition hall. Hosts plenary sessions and the general commissariats of large trade shows.
- **Ardesia**: 4,000 sqm column-free, with a 7-metre ceiling height, bathed in natural light.
- **Novaxia**: 2,800 sqm across two levels (1,500 sqm downstairs, 1,300 sqm upstairs), ideal for trade shows with separate zones.
- **Hall C**: 4,500 sqm.
- **Galerie de Liaison**: 1,000 sqm of glazed space for reception, catering or complementary exhibition use.

The Centre de Congrès Jean-Monnier, run by the same Destination Angers group, is a separate site located at 33 boulevard Carnot in the centre of Angers. Reopened on 4 May 2019 after renovation, it has a 1,200-seat auditorium and modular rooms (up to 18 depending on the configuration), at the heart of a 4.5-hectare botanical park.

## Access & transport

- **Address**: 3520 Route de Paris, Saint-Sylvain-d''Anjou, 49480 Verrières-en-Anjou.
- **By car**: A11 (Paris-Nantes) and A87 motorways; the venue is directly connected to the western motorway network.
- **By bus**: line 4 of the Irigo network serves the venue from the centre of Angers (terminus Parc Expo).
- **By train**: Angers-Saint-Laud station, around 10 km away, connected to Paris-Montparnasse in 1 hr 30 by TGV.
- **By air**: Nantes-Atlantique airport, around 45 minutes away via the A11.
- **Parking**: free on-site parking.

## Services

The venue offers comprehensive services for organisers and exhibitors: on-site catering, reception, signage, cloakroom, and event support through Destination Angers. The Galerie de Liaison allows reception areas and business zones adjoining the halls to be set up. The site is **ISO 20121**-certified (responsible event management) and holds the "Destination Innovante Durable" label as well as the Qualité Tourisme certification.

## News

Destination Angers has maintained its ISO 20121 certification for several years, a marker of its CSR commitment to waste management, energy consumption and the eco-design of events. The Centre de Congrès Jean-Monnier, part of the Destination Angers offering, was reopened on 4 May 2019 after a complete renovation, extending the metropolitan area''s overall capacity for congresses and conventions.

## Flagship trade shows

- **[SIVAL](/salons/sival-angers)**: the international trade show for specialised plant production, a long-standing fixture at the venue, held every year in January. The 2026 edition brought together around 700 exhibitors across 38,000 sqm.
- **Foire d''Angers**: an annual edition in late April, the largest public event in the Maine-et-Loire department, organised by Destination Angers Événements (102nd edition in 2026).

The venue also hosts home and lifestyle trade shows, professional conventions, and the concert programme at the Amphitéa.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-expo-angers';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Exhibition centre in Châlons-en-Champagne (16,100 sqm of covered exhibition space, 4 halls). Combines exhibition spaces, a conference centre and a performance hall. Notably hosts the Foire de Châlons.', editorial_mdx_en='Le Capitole en Champagne is the exhibition centre of Châlons-en-Champagne, located on avenue du Président Roosevelt. It combines a modular exhibition space, a conference centre and a performance hall. It notably hosts the Foire de Châlons, one of the leading agricultural and economic gatherings in the Grand Est region.

## Halls and surface areas

The site has 4 halls representing roughly 16,100 sqm of covered exhibition space, three of which benefit from natural light. This is complemented by a conference and seminar space (modular rooms for 15 to 800 people) and a modular performance hall that can seat up to 3,000 thanks to retractable tiered seating.

## Access and transport

The centre is located at 68 avenue du Président Roosevelt, in Châlons-en-Champagne (51000). Châlons-en-Champagne SNCF railway station is around 2 km from the site. A free landscaped car park with 2,000 spaces directly adjoins the venue. For the region''s other trade shows, see the [Châlons-en-Champagne](/villes/chalons-en-champagne) page.

## Services

The site offers on-site catering (restaurant and bar), wifi access, catering services and audiovisual equipment (sound system, projection, screens). The conference and seminar rooms are modular depending on the capacity required for each event.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-expo-chalons';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Exhibition centre located to the east of Colmar (Haut-Rhin), operated by SA Colmar Expo (capital shared between CCI Alsace Eurométropole, the Chambre des Métiers d''Alsace, the Chambre d''Agriculture du Haut-Rhin, CIVA and STUCE SEM). 16,668 m² of covered halls spread across 6 modular halls and an Espace Congrès with 8 rooms. Hosts the Foire aux Vins d''Alsace, created in 1948, every summer.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The Parc des Expositions de Colmar is located to the east of the city, along the avenue de la Foire aux Vins (68000 Colmar, Haut-Rhin). It is operated by **SA Colmar Expo**, a public limited company whose capital is shared between CCI Alsace Eurométropole, the Chambre des Métiers d''Alsace, the Chambre d''Agriculture du Haut-Rhin, the Comité Interprofessionnel des Vins d''Alsace (CIVA) and STUCE SEM (the urban transport company for Colmar and its surroundings, which provides indirect representation for the Ville de Colmar).

The site offers 16,668 m² of covered halls across a total estate of more than 43,500 m² (indoor and outdoor), with 6 modular halls, an Espace Congrès of 8 rooms and an open-air theatre.

The venue is known nationally for hosting the **Foire aux Vins d''Alsace** every summer, created in 1948 under the name Foire Régionale des Vins d''Alsace at the initiative of the Chamber of Commerce and the Ville de Colmar (source: [colmar-expo.fr / Notre histoire](https://www.colmar-expo.fr/qui-sommes-nous/), [Wikipédia](https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foire_aux_vins_d%27Alsace)).

## Halls

Six covered halls, configurable independently or grouped together via movable partitions:

- **Hall 1, Halle aux Vins**: 2,088 m², up to 2,080 places for a standing reception or 1,300 seated in a performance configuration, retractable tiered seating
- **Hall 2**: 3,000 m², 3,000 standing places, 2,130 seated for a meal
- **Hall 3**: 3,500 m², the largest hall on the site, 3,500 standing places
- **Hall 4**: 3,200 m², 3,200 standing places
- **Hall 5, "Cabaret"**: 1,200 m², a multipurpose space transformed into the Cabaret Colmarien during the Foire aux Vins
- **Hall 6**: 1,200 m², the most recently delivered hall, which becomes the festive hub (afterworks, DJ sets) during the Foire aux Vins

The **Espace Congrès** adds 8 modular rooms across two levels (from the 91 m² salle Riesling to the 425 m² salle Gewurztraminer), for a total of around 1,700 m² of meeting and conference rooms. The rooms are named after Alsatian grape varieties (Riesling, Pinot Blanc, Crémant, Muscat, Gewurztraminer on the ground floor; Sylvaner, Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris on the upper floor).

## Access & transport

- **Car**: A35 motorway. From Strasbourg, exit 23 "Colmar / Munster / Kaysersberg / Bennwihr Houssen". From Mulhouse, exit 24 "Nancy / Saint-Dié / Épinal / Kaysersberg / Colmar-Nord". Then follow "Parc des Expositions"
- **Bus**: line D on the [TRACE Colmar](https://www.trace-colmar.fr/) network (towards the Airport), "Parc des Expositions" stop. Weekday frequency: one bus every 20 minutes from 6.00am to 8.15pm
- **Train**: Colmar station, around 10 minutes from the venue by bus, served by TER Alsace trains and TGV services from Paris-Est, Strasbourg and Mulhouse
- **Car parks**: around 2,800 spaces split between the main P2 car park (1,400 spaces, paid), the Airport P3 car park (800 spaces, paid) and the Liebherr P6 car park (600 spaces, free, near the A35 Mulhouse exit)

## Services

- **Catering**: in-house catering areas integrated into each hall (1 to 4 service kitchens depending on the hall). During the Foire aux Vins d''Alsace, the site is transformed into an Alsatian gastronomic hub, with winegrower stands and local catering until 1am
- **Espace Congrès**: 8 modular rooms, video and sound equipment, dedicated catering, enhanced accessibility for people with reduced mobility
- **Artist dressing rooms**: 5 dressing rooms in Hall 1, Halle aux Vins, including 1 accessible for people with reduced mobility
- **Logistics**: lorry doors and loading docks on all main halls
- **Air conditioning**: halls 1, 5 and 6 are air-conditioned

## News

The site has undergone several successive phases of expansion, with Hall 6 the most recent delivery to date. Colmar Expo also operates a second site in the city centre, the **Espace Auguste**, dedicated to urban events and conferences.

No publicly available data on the works schedule or any further expansion plan at this stage. No data available either on the site''s environmental certifications (ISO 20121, ISO 14001).

## Flagship trade shows

The venue hosts both consumer trade shows and professional trade shows:

- **Foire aux Vins d''Alsace**: 77th edition from 31 July to 9 August 2026. A trade show and festival created in 1948, organised by Colmar Expo in partnership with CCI Alsace Eurométropole. Around 350 exhibitors and 300,000 visitors (organiser figures). The festival''s musical programme features French and international headline acts every year. The Foire aux Vins d''Alsace is today the third-largest fair in France behind Paris and Marseille (source: [foire-colmar.com / Historique](https://www.foire-colmar.com/en/la-foire/historique/))
- **[Salon Énergie Habitat](/salons/salon-energie-habitat-colmar)**: from 20 to 23 March 2026, a consumer trade show dedicated to energy renovation and the home
- **[SEPEM Industries Est](/salons/sepem-colmar)**: from 2 to 4 June 2026, a B2B trade show for industrial services, equipment and subcontracting
- **Salon Maison et Jardin**: from 25 to 27 September 2026 (source: [colmar-expo.fr / Agenda](https://www.colmar-expo.fr/evenement/))
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-expo-colmar';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Parc des Expositions de l''Eurométropole de Metz, located in the Grigy / Grange-aux-Bois district, 6 km from the city centre. 21,000 sqm of indoor space across 3 halls + 2 mezzanines, on a 50-hectare site. Operated by GL Events under the Metz Événements brand. Around 50 events and 800,000 visitors per year.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The Parc des Expositions de l''Eurométropole de Metz is located in the Grigy / Grange-aux-Bois district, 6 km south of the city centre. Opened in 1977, it occupies a 50-hectare site, including 21,000 sqm of indoor space spread across 3 halls and 2 mezzanines, organised around an 800 sqm entrance gallery.

The site is operated by **GL Events**, through the SASU *GL Events Parc des Expositions de Metz Métropole*, under the commercial brand **Metz Événements** (also referred to as *Metz Expo Événements*). The venue hosts around 50 events per year and 800,000 visitors annually.

The site combines consumer trade fair / public show activity with business tourism, in keeping with the events ecosystem of Metz Métropole: the Centre des Congrès Robert Schuman and the Centre de Congrès du Technopôle, both also operated by GL Events.

## Halls

3 modular halls, complemented by 2 mezzanines and a 600-seat auditorium.

- **Hall A**: 8,000 sqm, minimum ceiling height 9 m. The site''s main hall, sized for large-format trade shows (Foire Internationale de Metz, Agrimax).
- **Hall B**: around 6,000 sqm. The most recent hall, with a modern layout.
- **Hall C**: around 4,800 sqm. A more compact space, suited to smaller-scale trade shows.
- **Auditorium**: 600 seats.
- **Meeting rooms**: 12 additional spaces.

Around 34,000 sqm of usable outdoor space for marquees and open-air exhibitions (for example: the Foire Internationale de Metz, which uses halls + marquees + open-air space across some 44,000 sqm in total).

## Access & transport

**By car**
- From the **A31** (Luxembourg-Nancy route): take the *Metz Est* or *Metz Centre* exit, then follow the "Parc des Expositions" signs.
- From the **A4** (Paris-Strasbourg route): join the A31, then take the same exits.

**Parking**
- 5,000 spaces, free of charge.

**Public transport**
- **Le Met''** bus network: *Mettis P+R Foire Expo* stop, served by the **METTIS B** line (every 10 min), with direct access from the city centre and the railway station.

**Train**
- **Metz-Ville station** (TGV) around 6 km away: direct TGV connections to Paris, Reims, Lille, Frankfurt, Nantes and Bordeaux. Onward connection by bus from the station via the Le Met'' network.

**Address**
Rue de la Grange aux Bois, BP 45059, 57072 Metz Cedex 03.

## Services

- **Catering**: on-site catering during events (provided by the operator).
- **Auditorium**: 600 seats, integrated into the venue.
- **Meeting rooms**: 12 modular spaces for conferences, workshops and training sessions.
- **Business tourism**: operational continuity with the Centre de Congrès Robert Schuman and the Centre du Technopôle, also managed by Metz Événements.
- **Exhibitor services**: technical assistance from GL Events (logistics, furniture, staging equipment, AV, security).

## News

The Parc des Expositions de l''Eurométropole de Metz remains part of the **GL Events** group, which has renewed its operational investment across the 3 Metz Événements sites (Parc Expo, Robert Schuman, Technopôle).

No publicly available information on any potential extension project or new environmental certifications (ISO 20121, etc.) to date.

## Flagship trade shows

- **Foire Internationale de Metz (FIM)**: created in 1928 by Camille Hocquard, a Metz merchant, under the name *Foire Exposition de Metz*. The 2026 edition (91st) runs from **25 September to 5 October 2026**, on the theme *Polynesia*, with around 550 stands across 44,000 sqm (halls + marquees + open-air space). Organised by Metz Expo Événements (GL Events).
- **Agrimax**: a professional agricultural trade show for the Grand Est region (machinery, livestock, crop production, services). The 2026 edition is scheduled for 28-30 October 2026 (Wednesday-Friday). Organised by Metz Expo Événements. See [Agrimax 2026](/salons/agrimax-metz).
- **Salon Habitat & Jardin**: 20-23 March 2026, around 120 stands and around 10,000 expected visitors. Building, renovation, interior fit-out, energy and decoration. Organised by Metz Expo Événements.
- **Aux Vignobles!**: a wine and gastronomy trade show, 26th edition on 27-29 March 2026, with 95 winegrowers and producers.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-expo-metz';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Parc Expo de Rennes offers 44,000 sqm of exhibition space in the heart of Brittany. It hosts the renowned SPACE trade show along with numerous professional agricultural and industrial events.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

Parc Expo Rennes Aéroport is located in **Bruz**, around **12 km south-west of Rennes**, immediately bordering **Rennes-Saint-Jacques airport**. The site occupies a footprint mainly within the municipality of Bruz, with a few plots extending into Saint-Jacques-de-la-Lande and Chartres-de-Bretagne. According to Wikipedia, the land and the halls are owned by **Groupe Pigeault Immobilier**, which also operates them: it is one of the few large French exhibition centres to run under a private regime rather than public or semi-public management.

The centre totals **more than 54,000 sqm of heated covered space**, supplemented by approximately **58,000 sqm of outdoor space**, for a total of more than **110,000 sqm of exhibition space**. The site''s distinctiveness lies in the combination of an active airport platform less than 2 km away and road access directly off the Rennes ring road, making it one of the few exhibition centres in the West capable of hosting an international-scale agricultural format (SPACE) within easy reach of both plane and road.

## Halls

The centre spreads across **11 modular halls**, laid out at ground level, without intermediate columns, with a black bitumen floor. All the halls are equipped with full LED lighting, sound systems and WiFi access points.

- **Reception hall**: main entrance, visitor and exhibitor reception, event ticketing.
- **Halls 1, 2/3, 4, 5, 7, 8**: modular exhibition halls, used in a unified or partitioned configuration depending on the trade show.
- **Hall 9**: **4,971 sqm**, also operated under the **MusikHALL** brand for concert formats and stage events (performance-venue configuration).
- **Halls 10A and 10B**: two twin halls, used in tandem or separately.
- **Hall 11**: additional hall.
- **Espace Bretagne** and **Central Parc**: complementary spaces usable for receptions, conference areas or as an extension of neighbouring halls.

The detailed individual surface areas per hall are not published openly by the operator: technical plans and quotes are available on request from the organiser.

## Access and transport

The site sits alongside the **Rennes ring road**, accessible from **exit 8 Porte de Saint-Nazaire**, and close to the **RN137** and the **RD37**.

- **Address**: 2 La Haie Gautrais, CS 27211, 35172 Bruz Cedex.
- **Rennes-Saint-Jacques airport**: less than 2 km away, around 10 minutes by taxi.
- **STAR bus**: **line 13** serves the *Parc Expo* stop Monday to Saturday, with a metro connection at Saint-Jacques - Gaîté (line B) and at the Henri Fréville, Clémenceau and La Poterie stations (line A). On **Sundays and public holidays**, **line C7** takes over, running hourly in the morning and every 35 minutes in the afternoon.
- **Additional lines**: 57 and KER-LANNEX also serve the *Parc Expo* stop depending on the period.
- **Event shuttles**: set up by some organisers for the major trade shows (SPACE, CFIA, Artibat) from Rennes Centre or Rennes TGV station.
- **Rennes TGV station**: around 20 minutes by public transport or taxi (Paris 1h30 away by TGV).
- **Parking**: **8,000 free spaces** on site according to Rennes Parc Expo.

## Services

The range of services is sized for major professional and public events.

- **Catering**: catering points and food courts open during events, operated by the operator''s partner caterers. No catering open outside the exhibition period.
- **Connectivity**: WiFi coverage across all 11 halls.
- **Technical equipment**: full LED lighting and integrated sound systems in the halls and across the outdoor areas.
- **Meeting rooms**: *Espace Bretagne* and *Central Parc* available for receptions, conference areas and press points.
- **Accommodation**: no hotel integrated within the centre. The offering is concentrated in Bruz, Saint-Jacques-de-la-Lande and the centre of Rennes (~12 km), supplemented by the airport zone.

Detailed pricing terms (room hire, premium exhibitor services) are provided by the operator on request.

## News

The centre operates under the private management of Groupe Pigeault Immobilier, which runs and develops the facility in line with the calendar of flagship trade shows. **Hall 9 / MusikHALL** has shaped the site''s events positioning in recent years, notably hosting concerts as part of the **Trans Musicales**, alongside the trade show calendar.

No consolidated public schedule of further expansion has been communicated to date by the operator. The airport proximity and the pressure on land in the southern Rennes belt remain medium-term issues for the next generations of halls.

## Flagship trade shows

Parc Expo Rennes Aéroport concentrates the structuring events of several French and Breton sectors. A selection referenced on Agoris:

- [SPACE](/salons/space-rennes): the **Salon International des Productions Animales**, a global benchmark for the livestock sector. The **40th edition** is announced for **15 to 17 September 2026**, with more than 1,200 exhibitors announced and around 100,000 visitors expected according to the organiser.
- [CFIA Rennes](/salons/cfia-rennes): the **Carrefour des Fournisseurs de l''Industrie Agroalimentaire**, the long-standing sourcing platform for the food industry, with its **30th edition** announced for **9 to 11 March 2027**.
- [Artibat](/salons/artibat-rennes): the construction and public works trade show for the Greater West, a biennial format held in odd-numbered years, with its **2027 edition** announced for the autumn, featuring around **1,100 exhibitors** and **45,000** professional **visitors** across **62,000 sqm** of exhibition space.

The site also hosts the **Foire de Rennes** (a general-public event held annually in spring), the **Carrefour de l''Eau** and several regional trade shows that structure the West.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-expo-rennes';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='The leading events venue in the Hautes-Pyrénées, the Parc des Expositions de Tarbes (Tarbes Expo Pyrénées Congrès) offers 24,695 sqm spread across seven halls and extensive outdoor areas, on the Kennedy zone straddling Tarbes and Laloubère.', editorial_mdx_en='The Parc des Expositions de Tarbes, operated under the Tarbes Expo Pyrénées Congrès brand, is the leading events venue in the Hautes-Pyrénées. Located on the Kennedy business park, straddling the municipalities of Tarbes and Laloubère, it hosts fairs, trade shows, conferences and sporting events. The site combines several covered halls with extensive outdoor areas, close to the town centre.

## Halls and surface areas

The venue offers a total surface area of 24,695 sqm, including 12,695 sqm of covered space and around 12,000 sqm of outdoor areas. It comprises seven halls and rooms:

- Hall Vignemale: 5,200 sqm, column-free
- Hall Pic du Midi: 3,450 sqm
- Salle Arbizon: 1,150 sqm
- Salle Néouvielle: 1,340 sqm
- Salle Marboré: 1,000 sqm
- Espace des Lacs: 300 sqm
- Espace des Cirques: 119 sqm

## Access and transport

The venue is bordered to the north by the Boulevard du Président-Kennedy (southern ring road) and to the east by the Rue des Évadés-de-France. It adjoins the Tarbes-Laloubère aerodrome.

## Services

The site has free on-site parking. The spaces are modular and fitted with technical installations for organising trade shows, conferences and events. For visitors travelling to the area, the accommodation and dining options of the Tarbes conurbation are available nearby (see the /villes/tarbes page for local context).', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-expo-tarbes';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Exhibition centre serving the Montpellier metropolitan area, located in Pérols. 60,000 sq m of covered space spread across 10 pavilions, served by line 3 of the tramway (Parc Expo stop) and close to Montpellier-Méditerranée airport.', editorial_mdx_en='The Parc des Expositions de Montpellier is the main venue for trade shows and fairs in the Montpellier metropolitan area. Located in Pérols, to the east of Montpellier and in the immediate vicinity of the airport and the coastal lagoons, it belongs to the Occitanie Region and hosts major professional events every year, such as SITEVI. The complex also incorporates the Sud de France Arena.

## Halls and surface areas

The centre offers 60,000 sq m of covered exhibition space, spread across 10 pavilions (halls A1 to A6 and B1 to B5). The site additionally includes a conference centre and reception areas, as well as the adjoining Sud de France Arena.

## Access and transport

The centre is served by line 3 of the Montpellier tramway, "Parc Expo" stop, which connects directly to Montpellier Saint-Roch railway station and the city centre. Montpellier-Méditerranée airport is just a few minutes from the site. The centre has several thousand parking spaces.

## Services

The site offers reception and meeting spaces, a conference centre and catering areas used during events. Numerous hotels are located nearby, between Pérols, the airport and the centre of Montpellier, which is accessible by tramway.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-expositions-montpellier';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='A 23,000 sqm events venue at the heart of the Parc Floral, in the bois de Vincennes (Paris 12th), with a main hall and five botanically themed pavilions. Metro line 1 and RER A, Château de Vincennes station.', editorial_mdx_en='The Parc Floral de Paris is a 35-hectare public park set in the bois de Vincennes, at the heart of the 12th arrondissement. Opened in 1969, it houses at its centre the Espace Evenements, a complex dedicated to hosting trade shows, congresses and professional events. The site combines a vast covered hall with several botanically themed pavilions opening onto the greenery, offering a natural setting that is rare for an events venue within central Paris.

## Halls and surface areas

The Espace Evenements covers around 23,000 sqm at the centre of the Parc Floral, including nearly 12,000 sqm of indoor exhibition space. It is built around a main hall (the Hall de la Pinede, 10,200 sqm) and five pavilions (one of 282 sqm and four of 384 sqm), one of which has a conference room. Depending on the chosen configuration, the site can accommodate from 100 to 12,000 people.

## Access and transport

The Parc Floral is served by metro line 1 and RER A, with Château de Vincennes station located a few hundred metres from the entrance. Gare de Lyon is reachable in around fifteen minutes. By car, the site is connected to the ring road and the A4 motorway, with a car park nearby. Orly and Roissy-Charles-de-Gaulle airports are around 20 and 35 minutes away respectively.

## Services

The venue offers modular spaces both indoors and outdoors, within an exceptional landscaped setting. Catering services and associated provisions are arranged on a case-by-case basis with the site''s events operator. The Parc Floral is in the immediate vicinity of Vincennes and the 12th arrondissement of Paris, which offer a wide range of accommodation and services. Other trade shows take place in Paris: see the /villes/paris page.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='parc-floral-de-paris';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Located in the heart of Paris, the Parc des Expositions de la Porte de Versailles is the leading urban event venue in Europe. Renovated as part of the Viparis project, it offers 228,000 sqm of space.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

Paris Expo Porte de Versailles is France''s leading exhibition centre located in the heart of the city. Set in the 15th arrondissement, it belongs to the **City of Paris** and has been operated since 1987 by **Viparis**, a joint subsidiary of the CCI Paris Île-de-France and Unibail-Rodamco. The site covers **228,000 sqm** in total, including **212,545 sqm of indoor exhibition space** spread across **seven halls**, complemented by **72,000 sqm of green space**.

Construction of the venue began in **1923** at the joint initiative of the City of Paris and the Foire de Paris, then in search of a permanent site for its agricultural and equestrian editions. The site opened to the public in January 1925 with the Salon de la Machine Agricole. The monumental entrance with its four bell towers was built in 1937, on the occasion of the Exposition Internationale des Arts et Techniques. The venue today hosts more than **200 events per year** for around **7.5 million visitors**, across formats ranging from B2B professional trade shows to paid public-facing events. In 2024, it served as the "South Paris Arena" for the Olympic handball, weightlifting, volleyball and table tennis events.

## Halls

The venue spans **seven** operational **halls**, each with its own logic of surface area and modularity.

- **Hall 1**: the largest single-span hall, **44,936 sqm**, a central nave in natural light, with direct access to the grounds. It typically hosts large-scale bare-floor formats (Foire de Paris Home & Co zone, Concours Lépine).
- **Halls 2, 3 and 4**: a poolable ensemble used in a unified or partitioned configuration depending on requirements. **Halls 2 and 3 are undergoing complete reconstruction** over the period December 2025 to September 2028 as part of the final modernisation phase.
- **Hall 5**: a modular structure split across three sub-spaces (5.1, 5.2, 5.3), used for trade shows with variable formats.
- **Hall 6**: the architectural signature of the site, delivered in **2019** by the firm **Valode et Pistre** (lead architect, façade of vertical white slats), topped by a **monumental canopy designed by Jean Nouvel**: a triangular steel monolith (70 m per side, up to 50 m in height when raised) fitted with more than **600 LED battens**. **21,400 sqm**, with a three-level parking base and a planted rooftop terrace hosting a **14,000 sqm urban farm** (one of the largest in Europe) as well as restaurants and an event space with views over Paris.
- **Hall 7**: three interconnected levels totalling **70,163 sqm**, with up to **36 configurable spaces**, an atrium with Eiffel Tower views and a rooftop terrace. The top level houses the **Paris Convention Centre**, the largest congress centre in Europe, with a **5,200-seat** plenary, **28 conference rooms** (160 to 5,200 seats) and **48 meeting rooms** (20 to 602 seats).
- **Hall 8**: a new hall under construction, with delivery scheduled for September 2028, as part of the same project as Halls 2 and 3.

## Access and transport

The venue stands out for its multimodal connections within the city, a rarity for a site of this scale.

- **Métro**: line **12, Porte de Versailles station** (exit 1 for Halls 2 to 7, exit 2 for Hall 1). **Balard** station on line **8** nearby.
- **Tram**: lines **T2 and T3a**, stop **Porte de Versailles - Parc des Expositions**.
- **Bus**: RATP lines 39 and 80, as well as the Noctilien night service.
- **Vélib''**: dedicated station on avenue Ernest Renan.
- **Car**: several on-site car parks (P1, P6, P7, P8), with direct access to the halls from P6 underground. Disabled parking spaces near the lifts and pedestrian exits.

The official address is **1 Place de la Porte de Versailles, 75015 Paris**. The site is accessible from Orly via the T7 tram and Paris-Charles-de-Gaulle via the RER B + métro 12.

## Services

The venue operates like an event micro-city. The range of services includes:

- **Catering** in multiple forms: food courts spread across the halls, rooftop restaurants on Hall 6, cafés and quick-service points throughout the site.
- **Paris Convention Centre** (Hall 7): an integrated congress centre with 28 conference rooms and 48 modular meeting rooms.
- **On-site accommodation**: Mama Shelter and Novotel for a total of 450 rooms, complemented by Eklo, Le Perchoir and the wider Île-de-France offering (149,000 rooms across the region).
- **Disabled access**: wheelchairs available at the security control point, the MAP''EXPO interactive navigation tool, dedicated assistance at door A2.
- WiFi across the site, Viparis event concierge services at major occasions.

## News

The venue is in the final stretch of a modernisation programme launched in 1996 and structured in three major phases.

- **Hall 7**: a major renovation completed in 2017, incorporating a 30% reduction in the hall''s energy consumption, 8-metre mobile partitions in hall 7.3, and the creation of the Paris Convention Centre on the upper level (opened 2017).
- **Hall 6**: delivered in 2019, designed by Valode et Pistre, with a monumental LED-batten canopy designed by Jean Nouvel and a 14,000 sqm rooftop urban farm (the largest rooftop farm in Europe), a European benchmark for integrating nature into an inner-city event site.
- **Halls 2 and 3**: complete demolition and reconstruction, plus the creation of **Hall 8**, over the period **December 2025 to September 2028**, the final phase of the Viparis ten-year plan.

Once the 2028 works are complete, the venue will have eight operational halls.

## Flagship trade shows

Paris Expo Porte de Versailles hosts a broad spectrum of public-facing and B2B trade shows. Among the defining dates of the calendar:

- [Foire de Paris](/salons/foire-de-paris): the largest public-facing commercial fair in Europe, 12 days over the May Day bank holiday weekend.
- [Maison&Objet](/salons/maison-objet-paris): the global benchmark for decoration and the art of living, two editions per year (January and September).
- [Wine Paris & Vinexposium](/salons/wine-paris): the global leader in professional wine and spirits trade shows.
- [Mondial de l''Auto](/salons/mondial-auto-paris): the biennial international motor show, Halls 1, 4, 6.
- [VivaTech](/salons/vivatech): the major European tech trade show, scheduled for Hall 7 in 2026.
- [Salon des Maires et des Collectivités Locales](/salons/smcl): the benchmark for the local public sector.
- [MIF Expo](/salons/mif-expo): the Made in France trade show.
- [Paris Photo](/salons/paris-photo): the international art photography fair.
- [Equip''Hôtel](/salons/equip-hotel-paris): the European benchmark for hospitality and catering.
- [RENT](/salons/rent): Real Estate & New Technologies, the proptech trade show.
- [Pharmagora Plus](/salons/pharmagora-plus): the annual community pharmacy congress.
- [SantExpo](/salons/santexpo-paris): the trade show for hospital decision-makers.
- [SIMI](/salons/simi): the commercial real estate trade show.
- [Paris Games Week](/salons/paris-games-week): the benchmark video game trade show in France.

The venue also hosts the **Salon International de l''Agriculture** ([SIA 2027](/salons/sia-paris)), Hall 1 and associated halls, over nine days at the end of February.
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='paris-expo-porte-de-versailles';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Europe''s largest indoor arena, opened in 2017 in the Jardins de l''Arche in Nanterre, on the edge of the La Défense business district. An ultra-modular venue covering around 94,000 sqm (full complex), hosting concerts, sport and trade shows.', editorial_mdx_en='## Paris La Défense Arena

Opened in 2017 at the heart of the Jardins de l''Arche in Nanterre, immediately adjacent to the La Défense business district, Paris La Défense Arena is the largest indoor arena in Europe. Designed by the Atelier Christian de Portzamparc, this ultra-modular venue hosts everything from Racing 92 matches and major concerts to trade shows, corporate conventions and conferences, thanks to a retractable roof, retractable stands and spaces that can be reconfigured to suit requirements.

### Capacity and spaces

The complex as a whole covers a total surface area of around 94,000 sqm, including the arena, office spaces and student housing. For professional events, the venue offers modular spaces that can be reconfigured according to the nature of the event.

### Access and transport

The venue is served by the RER A (La Défense and Nanterre Préfecture stations) and by metro line 1 (La Défense terminus). Numerous parking spaces are available throughout the La Défense district.

### Services

The site features catering facilities, including a brasserie and a restaurant, as well as shops. For accommodation, the dense hotel offering of the La Défense district is just a few minutes'' walk away.

To discover all the trade shows and professional events held in Nanterre, see the [Trade shows in Nanterre](/villes/nanterre) page.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='paris-la-defense-arena';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Located at Le Bourget airport, this exhibition centre is world-renowned for the Salon International de l''Aéronautique et de l''Espace. It offers 100,000 sqm of indoor and outdoor space.', editorial_mdx_en='## Description

The Parc des Expositions Paris Le Bourget is located on the grounds of Le Bourget airport, in Seine-Saint-Denis. The airport, inaugurated in 1919 on a military airfield created in 1914, was the first civil airport in Paris and remained so until the opening of Orly in the 1940s. It was notably at Le Bourget that Charles Lindbergh completed the first non-stop transatlantic crossing in May 1927. Since 1981, the platform has been dedicated to business aviation: it remains to this day the leading business airport in Europe.

The exhibition centre was developed from the 1950s onwards at the initiative of **GIFAS** (Groupement des industries françaises aéronautiques et spatiales), to host the **Salon International de l''Aéronautique et de l''Espace**, founded in 1909 and established at Le Bourget in 1953. The legal structure derives directly from this: the land belongs to **Groupe ADP** (Aéroports de Paris), operator of the airport platform; the SIAE company holds a long-term ground lease running until 2055 over the centre''s halls and infrastructure; **Viparis** has operated everything outside the SIAE since 2003, under a delegation granted by the CCI Paris Île-de-France and Unibail-Rodamco-Westfield.

Le Bourget''s distinctiveness lies in the rare combination of **covered halls**, **esplanades** and **direct access to the airport''s active tarmac**. It is the only site in the Paris region that allows the display of full-scale aircraft (airliners, helicopters, military equipment, heavy drones) statically on the runway and in dynamic demonstrations. This specificity shapes the programming: the site is less generalist than [Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles) or [Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte), and concentrates its major events on the aeronautics, defence, mobility and textile sourcing sectors.

## Halls & infrastructure

The centre totals **86,242 sqm of covered space** across **5 halls**, supplemented by approximately **250,000 sqm of outdoor space** (esplanades, car parks, tarmac areas). The overall footprint of the site is close to 60 hectares, a significant portion of which is green space.

- **Hall 1**: ~4,000 sqm, an intimate format suited to specialised trade shows.
- **Hall 2**: divided into three sub-halls: 2A (~10,500 sqm), 2B (~24,300 sqm, the largest volume in the centre) and 2C (~3,300 sqm).
- **Hall 3**: ~19,300 sqm. Demolished then rebuilt between 2021 and 2023 (architect Atelier de Midi, project owner SIAE, partial funding from SOLIDEO). A hybrid structure of French timber and low-carbon concrete, certified NF HQE Excellent and BREEAM Very Good. It served as the Main Media Centre (MMC) for the Paris 2024 Olympic and Paralympic Games before returning to exhibition use.
- **Hall 4**: ~19,400 sqm.
- **Hall 5**: ~5,600 sqm.

The centre also features a **195-seat auditorium**, **6 meeting rooms** and storage areas. Tarmac access, managed jointly with ADP, makes it possible to line up the equivalent of several hundred linear metres of aircraft on the runway during the major editions.

## Access & transport

The site is located 12 km north-east of Paris, alongside the A1 and A3 motorways.

- **RER B**: **Le Bourget** station, then bus 152 or a dedicated shuttle laid on during major events. The **Dugny - La Courneuve** station (RER B, opened in 2017) serves the southern part of the centre.
- **Metro line 17**: the **Le Bourget Aéroport** station, planned for 2027 as part of the Grand Paris Express, will bring the journey time to Roissy-CDG airport down to around 12 minutes.
- **Road**: A1 "Le Bourget" and A3 "Stains" exits; around 20 minutes from central Paris outside peak hours.
- **Roissy-Charles-de-Gaulle airport** 8 km away, **Orly** 35 km away.
- **Heliport**: the Le Bourget helicopter base, which is operational, allows the direct reception of delegations and VIP guests during the major trade shows.
- **Parking**: several thousand spaces around the halls, plus event car parks mobilised for the major trade shows (the SIAE in particular).

## Services

The service offering follows the site''s event rhythm, with no permanent catering on site. Services are mobilised at the organisers'' request:

- **Catering**: temporary outlets, food courts and premium catering for major events (notably the SIAE), operated by Viparis''s partner caterers. No catering open outside events.
- **Meeting rooms**: 6 modular rooms and a 195-seat auditorium, available to supplement the halls for conferences, press briefings and B2B programmes.
- **Accommodation**: no hotel integrated within the centre. The offering is concentrated in the **Aulnay-sous-Bois**, **Saint-Denis**, **La Plaine** areas and the **Roissy-CDG** airport zone (~8 km), with several thousand rooms available for the major trade shows.
- **Logistics services**: technical management, handling, utilities, security and tarmac coordination provided by Viparis and, for the SIAE, by the show''s dedicated teams.

## News

The site has entered a phase of continuous modernisation. Three projects shape the agenda:

- **Hall 3 delivered in 2023**: complete reconstruction and commissioning as the Main Media Centre for the Paris 2024 Olympic and Paralympic Games. The hall switched to exhibition use in autumn 2024 and hosted its first SIAE edition in June 2025 (55th edition). The next edition will take place from 14 to 20 June 2027 (56th).
- **Grand Paris Express**: the opening of the **Le Bourget Aéroport** station (line 17), planned for 2027, will transform the centre''s accessibility from Saint-Denis-Pleyel and Roissy.
- **ADP 2030 plans**: Groupe ADP is continuing the redevelopment of the airport zone and its surroundings, with an overall vision for Le Bourget''s aeronautics ecosystem (the adjacent Air and Space Museum, the business aviation hub, the exhibition centre). No consolidated public timetable as yet for the next generation of halls.

In terms of programming, the site is strengthening its aeronautics-defence positioning (SIAE) and textile sourcing (the Texworld cluster, back at Le Bourget since 2025) while occasionally hosting large-format event operations (community gatherings, niche motor shows, audiovisual productions).

## Flagship trade shows

Le Bourget concentrates a benchmark programme across three sectors: **aeronautics-defence-space**, **textile sourcing**, and **classic cars / heritage vehicles**.

- [Salon International de l''Aéronautique et de l''Espace (SIAE) - Paris Air Show](/salons/siae-le-bourget): biennial in odd-numbered years, 56th edition from 14 to 20 June 2027. The world''s leading civil and military aeronautics trade show; organised by SIAE SA, a subsidiary of GIFAS. Mobilises all the halls and the tarmac.
- [Texworld Apparel Sourcing Paris](/salons/texworld-paris): two editions per year (February and September). Historically held at Le Bourget, the trade show was moved to Paris Expo Porte de Versailles in 2023 and 2024 during the reconstruction of Hall 3 and the 2024 Olympic Games, before returning to Le Bourget in February 2025. An international textile sourcing cluster run by Messe Frankfurt France. Halls 2, 3 and 4.
- **Fatex**: a fashion sourcing trade show, co-located with Texworld for the February editions.
- **Automédon**: a classic vehicles trade show, held annually since 2001, generally in October.
- **Rencontre Annuelle des Musulmans de France (RAMF)**: organised by the UOIF, a large-scale annual event.
- **Salon des Formations et Métiers Aéronautiques**: the 2026 edition organised by GIFAS, aimed at young people and career changers moving into the aeronautics sectors.
- **Salon des Véhicules de Loisirs**: an annual edition dedicated to motorhomes and caravans.

For planning an industrial or international event that does not require tarmac access, the alternative options in the Paris region are [Paris Nord Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte) (greater capacity, 246,000 sqm covered) and [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles) (inner-city, public access).', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='paris-le-bourget';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='The largest exhibition centre in France with 246,000 sqm of covered space, located close to Paris-Charles de Gaulle Airport. It hosts the leading international B2B trade shows.', editorial_mdx_en='## Overview

Paris Nord Villepinte is the largest exhibition centre in France, positioned as a benchmark B2B hub on a European scale. Located 15 minutes from Paris-Charles-de-Gaulle Airport and around 30 minutes from central Paris, the site totals nearly **243,500 sqm** of modular covered space, complemented by **200,000 sqm of green outdoor space** across a **135-hectare** estate.

The centre is operated by [Viparis](https://www.viparis.com), a joint venture owned in equal shares by the **Chambre de commerce et d''industrie Paris Île-de-France** and **Unibail-Rodamco-Westfield**. Inaugurated in December 1982 with an initial phase of 70,000 sqm, the site has undergone several rounds of expansion to reach its current configuration of **9 halls** (8 main halls numbered Hall 1 to Hall 7, plus the standalone Hall 8). Each year it welcomes more than **2 million visitors**, leading international trade shows, corporate conventions, congresses and consumer events.

## Halls and configuration

The centre is organised around a central glazed reception gallery that distributes the halls. Seven halls are interconnected and two halls operate independently. All offer a minimum clear height of 9 metres, rising to 12 or 15 metres in certain areas.

- **Hall 1**: 16,455 sqm, mezzanine and meeting rooms, connects with Hall 2.
- **Hall 2**: 16,389 sqm, mezzanine and meeting rooms, connected to Hall 1.
- **Hall 3**: 19,347 sqm, mezzanine, gives access to Hall 4.
- **Hall 4**: 19,316 sqm, clear heights up to 15 m, adjacent to a conference centre featuring a 628-seat auditorium.
- **Hall 5A**: 49,734 sqm, internal patio, central convergence point linking Halls 4, 5B and 6, with 18 meeting rooms nearby.
- **Hall 5B**: 26,611 sqm, column-free, divisible and combinable with the neighbouring halls.
- **Hall 6**: 46,598 sqm, two dedicated entrances, integrated restaurant and shop.
- **Hall 7**: 34,065 sqm, HQE-certified, 17,000 sqm green roof, internal dimensions 90 to 120 m wide by 350 m long.
- **Hall 8**: 15,000 sqm, divisible and independent, located closest to the Parc des Expositions RER station.

The centre also offers **38 equipped meeting rooms** and **2 auditoriums** (a large one seating 628 and a second seating around 170), as well as several conference centres distributed across the halls.

## Access & transport

**RER B**: line B of the RER serves the centre at the **Parc des Expositions** station, located right on the edge of the site. The journey from Gare du Nord takes around **20 minutes**, and **5 to 7 minutes** from Paris-CDG Airport. Note that not all line B trains stop at Parc des Expositions: check the service displayed on the platform.

**Car**: the centre is accessible via the **A1 motorway** (Paris-Lille axis) and the **A104 motorway** (la Francilienne), in the immediate vicinity of the ZAC Paris Nord 2. Several visitor car parks are distributed around the halls (P1, P2, P3, P10, P11). Accessible parking spaces are reserved in most car parks.

**Bus**: several lines serve the site, including the **RATP 350** line and the buses of the T''Bus network run by the Terres d''Envol conurbation.

**Event shuttles**: during major trade shows, free shuttles are frequently set up between central Paris, Paris-CDG Airport and the centre, generally running every 30 minutes during opening hours.

**Airport**: Paris-Charles-de-Gaulle is 15 minutes away by car, and international carriers operate direct connections there from all the major global hubs.

**Future metro**: **line 17 of the Grand Paris Express** will serve the Parc des Expositions station from **2028** (phase 2 opening between Le Bourget Aéroport and Parc des Expositions), with an elevated station whose platforms sit 14 m above ground. Phase 3, scheduled for 2030, will extend the line as far as Le Mesnil-Amelot via Roissy-CDG.

## On-site services

**Catering**: fixed catering outlets (food courts, sit-down restaurants) and takeaway kiosks are located in the reception gallery and within the main halls. Hall 6, for example, includes a catering area and a shop. Exhibitors can also order on-stand catering services through the site''s official service providers.

**Exhibitor services**: on-stand delivery, furniture hire, stand security and surveillance, business centre, meeting rooms equipped with high-speed WiFi, reception concierge.

**Accessibility**: fully accessible routes, accessible parking spaces in the car parks (P1: 15 spaces, P2: 5 spaces, P3: 16 spaces) and an internal shuttle service between car parks and the esplanade.

**Connectivity**: high-density WiFi across the halls, network infrastructure sized for major international events.

## News

In **September 2010** the centre inaugurated **Hall 7**, a 34,065 sqm facility certified **HQE** and fitted with a **green roof** of more than 15,000 sqm, for an investment of around 67 million euros. It is the most recent and most sustainable hall on the site, designed to host up to 1.5 million visitors a year and 900 to 1,200 stands.

In 2024, [Eurosatory](/salons/eurosatory-paris) introduced the use of Hall 5B to meet the growth of the global defence trade show. The **2026 edition of Eurosatory** (15 to 19 June) marks the first use of **Hall 4** for the event, which will thus span **Halls 4, 5A, 5B and 6**, i.e. more than 185,000 sqm occupied, reflecting the strong momentum of the defence and security sector.

The centre also hosted, in **2024**, several events of the Paris Olympic and Paralympic Games (preliminary boxing rounds, modern pentathlon fencing, Paralympic sitting volleyball), confirming its versatility beyond B2B.

## Flagship trade shows hosted

Paris Nord Villepinte concentrates a very significant share of the largest French and international B2B trade shows held in Paris:

- **Industry & defence**: [Eurosatory](/salons/eurosatory-paris) (land defence, biennial), [Milipol Paris](/salons/milipol-paris) (homeland security), [Euronaval](/salons/euronaval-paris) (naval defence), [World Nuclear Exhibition](/salons/world-nuclear-exhibition) (civil nuclear).
- **Food & beverage**: [SIAL Paris](/salons/sial-paris), the global food trade show, biennial.
- **Materials & composites**: [JEC World](/salons/jec-world), the global benchmark for composites.
- **Construction**: [Batimat](/salons/batimat), the international construction trade show.
- **Agriculture**: [AgriSIMA](/salons/sima-paris), the agricultural equipment trade show.
- **Home & decor**: [Maison&Objet](/salons/maison-objet-paris), the biannual art-of-living event.
- **Fashion & textiles**: [Première Vision Paris](/salons/premiere-vision-paris), the global high-end textile trade show.
- **Packaging & logistics**: [ALL4PACK Emballage Paris](/salons/all4pack-paris), [SITL](/salons/sitl) (transport and logistics).
- **Consumer B2C**: [Japan Expo](/salons/japan-expo-paris), [Comic Con France](/salons/comic-con-france).
', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='paris-nord-villepinte';
UPDATE venues SET description_en='Rangueil university campus of Université Toulouse III - Paul Sabatier, along the route de Narbonne in the south-east of Toulouse. Hosts conventions and professional events in its lecture theatres and conference rooms. Served by metro line B at the Université Paul-Sabatier station and by the Téléo urban cable car.', editorial_mdx_en='Université Toulouse III - Paul Sabatier occupies the Rangueil campus, in the south-east of Toulouse, along the route de Narbonne. A scientific and health-sciences institution descended from the Université de Toulouse, it occasionally hosts professional events and conventions in its lecture theatres and conference rooms. It is not an exhibition centre but a university campus, some of whose spaces serve as conference venues.

## Halls and surface areas

The Rangueil campus is not a conventional exhibition site: it has no exhibition halls or pavilions in the sense of an exhibition centre. Events are held in lecture theatres and conference rooms. No standardised exhibition surface area is published for this venue.

## Access and transport

The campus is served by the Université Paul-Sabatier metro station (line B), located directly along the route de Narbonne, between the Faculté-de-Pharmacie and Ramonville stations. The Téléo urban cable car also serves an Université Paul Sabatier stop. From Toulouse Saint-Agne station, line B (towards Ramonville) provides access to the campus. Toulouse-Blagnac airport lies to the west of the metropolitan area.

## Services

As a university campus, the site offers the usual amenities of a university (university catering, reception areas). Specific event services (on-site catering, wifi, nearby accommodation) depend on the organiser of each event and are not centralised for this venue.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='universite-paul-sabatier-toulouse';

UPDATE salons SET description_en='Trade show for additive manufacturing and 3D printing. Technologies, materials and applications for industry and prototyping.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- 3D PRINT Lyon (3D Print Congress & Exhibition) is the leading French trade show dedicated 100% to additive manufacturing and 3D printing, organised by **Infopro Digital Trade Shows** at **Eurexpo Lyon**.
- The 2026 edition ran from 2 to 4 June 2026 (Hall 7), co-located with the **[FIP - France Innovation Plasturgie](/salons/plastic-expo-lyon)** trade show: the 3D PRINT badge grants access to FIP, at the crossroads of additive manufacturing and plastics, composites and rubber solutions.
- The next edition is announced for September 2027 at Eurexpo Lyon (exact dates not yet published on the official site).
- Latest available figures (2025 edition, 12th edition, 3 to 5 June): more than 3,351 participants (source: 3dprint-exhibition-lyon.com/bilan-2025). The 2026 figures have not yet been published.
- Four awards structure the show''s innovation: 3D INNOVATION AWARD, 3D COLLAB AWARD, START-UP CONTEST and the 3D APPLICATION EXCELLENCE AWARD (introduced in 2026).

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Manufacturers and distributors of 3D printing machines (FDM, SLA, SLS, DMLS), suppliers of materials and powders, CAD and manufacturing software publishers, engineering offices and prototyping services, scanning and reverse-engineering providers, research centres and start-ups. A professional visitor base from industry: aerospace, automotive, medical, consumer goods, plastics processing.

Co-location with [FIP - France Innovation Plasturgie](/salons/plastic-expo-lyon) broadens the scope to the entire value chain of industrial materials and processes (plastics, composites, rubber).

### Notable exhibitors

List of exhibitors on the official site: [3dprint-exhibition-lyon.com](https://www.3dprint-exhibition-lyon.com/fr/liste-des-exposants). The 2025 edition brought together around 150 exhibitors and brands in additive manufacturing, notably with a strong presence of consumer and Asian players (Atome3D City area) alongside industrial firms (source: 3dnatives.com).

### Visitor quality

- A professional visitor base with an industrial and decision-maker focus (executives, heads of engineering offices and project managers) (source: 3dnatives.com).
- More than 3,351 participants at the 2025 edition (source: 3dprint-exhibition-lyon.com/bilan-2025).
- A significant share of visitors report concrete projects under way and an already established use of additive manufacturing (source: 3dnatives.com).

## Events & community

### Conference programme

The show offers a programme of free-access conferences and workshops, complemented by the themed Soft & Scan areas (software and 3D digitisation). The programme covers processes (FDM, SLA, SLS, DMLS), materials, industrial applications and case studies. The detailed programme for the September 2027 edition will be published as the show approaches.

### Awards and prizes

Four awards are presented at the show:

- **3D INNOVATION AWARD**: rewards the best innovation in additive manufacturing (machine, material, software), across all categories.
- **3D COLLAB AWARD**: rewards the best client-supplier collaboration on an industrial project integrating additive manufacturing.
- **START-UP CONTEST**: distinguishes the most innovative start-ups in additive manufacturing.
- **3D APPLICATION EXCELLENCE AWARD**: rewards an innovative application demonstrating the practical potential of additive manufacturing (introduced at the 2026 edition).

### Community and networks

The show brings together the French additive manufacturing community (industrial firms, SMEs, start-ups and research centres) and draws on the sector''s specialist press ecosystem. Co-location with [FIP - France Innovation Plasturgie](/salons/plastic-expo-lyon) strengthens the links with the plastics-processing sector.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Eurexpo Lyon](/lieux/eurexpo-lyon) listing.

Specific to the show:

- **Venue:** Eurexpo Lyon, Hall 7 (Boulevard de l''Europe, 69686 Chassieu) (source: 3dprint-exhibition-lyon.com/informations-pratiques).
- **Visitor badge:** reserved for professionals (entry forbidden to under-15s and to animals). The 3D PRINT badge also grants access to the [FIP - France Innovation Plasturgie](/salons/plastic-expo-lyon) trade show.
- **Access:** Eurexpo shuttle from the Vaulx-en-Velin La Soie station (Rhônexpress from Lyon Saint-Exupéry airport), public transport from Lyon Part-Dieu, road access via the A43 and A46 (source: 3dprint-exhibition-lyon.com/informations-pratiques).

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates not published on the official site: participation is arranged on request via the exhibitor area ([3dprint-exhibition-lyon.com](https://www.3dprint-exhibition-lyon.com/fr/espace-exposant)). Figure not publicly available.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: making contact with Infopro Digital Trade Shows, choosing positioning and deciding on co-location with FIP.
- 9 months out: applying for the show''s awards (3D INNOVATION AWARD, 3D COLLAB AWARD, START-UP CONTEST, 3D APPLICATION EXCELLENCE AWARD).
- 6 months out: preparing the stand and live machine demonstrations (a strong asset of the show).
- 3 months out: preparing conference and workshop highlights, briefing the team on the Soft & Scan areas.
- 6 weeks out: confirming decision-maker meetings and the commercial briefing.

### For the decision-making visitor

- Online pre-registration on the official site (professional badge granting access to 3D PRINT and [FIP](/salons/plastic-expo-lyon)).
- Allow one day to cover the additive manufacturing scope, two if combining with FIP.
- Target conferences and workshops in advance via the programme published before the show.
- Take advantage of live machine demonstrations, across all technologies and materials.

## Policy & CSR initiatives

3D PRINT Lyon sets out its CSR approach on its dedicated page ([CSR initiatives](https://www.3dprint-exhibition-lyon.com/fr/demarches-rse)). Concrete published commitments: selective collection of cardboard and wood from exhibitors during set-up and dismantling, selective sorting offered to visitors with sorting bins distributed around the show, and raising participants'' awareness of reducing consumption and of soft mobility.

On catering: glass and porcelain tableware in the restaurants, bio-sourced and biodegradable disposable containers at the points of sale, and enhanced collection including biowaste. No ISO 20121 certification mentioned to date (source: 3dprint-exhibition-lyon.com/demarches-rse).

## How the show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
  { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
  { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Participants", "align": "right" },
  { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
  { "edition": "2027 (upcoming, September)", "visiteurs": "n/a", "exposants": "n/a" },
  { "edition": "2026", "visiteurs": "n/a", "exposants": "n/a" },
  { "edition": "2025 (12th)", "visiteurs": "3,351+", "exposants": "~150" }
 ]}
 sources="3dprint-exhibition-lyon.com, 3dnatives.com, devicemed.fr"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

3D PRINT has established itself as France''s benchmark event for additive manufacturing, positioned among the sector''s influential events in Europe. The 2025 edition (12th edition, 3 to 5 June) gathered more than 3,351 participants at Eurexpo Lyon, with a visitor base heavily weighted towards decision-makers and concrete projects. The 2026 edition, from 2 to 4 June, confirmed the model by drawing on co-location with [FIP - France Innovation Plasturgie](/salons/plastic-expo-lyon), which broadens the scope to plastics, composites and rubber solutions. The show next meets in September 2027 at Eurexpo Lyon: the exact dates and target figures will be published as the event approaches.', seo_title_en='3D Print Lyon 2027: additive manufacturing and 3D printing trade show | Agoris', seo_description_en='3D PRINT Lyon, the French trade show for additive manufacturing and 3D printing at Eurexpo Lyon. Next edition September 2027, organised by Infopro Digital, co-located with FIP.' WHERE slug='3d-print-lyon';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Aerosol, dispensing, cosmetic and perfumery packaging trade show. Innovations in beauty packaging.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- ADF&PCD brings together two sections of the **Paris Packaging Week**: ADF (Aerosol & Dispensing Forum, "The home of aerosol and dispensing innovation") and PCD (Packaging of Perfume, Cosmetics & Design, "The future of beauty packaging"), dedicated to aerosol/dispensing packaging and to perfumery and cosmetics packaging.
- Organiser: **Easyfairs**. For the ADF section, co-organised with the **FEA (European Aerosol Federation)** since the January 2023 edition.
- Next edition: **3-4 February 2027**, at [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles), **Hall 1**. Held annually.
- The 2026 edition (25th anniversary of Paris Packaging Week) drew a total of **14,442 visitors and 915 exhibitors** across the whole week (organiser''s post-event figures). At section level: PCD is announced at around 600 exhibitors, ADF at around 150 (marketing targets shown on the section pages, not audited).
- Co-located within a single hall with PLD, Packaging Première and the Pentawards Festival: a single visitor covers aerosol, beauty, premium drinks and luxury goods.
- Prominent editorial themes: packaging innovation (ADF Innovation Awards, PCD Innovation Awards) and sustainability (Circular Economy Stage, Refill & Reuse Zone, PPWR alignment).

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

On the **PCD** side, the offering covers packaging for perfumery, cosmetics and personal care: bottles, tubes, pumps and dispensing systems, decoration, sustainable materials. On the **ADF** side, exhibitors are suppliers of aerosols and dispensing systems for the personal care, household care, pharmaceutical, paint and industrial product markets.

### Notable exhibitors

The exhibitor list for each section is published on the official website: see the [ADF exhibitors](https://www.parispackagingweek.com/en/adf/) and [PCD exhibitors](https://www.parispackagingweek.com/en/pcd/) pages.

### Quality of the visitor base

The visitor base is made up of buyers and decision-makers from beauty and aerosol product brands. The PCD website highlights the presence of brands such as Estée Lauder, L''Occitane, Puig, Shiseido, Sephora, Chanel, Guerlain and Hermès within its audience.

Across the whole of Paris Packaging Week 2026, the organiser announced **14,442 visitors**, **20,462 participants** in total and **213 speakers** from **87 nationalities** (organiser''s post-event figures, +15% vs the previous edition). These figures cover the entire week, not the ADF&PCD section alone.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

The conference programme is built around themed stages named by the organiser: **Circular Economy Stage**, **Future Innovation Forum**, **Global Aerosol & Dispensing Summit** and **Discovery Zone Pitch Stage**. The dominant topics: circular design, PPWR regulation, refill/reuse and materials innovation.

### Awards and accolades

Two innovation competitions specific to the sections, with their official titles:

- **ADF Innovation Awards**: reward the most technically and creatively innovative aerosol and dispensing packs.
- **PCD Innovation Awards**: reward innovation in beauty packaging.

For the 2026 edition, the organiser reports **34 innovations** recognised in total across all the Paris Packaging Week competitions (ADF, PCD, PLD, Packaging Première).

### Community & networks

Paris Packaging Week fosters a community of suppliers, brands and agencies around beauty and premium packaging. Activity programmes cited by the organiser: Future Leaders, Discovery Zone, Refill & Reuse Zone.

## On site: what you need to know

For access, parking and accommodation, see the venue page: [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles).

Specific to the trade show:

- Dates: **3 and 4 February 2027** (Wednesday and Thursday). Opening hours announced by the organiser: 9am-7.30pm on the first day, 9am-5pm on the second.
- Layout: **Hall 1** of Paris Expo Porte de Versailles.
- The badge grants access to all the co-located sections (ADF, PCD, PLD, Packaging Première, Pentawards Festival).

## What it costs to exhibit

The organiser does not publish an open price list: participation is by quotation via the Exhibit page on the official website. The estimate below is a sector benchmark for an international professional trade show of this size, to be confirmed with Easyfairs.

<BudgetTable warning="Prices not published by the organiser. Indicative sector ranges, to be confirmed by quotation with Easyfairs." rows={[{ "poste": "Bare space hire (stand approx. 9-18 m²)", "low": "€6,000", "high": "€14,000" }, { "poste": "Fit-out / equipped stand", "low": "€5,000", "high": "€20,000" }, { "poste": "Logistics, utilities, furniture", "low": "€2,000", "high": "€6,000" }, { "poste": "Staff, travel, accommodation", "low": "€3,000", "high": "€10,000" }, { "poste": "Communication, samples, activities", "low": "€2,000", "high": "€8,000" }]} totals={[{ "label": "ESTIMATED TOTAL (average stand, indicative)", "low": "~€18,000", "high": "~€58,000" }]} footer="Indicative benchmark. The actual quotation depends on the surface area, the location (ADF or PCD) and the level of fit-out." />

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 9 to 6 months out: request a quotation and book a space (the best PCD positions go early).
- 4 months out: stand design, submission of ADF Innovation Awards / PCD Innovation Awards entries if applying.
- 2 months out: logistics, team badges, buyer appointment scheduling via the platform.
- 2 weeks out: team briefing, samples, materials.

### For the decision-making visitor

- Pre-register online for a badge covering all the co-located sections.
- Target the conferences (Circular Economy Stage, Future Innovation Forum) and the Discovery Zone in advance.
- A single trip lets you cover aerosol/dispensing (ADF) and beauty (PCD), plus premium drinks and luxury goods via PLD and Packaging Première.

## Policy & CSR initiatives

Paris Packaging Week is part of the **Act for the Future** programme run by the Easyfairs group, which aims for carbon neutrality by 2050 and a halving of greenhouse gas emissions by 2030 across all the group''s events. On the content side, the **Refill & Reuse Zone** showcases operational reuse models (10 exhibitors present in 2026), and the **Circular Economy Stage** covers PPWR regulation, recycling and eco-design. Student competitions on eco-design (Emballé 5.0, ESEPAC Résonances) were also highlighted at the 2026 edition.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable columns={[{ "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" }, { "key": "dates", "label": "Dates" }, { "key": "lieu", "label": "Venue" }, { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors (PPW)", "align": "right" }, { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors (PPW)", "align": "right" }]} rows={[{ "edition": "2027 (upcoming)", "dates": "3-4 February 2027", "lieu": "Porte de Versailles, Hall 1", "visiteurs": "n/a", "exposants": "n/a" }, { "edition": "2026 (25th PPW)", "dates": "5-6 February 2026", "lieu": "Porte de Versailles", "visiteurs": "14,442", "exposants": "915" }]} sources="Paris Packaging Week figures (full week), organiser Easyfairs, post-event 2026." />

### Trajectory analysis

ADF&PCD no longer exists as a standalone trade show: since its acquisition by Easyfairs and its integration into Paris Packaging Week, the event functions as a meta-show bringing together aerosol/dispensing (ADF), beauty (PCD), premium drinks (PLD) and luxury goods (Packaging Première). The 2026 edition, which marked the week''s 25th anniversary, posted record attendance (14,442 visitors, 915 exhibitors across the week, +15% year on year). The trajectory is driven by the scaling-up of the offering and by a positioning anchored in sustainability (PPWR, refill/reuse). The 2027 edition maintains the annual rhythm in February, in Hall 1 of Porte de Versailles.', seo_title_en='ADF&PCD Paris 2027: dates, exhibitors, programme | Agoris', seo_description_en='ADF&PCD 2027: 3-4 February, Paris Expo Porte de Versailles (Hall 1). Within Paris Packaging Week (Easyfairs): 915 exhibitors, 14,442 visitors in 2026.' WHERE slug='adf-pcd-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Aeronautics business convention. B2B meetings between prime contractors and subcontractors in the aerospace sector.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- Aeromart Toulouse is the international business convention for the aeronautics and space supply chain: not a conventional stand-based trade show, but pre-scheduled B2B meetings between prime contractors and subcontractors across the aerospace sector.
- Next edition: 16th edition, from 1 to 3 December 2026 at the [MEETT Toulouse](/lieux/meett-toulouse), in Aussonne.
- Biennial rhythm (even years): the 15th edition took place from 3 to 5 December 2024, the 14th in 2022.
- 2024 results (15th edition): 800 exhibitors, 1,200 companies represented, 5,000 participants, 40 countries and more than 17,000 business meetings organised (source: aeromart-toulouse.com).
- Organised by Advanced Business Events (ABE) and BCI Aerospace, with the support of the regional aerospace ecosystem (Région Occitanie, Aerospace Valley, ad''occ).

## The essentials

- **Official website:** [aeromart-toulouse.com](https://aeromart-toulouse.com/)
- **Organiser:** Advanced Business Events (ABE).
- **Co-organiser:** BCI Aerospace.
- **Frequency:** biennial, even years (2022, 2024, 2026 editions).
- **Venue:** [MEETT Toulouse](/lieux/meett-toulouse), Aussonne.
- **2026 dates:** from 1 to 3 December 2026 (16th edition).
- **Format:** business convention based on pre-scheduled meetings. The event is built around two days of B2B business conventions and parallel conferences (2 and 3 December), preceded by an afternoon of set-up and registration (1 December). Themed workshops are held on the morning of 3 December.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Aeromart brings together the entire aerospace value chain: aircraft manufacturers and prime contractors, equipment makers, tier 1 to tier 3 subcontractors, service and special-process providers, technology companies. The format relies on qualified business meetings between buyers and suppliers, with a strictly professional and international audience (40 countries represented in 2024).

The trade show positions itself as the qualified convention for the aeronautics supply chain in Europe, with a selection of participants on the buyer side.

### Notable exhibitors

List of participants on the official website: [aeromart-toulouse.com](https://aeromart-toulouse.com/). At the 2024 edition, leading speakers and prime contractors took the floor: Airbus, Safran, Thales, ATR, Liebherr Aerospace, alongside professional associations from the sector (source: aeromart-toulouse.com).

### Quality of the audience

- Participation on the buyer side subject to qualification criteria, ensuring a targeted audience
- 5,000 participants and 1,200 companies represented in 2024, including a high proportion of purchasing and development decision-makers
- Strong international dimension: 40 countries represented in 2024

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

The event is built around two days of B2B business conventions and parallel conferences (2 and 3 December 2026), with themed workshops on the morning of 3 December. The 2026 programme covers the sector''s major challenges: aeronautics and space, artificial intelligence, cybersecurity, digitalisation, green technologies, industrialisation, operational excellence, supply chain and sustainability. In 2024, the programme brought together 55 speakers around innovation and the decarbonisation of the sector (source: aeromart-toulouse.com).

### Community and networks

The event draws on the regional aerospace ecosystem. Listed partners: Région Occitanie, ad''occ, Toulouse Métropole, CCI Toulouse Haute-Garonne, Invest in Toulouse and the Aerospace Valley competitiveness cluster.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [MEETT Toulouse](/lieux/meett-toulouse) listing.

Specific to the trade show:

- **Participation format:** convention by pre-scheduled meetings via the show''s meeting platform, in addition to visiting the exhibition spaces.
- **Visitor badge:** online registration on the official website, subject to validation by the organising team according to professional profile (criteria: involvement in the design, engineering, procurement, logistics, R&D and quality functions of the aerospace sector).
- **Themed pavilions:** a Sustainability Pavilion dedicated to green technologies and a Startup Pavilion for young companies in the sector.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates not published on the official website: stand booking and participation via the online form available at [aeromart-toulouse.com](https://aeromart-toulouse.com/index.php/exhibit). Figures not publicly available.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: make contact with Advanced Business Events, book a stand via the meeting platform.
- 6 months out: complete a detailed company profile and industrial capabilities to optimise matching with prime contractors.
- 3 months out: select and confirm the pre-scheduled business meetings, prepare a sales brief on target accounts.
- 1 month out: prepare demonstrations and materials, in line with the decarbonisation and innovation themes being showcased.

### For the decision-maker visitor

- Online registration on the official website, subject to validation according to professional profile (design, engineering, procurement, logistics, R&D, quality).
- Plan your meetings carefully: the heart of the event lies in the pre-scheduled B2B agenda.
- Target the conventions and workshops of 2 and 3 December, the conferences run in parallel with the B2B meetings.
- Visit the Sustainability and Startup pavilions to spot emerging innovations.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
 { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
 { "key": "participants", "label": "Participants", "align": "right" },
 { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" },
 { "key": "surface", "label": "Floor space", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
 { "edition": "2026 (16th, upcoming)", "participants": "n/a", "exposants": "n/a", "surface": "n/a" },
 { "edition": "2024 (15th)", "participants": "5,000", "exposants": "800", "surface": "32,000 m²" }
 ]}
 sources="aeromart-toulouse.com"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

The benchmark convention for the aerospace supply chain, Aeromart Toulouse confirmed its status in 2024 (15th edition) as the largest European business event in the sector, with 800 exhibitors, 5,000 participants and more than 17,000 business meetings organised over three days, across an exhibition floor space of 32,000 m². The biennial rhythm in even years makes it a structuring event for the sector, anchored in the Toulouse ecosystem (Aerospace Valley, Région Occitanie). The 2026 edition, from 1 to 3 December at the MEETT, extends the direction taken in 2024 around innovation and decarbonisation, with a Sustainability Pavilion dedicated to green technologies. The target figures and the detailed 2026 programme will be published as the show approaches.', seo_title_en='Aeromart Toulouse 2026: aeronautics convention at the MEETT | Agoris', seo_description_en='Aeromart Toulouse 2026, 16th edition of the international business convention for the aeronautics and space supply chain, from 1 to 3 December 2026 at the MEETT. Exhibitors, programme, access.' WHERE slug='aeromart-toulouse';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Trade show for professional agriculture in the Grand Est region. Agricultural machinery, livestock, crop production and services for farmers.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- Agrimax is the major professional agricultural trade show for the Grand Est region, dedicated to livestock farming and mixed crop-and-livestock farming, organised at the Metz exhibition centre by **Metz Événements**, in partnership with **GL Events**.
- 2026 edition: from Wednesday 28 to Friday 30 October 2026 at the [Centre Foires et Conventions de l''Eurométropole de Metz](/lieux/parc-expo-metz).
- 2024 results (13th edition, 23-25 October): several tens of thousands of visitors, 250 exhibitors across nearly 21,000 m² of covered space (source: jds.fr).
- Agrimax has set its sights on becoming the third major national livestock event, behind the [Sommet de l''Élevage](/salons/sommet-elevage-clermont) in Clermont-Ferrand and the [SPACE](/salons/space-rennes) in Rennes.
- Mixed crop-and-livestock sector: agricultural products and equipment, plus cattle, sheep, equine and poultry competitions of regional and cross-border scale, with an open-air area at the show entrance.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Agricultural machinery dealers and manufacturers, livestock equipment suppliers, input and crop-product suppliers, genetics and breed organisations, cooperatives, agricultural banks and insurers, training bodies and institutional players from the Grand Est region. The audience is mainly made up of livestock and mixed-farming professionals, complemented by a regional farming public.

### Notable exhibitors

The exhibitor list is available on the official website: [agrimax-expo.com](https://www.agrimax-expo.com/liste-exposants). The show brings together, in particular, the breed and genetics organisations present at the competitions: Charolais, Blonde d''Aquitaine, Limousine, Rouge des Prés, Jersiaire France, Chevaux de trait Grand Est, along with the regional players across the sectors (Avenir Génétique Moselle, LORCA, Cerfrance, Crédit Agricole Lorraine).

### Quality of the audience

- Several tens of thousands of visitors at each edition, with a strong cross-border dimension (Grand Est, Luxembourg, Saarland, Belgium), in keeping with the show''s regional positioning.
- A mixed audience: professional breeders and farm operators, students from agricultural courses, and regional visitors.

## Activities & community

### Competitions and activities

Agrimax builds each edition around cattle, sheep, equine and poultry competitions of regional and cross-border scale. The 2024 edition put the Aubrac breed in the spotlight and presented several hundred animals from beef breeds (Charolaise, Limousine, Blonde d''Aquitaine, Rouge des Prés) and dairy breeds (Prim''Holstein), alongside the sheep and poultry competitions (source: paysanvosgien.agri-info-nordest.fr). An open-air area is set up at the show entrance.

### Community and networks

Institutional and professional partners: Chambres d''Agriculture, Région Grand Est, Eurométropole de Metz, Ville de Metz, Ministère de l''Agriculture.

Sector and professional bodies: Institut de l''Élevage (Idele), Interbev, FDSEA 57, FRSEA Grand Est, Jeunes Agriculteurs Moselle, Coordination Rurale, Confédération Paysanne, GDS Moselle, Arvalis, ENSAIA, CUMA, CUMA Grand Est.

Regional agricultural press partners: Le Paysan Lorrain, Paysan Vosgien, La Moselle Agricole, Réussir, France Bleu, Moselle TV, Le Républicain Lorrain, L''Ami Hebdo, La Semaine, La Vie Agricole.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the listing for the [Centre Foires et Conventions de l''Eurométropole de Metz](/lieux/parc-expo-metz).

Specific to the show:

- **Opening hours:** Wednesday 28 October 10 am-8 pm, Thursday 29 October 9 am-10 pm (late-night opening), Friday 30 October 9 am-6 pm.
- **Admission prices:** €9 (adults), €4.50 (students and 12-18 year-olds).
- **Address:** Rue de la Grange aux Bois, 57072 Metz. Contact: 03 87 55 66 00.

## Planning your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 9 months out: get in touch via the exhibitor area to choose a location (covered space or open-air area at the show entrance).
- 6 months out: prepare equipment demonstrations and the logistics for animals taking part in the competitions.
- 3 months out: coordinate with breed organisations and sector partners for the rings and presentations.
- 6 weeks out: confirm business appointments with the breeder and farm-operator clientele of the Grand Est region.

### For the professional visitor

- Ticket online or on site (€9 adult). Allow a full day to cover machinery, livestock and crop production.
- Target the competition sessions according to the breeds and sectors of interest.
- Thursday''s late-night opening (until 10 pm) makes it easier to visit outside working hours.

## CSR policy & initiatives

The show''s operator publishes a CSR Policy (accessible via the official website), led by Metz Événements and GL Events, aimed at limiting the environmental impact of events held at the Centre Foires et Conventions de l''Eurométropole de Metz.

## How the show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
  { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
  { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors", "align": "right" },
  { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
  { "edition": "2026", "visiteurs": "to come", "exposants": "to come" },
  { "edition": "13th: 2024", "visiteurs": "several tens of thousands", "exposants": "250" },
  { "edition": "12th: 2023", "visiteurs": "n/a", "exposants": "n/a" }
 ]}
 sources="jds.fr, paysanvosgien.agri-info-nordest.fr"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

Agrimax has confirmed its growth over recent editions, with several tens of thousands of visitors and 250 exhibitors in 2024, which underpins its ambition to become the third national livestock event behind the Sommet de l''Élevage and SPACE. Its Grand Est regional and cross-border roots, its format mixing professionals and the regional general public, and its annual regularity are among its strengths. The 2026 edition aims to consolidate this momentum in its late-October slot.', seo_title_en='Agrimax 2026 Metz: Grand Est agriculture and livestock show', seo_description_en='Agrimax 2026, running from 28 to 30 October in Metz. The Grand Est livestock event: cattle and sheep competitions, machinery and mixed farming, with several tens of thousands of visitors.' WHERE slug='agrimax-metz';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='International trade show for professional drones. Civil and military drones, counter-drone systems, regulation and sector-specific applications.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- UAV Show is the European professional trade show for drones and autonomous systems, organised in Bordeaux by **Bordeaux Technowest** (aeronautics technology park), with **BEAM (Bordeaux Events And More)** as event operator.
- 9th edition in 2026: 7 to 9 October 2026 at Palais 2 l''Atlantique of the [Parc des Expositions de Bordeaux](/lieux/parc-des-expositions-de-bordeaux). The 7th and 8th of October are devoted to the exhibition, round tables and workshops, with the 9th of October given over to the Demo Day (flight demonstrations).
- Held in 2025 (8th edition, 14 to 16 October): 4,000 professionals and more than 100 exhibitors, with attendance up 50%, the number of exhibitors up 17% and net exhibition space up 62% compared with the previous edition (source: aerobuzz.fr, nae.fr). Expected in 2026: 110 exhibitors.
- The trade show covers civil, defence and dual-use drones. After the 2025 edition, marked by a strong rise in the defence dimension, the event moved to an annual rhythm to keep pace with the acceleration of innovation in the sector (source: aerobuzz.fr).
- A trade show reserved for professionals, certified to ISO 20121 (responsible event management).

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Drone manufacturers and industrial players, operators, project owners and contracting authorities, defence forces, internal security and civil protection forces, innovation and research ecosystem players, French and European public institutions, local authorities and infrastructure operators, sector-specific users (agriculture, construction). Strictly professional attendance.

### Notable exhibitors

Exhibitor catalogue on the official website: [uavshow.com](https://www.uavshow.com/). The 2025 edition brought together major players from the civil, dual-use and defence drone sector.

### Quality of the attendees

- 4,000 professionals gathered in 2025, up 50% compared with the previous edition (source: nae.fr)
- A stronger presence of the French armed forces, security forces and public contracting authorities, against a backdrop of civil-military convergence
- A complete ecosystem: industrial players, operators, researchers, start-ups and institutions

## Events & community

### Conference programme

Round tables, keynotes and workshops addressing the sector''s challenges over the first two days. In 2025, the round tables devoted to defence saw their attendance double compared with 2023 (source: nae.fr).

### Demo Day

The 9th of October 2026 is dedicated to the Demo Day: outdoor flight demonstrations in real-world conditions, organised with the CESA Drones cluster, allowing professionals to showcase their latest innovations.

### Community and networks

A major UAV Show evening brings together institutions, industrial players, armed forces and start-ups around the regional aeronautics ecosystem (source: technowest.com).

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the listing for the [Parc des Expositions de Bordeaux](/lieux/parc-des-expositions-de-bordeaux).

Specific to the trade show:

- **Venue:** Palais 2 l''Atlantique, within the Parc des Expositions de Bordeaux. The Demo Day takes place at a dedicated outdoor site for flight demonstrations.
- **Visitor badge:** access reserved for professionals, on presentation of the visitor badge and proof of identity. Prior online registration required.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates not published: exhibitor packages and sponsorship are provided on request via the official website or at contact@uavshow.com. Figures not publicly available.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 9 months out: making contact with the organising team, choosing the exhibitor package and positioning (civil, defence, dual-use).
- 6 months out: preparing the stand and demonstrations, and a possible application to the Demo Day for real-world flights.
- 3 months out: activating institutional partnerships and meetings with contracting authorities and defence forces.
- 6 weeks out: confirming business meetings and commercial briefing.

### For the decision-making visitor

- Pre-register online with proof of professional activity (trade show reserved for professionals).
- Plan for the Demo Day on 9 October to see the drones in real flight conditions.
- Target the round tables and keynotes in advance according to your scope (civil, defence, dual-use).

## Policy & CSR initiatives

UAV Show is certified to ISO 20121, the international standard dedicated to the responsible management of event activities (source: uavshow.com). The detail of further commitments is not published to date on the trade show''s website.

## How the trade show has evolved

