### Trajectory analysis

Big Data & AI Paris is the longest-running French data/AI trade show: launched in 2012 by **Corp Agency** at the Cité Universitaire (600 attendees), settled at the **Palais des Congrès Porte Maillot from 2013 to 2023**, and **moved to Paris Expo Porte de Versailles from 2024**. The 2023 edition marked a triple shift: the addition of the "& AI" element to the name, the acquisition of the assets by **RX France** (Reed Exhibitions), and crossing the 16,000-attendee threshold. The 2025 edition structured its editorial focus around digital sovereignty, frugal and responsible AI, and emerging open-source technologies. The 2026 shift is strategic: **the move to paid visitor access**, a refocusing to 200 exhibitors/sponsors (versus 220), an Advisory Board weighted towards large accounts (AXA, L''Oréal, Crédit Agricole, Suez), and 3 new targeted spaces (Advanced Computing, Talent Hub, SME Support). Reading: RX France is locking in the IT & Data decision-maker positioning during the AI industrialisation phase, against a market of competing trade shows (Tech Show Paris, Data & AI Leaders Summit) attempting to occupy the same niche.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='big-data-ai-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='B2B jewellery, fine-jewellery and watchmaking trade show at Paris Expo Porte de Versailles, Hall 7, co-located with Who''s Next under Comexposium''s Room 0126 cluster. January 2026 edition: around 180 brands, 25% new entrants, 50% international visitors.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key facts

- Autumn edition **5 to 7 September 2026** at **Paris Expo Porte de Versailles, Hall 7**, co-located with Who''s Next under Comexposium''s "Room 0126" umbrella.
- Biannual B2B jewellery, fine-jewellery and watchmaking trade show, organised by **WSN Développement** (a Comexposium subsidiary) on behalf of the **BOCI** (Bureau Officiel pour le Commerce de l''Industrie de la Bijouterie/joaillerie).
- January 2026 edition: around **180 brands** within the Bijorhca perimeter alone, **25% new entrants**, top 5 exhibitor countries Spain 13%, Greece 9%, Italy 8%, Germany 8%, India 6%. **50% international visitors**.
- Short, dense **3-day** format, prepared buyer meetings via platform.wsn.community, single badge to move across the whole Who''s Next + Bijorhca + Shoppe Object + SIL + Interfilière cluster.
- Next winter edition announced: **16 to 18 January 2027**.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Bijorhca Paris brings together two large, complementary worlds:

- **Finished product**: precious jewellery, costume jewellery, fine jewellery, watchmaking, accessories, goldsmithing.
- **Elements** (with its "Brilliant" section): suppliers of raw materials, components, display units, packaging, tooling, service providers and technical innovations (CAD, casting, 3D printing).

This dual reading sets Bijorhca apart from **Vicenzaoro** (Italy, pure precious fine jewellery) and **Inhorgenta Munich** (watchmaking-dominant): in Paris, a multi-brand boutique buyer can source a costume-jewellery designer, negotiate a watchmaking licence and find their display-unit supplier in the same hall.

The January 2026 edition gathered around **180 brands** within the Bijorhca perimeter alone, a figure confirmed by the BOCI. The top 5 exhibitor countries reflect the show''s European and Mediterranean calling: Spain 13%, Greece 9%, Italy 8%, Germany 8%, India 6%. A notable turnover rate with **25% new entrants**.

### Brands present (January 2026 edition)

Taratata, Philippe Ferrandis, Katerina Vassou, Christina Brampti, Belle Mais Pas Que, Bill''s Watches, Laval 1878, Canyon, Nikolis, Indo Gems, Pak Emballages, Plata y Minerales, Estampadora Mahonesa, Maharaja Arts Palace, Tagua & Co, Loop and Co, Madreperla Elements, By Olivier Lafond, Lye Paris, Cocktail Collection, Agapi Jewellery, Miss Pompon, Muja Juma.

### Visitor quality

- **50% international visitors** at the January 2026 edition (Bijorhca perimeter).
- **Room 0126** cluster January 2026 (Who''s Next + Bijorhca + Shoppe Object + SIL + Interfilière): **1,760 brands** in total, around **38,000 unique visitors** according to Comexposium.
- Strictly professional audience: independent boutique buyers, buying groups, department stores, specialist e-retailers, design workshops, jewellery designers, watchmaking manufacturers, distributors.
- Access subject to proof of activity (Kbis, professional card, proof of business activity).

## Events & community

### Bijorhca Awards

A competition dedicated to costume-jewellery designers, organised in collaboration with **Chall''angel**. **3 winners** selected by a professional jury. Applications to be sent to `awards@bijorhca.com` in the form of drawings, sketches or 3D files. Winners benefit from visibility at the show and introductions to the buyers present.

### "Eco-conscious fine jewellery" conferences

An editorial series launched in January 2026 around four themes:

- **Recycled gold and Fairmined gold**, with the mechanism of €4,000 paid per kilo of Fairmined gold to partner mining communities.
- **Stone traceability** (origin, extraction conditions, certifications).
- **Transparent sourcing** of components and raw materials.
- **Reduced packaging** and eco-design approaches.

Responsible and ethical jewellery is establishing itself as a structuring editorial theme for upcoming editions.

### Partner schools

Presentation of work by students in jewellery and accessory design:

- **DNMADe École Boulle** (Paris)
- **LISAA Mode Paris**
- **Köln International School of Design**

### Meeting platform

The **platform.wsn.community** tool: advance appointment booking between buyers and exhibitors, personalised agenda, single Room 0126 cluster badge.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles) page.

Specific to the show:

- **Hall**: **Hall 7** of the exhibition centre, co-located with Who''s Next.
- **2026 dates (autumn edition)**: from **Saturday 5 to Monday 7 September 2026**.
- **Room 0126 cluster visitor pricing**:
  - **€55 incl. VAT** in pre-sale (online purchase in advance).
  - **€70 incl. VAT** on site.
  - The badge gives access to all the shows in the cluster (Who''s Next, Bijorhca, Shoppe Object, SIL, Interfilière).
- **Access**: strictly professional on proof of activity (Kbis, professional card, proof of business activity).
- **Appointment booking**: via platform.wsn.community before the show.

## How much it costs to exhibit

WSN Développement does not publish a public price list. Participation terms are provided on application to the WSN sales teams, on a packs basis (space, equipment, scenography services, visibility in the official programme and on the meeting platform). Young brands can apply to support schemes (incubation, dedicated emerging-designer zones).

To apply for the **Bijorhca Awards** (costume-jewellery designers), send an application to `awards@bijorhca.com` several weeks before the edition.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 9 months before: contact WSN sales, choose the stand pack, position in Finished product or Elements.
- 6 months before: stand validation, scenography setup, possible application to the Bijorhca Awards.
- 4 months before: opening of appointment booking via platform.wsn.community, sales team briefing.
- 2 months before: client invitation campaign, social-media push, finalisation of the product catalogue.
- 3 weeks before: final meetings set, logistics preparation for transporting pieces and secured display cabinets.

### For the decision-maker visitor

- A dense **3-day** format: plan a structured schedule, ideally with a full day on Bijorhca then a Who''s Next day to make the most of the fashion co-location.
- Pre-sale **€55 incl. VAT** strongly recommended over **€70 incl. VAT** on site.
- Prepare your meetings via the official platform at least 3 weeks before the opening.
- For design workshops: allow a half-day in the **Elements** space (components, stones, packaging, display units, technical service providers).

## CSR policy & initiatives

Bijorhca structures its CSR message around **responsible and ethical jewellery**, an editorial theme highlighted at the January 2026 edition through the "eco-conscious fine jewellery" conference series. The topics covered include recycled gold, Fairmined gold (€4,000 paid per kilo to partner mining communities), the traceability of precious and semi-precious stones, transparent sourcing of raw materials and packaging reduction.

This theme is part of the group strategy of Comexposium and WSN Développement, and echoes the growing demands of boutique buyers and brands for transparency across the supply chain.

## How the show has evolved

