### Trajectory analysis

The forum, created in 2007 by the **Gendarmerie nationale** with the support of the Hauts-de-France Region, was for a long time co-organised by **CEIS**, acquired by **Avisa Partners** in the 2010s. Following the restructuring of Avisa Partners (legal proceedings 2022-2023, split of the group), the events business was transferred to **Forward Global** in 2024, which rebranded the event from **FIC to Forum InCyber Europe**. The exclusivity contract with **Lille Grand Palais runs until 2029**.

The audience trajectory is in continuous growth: from 16,000 on-site visitors in 2023 to 18,200 on site in 2025, with a stated target of expanding to **30,000 visitors by 2027-2028**. The internationalisation strategy is accelerating: opening of **Forum InCyber North America** in Montreal (2022, 4,000 visitors in 2024), **Forum InCyber Japan** in Tokyo (December 2025, 900 participants for the 1st edition), and an announced spin-off in San Antonio (Texas). The 2027 edition, centred on agentic AI, takes place within a busy regulatory framework (NIS 2 transposition, AI Act application, DORA), which should work in favour of professional attendance.

**Points to watch**: the 2024 rebranding coincided with a slight decline in French regional presence (only 4 regions out of 18 had a stand in 2025, flagged by the specialist press). The historic departure of the Gendarmerie nationale and Interior oversight in 2023 left a mark that Forward Global is gradually rebuilding, notably via the institutional pavilions (ANSSI, ComCyber, Europol, European Commission).', seo_title_en='Forum InCyber Europe 2027 - Lille Grand Palais, 9-11 March 2027', seo_description_en='Forum InCyber Europe 2027: 19th edition at Lille Grand Palais, 9-11 March 2027. Theme: agentic AI. 200+ conferences, 700+ cyber exhibitors, 100 countries represented.' WHERE slug='fic-lille';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Trade exhibition and agricultural show of the Pyrenees. Livestock, machinery and regional produce from the Occitanie region.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- 49th edition of the Salon Régional de l''Agriculture de Tarbes, from 5 to 8 March 2026 at the [Parc des Expositions de Tarbes](/lieux/parc-des-expositions-de-tarbes), across 24,000 m² of exhibition space.
- Organiser: **SEAE** (Société d''Encouragement à l''Agriculture et à l''Élevage), an association based at 20 place du Foirail in Tarbes, chaired by Jean-Louis Cazaubon and directed by Cédric Abadia. Co-organised in institutional partnership with the Chambre d''agriculture des Hautes-Pyrénées, the Région Occitanie, the Département des Hautes-Pyrénées, the Ville de Tarbes, Crédit Agricole, Groupama and the MSA.
- **Edition without cattle**: the cattle sector will not present any animals in 2026, owing to the health situation linked to lumpy skin disease (LSD). Cattle breeders will remain present for sales sessions (fat cows sold by open bidding on the Thursday) and for discussion. Sheep, equines and goats take pride of place in Hall 1.
- 315 exhibitors announced, more than 300 animals on display and in competition (target communicated by the organisers), around sixty farm producers and artisans, 9 restaurants and 23 refreshment stands, and daily gourmet workshops.
- A regional, general-public trade show with a strong professional dimension (livestock and machinery sectors), founded in 1977 (source: Wikipedia); the 48th edition in March 2025 set a record with 76,748 visitors.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

A general-purpose regional agricultural trade show for the South-West of France, blending the professional sector (livestock, machinery, cooperatives, agricultural banking and insurance, renewable energy, employment and training) with the general public (farm producers, gourmet village, family activities). The exhibitor profile covers the six major regional cattle breeds usually present (Blonde d''Aquitaine, Aubrac, Limousine, Bazadaise, Charolaise, Gasconne des Pyrénées), although no animals will feature in 2026, as well as the local sheep breeds (Tarasconnaise, Barégeoise, Lourdaise, Auroise, Montagne Noire), draught horses and goats.

### Notable exhibitors

Exhibitors visible on the official 2026 page by category: agricultural machinery (Krone France, among 43 equipment exhibitors), cooperatives and trading (Vivadour), renewable energy (Reden Technique, among 9 exhibitors), banking and insurance (Banque Populaire), employment and training (La Pyrénéenne). Nearly 44 farm producers and artisans are registered. The full list and categories are available on the [official exhibitors page](https://www.salon-agricole.com/evenements/salon-tarbes-2026/exposants/).

### Visitor quality

A pronounced B2B-B2C mix. On the professional side: breeders, farmers, cooperative technicians, and students from regional agricultural colleges (the Lycée Jean Monnet in Vic-en-Bigorre is involved via the Ovinpiades). On the general-public side: families from the Bigorre area, a regional audience from Occitanie and Nouvelle-Aquitaine, and school groups (dedicated programme). Visitors are predominantly regional, with strong roots in the Hautes-Pyrénées and neighbouring départements.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

The programme is geared mainly towards competitions and demonstrations rather than conferences. One conference is announced for Saturday 7 March at 3pm: "Quitte ou double ?" on organic farming, led by an economist and an organic-farming specialist.

### Competitions and presentations in 2026

- **Thursday 5 March**: 11am, sale of fat cows by open bidding (Blonde d''Aquitaine, Aubrac, Limousine, Bazadaise, Charolaise, Gasconne des Pyrénées) in the Hall 1 Ring; 2.30pm, presentation of draught-horse breeds; 3.30pm, presentation of goat breeds.
- **Friday 6 March**: 10am, national Ovinpiades with students from the Lycée Agricole Jean Monnet; 2pm, competition and presentation of sheep breeds (Tarasconnaise, Barégeoise, Lourdaise, Montagne Noire, Auroise).
- **Saturday 7 March**: 10am, presentation of draught horses; 3pm, handling competition (long reins) with draught horses.
- **Sunday 8 March**: 10am, competition and presentation of the Chèvres des Pyrénées breed; 12pm, presentation of draught horses.

### Satellite events

The Département des Hautes-Pyrénées stand in Hall 3 offers an interactive game on the theme "Pastoralism in the Hautes-Pyrénées: a living tradition serving the mountains". Daily demonstrations of herding and working dogs, an educational mini-farm, demonstrations of vintage equipment and threshing with vintage tractors, a wool and weaving workshop, and a Légumineuses d''Occitanie (Occitanie pulses) stand in Hall 4. Since 2022, SEAE has also organised La Fête de l''Agriculture in Rabastens (a satellite event held outside March).

### Community & networks

Long-standing media partner: La Dépêche du Midi. Coverage by France Bleu Béarn Bigorre, La Semaine des Pyrénées, City65 and Lourdes Actu. Partners displayed on the official homepage: AGRICA, Sud de France Occitanie, Mairie de Tarbes, MSA, Région Occitanie, Chambre d''Agriculture des Hautes-Pyrénées, Groupama, La Dépêche du Midi, Département des Hautes-Pyrénées, Crédit Agricole and Bienvenue à la ferme.

Online ticketing has been in place since 2024 to streamline entry. A presence on Facebook and Instagram via @departementhapy and the official Salon de l''Agriculture Tarbes page.

### What''s new in 2026

- **Edition without cattle**: a decision taken by the organisers and breeders in the face of the LSD health risk. Cattle breeders remain involved via the Thursday open-bidding sale and their presence at the show, but without presenting animals.
- **Focus on sheep, equines and goats** in Hall 1, which concentrates the live competitions and presentations.
- **International Year of Rangelands and Pastoralists** (declared by the UN): a cross-cutting theme explored notably through the départemental stand and the shepherd/herding-dog demonstrations.

## On site: what you need to know

- **Venue**: [Parc des Expositions de Tarbes](/lieux/parc-des-expositions-de-tarbes), Boulevard du Président Kennedy, 65000 Tarbes. Vignemale and Pic du Midi halls, and the Néouvielle and Arbizon rooms. Hall 1 (Ring) hosts the livestock competitions, Hall 3 the départemental stand, and Hall 4 the Légumineuses d''Occitanie space.
- **Train**: Tarbes SNCF station is 10 minutes from the Parc, with a TER line to Pau, Toulouse and Bordeaux, and TGV services to Paris-Montparnasse (approx. 5 hours).
- **Air**: Tarbes-Lourdes-Pyrénées airport is 15 minutes away, with regular connections to Paris-Orly.
- **Car**: A64 motorway, exit 12 (Tarbes Ouest) or 13 (Tarbes Est). On-site car park.
- **Bus** (Alézan urban network from the station): lines T1 (Ibos-Verdun), T2 (Laubadère-Verdun) or T5 (Bordères-Verdun), then a connection on line T4 (Château d''Urac-Parc des Expositions).
- **Opening hours in 2026**: Thursday 5, Friday 6 and Saturday 7 March from 9am to 8pm; Sunday 8 March from 9am to 7pm. Catering areas remain accessible later into the evening.
- **Prices**: full price €7 at the gate, concession (students, persons with reduced mobility) €4, evening entry from 8pm €5, free for under-7s. Online ticketing available.

