### Trajectory analysis

The Salon Régional de l''Agriculture de Tarbes is the major agricultural gathering of the Pyrenean South-West, with steadily growing visitor numbers and a new record in 2025 (76,748 visitors, against ~74,800 in 2024). The format remains robust over time: a strong regional positioning, deep roots in the Hautes-Pyrénées, dialogue between breeders and the general public, and a mixed B2B-B2C economic model. The 2026 edition is marked by an unprecedented break: the absence of cattle owing to the LSD health risk, which is affecting all the major French agricultural shows (the Salon International de l''Agriculture in Paris took the same decision in 2026). Holding the show without its flagship sector demonstrates the diversity of the offering (sheep, equines, goats, machinery, farm producers) and the organisers'' determination to preserve the event for the other sectors. With an announced budget of €750,000 and €45,000 in public subsidies, the show remains a territorial asset openly supported by local institutions (Région, Département, Ville, Chambre d''agriculture).', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='foire-agricole-tarbes';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Major agricultural and commercial fair of the Grand Est region. Agricultural machinery, livestock, regional produce and crafts.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key facts

- The Foire de Châlons is a major agricultural and commercial fair in the Grand Est region, organised by the **UCIA** (Union Commerciale, Industrielle et Artisanale de Châlons) through its company **UCIA Expos**. It positions itself as **France''s second-largest agricultural gathering** after the [Salon de l''Agriculture](/salons/sia-paris) (source: foiredechalons.com/decouvrir/presentation).
- 80th edition in 2026: from **28 August to 7 September 2026** at the [Capitole en Champagne](/lieux/parc-expo-chalons) in Châlons-en-Champagne, the city''s exhibition centre.
- Founded in **1947** for the farming community, it has since broadened to trade, crafts and services (source: fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foire_de_Châlons).
- 2025 results (79th edition): more than **250,000 admissions**, **765 exhibitors** and **200 talks** (source: lecapitole-en-champagne.fr).
- A paid-entry fair open to the general public: the ticket also grants access to the free concerts of the partner festival Foire en Scène (source: foiredechalons.com/infos-pratiques/tarifs-dentrees).

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Players in agricultural machinery and equipment, livestock farmers and breeding sectors, agricultural cooperatives and trading firms, regional producers and craftspeople, home and home-improvement, health, sport and leisure. The fair combines a strong agricultural professional dimension with a regional general-public visitorship.

### Pavilions and spaces

The fair structures its offering around several hubs, including **Equip''Agro** (agricultural machinery and equipment) and the **Village de la Bioéconomie**, a platform promoting emerging sectors. Exhibitor list and floor plan on the official website: [exhibitor area](https://www.foiredechalons.com/espace-exposants).

## Activities & community

### Programme and talks

Around **200 talks and meetings** punctuate the eleven days of the fair, covering the economy, agriculture and regional issues (source: lecapitole-en-champagne.fr). The fair is traditionally a back-to-work political and economic gathering for the Grand Est.

### Concerts and festival

The partner festival **Foire en Scène** programmes a series of concerts included in the entry ticket (source: foiredechalons.com/infos-pratiques/tarifs-dentrees).

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Capitole en Champagne](/lieux/parc-expo-chalons) listing.

Specific to the fair:

- **Opening hours:** open every day from 10.30 a.m., closing at 9 p.m.; late openings until midnight on Saturday 29 August and Saturday 5 September (halls 2 and 3 until 10 p.m.); closing at 7 p.m. on Monday 7 September (source: foiredechalons.com/preparez-votre-visite/infos-pratiques/horaires-douverture).
- **Entry prices:** on weekdays (Monday to Thursday) 7 euros for adults, 3.50 euros for children under 12; at the weekend (Friday to Sunday) 8 euros for adults, 4 euros for children; free for under-5s. Free for people with disabilities and their companion, and for those aged 65 and over on 31 August, 3 and 7 September.
- **Access:** free car park with regular shuttles, paid car park on avenue du Président Roosevelt, reduced-mobility car park with buggies; bus lines 1, 2, 3 and 5 (Croix Dampierre stop); bicycle parking in front of the main entrance.

## Policy & CSR initiatives

The Foire de Châlons sets out several eco-responsible commitments on its [dedicated page](https://www.foiredechalons.com/decouvrir/foire-eco-responsable): recycling bins provided for visitors across the site, reduced paper use with largely digital communication (email invitations, online programmes), recovery of exhibitors'' unsold food by partner charities, and awareness activities on eco-responsible practices. These commitments are formalised in a charter. No ISO 20121-type certification is mentioned to date.

## Planning your visit

### For exhibitors

- Applications to take part are made online via the organiser''s portal ([exhibitor area](https://www.foiredechalons.com/espace-exposants)).
- Target the right hub for your activity: Equip''Agro for agricultural machinery and equipment, Village de la Bioéconomie for emerging sectors, and the trade, home and regional-produce spaces for the general public.
- Anticipate massive general-public attendance over eleven days, with peaks at weekends and on public holidays.

### For visitors

- Tickets can be bought on site or online; prices are more favourable on weekdays than at the weekend.
- The ticket grants access to the Foire en Scène festival concerts.
- Favour the free shuttles from the free car park to avoid congestion around the site.

## How the fair has evolved

<HistoryTable
