### Trajectory analysis

A historic fair founded in 1947, the Foire de Châlons has established itself as France''s second-largest agricultural gathering behind the [Salon de l''Agriculture](/salons/sia-paris). It blends a leading agricultural and economic vocation (machinery, livestock, bioeconomy, back-to-work talks) with a general-public, festive dimension (regional produce, crafts, free concerts). With more than 250,000 admissions and 765 exhibitors in 2025, it remains a major anchor point of the Grand Est''s autumn season.', seo_title_en='Foire de Châlons-en-Champagne 2026: dates, exhibitors, visitor info', seo_description_en='The 80th Foire de Châlons-en-Champagne runs from 28 August to 7 September 2026 at the Capitole en Champagne. France''s 2nd-largest agricultural fair: exhibitors, opening hours, prices and access.' WHERE slug='foire-chalons-en-champagne';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='France''s largest commercial trade fair. Home interiors, gastronomy, shopping, innovations and creative leisure.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- 122nd edition of the Foire de Paris: **30 April to 11 May 2026** (12 days), at Paris Expo Porte de Versailles. The event spans the 1 May bank holiday weekend, traditionally the peak attendance window of the calendar.
- Europe''s largest consumer trade fair: **400,000+ visitors** over 12 days in 2025 (+5% YoY) and **~1,250 exhibitors** (+12% vs 2024), with 1,000 hours of programmed activities.
- Four themed worlds structure the 2026 edition: **Home & Co Paris**, **World in Paris**, **Auto Mobil Paris** (new for 2026), **Made in Paris** (new for 2026).
- The **Concours Lépine**, hosted in Pavillon 1, celebrates its **126th edition** in 2026 over the same 12-day window. The 2025 edition lined up 250 inventions competing across 6 themes.
- Organiser: **Comexposium**. Paid B2C trade fair: **14 euros online, 16 euros on site**, ticket valid for one day of your choice.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

The Foire de Paris is a paid, general-interest consumer trade fair covering almost every segment of household spending: home interiors, household equipment, international crafts, gastronomy, mobility, fashion, beauty and personal services. The attendee base is predominantly from the Paris region (historically more than 70% from Île-de-France), with a marked family and upper-income (CSP+) skew across the home and décor sections.

On the exhibitor side, the mix is balanced between:

- **French SMEs and craftspeople** (Made in Paris, Home & Co)
- **International craft and gastronomy brands** (World in Paris)
- **Major manufacturers and brands** (Auto Mobil Paris)
- **Independent inventors** (Concours Lépine)

### The four 2026 worlds

The 2026 edition structures its offering around four distinct worlds (the Concours Lépine remains hosted in Pavillon 1 but is no longer counted as a world in its own right):

- **Home & Co Paris**: home interiors, décor, kitchen, renovation, furnishings. ~300 brands across **4 halls (1, 5, 6 and 7)**, in partnership with l''Ameublement Français. This has historically been the most visited section of the fair.
- **World in Paris**: international crafts and world gastronomy, **25,000 m²**, **500 craftspeople** from around **50 destinations**. Countries in the spotlight for 2026: Cameroon and Senegal for Africa, Kosovo, Italy and Greece for Europe, China, India and Vietnam for Asia.
- **Auto Mobil Paris**: **new for 2026**, **5,000 m²** in Hall 5 plus an outdoor extension. **6 manufacturers** present, +65% floor space vs 2025, with supercars unveiled as premieres.
- **Made in Paris**: **new for 2026**, **15,000 m²** dedicated to Made in France, showcasing know-how and brands produced in France.

### Quality of the attendee base

- **400,000+ visitors** over 12 days in 2025, +5% vs 2024, an average of ~33,000 visitors per day with a peak over the 1 May bank holiday weekend.
- Primary target: private individuals from the Paris region, with home and décor purchase projects, looking for kitchen and lifestyle inspiration, plus family audiences at weekends.
- Average spending power above that of a typical major consumer trade fair, with the paid ticket acting as a natural filter.
- A growing international attendee base via the World in Paris and Concours Lépine sections.

## Activities and community

### Concours Lépine

In Pavillon 1, the Concours Lépine is the fair''s historic attraction. **126th edition** in 2026 (the 125th edition in 2025 lined up **250 inventions** in competition and ran from 30 April to 11 May), organised around **6 themes**:

- Univers Connecté Lépine
- Quotidien et Art de Vivre
- Art et Création
- Santé et Inclusivité
- Transport et Industrie
- Le Monde Invente

Created in **1901** by **Louis Lépine**, Prefect of Police of Paris, to support small inventors and craftspeople in the face of industrial competition, the contest is today the emblematic gathering for independent French inventors. The **prize-giving ceremony** traditionally takes place on the Friday evening, usually at 7:30 pm, with a high-profile prize list that guarantees significant press coverage for the fair.

### Activity programme

**1,000 hours of programmed activities** over 12 days, placing the Foire de Paris at the top of Europe''s consumer event offering. Stages include:

- **Food Truck Village**: street food and culinary demonstrations
- **Guinguette**: musical and festive evening atmosphere
- **Foire de Paris Junior**: space dedicated to children and families
- **Tastes & Trends**: gastronomy and lifestyle trends
- **Marché Seconde Main**: second-hand fashion and accessories, a circular-economy anchor
- Talks, demonstrations, concerts

### Saturday 9 May late-night opening

A single late-night opening is scheduled for 2026: **Saturday 9 May until 10 pm**. Specific late-night pricing: **7 or 9 euros** depending on channel and profile. The format is designed to capture working professionals heading out after the week and to extend the Guinguette atmosphere.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the listing for [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles).

Specific to the trade fair:

- **Halls in use**: 1, 5, 6, 7 for Home & Co Paris, Hall 5 and outdoor space for Auto Mobil Paris, Pavillon 1 for the Concours Lépine.
- **Standard opening hours**: 10 am - 7 pm every day.
- **Late-night opening**: Saturday 9 May until 10 pm.
- **Bank holidays**: 1 May and 8 May open during normal hours (10 am - 7 pm), a programming highlight since 1 May has historically been the peak attendance day.
- **Ticket** valid for **one day of your choice** across the 12 days, **access to all 4 worlds** included.
- **RATP partnership**: public transport encouraged, with the ADEME calculator made available to estimate each visitor''s carbon impact.

## Visitor pricing 2026

The Foire de Paris remains a **paid** trade fair on the consumer side, with a pricing structure designed to reward advance online booking:

- **Adult ticket online: 14 euros**
- **Adult ticket on site: 16 euros**
- **Child aged 7 to 14: 7 euros**
- **Under 7: free**
- **Late-night ticket (Saturday 9 May): 7 or 9 euros** depending on channel
- **Reduced rates** for students, seniors, groups
- **Family pass** (2 adults + 2 children) available

The ticket is valid for one day of your choice across the 12 days and grants access to all 4 worlds.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates not published. Quote on request from **Comexposium**. The structuring into 4 worlds (Home & Co Paris, World in Paris, Auto Mobil Paris, Made in Paris) influences placement and the price per m². The partnership with l''Ameublement Français on Home & Co Paris and the dedicated Made in Paris format open up specific arrangements for French SMEs and craftspeople.

Figures not publicly available at this date.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: make contact with Comexposium, choose a world (Home & Co Paris, World in Paris, Auto Mobil Paris, Made in Paris).
- 9 months out: if you are an independent inventor, apply to the **Concours Lépine** (maximum visibility, specialist press leverage).
- 6 months out: finalise the stand, brief the contractor, sort logistics for the heavier home interiors sections.
- 3 months out: team accreditations, consumer activation plan, regional press partnerships.
- 6 weeks out: social media campaign, pre-booked B2B visit plan where relevant.

### For the visitor

- **Buy your ticket online**: 2 euros saved immediately per ticket vs on-site sales.
- **Choose your day**: avoid 1 May if you are after a quieter experience (historic peak); favour weekdays in the first week for a smoother route round.
- **Saturday 9 May late-night opening**: reduced rate and Guinguette atmosphere, a format suited to working professionals.
- **Allow a full day** to cover 4 worlds spread across 4 halls plus the outdoor space.
- **Access**: RATP encouraged via partnership, ADEME calculator to estimate your footprint.

## CSR policy and initiatives

The Foire de Paris runs an **Ecogestes** programme structured for exhibitors and visitors alike:

- **Exhibitor awareness** of responsible practices, with a best-practice kit circulated beforehand.
- **Waste sorting**: 34% recovered in 2024, out of a volume of around **400 tonnes** generated over 12 days.
- **ADEME calculator** made available to visitors to estimate their carbon impact (transport, on-site catering).
- **RATP partnership**: a strong incentive towards modal shift to public transport.
- **Circular-fashion pop-up shops** integrated into the visitor route.
- **Marché Seconde Main**: confirmed for 2026, second-hand fashion and accessories, on a circular-economy line.

These measures sit within Comexposium''s CSR strategy, operating Paris Expo Porte de Versailles with Viparis. No ISO 20121 certification specific to the trade fair is mentioned at this date.

## How the trade fair has evolved

<HistoryTable
