### Trajectory analysis

Global Industrie is the direct heir of **Midest** (1962, industrial subcontracting), **Industrie** (machine tools), **Smart Industries** (industrial digital) and **Tolexpo** (sheet metal processing), brought together in 2018 under the joint impetus of the sector and Emmanuel Macron, then Minister of the Economy in 2015. The first merged edition was held at Paris Nord Villepinte from 27 to 30 March 2018, inaugurated by the President of the Republic.

The event has remained firmly anchored under the GL Events Exhibitions banner. The historical distinction between the organisers of the original trade shows (Reed Expositions / RX France for Midest, GL Events for Industrie/Smart Industries) was settled at the time of the merger in favour of GL Events.

The Paris-Lyon alternation is now established: odd-year editions in Lyon (Eurexpo), even-year editions in Paris (Villepinte). The post-COVID trajectory is solid: 34,000 visitors in 2024 in Paris, 45,000 in Lyon in 2025 (+20%), 60,000 announced in Paris in 2026. The 2027 Lyon edition aims to confirm the plateau and strengthen the international strand (91 target countries vs 86 in 2024).', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='global-industrie-lyon';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Hackathon and conference on digital health innovation. Bringing together healthcare professionals, developers and designers.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- "INNOVATION IN HEALTH STARTS HERE": Hacking Health Camp is positioned by its organisers as the largest healthcare hackathon in the world, launched in Strasbourg in 2014.
- Organisers: Health Factory & OpenCare Lab, in partnership with the Hacking Health Foundation and the Université de Strasbourg.
- 2027 edition: 20-22 March 2027 at the [Faculté de Médecine de Strasbourg](/lieux/faculte-medecine-strasbourg) (4 rue Kirschleger, 67000 Strasbourg). Registration open on the [official website](https://hackinghealth.camp/en/).
- Hybrid format over 3 days: 50 hours of hackathon, plus workshops and talks beforehand (January to March). This is not a stand-based trade show: there is no conventional exhibitor floor plan.
- Audience blending healthcare professionals, patients, engineers, designers, developers, makers and entrepreneurs, brought together in multidisciplinary teams to prototype over a weekend.
- 2027 prices: EUR 40 incl. VAT (students, jobseekers, patient associations), EUR 120 incl. VAT (SMEs, associations, healthcare professionals, individuals), EUR 600 incl. VAT (organisations with turnover above EUR 500k).

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Hacking Health Camp is not a stand-based trade show but a hackathon-conference. The "exhibitor community" here consists of participants and institutional partners rather than commercial exhibitors. The profiles brought together: healthcare professionals (doctors, carers), patients and informal carers, engineers and developers, designers, makers, students and digital-health entrepreneurs. Teams form on site around real clinical challenges.

### Notable exhibitors

The event draws on a dense network of local and national partners and sponsors. Among the partners and sponsors listed for the most recent edition: Université de Strasbourg, Faculté de Médecine, Eurométropole de Strasbourg, ARS Grand Est, IHU Strasbourg, Fondation FORCE, Epitech, EM Strasbourg, La French Tech Est, BioValley, NextMed, OpenCare Lab, Agence du Numérique en Santé, Alsace Business Angels. The up-to-date list is published on the [official website](https://hackinghealth.camp/en/).

### Quality of attendance

The event brings together an international digital-health innovation community over three days. The organiser reports welcoming more than 400 participants per edition (figure announced for the 2026 edition, not audited). Participation volumes are therefore self-declared by the organiser, not based on an audited report.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

The edition unfolds in two stages: a phase of workshops and talks beforehand (from January to March), then the 50-hour hackathon from Friday evening (6 pm) to Sunday evening (6 pm). The multidisciplinary teams work on real clinical and usage challenges, from chronic-disease management to artificial intelligence in healthcare, with the aim of delivering a working prototype on the Sunday.

### Awards and prizes

Several prizes reward the best projects at the end of the hackathon. At the 2026 edition, the winners included: the MediSignes Urgence project (a carer-patient communication tool for emergency situations, led by an EM Strasbourg student), awarded the "Best Patient Solution award"; the REPER project (detection of drug interactions in elderly patients, led by pharmacology students and a developer from Télécom Physique), awarded the "Quest for Health prize"; and the PRIORA project (access to adaptive clinical trials). The detailed prize categories for the upcoming edition are published on the website as the event approaches.

### Community & networks

Hacking Health Camp runs an active community beyond the event, relayed on LinkedIn and the organiser''s networks, and anchored in Strasbourg''s healthcare ecosystem (CHRU, IHU, BioValley, NextMed). The event is part of the international Hacking Health movement.

## On site: what you need to know

For access, parking and accommodation, see the venue page: [Faculté de Médecine de Strasbourg](/lieux/faculte-medecine-strasbourg) (4 rue Kirschleger, 67000 Strasbourg).

Specific to the event:
- 2027 dates: 20-22 March 2027 in Strasbourg.
- Intensive residential format of 50 hours, from Friday evening (6 pm) to Sunday evening (6 pm): plan for suitable logistics (on-site catering included in the ticket, continuous workspaces).
- Registration open via the [official website](https://hackinghealth.camp/en/).
- Participant access is by ticket registration, distinct from a trade-show visitor model.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Hacking Health Camp is not a stand-based trade show: there is no exhibitor price list. The business model rests on two levers. On the one hand, participation: the profiles (hackers, healthcare professionals, students, mentors) register via ticketing across three pricing tiers confirmed for 2027: EUR 40 incl. VAT (students, jobseekers, patient associations), EUR 120 incl. VAT (SMEs with turnover below EUR 500k, associations, healthcare professionals, individuals), EUR 600 incl. VAT (organisations with turnover above EUR 500k). The preparatory workshops beforehand are free. On the other hand, partnership and sponsorship: companies and institutions support the event as sponsors or partners (visibility, access to talent, sponsored challenges), under terms negotiated directly with the organiser. For a corporate presence, contact is made via the [official website](https://hackinghealth.camp/en/contact/).

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

Here, "exhibiting" should be read as "becoming a partner or sponsor" or "proposing a challenge".

- 6 months out: get in touch with the organiser to frame the partnership format (sponsorship, sponsored challenge, mentoring).
- 4 months out: define the clinical or usage challenge submitted to the teams, mobilise your in-house experts as mentors.
- 2 months out: prepare the resources (anonymised datasets, specifications) made available to the teams.
- 1 month out: brief the mentors and spokespeople, lock in your presence across the conference and hackathon phases.

### For the decision-making visitor

- Target the phase that interests you: talks and workshops beforehand, or hackathon immersion over the weekend.
- To spot solutions or talent, aim for the pitch sessions and the prize-giving on the Sunday.
- Register as soon as registration opens: capacity for a residential format is limited.
- Prepare your objectives: recruitment, innovation watch, identification of projects to incubate.

## Policy & CSR initiatives

The social commitment of Hacking Health Camp is expressed first through its mission: the Hacking Health Foundation, a volunteer-run partner NGO, states "fostering inclusive innovation by connecting people to solve real-world health problems" and sets out four founding values: Inclusivity, Collaboration, Integrity, Inspiration. Co-organiser of the Strasbourg event, OpenCare Lab is constituted as a cooperative society of collective interest (SCIC), a structure with a general-interest mission: it explicitly aims to "improve citizens'' health, empower citizens on health matters and guarantee equal access to new health offerings", co-building solutions with patients, informal carers and front-line professionals. The host venue, the Faculté de Médecine of the Université de Strasbourg, is engaged in a formalised approach: the Université de Strasbourg has obtained the DD&RS label (Sustainable Development & Social Responsibility) for four years, covering five areas: strategy and governance, teaching, research, environmental management and social policy. By contrast, an environmental CSR policy specific to the event (carbon footprint, eco-design, ISO 20121 certification of the event itself) is not publicly documented at the time of writing.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable columns={[{ "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" }, { "key": "dates", "label": "Dates" }, { "key": "lieu", "label": "Venue" }, { "key": "format", "label": "Format" }]} rows={[{ "edition": "2027 (upcoming)", "dates": "20-22 March 2027", "lieu": "Faculté de Médecine, Strasbourg", "format": "50 h hackathon + talks" }, { "edition": "2026", "dates": "20-22 March 2026", "lieu": "Faculté de Médecine, Strasbourg", "format": "50 h hackathon + talks" }, { "edition": "2014", "dates": "Launch", "lieu": "Strasbourg", "format": "First edition" }]} sources="Official website hackinghealth.camp; alsace.com; ihu-strasbourg.eu" />

### Trajectory analysis

Born in Strasbourg in 2014, Hacking Health Camp has established itself as France''s benchmark event for open innovation in healthcare, positioned by its organisers as the largest healthcare hackathon in the world. Its geographic stability (Faculté de Médecine de Strasbourg) and calendar stability (March) make it an anchor point for the Grand Est healthcare ecosystem, backed by the CHRU, the IHU and BioValley. The continuity of organisation by Health Factory & OpenCare Lab, and the public-private partnership network (Université, ARS, Eurométropole, industry players), explain the format''s longevity. The challenge for the coming editions remains the conversion of prototypes into startups and deployed solutions, as illustrated by Pixacare and Hypno VR, cited among the startups to come out of the hackathon.', seo_title_en='Hacking Health Camp 2027 Strasbourg: 20-22 March, 50-hour health hackathon | Agoris', seo_description_en='Hacking Health Camp 2027: a 50-hour health hackathon, 20-22 March at the Faculté de Médecine in Strasbourg. Organised by Health Factory & OpenCare Lab. Format, prices, programme.' WHERE slug='hacking-health-strasbourg';
UPDATE salons SET description_en=NULL, editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- HANNOVER MESSE is the world''s largest trade fair for industry and production technologies: automation, energy, industrial AI, hydrogen, electric mobility and R&D transfer, brought together on a single site in Hannover.
- Organiser: **Deutsche Messe AG**, operator of the Hannover exhibition centre and the long-standing producer of the trade fair.
- Next edition: **5 to 8 April 2027** at the [Deutsche Messe Hannover](/lieux/deutsche-messe-hannover) (Hannover Exhibition Centre).
- Latest results: the 2026 edition (20-24 April) drew around **110,000 visitors** from more than 150 countries and over **4,000 exhibitors**, down on the 127,000 visitors of 2025.
- **Partner country 2027: Spain**, following Brazil (2026) and Canada (2025). The partner country changes with each edition.
- Headline themes: artificial intelligence (the common thread running through every hall), Physical AI and robotics, automation, energy infrastructure, green hydrogen and electromobility.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

HANNOVER MESSE brings together the value chain of the manufacturing and energy industries: mechanical engineering, electrical engineering, automation and robotics, software vendors and industrial platforms (Industry 4.0, digital twins, industrial AI), energy players (grids, storage, hydrogen, fuel cells), electromobility, subcontracting and engineering, research and technology transfer. The main exhibition categories announced are **Automation & Digitalization**, **Energy & Industrial Infrastructure** and **Research & Technology Transfer**. In 2025, around 4,000 companies from 60 countries presented more than 10,000 products and solutions.

### Notable exhibitors

The exhibitor list and hall plan are available on the official website: [hannovermesse.de](https://www.hannovermesse.de/en/exhibitors-products/). The trade fair regularly brings together more than 4,000 companies from the electrical engineering, mechanical engineering, digital and energy sectors. The partner country''s pavilion is a highlight every year: in 2026, Brazil deployed around 300 companies across 6 halls and 2,700 m², coordinated by ApexBrasil and SEBRAE (source: hannovermesse.de).

### Quality of the visitor base

- Around 110,000 visitors in 2026, of whom roughly 40% were international, from more than 150 countries (source: Deutsche Messe AG).
- In 2025, around 127,000 visitors from 150 countries, more than 40% from abroad. Main countries of origin after Germany: China, the Netherlands, Canada, Poland, South Korea, Japan (source: Deutsche Messe AG).
- A professional visitor base of industrial decision-makers: technical, production, purchasing, R&D, energy and industrial information systems management.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

The trade fair is paired with a dense conference programme built around the edition''s main themes: industrial AI, Physical AI and robotics (including humanoid robots), automation, energy infrastructure, hydrogen, cybersecurity and industrial security. Artificial intelligence is positioned as a common thread running across all the halls. The detailed programme and speakers for the 2027 edition will be published as the trade fair approaches on hannovermesse.de (source: hannovermesse.de).

### Community & networks

HANNOVER MESSE is run by **Deutsche Messe AG**, working closely with the German industrial federations, notably the **VDMA** (mechanical engineering) and the **ZVEI** (electrical engineering and digital), long-standing partners of the trade fair. The partner-country approach (Spain in 2027) shapes an annual exercise in economic diplomacy: memoranda of understanding, official delegations and national pavilions. The 2026 edition was opened by the German Chancellor and the Brazilian President (source: hannovermesse.de).

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking, transport and accommodation: see the [Deutsche Messe Hannover](/lieux/deutsche-messe-hannover) listing.

Specific to the trade fair:

- **2027 dates:** 5 to 8 April 2027 (Monday to Thursday).
- **Scale of the site:** the trade fair occupies many halls of the Hannover exhibition centre, one of the largest in the world. Expect significant distances between halls and plan your route by main category (Automation & Digitalization, Energy & Industrial Infrastructure, Research & Technology Transfer).
- **Partner country pavilion:** identify the location of the Spanish pavilion in advance, a focal point for delegations and official highlights.
- **Accommodation:** hotel demand in Hannover is very high during the trade fair, so book well in advance.

## How much it costs to exhibit

