### Trajectory analysis

The Mondial de l''Auto has lived through a decade of visible crisis: from the historic peak of more than a million visitors in 2018 to the 2022 low (around 398,000), the show lost a share of premium manufacturers and its status as an unmissable global event. The 2024 edition marked a clear rebound (+27.7% in general-public attendance, 48 manufacturers, the return of brands absent in previous years), validating the repositioning strategy led by PFA and Hopscotch since 2019. **The 2026 edition plays a pivotal role**: with more than 20 Chinese brands announced and all the major Western groups confirmed, the show becomes the global arena of industrial confrontation over electric vehicles. The format extended to 7 days, Denza''s European premiere and BYD''s investment in Hall 6 confirm that Paris is once again becoming a strategic stop on the global automotive calendar, at a time when the European sector is playing for its future against the Chinese offensive.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='mondial-auto-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Global gathering for mountain planning and development at Alpexpo Grenoble, on a biennial cycle in even years. 2024 edition (50th anniversary): 23,604 trade visitors, 462 exhibitors, 67 countries represented across 50,000 sq m.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- Mountain Planet 2028 is the global gathering for mountain planning and development, scheduled from Tuesday 11 to Thursday 13 April 2028 at [Alpexpo Grenoble](/lieux/alpexpo-grenoble), on the biennial even-year cycle unchanged since the trade show was created in 1974.
- The venue does not change: the trade show remains anchored at Alpexpo Grenoble, its historic site since 1974, across a surface area of 60,000 sq m mobilised at the most recent edition.
- Review of the previous edition (21-23 April 2026): +4.5% trade visitors vs the 2024 edition (i.e. ~24,650 trade visitors), 460 exhibitors, across 60,000 sq m with a second hall opened for this edition. These figures confirm the upward trajectory of the event as the world''s largest mountain trade show by size.
- A 100% professional trade show on personalised badge, free entry with pre-registration. The trade show is operated by Alpexpo, a Société Publique Locale whose shareholders are Grenoble Alpes Métropole and the Ville de Grenoble.
- The 2028 edition takes place two years out from the 2030 Olympic and Paralympic Winter Games awarded to the French Alps, making it a pivotal occasion for those working in mountain planning, mobility and hospitality. The detailed editorial programme is published by Alpexpo as the edition approaches.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

A B2B professional trade show covering the entire mountain sector: ski lifts, snowmaking, slope grooming and maintenance, safety and rescue, ticketing, soft and urban mobility, outdoor equipment, natural risk management, accommodation and tourism services, training and local authorities. Audience: ski area operators, resort elected officials and technicians, engineering consultancies, equipment manufacturers, tourism operators, local mountain authorities, government departments and risk-prevention bodies.

### Notable exhibitors

The trade show''s historic core brings together the sector''s major equipment manufacturers: ski-lift builders (POMA, Doppelmayr, Leitner), grooming specialists (Kässbohrer PistenBully, Prinoth), snowmaking (TechnoAlpin, Demaclenko, MND Group), ticketing (Axess), alongside the construction and resort-development fabric. The trade show also hosts collective pavilions led by the Grenoble ecosystem and French Tech Alpes.

The full list of 2028 exhibitors will be available on the official website: [mountain-planet.com](https://www.mountain-planet.com).

### Visitor quality

100% professional, access strictly reserved on personalised badge after verification. In 2024: 23,604 trade visitors from 67 countries, with strong representation from the Alpine countries (France, Italy, Switzerland, Austria, Germany) and export markets (North America, East Asia, emerging mountain regions of Asia). The 2026 review stands at +4.5% visitors vs 2024, confirming the upward momentum. Decision-maker profiles: ski area management, resort elected officials, planning and equipment departments of local authorities, technical directors of engineering consultancies, equipment buyers, safety and rescue managers, prevention bodies.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

The trade show traditionally rolls out more than 50 events over 3 days: plenary conferences, round tables, innovation pitches and technical workshops structured around the sector''s major challenges. The 2026 edition had built its programming around three themes (sport, health and wellbeing; natural risk management and resilience; 2030 Olympic Winter Games) and welcomed ministerial addresses at the opening. The 2028 programme and editorial themes are published by Alpexpo as the trade show approaches.

### Awards & accolades

The **Mountain Innovation Awards** reward, at each edition, the sector''s most notable innovations, by application and selection by a jury. The 2028 winners are announced by the organisers during the trade show week. Candidate exhibitors prepare their submission in advance with Alpexpo.

### Satellite events

The trade show maintains distinct institutional partnerships with **ANEM** (Association Nationale des Élus de la Montagne) and **ANENA** (Association Nationale pour l''Étude de la Neige et des Avalanches), which contribute to programming on territorial resilience and avalanche risk prevention. These two partnerships are distinct and should be identified as such by exhibitors applying for thematic conference cycles.

### Community & networks

**Alpexpo** has been the organiser and operator of the trade show since its creation in 1974. The body is a Société Publique Locale whose shareholders are **Grenoble Alpes Métropole** and the **Ville de Grenoble**.

The partner institutional ecosystem brings together Grenoble Alpes Métropole, Isère Attractivité, the SMMAG, the CCI Grenoble, French Tech Alpes, ANEM and ANENA. The trade show maintains historic ties with the Grenoble mountain industrial fabric, behind its creation in 1974 under the name Salon de l''Aménagement en Montagne.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Alpexpo Grenoble](/lieux/alpexpo-grenoble) listing for the 2028 edition.

Specific to the trade show:

- **Dates and times:** Tuesday 11 to Thursday 13 April 2028, strictly professional access.
- **Visitor badge:** free with online pre-registration, personalised badge on proof of professional activity. No on-site ticketing for the general public.
- **Accommodation:** central Grenoble as a priority for direct tram A access. Booking 3 to 4 months in advance recommended, as the trade show mobilises the entire global mountain sector over 3 days. The nearby Alpine resort hotels (Chamrousse, Les Sept Laux, Vercors) make it possible to combine skiing and the trade show.
- **Services:** multi-point catering across the halls, exhibitor VIP areas, press room, dedicated spaces for collective pavilions, interpreting services for international delegations.

## What it costs to exhibit

