THCon grew from around 300 participants in 2018 to 1,800 announced in 2022, a sixfold increase in 4 years, driven by an upgrade in keynotes (Eurecom, CISPA, Airbus, ANSSI) and the international broadening of the CTF (12 countries). The event has found its balance between an academic dimension (CFP, research presentations) and pro/student accessibility (preserved prices, free online CTF). The slug `numeriquest-toulouse-2026` is a legacy that should be corrected editorially: the real event is THCon, and the slug should eventually reflect that name to reduce user friction. For now the URL is being kept so as not to lose the Google indexing already acquired.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='numeriquest-toulouse';
UPDATE salons SET description_en=NULL, editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- "The meeting place for smart energy": Open Energies is the trade show dedicated to renewable energy and the intelligent management of energy systems (photovoltaic generation, storage, flexibility, new applications).
- Organiser: GL events Exhibitions Opérations (SA). The trade show was launched in 2024 as a complement to [BePositive](/salons/bepositive-lyon), the group''s other energy-transition event in Lyon.
- Next edition: 3rd edition, expected in 2028 at [Eurexpo Lyon](/lieux/eurexpo-lyon) (Chassieu). Dates not yet announced by the organiser as of this listing.
- Frequency: biennial, in even-numbered years. Open Energies (2024, 2026, 2028) and BePositive (2025, 2027) alternate in March at GL events.
- 2026 results (2nd edition): 3,200 professionals, 90 exhibitors and brands, 24 conferences, 65 speakers and 20 institutional and media partners over two days. The trade show was co-located with [Expobiogaz](/salons/expobiogaz) to create bridges between solar and biogas.
- Target audience: renewable energy producers and distributors, integrators, grid operators, electric mobility players, technology developers and public and private decision-makers.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Open Energies brings together the upstream and downstream of the renewable energy system: photovoltaic equipment manufacturers, storage solution providers, electric mobility players, energy digitalisation companies, integrators and smart building specialists. The positioning centres on renewable energy generation (solar in the lead), new applications and the intelligent management of equipment.

### Notable exhibitors

90 exhibitors and brands were present at the 2026 edition. The official list is available on the [Open Energies partners page](https://www.open-energies.com/fr/partners).

### Visitor quality

3,200 professionals attended the 2026 edition, compared with more than 4,800 at the first edition in 2024. These figures are reported by the organiser (not OJS audited). In 2026, the trade show shared its halls with Expobiogaz: the two events combined drew more than 5,700 visitors, but this combined total should not be attributed to Open Energies alone.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

The programme is built around the Forum (round tables on key energy issues) and workshops (presentations of tools, projects and demonstrations). In 2026, the Syndicat des énergies renouvelables (SER) held the first edition of its meetings on the flexibility of the energy system there, devoted to "Flexibilité du Système Energétique : la responsabilité croissante des renouvelables", focusing on the large-scale integration of renewables and grid stability.

### Awards and accolades

The European Solar Games, a competition bringing together pairs of professionals in real-time photovoltaic installation events (organised by Enstall with the support of Qualit''EnR, LONGi Solar and Enphase Energy), set the pace of the 2026 edition with their grand prize, the Crochet d''or. Rgreen Energy won the Crochet d''or in 2026. The winners change with each edition: refer to the official programme for the upcoming edition.

### Community & networks

Open Energies brings together the renewable energy sector of the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes region, a pioneering territory for renewable energy, and draws on the ecosystem of institutional and media partners mobilised by GL events around the energy transition.

## On site: what you need to know

For access, parking and accommodation, see the venue listing: [Eurexpo Lyon](/lieux/eurexpo-lyon) (Boulevard de l''Europe, 69686 Chassieu).

Specific to the trade show: an event reserved for professionals. The 2026 edition was held over two days (Tuesday 9am-6pm, Wednesday 9am-5pm). The dates and opening hours of the 2028 edition will be published on the official website. In 2026, co-location with Expobiogaz allowed cross-access to both trade shows with a single badge.

## How much it costs to exhibit

GL events does not publish a freely available exhibitor pricing schedule for Open Energies. The terms of participation (bare floor space or fitted stand rental, visibility packages, partnerships) are provided on request via the trade show''s sales department. The amounts below are an indicative sector benchmark for a B2B trade show of this size at Eurexpo, and not prices published by the organiser.

<BudgetTable warning="Indicative sector benchmark: no official Open Energies pricing schedule is published. Request a quote from the organiser." rows={[{ "poste": "Bare floor space rental (per m²)", "low": "€250", "high": "€400" }, { "poste": "Fitted stand / turnkey package (12 m²)", "low": "€6,000", "high": "€12,000" }, { "poste": "Fit-out, furniture, electricity", "low": "€2,000", "high": "€6,000" }, { "poste": "Logistics, transport, staff", "low": "€3,000", "high": "€8,000" }]} totals={[{ "label": "ESTIMATED TOTAL (12-18 m² stand)", "low": "~€12,000", "high": "~€30,000" }]} footer="Indicative estimate excluding premium visibility and conference sponsorship." />

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 9 to 6 months out: initial sales contact, space booking and choice of location (close to the Forum or the workshops depending on your target).
- 4 to 3 months out: stand design, application for activities (European Solar Games, workshop speaking slots).
- 2 months out: client invitation campaign, qualifying meetings, build-up logistics.
- 2 days out: build-up, team briefing, checking of product demonstrations.

### For the decision-maker visitor

Identify in advance the Forum round tables and the SER meetings on flexibility, which concentrate regulatory and grid news. Prepare a short-list of exhibitors from the official partners page. The dense two-day format lends itself to a targeted visit rather than an exhaustive walkthrough.

## Policy & CSR initiatives

Open Energies is by its very nature an energy-transition trade show: renewable generation, self-consumption, grid flexibility and electric mobility make up the heart of its programme. Beyond the theme, the verifiable organisational commitment operates on two levels. On the venue side, Eurexpo Lyon is ISO 20121 certified (responsible event management), alongside the Centre de Congrès de Lyon and La Sucrière, as part of the CSR approach of GL Lyon Events. On the group side, GL events runs a CSR policy across its venues and events. No ISO 20121 certification specific to the Open Energies event is publicly documented at this stage.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable columns={[{ "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" }, { "key": "dates", "label": "Dates" }, { "key": "lieu", "label": "Venue" }, { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors", "align": "right" }, { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }]} rows={[{ "edition": "3rd (upcoming)", "dates": "2028 (dates TBC)", "lieu": "Eurexpo Lyon", "visiteurs": "TBC", "exposants": "TBC" }, { "edition": "2nd", "dates": "10-11 March 2026", "lieu": "Eurexpo Lyon", "visiteurs": "3,200", "exposants": "90" }, { "edition": "1st", "dates": "23-24 January 2024", "lieu": "Eurexpo Lyon", "visiteurs": ">4,800", "exposants": "129" }]} sources="Organiser figures (GL events Exhibitions Opérations / Open Energies), not OJS audited." />

### Trajectory analysis

Launched in 2024 by GL events Exhibitions Opérations as an extension of BePositive, Open Energies settled into a biennial rhythm in even-numbered years, alternating with BePositive in March. The second edition (2026) saw attendance fall compared with the launch year (3,200 against more than 4,800 professionals), against a backdrop of tension in the photovoltaic sector, but gained in sector coherence thanks to co-location with Expobiogaz, which broadens the scope from solar towards renewable gases. The trajectory to watch for 2028: the consolidation of the Open Energies / Expobiogaz pairing and the trade show''s ability to durably bring together grid flexibility and electric mobility beyond photovoltaics alone.', seo_title_en='Open Energies 2028 Lyon: dates, exhibitors, programme | Agoris', seo_description_en='Open Energies 2028 at Eurexpo Lyon: the GL events smart energy trade show. 3rd edition (even years, alternating with BePositive). 3,200 visitors and 90 exhibitors in 2026.' WHERE slug='open-energies-lyon';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Annual, travelling international forum on the place of women in gastronomy, food and nutrition. Talks, debates and networking. Founded in 2015 by culinary journalist Maria Canabal.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key points

- Parabere Forum is an annual international forum dedicated to the place of women in gastronomy, food and nutrition. Founded in 2015 by **Maria Canabal**, it is an independent, not-for-profit platform chaired by its founder.
- Format: two days of talks, debates and networking bringing together chefs, scientists, producers, academics and food activists. It is not a trade show with exhibitor stands, but a forum of ideas.
- Next edition: 12th edition, on 28 February and 1 March 2027 in Nice, on the theme "Disruption". The precise venue is to be confirmed by the organiser.
- Travelling: the forum changes city every year. Bilbao (2015, inaugural edition), Bari, Barcelona, Malmö, Oslo, Istanbul, Paris, Palma, Rome (2024), New York (2025), Barcelona (2026).
- Official tagline: "an independent, international and non-for-profit platform featuring women''s views and voices on major food issues".

## Who attends

### Typical profile

Parabere Forum brings together professionals from the wider food ecosystem: Michelin-starred chefs, sommeliers, scientists, anthropologists, producers, farmers, food journalists, academics and policymakers. The forum mobilises speakers from all five continents around the major issues facing food.

The event draws on the Parabere database, created in 2015, which today brings together more than 8,000 women from the food sector across the world (source: parabereforum.com/parabere-database).

### Notable speakers

The 2026 programme (Barcelona, 11th edition) features, among others, Carme Ruscalleda and Martina Puigvert (Michelin-starred chefs), Charles Spence (professor, University of Oxford), Dan Saladino (BBC journalist), Olia Hercules (Ukrainian chef and author) and Monica Berg (bartender). The full list of speakers is published on [parabereforum.com](https://www.parabereforum.com).

The speakers for the 2027 edition in Nice will be announced as the event approaches.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

The forum is structured around two days of talks, debates and panel discussions built around an annual theme: "The Politics of Food" in 2024 (Rome), "Food Design" in 2025 (New York), "Disruption" for the 2027 edition in Nice. The detailed programme is published online a few weeks before each edition (source: parabereforum.com).

### Parabere Grant

The forum awards the **Parabere Grant**, a bursary intended to support women in the food sector. The mechanics and the recipients are detailed on the dedicated page of the official website.

### Parabere Care Award

The forum also presents the **Parabere Care Award**, which recognises best practice in team management within hospitality: fair conditions, ethics and well-being at work. Recent recipients: Monica Berg (2024), Jess Murphy (2025), Alice Delcourt (2026).

### Community and networks

Beyond the annual event, Parabere runs an international network of food professionals and a partner programme (Partners Club). The partners highlighted for recent editions include Tayer, Di Martino, SPYCA and Petra, as well as the Generalitat de Catalunya and Marca Catalunya for the Spanish edition.

## On site: what you need to know

- **Format:** a two-day forum, entirely focused on talks and networking. Registration is in the attendee''s name.
- **Ticketing:** access is by paid ticket (various categories depending on the edition). The terms and prices for the 2027 edition are published on [parabereforum.com](https://www.parabereforum.com).
- **Venue:** the 2027 host city is Nice. The exact venue has not yet been confirmed by the organiser to date.

## Planning your visit

- Keep an eye on the opening of registration at parabereforum.com (tickets for previous editions went on sale a few months before the event).
- Consult the programme and the list of speakers, published in advance, to target the right sessions.
- Set aside both full days: the forum is designed as a continuous experience, not a one-off visit.
- For women in the food sector, look into the terms of the Parabere Grant.

## How the forum has evolved

Created in 2015 in Bilbao by Maria Canabal, culinary journalist and founder of the forum (source: farmersjournal.ie), Parabere Forum set out with the mission of giving women a voice on the major issues facing food.

The forum is travelling and changes city every year: Bilbao (2015, 1st edition), Bari (2016, 2nd), Barcelona (2017, 3rd), Malmö (2018, 4th), Oslo (2019, 5th), Istanbul (2020, 6th), Paris (2021, 7th), Palma (2022, 8th), Rome (2024, 9th, "The Politics of Food"), New York (2025, 10th, "Food Design"), then Barcelona (2026, 11th edition). The 2027 edition in Nice will mark the 12th edition, on the theme "Disruption", and the first to be held in France since Paris in 2021.

Recent editions do not publish audited audience figures. The forum brings together more than 8,000 food professionals in its international database (source: parabereforum.com).', seo_title_en='Parabere Forum 2027 Nice: women and gastronomy | Agoris', seo_description_en='Parabere Forum 2027 in Nice, 12th edition on 28 February and 1 March on the theme Disruption. The international forum for women in gastronomy, founded by Maria Canabal.' WHERE slug='parabere-forum-nice';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Design festival in Paris. A trail through showrooms, galleries and exhibition venues dedicated to design and interior decoration.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- **Paris Design Week 2026**, 16th edition, from **10 to 19 September 2026** in Paris, a roaming urban design festival spread across **4 official districts** (Rive Gauche / Marais - Bastille - République / Opéra - Concorde - Étoile / Palais-Royal - Place des Victoires - Pigalle), complemented by the central **Paris Design Week Factory** pavilion (Marais).
- An event organised by **SAFI**, a joint subsidiary of **Ateliers d''Art de France** and **RX France** (part of Reed Expositions), which also organises [Maison&Objet Paris](/salons/maison-objet-paris), of which Paris Design Week is the urban extension (a 10-day fringe event).
- 2025 edition (15th) results: **250,000 visitors**, **450 participating venues** (showrooms, boutiques, studios, hotels, restaurants, museums, institutions), **500+ talents** showcased, free public access. 2026 target: comparable scale, with greater internationalisation thanks to a **Special Focus China** at the Factory.
- The 2026 programme is built around the Maison&Objet curatorial theme **"Pulse in Motion"** (NellyRodi + Studio Masquespacio as ambassadors). The 2026 edition of the **Rising Talent Awards** is held under the banner of the **25th anniversary of Maison&Objet** (official wording: "our 25th anniversary"), under the patronage of the **Ministère de la Culture**.
- Official institutional partners for 2026: **Ville de Paris**, **Mobilier national**, **Galerie Joseph**, **MOM** (Maison&Objet and More, a 365-day B2B digital platform). Partner schools: Ecole Boulle, Ecole Camondo, ENSAD, ENSCi Les Ateliers, LISAA Archi/Design.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Paris Design Week is not a stand-based trade show but a **multi-site urban trail** that brings together **art and design galleries**, **publisher showrooms**, **concept-store boutiques**, **designers'' studios**, **applied arts schools**, **museums and institutions**, and **curated hotels and restaurants**. The format combines established players from the international design scene, emerging young creators and heritage houses of French design.

The scope covers **furniture, interior architecture, decorative arts, lighting, tableware, textiles, graphic design, fashion and gastronomy**. The official breakdown is organised into **4 geographical districts**, complemented by the central **Paris Design Week Factory** pavilion.

### 2026 districts and flagship venues

- **Rive Gauche**: Saint-Germain-des-Prés, the historic art and design gallery district, antique dealers.
- **Marais - Bastille - République**: a concentration of contemporary showrooms, **Espace Commines** and **Galeries Joseph** (home of the Factory).
- **Opéra - Concorde - Étoile**: luxury houses, department stores (Galeries Lafayette as an associate partner), international showrooms.
- **Palais-Royal - Place des Victoires - Pigalle**: contemporary design publishers, young creative scenes, concept stores.
- **Paris Design Week Factory**: the central pavilion in the Marais (Espace Commines + Galeries Joseph), from **10 to 14 September 2026**, organised around 4 themes: **Collectible**, **Publishing** (Editions), **Craft** and **Special Focus on China**.

### Official exhibitors and participants

- **Mobilier national** (Manufacture des Gobelins, Campus d''Excellence Mode Métiers d''Art et Design): dedicated annual exhibition.
- **Galerie Joseph**: a network of around thirty exhibition spaces in the Marais, a structural partner.
- **MAD - Musée des Arts Décoratifs**, **Archives Nationales**, **Institut du Monde Arabe**, **Centre des Monuments Nationaux**: institutional and cultural partners.
- Schools: **Ecole Boulle**, **Ecole Camondo**, **ENSAD** (Ecole Nationale Supérieure des Arts Décoratifs), **ENSCi Les Ateliers**, **LISAA Archi/Design**.
- Associate partners: Brasserie La Parisienne, Castrum Global, EPC Champagne, Esdac, Galeries Lafayette, Junot, La Cornue, La Fabrica, Le Festival du Monde, Lucett, Ace Education.

The official list of participants by district is published on the [PDW Participants](https://www.maison-objet.com/en/paris-design-week/pdw-participants) page.

### Quality of the visitor base

A hybrid audience by design: **design professionals** (interior architects, publishers, premium retailers, specialist decor/design journalists, prescribers), an **informed general public** (collectors, knowledgeable enthusiasts, art school students) and **international buyers** as an extension of Maison&Objet (50% international on the M&O Pulse side in September 2025). The **free, open-access** format, with no badge required, sets PDW apart from conventional B2B trade shows.

## Activities and community

### Rising Talent Awards 2026

The flagship distinction of the Maison&Objet / Paris Design Week ecosystem, the 2026 edition of the **Rising Talent Awards** is part of the **25th anniversary of Maison&Objet** (official wording: "our 25th anniversary"), under the patronage of the **Ministère de la Culture**. The format returns to its French origins: 6 design figures (nominators) each sponsor a young French talent presented to the jury. Named sponsors for 2026: **Pierre Yovanovitch** (interior architect), **Didier Krzentowski** (gallerist, Galerie kreo), **René-Jacques Mayer** (director of Ecole Camondo). Selected French winners for 2026: Adrien Garcia, Julie Richoz, Laureline Galliot, Mathieu Peyroulet Ghilini, Natacha & Sacha Hourcade-Poutoux, Wendy Andreu. List and details on [maison-objet.com](https://www.maison-objet.com/en/paris/programme/talents/the-rising-talent-awards).

### Paris Design Week Factory

A curatorial springboard for young designers, publishers and galleries, **from 10 to 14 September 2026** at Espace Commines and Galeries Joseph (Marais). Four sections: **Collectible**, **Publishing** (Editions), **Craft**, **Special Focus on China**. Eligibility criteria: under **5 years of professional activity**, a maximum of 2 previous participations, pieces produced within the last 18 months. Participation rates: from **€170 excl. VAT / m²** (individual designers), **€320 excl. VAT / m²** (publishing houses and galleries).

### Conference and event programme

Private views, meet & greets with designers, ephemeral installations, unveilings of new products, participatory workshops, exhibitions, cocktails. There is no unified academic conference programme as at Maison&Objet: the editorial line rests on the **district-by-district trail** and the signature of each participating venue.

### Community and networks

The official PARIS DESIGN WEEK app (App Store + Google Play) with geolocated trails. The **MOM (Maison&Objet and More)** B2B digital platform: 5,000+ brands listed, 30,000 products, 480,000+ active professionals as an extension of the physical event.

2026 press partners: **AD France**, **Ideat**, **Le Monde**, **Monocle**, **Radio Nova**, **The Socialite Family**, **Goodmoods**.

### What''s new for 2026

- **Rising Talent Awards** in a revisited format (6 French winners presented by 6 sponsors), to mark the 25th anniversary of Maison&Objet.
- **Special Focus on China** at the Paris Design Week Factory: a new annual geographical theme.
- **Studio Masquespacio** (Spain) as ambassadors, in connection with the Maison&Objet curatorial theme **"Pulse in Motion"** by NellyRodi.
- 2026 calendar: the event extended over **10 days** (10 to 19 September), partly overlapping with Maison&Objet Pulse September (10-14 September).

## On site: what you need to know

As Paris Design Week is a **roaming multi-site event**, there is no single venue page: each district operates with its own Paris-based logistics.

- **Access**: all districts are served by the Paris metro (mainly lines 1, 4, 7, 8, 9, 12, 14). Favour public transport and walking between venues within the same district.
- **Dense pedestrian districts**: Marais - Bastille - République and Palais-Royal - Place des Victoires concentrate their venues within a 1 to 2 km radius, walkable on foot.
- **More spread-out districts**: Rive Gauche (Saint-Germain to Sèvres-Babylone) and Opéra - Concorde - Étoile may require 2 to 3 metro journeys.
- **Paris Design Week Factory**: Espace Commines (5 rue Commines, 75003) + Galeries Joseph (several addresses in the 3rd arrondissement). Metro Filles du Calvaire (L8) or Saint-Sébastien-Froissart (L8).
- **Dates**: Thursday 10 September to Saturday 19 September 2026. The **Factory** runs from 10 to 14 September only (closing aligned with Maison&Objet Pulse).
- **Access**: **free for the general public**, with no prior registration for most venues. Some private views and evening events are by invitation only.
- **Official app**: PARIS DESIGN WEEK (App Store + Google Play), geolocated trails by district, an interactive map of the 450 venues.
- **Accommodation**: in the September period in Paris, plan to book 3 to 4 months in advance, especially over the Maison&Objet Pulse overlap (10-14 Sept). Recommended areas depend on your priority district.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- **9 months out**: make contact with SAFI via the [PDW application page](https://www.maison-objet.com/en/paris-design-week/participatepdw). Select the relevant district according to your brand positioning.
- **6 months out**: submit your application to the Paris Design Week Factory if eligible (under 5 years of activity), choosing your section (Collectible, Publishing, Craft, China). **2026 Factory application deadline: 25 May 2026** (forthcoming edition).
- **4 months out**: brief on the venue''s scenography (window display, private view, ephemeral installation), jointly validated with the PDW team.
- **3 months out**: listing on the official app and the trail map, validation of editorial content on the Maison&Objet website.
- **6 weeks out**: activation of the specialist decor/design press (AD France, Ideat, Le Monde, Goodmoods), private-view invitations, B2B plan linked to Maison&Objet Pulse for exhibitors present at both events.

### For the decision-making visitor

- **Free access**, no badge: plan your visit strategy by district rather than by brand.
- **The PARIS DESIGN WEEK app is essential**: a map of the 450 venues, geolocated trails, a private-view diary.
- **Allow 1 day per district** for serious coverage (4 districts + Factory = around 4 to 5 full days for the complete tour).
- **The Maison&Objet Pulse overlap period (10-14 Sept)**: maximum density, international press present, a good window for institutional private views.
- **15-19 September**: a quieter period, ideal for detailed showroom visits and designer meetings.
- **Private views**: generally at the end of the day (6pm-10pm), often by invitation for partner institutions. Use the app and the galleries'' Instagram accounts for announcements.

## CSR policy and initiatives

Paris Design Week is part of the **Maison&Objet CSR framework** led by SAFI, structured around the **Eco-Green Charter** (sustainable sourcing, a "less is more" principle on the scenography side, respect for people and the environment). On the operational ecosystem side: partnerships with **Les Canaux** (Ville de Paris, social and solidarity economy), **Re''up**, **Emmaüs Défi** (upcycling and reuse channels), and **Fab City Grand Paris** (the **Sustain''Lab** programme for reusing materials after the event).

The **Ateliers d''Art de France** stake in SAFI''s capital supports a heritage-led, low-carbon approach to the craft trades, visible on the Paris Design Week side in the prominence given to the **Craft** sector at the Factory and in the partnerships with applied arts schools and the Mobilier national (Manufacture des Gobelins).

## How the trade show has evolved

