 notes={["The 2026 figures are targets announced by the organiser (to be distinguished from post-event results).","Piscine Global follows a biennial cycle in even-numbered years. The show evolved from \"Piscine\" (until 2014) to \"Piscine Global\" (2014) and then \"Piscine Global Europe\" (2016)."]}
 sources="Eurospapoolnews, Enjeux Piscine, Idées Piscine, GL Events"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

Piscine Global Europe consolidates in 2026 its positioning as the leading global show in its sector, already secured in 2024 (113 visitor countries, 32 exhibitor countries). The post-COVID trajectory shows a stabilisation of the audience at around 24,000 professionals, with an internationalisation that is accelerating (35% in 2022 → 42% in 2024), while the French private pool market goes through a phase of normalisation after the 2020-2022 peak. The main strategic shift for 2026 is the explicit opening to the **collective aquatic facilities** market via AQUALIE, a growth lever identified at the 2024 edition (+137% collective-body visitors). The structural partnership with the **FPP** (1,400 member companies) and **WAPSA** gives the show a solid institutional footing from which to defend its leadership against international competitors (Aquanale Cologne, Forum Piscine Bologna).

---

*Sources for key figures (visitors, exhibitors, internationalisation): EuroSpaPoolNews (2024 and 2022 edition results), Idées Piscine. Pool and Spa Trophies: FPP (propiscines.fr).*', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='piscine-global-lyon';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Trade show of plastics processing and composites. Raw materials, equipment, moulds and technologies for plastics transformation.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- FIP is the official acronym for **France Innovation Plasturgie**, the leading national gathering for the plastics processing, composites and rubber sector. The trade show is operated by **Infopro Digital Trade Shows**.
- 2026 edition: from 2 to 5 June 2026 at [Eurexpo Lyon](/lieux/eurexpo-lyon). The trade show features more than 800 exhibitors and brands represented, 10,000 visitors expected and over 30 conferences and workshops.
- A **triennial** trade show: it is held in the years without the global K trade fair in Düsseldorf. The previous edition, in June 2024, brought together nearly 10,000 participants and 800 exhibitors ([industrie-magazine.fr](https://industrie-magazine.fr/fip-2026-la-plasturgie-francaise-se-donne-rendez-vous-a-lyon/)).
- The trade show is co-located with the [3D Print](/salons/3d-print-lyon) trade show: a single badge grants access to both events over the same period at Eurexpo.
- A CSR commitments page is published and detailed on the official website.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

The trade show brings together "the entire Plastics Processing, Composites and Rubber sector, with qualified brands and clients": processors and subcontractors, machine manufacturers (injection, extrusion, rotomoulding, blow moulding, thermoforming, machining), producers of raw materials, additives and bio-based solutions, suppliers of moulds, peripherals and decoration solutions. Strictly professional visitor base.

The offering is structured into five complementary areas: **FIP** (central technology platform), **FIP Transform** (subcontracting and processors), **FIP Valorize** (solutions in support of the circular economy), **FIP Decoration** (decoration and finishing technologies) and **FIP Materials** (materials, additives and bio-based solutions).

### Notable exhibitors

List of exhibitors on the official website: [f-i-p.com](https://www.f-i-p.com/fr/devenir-exposant).

### Visitor quality

- Four thematic itineraries guide the visit according to the trade show''s official programme: Packaging, Mobility, Aerospace and Medical.
- The 2026 Medical itinerary is developed with the **Polymeris** competitiveness cluster (plastics processing, composites and rubber) (source: [devicemed.fr](https://www.devicemed.fr/dossiers/equipements-de-production-et-techniques-de-fabrication/plasturgie/fip-2026-la-plasturgie-tient-salon-a-lyon-du-2-au-5-juin-2026/53368)).
- Nearly 10,000 professional participants at the 2024 edition.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

More than **30 conferences and workshops** led by **80 speakers** from France and abroad announced by the organiser: practical case studies, technological advances and strategic perspectives for the plastics processing, composites and rubber industries.

The 2026 edition hosts the exhibition **"Plastiquement Indispensable l''Expo"**, which showcases emblematic finished plastic products across several markets, including health and medical.

### Community and networks

Pierre-Jean Leduc, president of Polyvia and of the DEMGY group, has been appointed **honorary president** of the FIP 2026 trade show (source: [polyvia.fr](https://www.polyvia.fr/fr/affaires-publiques/pierre-jean-leduc-nomme-president-du-salon-fip-2026)). The trade show draws on the sector''s ecosystem, including the Polyvia federation and the Polymeris cluster.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Eurexpo Lyon](/lieux/eurexpo-lyon) listing.

Specific to the trade show:

- **Visitor badge:** online registration on the official website. The FIP badge also grants access to the co-located [3D Print](/salons/3d-print-lyon) trade show.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates are not published on the official website: stand offers and available locations can be obtained from the trade show''s sales team ("For more information on stand offers and available locations, contact our sales team."). Figures not publicly available.

## CSR policy & initiatives

The trade show publishes a "Our CSR approach" page structured around three pillars: reducing environmental impact, social involvement and continuous improvement. Concrete commitments displayed: waste management with Véolia and Ecovalim, LED lighting, promotion of public transport, dematerialisation of marketing tools, catering using biodegradable materials, stand partitions in FSC- or PEFC-certified wood. Social dimension: support for the "Nos Quartiers ont du Talent" association and partnerships with Coeur2bouchons, Le Chaînon Manquant and Hop Hop Food. No ISO 20121 certification mentioned to date.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
  { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
  { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors", "align": "right" },
  { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
  { "edition": "2026", "visiteurs": "10,000 expected", "exposants": "800+ announced" },
  { "edition": "2024", "visiteurs": "~10,000", "exposants": "800" }
 ]}
 sources="f-i-p.com, industrie-magazine.fr, devicemed.fr"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

FIP follows a triennial rhythm aligned with the years without the global K trade fair in Düsseldorf, making it the major national gathering for plastics processing between two K editions. The 2024 edition confirmed the trade show''s appeal with nearly 10,000 participants and 800 exhibitors. The 2026 edition, organised by Infopro Digital Trade Shows at Eurexpo Lyon, repeats this format with five complementary areas and a strong focus on the circular economy (FIP Valorize) and application markets (Packaging, Mobility, Aerospace and Medical itineraries). The next edition is announced for 2029.', seo_title_en='FIP 2026 - France Innovation Plasturgie, Lyon Eurexpo (2-5 June)', seo_description_en='FIP - France Innovation Plasturgie 2026: the triennial trade show for plastics processing, composites and rubber, from 2 to 5 June 2026 at Eurexpo Lyon. 800+ exhibitors, 10,000 visitors expected.' WHERE slug='plastic-expo-lyon';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='International trade show for children''s fashion and nursery goods. Kids, junior and baby accessory collections for buyers.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- "The Big Small Show": the leading international trade show for children''s fashion, nursery goods and family lifestyle, positioned around a tight, creative selection of brands.
- Organiser: Picaflor (trading as Playtime), a Paris-based house that also runs the New York (Playtime New York) and Los Angeles (The Tiny Show on Tour LA) editions.
- Next Paris session: summer 2026 edition, from Saturday 27 to Monday 29 June 2026, at the [Parc Floral de Paris](/lieux/parc-floral-de-paris) (Espace Événement, Route de la Pyramide, 75012 Paris).
- Scale: around 280 to 350 brands depending on the session, 38 countries represented (January 2026 edition), and a stated attendance of roughly 12,000 professional buyers per session.
- Twice-yearly rhythm: two Paris sessions a year (January and summer), aligned with the buying calendars of multi-brand boutiques.
- Strong editorial angle: a "Made With Love" positioning, in declared opposition to ultra fast fashion, centred on craftsmanship, committed brands and family-run businesses.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Playtime Paris brings together brands in children''s fashion (from birth to adolescence), nursery goods and family lifestyle, across every price tier. The offer covers ready-to-wear, footwear, baby accessories, as well as a lifestyle component (gifts, decoration, products for the maternity and children''s world). The selection is curated, with a high proportion of European brands: France (20.3%), Spain (13.2%), Denmark (9.4%) and the Netherlands (8.7%) were among the most represented countries at the January 2026 edition.

### Notable exhibitors

The official, up-to-date list of brands by edition is available on the [Playtime Paris exhibitors page](https://www.iloveplaytime.com/brands/?events=paris). The session regularly includes a significant share of new brands (around 22% at the January 2026 edition).

### Quality of attendance

Attendance is made up of buyers from multi-brand boutiques, concept stores and international distributors. Playtime Paris reports a stated attendance of roughly 12,000 professionals per session (organiser figure, not OJS/UFI audited). Positioned as the largest European children''s fashion trade show, the event is a global benchmark in the segment.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

Each session features trend spaces, expert conferences and themed activities. For the January 2026 edition, around twenty brands activated around the "Made With Love" theme (contemporary artist exhibitions, customisation workshops for children led by illustrator Tiphaine, expert round tables via the Future Snoops agency, curated lifestyle spaces). The detailed programme for the upcoming edition is published on the official site as the event approaches.

### Selections and awards

The trade show highlights several selections and dedicated discovery spaces, whose official names are: "THE NEW NOW SELECTION" (10 ready-to-wear and footwear brands taking part for the first time), "THE SINGULAR SELECTION" (single products and capsule collections, pop-up store format with 6 brands), "FALL IN LOVE WITH FIRST TIMERS" (a space spotlighting new exhibitors), "Design Hunger Prizes" (5 lifestyle and accessory brands recognised by Design Hunger magazine) and "The Creative Studio" (12 illustrators and pattern designers). These selections evolve over time: check whether they are being repeated and their exact scope on the official site for the session concerned.

### Community and networks

Playtime draws on an international community of buyers and brands, kept active between sessions through its digital platform and social channels. Picaflor also runs a network of children''s trade shows (Playtime New York, The Tiny Show on Tour LA) that extends the Playtime community internationally.

## On site: what you need to know

For access, parking and accommodation, see the [Parc Floral de Paris](/lieux/parc-floral-de-paris) page (Espace Événement, Route de la Pyramide, 75012 Paris, in the Bois de Vincennes).

Specific to the trade show: the summer 2026 session takes place from Saturday 27 to Monday 29 June 2026. Opening hours: 9.30am to 6.30pm on the first two days, 9.30am to 4.30pm on the final day (source: official site at the time of writing). Access is restricted to professionals on a badge basis; registration and pre-registration are done online.

## How much it costs to exhibit

<BudgetTable warning="Pricing schedule not published by the organiser. Indicative sector ranges for a B2B fashion/lifestyle trade show at the Parc Floral, to be confirmed with Picaflor." rows={[{ "poste": "Stand (space + fit-out)", "low": "6,000 €", "high": "18,000 €" }, { "poste": "Registration / application fees", "low": "500 €", "high": "1,500 €" }, { "poste": "Logistics, transport, set-up", "low": "1,500 €", "high": "5,000 €" }, { "poste": "Team, travel, accommodation", "low": "2,000 €", "high": "8,000 €" }]} totals={[{ "label": "ESTIMATED TOTAL (indicative)", "low": "~10,000 €", "high": "~32,000 €" }]} footer="Indicative estimates excluding an official quote. Contact the organiser (Picaflor) directly for the session''s pricing schedule." />

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 6 months out: get in touch with Picaflor, apply and confirm the brand''s eligibility for the selection.
- 4 months out: book a stand, choose a location, and apply where relevant to the selections (THE NEW NOW, THE SINGULAR, FALL IN LOVE WITH FIRST TIMERS).
- 2 months out: finalise merchandising, prepare collections (upcoming season), logistics and transport.
- 2 weeks out: pre-register your teams, brief the sales team, and book buyer appointments.

### For the decision-maker visitor

Pre-register your professional badge online in advance. Target the selection spaces (new brands, lifestyle, Creative Studio) and the session''s theme to prioritise your appointments. Ideally allow a full day given the density of the offer. The Parc Floral is reachable by metro (Château de Vincennes) then shuttle or on foot.

## Policy & CSR initiatives

Playtime Paris promotes a "Made With Love" positioning, in declared opposition to ultra fast fashion, centred on protecting and passing on textile craftsmanship, family-run businesses and committed brands. This angle translates into dedicated activations (exhibitions, workshops, curated spaces) during the sessions. Details of a structured, quantified CSR policy (eco-design of stands, waste management, carbon footprint) are not publicly documented on the site and remain to be confirmed with the organiser.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable columns={[{ "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" }, { "key": "dates", "label": "Dates" }, { "key": "lieu", "label": "Venue" }, { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors", "align": "right" }, { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }]} rows={[{ "edition": "Summer 2026 (upcoming)", "dates": "27-29 June 2026", "lieu": "Parc Floral de Paris", "visiteurs": "n/a", "exposants": "n/a" }, { "edition": "Winter 2026", "dates": "24-26 Jan. 2026", "lieu": "Parc Floral de Paris", "visiteurs": "n/a", "exposants": "~280" }, { "edition": "Winter 2023", "dates": "28-30 Jan. 2023", "lieu": "Parc Floral de Paris", "visiteurs": "n/a", "exposants": "~350" }]} sources="Official site iloveplaytime.com; FashionNetwork; FashionUnited." />

### Trajectory analysis

Founded in 2007, Playtime established itself within about fifteen years as the largest European children''s fashion trade show, ahead of Pitti Bimbo, according to its director Sébastien de Hutten (FashionNetwork, 2023). The twice-yearly rhythm (January and summer) aligns the Paris sessions with the buying calendars of multi-brand boutiques. After a return to pre-pandemic levels noted as early as 2023 by the organiser, the show adjusts its exhibitor scale to around 280 to 350 brands depending on the session, with a marked internationalisation (38 countries at the January 2026 edition). Its editorial direction, built around "Made With Love" and an opposition to ultra fast fashion, positions Playtime on value, craftsmanship and curation rather than volume.', seo_title_en='Playtime Paris 2026: dates, exhibitors, programme | Agoris', seo_description_en='Playtime Paris, children''s fashion and nursery trade show at the Parc Floral, from 27 to 29 June 2026. Organiser Picaflor, ~280-350 brands, 38 countries, ~12,000 visitors.' WHERE slug='playtime-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='International trade show for environmental and energy solutions. The benchmark for professionals in the environmental sector and the circular economy.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- Pollutec is Europe''s benchmark trade show for environmental and energy solutions, held at Lyon Eurexpo. The 2023 edition was audited at 42,496 visitors and 1,621 exhibitors (source: OJS). The organiser announces 51,000 participants and 2,000 exhibitors for 2025 (source: RX France).
- 11 sectors covered (waste, water, energy, contaminated land, sustainable cities, air, risks, biodiversity, finance, etc.) → a cross-cutting trade show spanning the entire environmental sector, not a vertical.
- More than 350 speaking sessions (conferences, workshops, forums) per edition centred on 4 themes (health, decarbonisation, bioeconomy, science) → very high editorial density.
- Organised by RX France, on a biennial cycle.
- Next edition: 12-15 October 2027.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Environmental industry players, local authorities, engineering consultancies, researchers, agencies and institutional bodies, across the 11 sectors covered. **85% of the visitor base from the private sector, 15% public**. International dimension: 21% of participants from abroad and 28% of exhibitors based internationally, with a promotional network deployed across 14 countries (source: pollutec.com/international).

### Notable exhibitors

List of exhibitors on the official website: [pollutec.com](https://www.pollutec.com/fr-fr/).

### Visitor quality

- 42,496 audited visitors in 2023, including 6,164 from abroad (source: OJS)
- Target audience: industry players, local authorities, engineering consultancies, institutional bodies

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

More than 350 speaking sessions (conferences, workshops, forums) per edition, structured around 4 themes: health, decarbonisation, bioeconomy, science. New for 2025: Forum Filières (agriculture, textiles, mobility) (source: RX France).

### Awards & accolades

Pollutec Innovation Awards, co-organised with PEXE (the eco-business network): 180 applications and 3 winners in 2025 (Entent, Plastic Vortex, Tallano Technologies) (source: RX France).

### Community & networks

A cross-cutting trade show for the environmental sector. An interim event between two Lyon editions: STEP (Salon de la Transformation Écologique by Pollutec), in Paris.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Eurexpo Lyon](/lieux/eurexpo-lyon) page.

Specific to the trade show:

- **Visitor badge:** free for professionals, with prior online registration.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor pricing not published: quote on request from RX France via the official website. As Pollutec is the largest environmental trade show in Europe, exhibitor budgets vary considerably depending on stand size and sector.

## Planning your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: initial contact with RX France, booking a space by sector.
- 6 months out: stand plan, application for the Innovation Awards.
- 3 months out: accreditations, accommodation.
- 6 weeks out: delivery of materials, final briefing.

### For the decision-maker visitor

- Free professional badge with prior registration.
- Allow 1 to 2 days: 11 sectors across a large venue, target the themed villages in advance.

## ESG policy & initiatives

Pollutec publishes its commitments on its dedicated page [Our commitments](https://www.pollutec.com/fr-fr/a-propos/nos-engagements.html): Net Zero 2040 trajectory, elimination of single-use plastics, reduction of event waste.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
 { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
 { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors", "align": "right" },
 { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
 { "edition": "2025", "visiteurs": "51,000 (organiser)", "exposants": "2,000" },
 { "edition": "2023", "visiteurs": "42,496 (OJS-audited)", "exposants": "1,621" }
 ]}
 sources="OJS, RX France"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

Pollutec confirms its status as the largest environmental trade show in Europe. The 2023 edition was audited by the OJS at 42,496 visitors; the organiser announces strong growth in 2025 (51,000), not yet certified. One to watch: the impact of the new interim STEP format (Paris) on the momentum of the 2027 Lyon edition.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='pollutec-lyon';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='The world''s leading fashion creation trade show. Fabrics, leathers, accessories, yarns and design for fashion designers and brands.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- **Première Vision Paris**, autumn 2026 edition: **1 to 3 September 2026** at Paris Nord Villepinte. The edition presents the **Spring-Summer 2027** collections and marks the second instalment of the "Innovation & Technology" cycle.
- The world''s benchmark gathering for upstream fashion sourcing, organised by **Première Vision SA**, a subsidiary owned **100% by GL Events since 2023** following a gradual increase in its stake (24.5% in 2007, 49% in 2010, full control acquired for the trade show''s fiftieth anniversary).
- Leadership: **Florence Rousson**, Chair of the Executive Board since 21 February 2024, with thirteen years'' experience at GL Events Exhibitions, formerly Director of the Green Tech+ division.
- Most recent edition (February 2026): **30,000 professionals**, **126 nationalities**, **13,000 companies** represented, attendance in line with the post-relaunch momentum of the mid-September calendar.
- Personalised e-pass **free for professionals** on production of proof. No access fee to the trade show. Only the cloakroom is chargeable (4 to 5 € per suitcase or garment).

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Première Vision Paris structures its offer around **9 complementary worlds** that cover the entire upstream fashion sector: **Fabrics** (womenswear and menswear weaving), **Leather** (leathers, skins, furs), **Accessories** (buttons, fastenings, braids, labelling), **Yarns** (spinning and yarns), **Designs** (textile and print creation studios), **Manufacturing** (makers and production workshops), **Smart Creation** (cross-cutting responsible offer), **Bag-Shoes & Leatherwear** (leather goods and footwear components) and **Maison d''Exceptions** (one-off pieces and exceptional savoir-faire).

Attendance is strictly reserved for fashion and luxury professionals: stylists, collection directors, buyers, purchasing departments, design studios, agents, distributors, makers and couture houses. A strong presence of European luxury houses, international premium brands and Asian and American sourcing offices.

### Iconic brands (February 2026 edition)

The September edition follows on from February 2026, which brought together in particular:

- **ILUNA Group**: a long-established Italian lace maker, present on the Blossom Première Vision side with a responsible lace and textile innovation offer.
- **Filatures du Parc**: an emblematic French spinning mill of Made in France, listed in the Smart Creation and Made in France selections.
- Leading Italian, Japanese and Turkish weavers across the Fabrics and Yarns hubs.
- Premium European tanneries on the Leather side, creators of one-off pieces and embroidery workshops for Maison d''Exceptions.

### Quality of attendance

- **30,000 professionals** in February 2026, **126 nationalities** present.
- **13,000 companies** represented on the visitor side, a sign of high decision-making density relative to the raw volume.
- Decision-maker profiles: collection directors of luxury houses and premium brands, textile and materials buyers, design studios, independent designers, makers and international agents. Strictly professional access, personalised non-transferable badge, proof of activity required on registration.
- Biennial editorial cycle: **February = Craftsmanship & Know-How** edition, **September = Innovation & Technology** edition. Autumn 2026 places the emphasis on the outlook for materials and processes.

## Activities and community

### PV Awards

The most closely followed awards programme among the textile press and buyers, the **PV Awards** comprise **8 prizes**: Grand Jury Prize, Handle Prize, Imagination Prize, Fashion Smart Creation Prize, with systematic counterparts across the Fabrics and Leather worlds. The juries are chaired by recognised figures from the fashion and materials sector, which makes it an editorial barometer as much as a commercial roll of honour.

### Smart Creation, a structuring CSR platform

Launched in **September 2015**, **Smart Creation** is the platform dedicated to showcasing exhibitors'' responsible approaches. It was unified under a dedicated **Smart Creation Area** in 2020 and continues to structure the trade show''s CSR dimension. It brings together:

- **Smart Talks**: conferences on the materials transition, traceability and eco-design.
- **Smart Keys**: a reading grid of responsible commitments by exhibitor (material, process, energy, social).
- **Smart Creation Podcast**: an editorial extension for the professional community.

### Prospective Area and Sourcing Hubs

For the second "Innovation & Technology" instalment of the 2025-2026 cycle, the programme is enhanced with a **Prospective Area** dedicated to new materials and processes, and **Sourcing Hubs** that guide buyers towards strategic production areas. The **opening towards the Beauty sector** announced in September 2025 is beginning to translate concretely on the offer side.

### Associated trade shows

The Première Vision ecosystem covers the entire upstream fashion calendar with two benchmark satellites:

- **Denim Première Vision**: a trade show dedicated to the denim sector (weavers, finishers, makers, accessory suppliers).
- **Blossom Première Vision**: a pre-collection format in June, anticipating trends and materials 18 months ahead.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Paris Nord Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte) page.

Specific to the trade show:

- **Dates**: Tuesday 1 to Thursday 3 September 2026.
- **Edition**: Spring-Summer 2027 collections.
- **Visitor e-pass**: personalised, free on production of professional proof, to be requested online via the official site premierevision.com.
- **Cloakroom**: 4 to 5 € per suitcase or garment, the only chargeable item for the visitor.
- **Personalised non-transferable badge**, strict access control at the entrance.
- **Next spring edition**: February 2027, "Craftsmanship & Know-How" format centred on savoir-faire.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates are not published publicly. Quotes on request from **Première Vision SA** via the application form on premierevision.com. The rate scale depends on the chosen world (Fabrics, Leather, Yarns, Accessories, Designs, Manufacturing, Smart Creation, Bag-Shoes & Leatherwear, Maison d''Exceptions), the surface area and the stand formula. Demanding curatorial selection, particularly for Maison d''Exceptions, which operates by invitation and editorial jury.

Numerical data not publicly available to date.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: initial contact with Première Vision SA, choice of world and initial application.
- 9 months out: submission of the curatorial file for Maison d''Exceptions or Smart Creation Area, Smart Keys qualification.
- 6 months out: stand finalisation, contractor brief, preparation of materials selections and colour ranges for the PV Awards juries.
- 3 months out: team accreditations, press content, international buyer meeting schedule.
- 6 weeks out: activation of the commercial agenda, specialist textile and fashion press, pre-scheduling of visits with priority houses.

### For the decision-making visitor

- Free e-pass to be requested online in advance, personalised badge mandatory.
- 3-day trade show: allow **2 days minimum** to cover the 9 worlds and the Smart Creation Area.
- Identify in advance the PV Awards nominees and the Maison d''Exceptions selection in order to pace the visit by creative priorities.
- September cycle = "Innovation & Technology": priority to the Prospective Area, Sourcing Hubs and Smart Creation for forward-looking materials sourcing.

## CSR policy and initiatives

Première Vision steers its CSR approach through **Smart Creation**, a platform launched in September 2015 and unified in 2020 under a dedicated **Smart Creation Area**. The scheme articulates:

- **Smart Keys**: a reading grid by exhibitor, broken down across the materials, processes, energy and social commitment axes.
- **Smart Talks**: a programme of conferences on the materials transition, traceability and eco-design, integrated into the trade show''s official programme.
- **Smart Creation Podcast**: an editorial extension on in-depth topics (responsible sourcing, materials innovation, European regulation).
- **Cross-cutting responsible sourcing**: integration of the Smart Creation offer within each world, in addition to the dedicated area, to make it easier for buyers to identify.

This CSR line has been integrated into the trade show''s editorial positioning for ten years and forms part of the 2026 "Innovation & Technology" cycle, with a particular emphasis on low-impact processes and substitute materials.

## How the trade show has evolved

