### Trajectory analysis

RENT was born in **2013** on the initiative of **Hervé Parent**, after a visit to Inman Connect New York in 2012, in a French landscape where proptech did not yet have a flagship event. The first edition at the **Palais Brongniart** brought together 40 exhibitors and 400 visitors. The show then moved through Brongniart (2013-2014), La Villette (2015), a return to Brongniart, before switching to **Paris Expo Porte de Versailles, Pavilion 6** in 2019 for the 7th edition. That same year, **Figaro Classifieds** acquired RENT outright and installed **Stéphane Scarella** as Managing Director. Cumulative historical figures announced by the organiser: 100,000+ visitors across all editions, 500+ startups exhibited. The 2025 edition confirmed a mature format: 11,500+ professionals, 400 exhibitors, 120+ conferences, 1,500 meeting requests via MySITL Connect, and the arrival of a "Festival de la PropTech" evening event that changes the rhythm of the show. The 2026 edition builds on three levers. First the editorial thread **"Real estate moves from the screen to invisible AI"**, which extends the AI sequence opened in 2025. Then the consolidation of the **Proptech Sweet Awards** with MySweetImmo (Pépite, Very Sweet, Sweet Ladies), which establish a clear recognition framework for startups and impact-driven solutions. Finally the renewal of the **Wednesday evening event**, which turns the show into a business and social occasion across 2 full days.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='rent';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Trade show for state-run institutional catering. Solutions, equipment and innovations for collective kitchens.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- The **Restau''Co** trade show is the annual gathering for professionals in **state-run (direct-management)** institutional catering (schools, universities, hospitals, social-care and government bodies). It is run by the **Restau''Co** network, a sector association founded in 1966 that celebrates its **60th anniversary** in 2026.
- 11th edition: **Wednesday 17 June 2026**, at Paris Expo Porte de Versailles, Hall 4.1, over a single day.
- A short, intensive format: a one-day trade show combining the exhibition, plenary conferences and an awards ceremony.
- Review of the 2025 edition (10th edition, organiser''s figures): **2,200 qualified visitors** (35% buyers), **167 exhibitors** and 215 stands.
- The 2026 edition places the network''s 60th anniversary and the **Mon Restau Responsable** initiative (its CSR strand) at the heart of the programme.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Suppliers to self-managed institutional catering: food product manufacturers and distributors, kitchen equipment and hardware suppliers, hygiene solutions, management and menu software, and short-supply-chain and organic operators. The audience is strictly professional: head chefs, catering managers and directors of institutional catering, public-sector buyers, elected officials and local-authority managers. The organiser reports 35% buyers among the 2025 visitors.

### Notable exhibitors

Exhibitor list and floor plan on the official website: [salonrestauco.com](https://www.salonrestauco.com/). A dedicated **Start-up Area** showcases new ventures and the sector''s latest solutions.

### Quality of the audience

- 2,200 qualified visitors in 2025, of whom 35% were buyers.
- Decision-makers in direct management: school and university catering, hospital and social-care catering, and government bodies.
- A trade show focused on a specialist audience rather than a mass event: a short format that favours business meetings.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

The 2026 edition builds its plenaries and briefings around food sovereignty and the localisation of food supply, energy efficiency, the fight against waste, food education and the promotion of home-cooked meals. The programme notably includes a review of **10 years of the Mon Restau Responsable initiative** (sources: restauration21.fr, restauration-collective.com).

### Awards and prizes

The **2026 Exhibitor Innovation Awards** (9th edition) are presented in two categories: "food" and "equipment & hygiene". For the second year running, the format has evolved: innovations are presented in person on the stands in the morning, rather than judged on paper submissions (sources: resto-today.com, restauration-collective.com).

### Community and networks

The trade show is the physical event of the Restau''Co network, an association that brings together and represents the players in state-run institutional catering and champions the sector''s responsible food policies.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles) listing.

Specific to the trade show:

- **Opening hours:** Wednesday 17 June 2026, from 9 am to 6 pm, Hall 4.1.
- **Visitor badge:** free with prior online registration, reserved for institutional catering professionals. Register at [salonrestauco.com](https://www.salonrestauco.com/).
- **Format:** a single-day trade show, best approached by planning your meetings and conferences in advance.

## CSR policy & initiatives

The responsible dimension is at the core of the Restau''Co network''s positioning. For around a decade it has championed the **Mon Restau Responsable** initiative, dedicated to the continuous improvement of food quality, sustainable sourcing, the fight against waste and well-being in institutional catering.

The 2026 edition devotes a plenary session to a review of 10 years of this initiative, along with conferences on energy efficiency and the fight against food waste (source: salonrestauco.com).

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
  { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
  { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors", "align": "right" },
  { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
  { "edition": "2026 (11th, upcoming)", "visiteurs": "n/a", "exposants": "n/a" },
  { "edition": "2025 (10th)", "visiteurs": "2,200", "exposants": "167" }
 ]}
 sources="salonrestauco.com, restauration-collective.com"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

Restau''Co has opted for a compact format: a one-day trade show, targeted at decision-makers in direct management, rather than a large generalist gathering. The 2025 edition drew 2,200 qualified visitors around 167 exhibitors, with a high proportion of buyers (35%) that reflects the quality of the audience. The 2026 edition, the 11th, coincides with the network''s 60th anniversary and makes food sovereignty, home cooking and the 10-year review of Mon Restau Responsable its guiding themes. The 2026 attendance figures will be published after the trade show.', seo_title_en='Restau''Co 2026: state-run institutional catering trade show, Paris', seo_description_en='Restau''Co 2026, 11th edition on 17 June at Paris Expo Porte de Versailles. The trade show for state-run institutional catering: exhibitors, conferences on food sovereignty and home cooking, Innovation Awards.' WHERE slug='restau-co-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='A reference trade show for natural stone in France, created in 2017 by GL Events Exhibitions and co-organised with the journal Pierre Actual. Co-located with Paysalia at Eurexpo Lyon. Covers the entire chain: extraction, processing, installation and fit-out for building and public spaces.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- Rocalia is France''s reference trade show for natural stone, created in 2017 by **GL Events Exhibitions** and co-organised with the specialist journal **Pierre Actual**. It covers the entire stone chain: extraction, processing, installation and fit-out for building and public spaces.
- Next edition: from 30 November to 2 December 2027 at [Eurexpo Lyon](/lieux/eurexpo-lyon), co-located with the landscaping trade show [Paysalia](/salons/paysalia-lyon) (same dates, same site).
- A biennial trade show held in odd years (2017, 2019, 2021, 2023, 2025, 2027).
- Most recent 2025 edition: more than 41,500 professional visitors combined across the two trade shows (combined Paysalia + Rocalia figures, not broken down by show).
- A dedicated and detailed sustainability commitments page has been published: [our CSR commitments](https://www.rocalia-events.com/fr).

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Quarry operators and natural stone producers, marble workers, stonemasons, processors, traders and distributors, manufacturers of stone-cutting machinery and tooling, as well as specifiers: architects, designers, landscapers and local authorities. The visitor base is strictly professional and spans building, outdoor fit-out and public spaces.

Co-location with [Paysalia](/salons/paysalia-lyon) broadens the visitor base to include landscaping and urban development professionals, direct targets for natural stone solutions.

### Notable exhibitors

The exhibitor list is available on the official website: [rocalia-events.com](https://www.rocalia-events.com/fr/liste-exposants).

### Visitor quality

- Qualified B2B visitor base: professionals in stone, building, outdoor fit-out, and specifiers (architects, landscapers, local authorities).
- International reach: Switzerland, Italy and Belgium are among the most represented visitor nationalities at recent editions (source: environnement-magazine.fr, 2023 data).

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

**The Rocalia Forum** offers conferences, round tables and expert interviews on the sector''s challenges, designed with editorial partner Pierre Actual. Recent editions have covered local stone sourcing, sustainable construction, innovation and training in stone-working trades. The 2027 programme will be published as the trade show approaches.

### Awards and prizes

The **Construire en Pierre Naturelle au XXIème siècle** competition takes place at the trade show. It was in its 8th edition in 2025, co-organised by the **SNROC** (Syndicat National des Industries de Roches Ornementales et de Construction) and the journal **Pierre Actual**, to recognise the most inspiring architectural and construction projects in natural stone (source: rocalia-events.com).

The **WorldSkills Arena** showcases young medallists from national stone-cutting and marble-working competitions, in preparation for the EuroSkills selections.

### Community and networks

The trade show draws on the journal **Pierre Actual** as editorial partner and on the **SNROC**, the sector''s trade union. Live demonstrations bring together the **Compagnons du Devoir** and the **FFPPS** (Fédération Française des Professionnels de la Pierre Sèche), including a biodiversity wall presented in Gallery 6 in coordination with Paysalia.

The **Innovation Booster** (Gallery 6) highlights exhibitors'' new products and services, and the **Espace Métiers et Compétences** is dedicated to discovering the trades and to professional development across the stone sector.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Eurexpo Lyon](/lieux/eurexpo-lyon) listing.

Specific to the trade show:

- **Visitor badge:** online registration on the official website, reserved for professionals.
- **Co-location:** a single pass grants entry to both Rocalia and Paysalia, on the same Eurexpo site and on the same dates.
- **Access:** visitor entrance on Boulevard de l''Europe in Chassieu, TCL public transport (routes on tcl.fr), 13,000 parking spaces spread across the Eurexpo entrances.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: make contact with GL Events Exhibitions, choose positioning and floor space.
- 9 months out: apply to the Construire en Pierre Naturelle au XXIème siècle competition (submit before the deadline).
- 6 months out: prepare the stand plan and demonstrations, brief on activating the Paysalia co-location.
- 3 months out: showcase new products via the Innovation Booster (Gallery 6).
- 6 weeks out: confirm specifier meetings and brief the sales team.

### For the decision-making visitor

- Pre-register online at rocalia-events.com with proof of professional activity.
- Allow 1 to 2 days depending on the scope covered (stone, landscaping, fit-out).
- Identify in advance the Rocalia Forum conferences and the trade demonstrations.
- Make the most of the co-location with Paysalia to cover both stone and landscaping in a single visit.

## CSR policy & initiatives

Rocalia details its sustainability commitments around three pillars: reducing its carbon footprint, limiting consumables, and developing diversity and local regions.

Concrete published commitments: waste sorting during set-up, operation and dismantling with recovery (partnership with **Valdelia** for wood, furniture and fittings), printed materials on recycled paper and paperless visitor badges, roll-ups collected by **Ecopack Solutions** for reuse, a green electricity supply contract and LED lighting, 24 charging points for electric vehicles in the car parks, plastic-free catering with compostable containers and recovery of unsold food by **Le Chaînon Manquant**.

The trade show supports the **Planting for the Future** reforestation fund (352 reforestation projects in France). No ISO 20121 certification is mentioned to date (source: rocalia-events.com).

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
 { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
 { "key": "dates", "label": "Dates" },
 { "key": "lieu", "label": "Venue" }
 ]}
 rows={[
 { "edition": "2027 (upcoming)", "dates": "30 Nov - 2 Dec", "lieu": "Eurexpo Lyon" },
 { "edition": "2025", "dates": "2 - 4 Dec", "lieu": "Eurexpo Lyon" },
 { "edition": "2023", "dates": "5 - 7 Dec", "lieu": "Eurexpo Lyon" },
 { "edition": "2021", "dates": "December", "lieu": "Eurexpo Lyon" },
 { "edition": "2019", "dates": "3 - 5 Dec", "lieu": "Eurexpo Lyon" },
 { "edition": "2017 (1st)", "dates": "5 - 7 Dec", "lieu": "Eurexpo Lyon" }
 ]}
 sources="rocalia-events.com, gl-events.com, batiactu.com"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

Launched in 2017 by GL Events Exhibitions as the first French trade show dedicated to natural stone, Rocalia has established a lasting place in the sector''s calendar by aligning itself with the landscaping trade show Paysalia, with which it shares dates and the Eurexpo Lyon site. This co-location, which brought together more than 41,500 combined visitors in 2025 (combined Paysalia + Rocalia figures), gives the trade show an audience that its sector alone would not reach. The 2027 edition, from 30 November to 2 December, extends this biennial odd-year positioning by drawing on the editorial partnership with Pierre Actual and on the SNROC to mobilise the entire stone ecosystem.', seo_title_en='Rocalia 2027: natural stone trade show in Lyon Eurexpo', seo_description_en='Rocalia 2027, the natural stone trade show from 30 November to 2 December at Eurexpo Lyon, co-located with Paysalia. Exhibitors, activities, competition and practical information.' WHERE slug='rocalia-lyon';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Regional agricultural trade show in Nouvelle-Aquitaine. Livestock, crops, regional produce and farming innovations.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- **22 to 28 May 2026** (7 days, Friday to Thursday), integrated into the **Foire Internationale de Bordeaux** (10 days, 22-31 May), at the Parc des Expositions de Bordeaux Lac.
- The largest consumer-facing agricultural trade show in Nouvelle-Aquitaine, a showcase for the South-West''s livestock, wine-growing and gastronomy sectors, under the slogan **"Le rendez-vous made in Sud-Ouest"**.
- New 2026 layout across **two halls**: **Hall 1** (Aquitanima, gastronomy and wines, producers'' market, careers of the future) and **Hall 4** (Équitaine, ornamental poultry, Conservatoire des races régionales).
- **800 farm animals** over the 7 days: around 300 cattle (Aquitanima), 300 horses (Équitaine), 100 sheep (Journées Ovines), 100 dairy cows in the ring.
- Organiser **BEAM (Bordeaux Events And More)**, rue Jean Samazeuilh CS 20088, 33070 Bordeaux cedex. Principal partner: **Chambre régionale d''agriculture Nouvelle-Aquitaine**, with support from Région Nouvelle-Aquitaine.
- A consumer-facing B2C trade show attached to the Foire Internationale: **4.50 euros pre-sale, 8 euros full price on the door**, free entry extended to under-18s, students and people with reduced mobility.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

A mixed agricultural and consumer trade show, structured around the South-West''s flagship sectors. Exhibitors break down between:

- **Breeders**: beef and dairy cattle (Limousine, Blonde d''Aquitaine, Bazadaise, Charolaise, Prim''Holstein, Jersiaise), sheep, equine, ornamental poultry, low-population breeds from the regional Conservatoire.
- **Food producers and artisans**: wine-growers, regional gastronomy, producers'' market.
- **Training and employment sector players**: DRAAF Nouvelle-Aquitaine, Chambre régionale d''agriculture, ANEFA, REANA, OCAPIAT, FRCUMA, APECITA, France Travail, Agri Cap Conduite, UNEP.
- **Agricultural colleges** from Nouvelle-Aquitaine, mobilised around the Trophée Aquitanima Junior.
- **Solutions for the future**: exhibitors positioned around innovation, the agroecological transition and emerging professions.

### Confirmed zones for 2026

The 2026 edition is organised around the following zones, spread across Halls 1 and 4:

- **Aquitanima** (Hall 1): cattle competitions, around **300 cattle** and more than **100 dairy cows in the ring**. Breeds: Limousine, Blonde d''Aquitaine, Bazadaise, Charolaise, Prim''Holstein, Jersiaise.
- **Équitaine** (Hall 4): equestrian events, around **300 horses**, a showcase for the regional equine sector.
- **Journées Ovines**: around **100 sheep**, shearing and sheepdog demonstrations.
- **Producers'' market** (Hall 1): direct sales, regional produce from Nouvelle-Aquitaine.
- **Gastronomy zone and wine counter** (Hall 1): tastings, culinary activities, regional AOP and IGP labels.
- **Conservatoire des races régionales** and **ornamental poultry** (Hall 4).

### Quality of the visitor base

- A mixed audience of the general public and sector professionals, with a strong family component.
- The **Foire Internationale de Bordeaux** reports **170,000 visitors** over its 10 days; the share specific to the Salon de l''Agriculture is not disclosed separately.
- A visitor base dominated by Nouvelle-Aquitaine (Gironde, Dordogne, Landes, Lot-et-Garonne, Pyrénées-Atlantiques), with a presence of breeders from across the southern half of France drawn by Aquitanima.

## Activities and community

### Aquitanima, the flagship activity

Aquitanima is the pillar of the trade show: cattle breed competitions, ring presentations and official judging across the 7 days. Around 300 cattle, more than 100 sheep and more than 100 dairy cows in the ring make it one of the largest cattle gatherings in the greater South-West, where breeders come to have their bloodlines recognised and to exchange views on genetics.

### Trophée Aquitanima Junior

From **22 to 24 May 2026**, the **Trophée Aquitanima Junior** mobilises **5 agricultural colleges from Nouvelle-Aquitaine** (digital activities and ring presentations). Teams compete on breed knowledge, animal handling and presentation in competition: a showcase for the region''s next generation of farmers.

### Équitaine, Journées Ovines and demonstrations

Équitaine occupies Hall 4 with around 300 horses and equestrian events across the 7 days (breeding, sport, leisure, equine therapy). The **Journées Ovines** (around 100 sheep) are complemented by **shearing demonstrations** and **sheepdog displays**. The **Conservatoire des races régionales** provides outreach around low-population breeds.

### Employment and training sector

The careers-of-the-future zone, run by DRAAF Nouvelle-Aquitaine and the Chambre régionale d''agriculture, brings together Région Nouvelle-Aquitaine, ANEFA, REANA, OCAPIAT, FRCUMA, APECITA, France Travail, Agri Cap Conduite and UNEP, to provide information on careers and to make employer sourcing easier.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the listing for the [Parc des Expositions de Bordeaux](/lieux/parc-des-expositions-de-bordeaux).

Specific to the trade show:

- **Halls used**: Hall 1 (Aquitanima, gastronomy and wines, producers'' market, careers of the future) and Hall 4 (Équitaine, ornamental poultry, Conservatoire des races).
- **Opening hours**: 10 am to 7 pm every day.
- **Format**: 7 days, Friday 22 to Thursday 28 May 2026, integrated into the 10 days of the Foire Internationale de Bordeaux (22-31 May).
- **Single ticket**: one ticket gives access to both the Foire Internationale and the Salon de l''Agriculture, at no extra cost.
- **Access**: tram line C, Parc des Expositions stop, shuttles from the city centre.

## Visitor prices for 2026 (Foire de Bordeaux ticketing)

A pricing scale shared with the Foire Internationale de Bordeaux:

- **Adult pre-sale ticket**: 4.50 euros
- **Adult full-price ticket on the door**: 8 euros
- **Reduced rate**: 3 euros (works councils, groups of 10 people or more)
- **Free**: under-18s, students, people with reduced mobility and their companion
- **Friday 22 May (opening day)**: free for all
- **Tuesday 26 and Thursday 28 May**: free for those aged 60 and over
- **Sunday 31 May**: free for women (a Foire de Bordeaux day outside the Salon de l''Agriculture window, which closes on the 28th)

Buying in pre-sale saves 3.50 euros per ticket versus full price on the door.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates are not published. Quotes are available on request from BEAM (Bordeaux Events And More), rue Jean Samazeuilh CS 20088, 33070 Bordeaux cedex. Positioning in Hall 1 or Hall 4 and the type of exhibitor (breeder, producer, sector service provider, training-and-employment player) influence the rate per square metre. Specific arrangements exist for breeders taking part in the Aquitanima, Équitaine and Journées Ovines competitions, managed in coordination with the Chambre régionale d''agriculture Nouvelle-Aquitaine (animal access, sanitary logistics, livestock housing).

No specific figures are publicly available at this date.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: make contact with BEAM, choose the hall and the zone.
- 9 months out: for competition breeders, register with the Chambre régionale d''agriculture, complete sanitary paperwork and declare the animals.
- 6 months out: finalise the stand, organise animal logistics, brief the sales and public-reception team.
- 3 months out: regional communications campaign, sector network activation, local agricultural press partnerships.
- 6 weeks out: pre-arranged meetings with breeders and buyers (genetic selection), specialist press briefing.

### For the visitor

- **Buy your ticket in pre-sale**: save 3.50 euros per ticket versus on the door.
- **Choose your day**: Friday 22 May (free) for the opening, Tuesday 26 or Thursday 28 May for those aged 60 and over (free).
- **Family visitors**: allow 4 to 5 hours to cover Halls 1 and 4, see the cattle and equine competitions and have lunch at the producers'' market.
- **Breeder or sector professional visitors**: aim for the major Aquitanima competition days in the middle of the show.
- **Access**: tram line C, on-site car parks, occasional shuttles from central Bordeaux.

## CSR policy and initiatives

The trade show is part of the CSR approach of the Foire Internationale de Bordeaux, operated by BEAM, around two structuring markers:

- **ISO 20121 certification**: a responsible management system applied to events (sourcing, waste, energy, transport, stakeholders).
- **Foires de France label**: recognition of the show''s regional economic character and its compliance with the commitments of the trade fairs federation.

The agricultural dimension draws on the principal partnership with the **Chambre régionale d''agriculture Nouvelle-Aquitaine** and the **Région Nouvelle-Aquitaine**: promoting local sectors, highlighting low-population breeds via the Conservatoire, raising awareness of the agroecological transition in the Solutions for the future zone, and promoting careers via the DRAAF.

No public figures (waste recovery rate, energy mix, mobility plan) are available specifically for the Salon de l''Agriculture on its own, as distinct from the Foire Internationale.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
 { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
 { "key": "dates", "label": "Dates" },
 { "key": "format", "label": "Format" },
 { "key": "particularite", "label": "Highlight" }
 ]}
 rows={[
