/>

### Trajectory analysis

A long-standing fixture of springtime in Colmar, the Salon Énergie Habitat is held each March at the exhibition centre and has established itself as the regional benchmark for home and habitat in the Grand Est. Over successive editions, its centre of gravity has shifted towards energy-efficient renovation and support for private individuals: a rising emphasis on low-carbon solutions (heat pumps, photovoltaics, geothermal) and a stronger presence of public renovation-support bodies. The 2026 edition confirms this direction with a conference programme entirely focused on grants, financing and home adaptation.', seo_title_en='Salon Énergie Habitat Colmar 2026: dates, exhibitors, practical info', seo_description_en='The Salon Énergie Habitat 2026 runs from 20 to 23 March at the Parc Expo de Colmar. 45th edition, nearly 180 exhibitors in renovation, energy and habitat across the Grand Est.' WHERE slug='salon-energie-habitat-colmar';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='40th edition at Paris Expo Porte de Versailles, Pavilions 5.2 and 5.3, a historic post-baccalaureate guidance trade show created in 1986, the first Parisian event of the year for sixth-form pupils and students in the Île-de-France region. Expected turnout: 35,000 to 45,000 visitors over 3 days.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- **40th edition** of the Salon de l''Étudiant Île-de-France: **30 January, 31 January and 1 February 2026** at **Paris Expo Porte de Versailles, Pavilions 5.2 and 5.3**.
- A historic post-baccalaureate guidance trade show created in 1986, the first Parisian event of the year for sixth-form pupils and students in the Île-de-France region, right in the middle of the Parcoursup phase.
- Organised by **L''Étudiant** (a media group founded in 1972, owned by NewsCo / Altice Media since 2015), the leading French operator of guidance trade shows with nearly 120 shows a year across the regions.
- Online exhibitor platform **from 15 January 2026** to help visitors prepare and identify target schools ahead of the physical event.
- Expected turnout: between **35,000 and 45,000 visitors** over 3 days, with several hundred exhibitors covering the full breadth of Île-de-France post-baccalaureate provision (universities, engineering schools, business schools, CPGE preparatory classes, IUT/BUT, BTS, specialist schools).

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

The trade show covers the full range of higher education pathways accessible from the Île-de-France region:

- **Engineering schools**, both public and private, post-baccalaureate and post-preparatory class.
- **Business and management schools** (Grandes Écoles, Bachelor''s programmes).
- **CPGE preparatory classes** in science, economics and humanities.
- **Île-de-France universities**: Université Paris Cité, Sorbonne Université, CY Cergy Paris Université and other institutions in the academic region.
- **IUT and BUT programmes**.
- **BTS and specialist schools** in the arts, communication, design, digital, health, social work, tourism and sport.
- **Éducation nationale area** dedicated to public post-baccalaureate programmes.
- Institutional stands: **ONISEP** and **Oriane Île-de-France**, the regional guidance portal.

### Notable exhibitors

The full list of 2026 exhibitors is published on the official platform from **15 January 2026**, two weeks before the trade show, so that visitors can plan their itinerary.

### Quality of the attendees

- Main audience: **Year 12 and Year 13 sixth-form pupils** in the process of formulating their Parcoursup choices, accompanied by their families.
- Secondary audience: **students changing course** (end of first university year, dropping out of BTS, leaving a preparatory class) and students looking for a Master''s or further study.
- Origin: **Île-de-France academic region** (Paris, Versailles, Créteil), grouped together by statute since 2020, plus visitors from neighbouring departments.
- High decision-making density: almost all visitors are actively deciding on their higher education choices.

## Activities & community

### Talks hosted by the L''Étudiant editorial team

A free programme of plenary talks hosted by the specialist journalists of the L''Étudiant editorial team. Recurring themes:

- **Parcoursup**: timeline, strategy for choices, personal statement.
- **Preparatory class vs post-baccalaureate school**: selection criteria, comparative career prospects.
- **Apprenticeships**: finding a company, apprenticeship contract, statuses.
- **Student life**: housing, grants, student health.
- **Financing** studies and **international mobility**.

### franceinfo workshops

A structuring editorial partnership with **franceinfo radio**: guidance workshops, coaching sessions and radio sets recorded live from the trade show.

### Institutional partnerships

- **Île-de-France academic region** (Paris, Versailles, Créteil): official reception for headteachers and guidance counsellors.
- **Île-de-France region** through the **Oriane** scheme, the regional guidance portal.
- **ARIUT** (Assemblée des Responsables d''IUT) for the BUT pathway.
- **ORACCLE** (Île-de-France youth information network).
- **APHEC** (Association des Professeurs des classes préparatoires HEC) for the economics CPGE.

### Dedicated Salon des Grandes Écoles

A complementary strand of L''Étudiant''s Paris offering: a specific **Salon des Grandes Écoles** for engineering and business schools is held in October at Porte de Versailles, in partnership with the **CDEFI** (Conférence des Directeurs des Écoles Françaises d''Ingénieurs).

### Cap sur la Rentrée

L''Étudiant''s second Parisian event: **Cap sur la Rentrée** at the Espace Champerret, scheduled for **20 June 2026**, dedicated to the Parcoursup supplementary phase and course changes.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the listing for [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles).

Specific to the trade show:

- **Pavilion location**: Pavilions **5.2 and 5.3**.
- **2026 edition opening hours**:
  - Friday 30 January: 10am to 5pm.
  - Saturday 31 January: 10am to 6pm.
  - Sunday 1 February: 10am to 6pm.
- **Visitor badge**: **free entry with named online registration** (one email per registrant).
- **Group registrations** (classes, schools): promosalons@letudiant.fr.
- **Visit preparation**: online exhibitor platform open from **15 January 2026**.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates are not published: quotes on direct request from L''Étudiant Événementiel. The pricing structure depends on the format (bare stand, equipped stand, package), the floor area and the category of institution (private school, public university, institutional body).

Quote requests should be anticipated **9 to 12 months before the trade show** for strategic locations (entrance, main aisles, proximity to the Éducation nationale area). Public universities and institutional bodies (ONISEP, academic region) benefit from dedicated spaces and specific terms.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 9 months out: request a quote, choose positioning (proximity to pathway, institutional or commercial area).
- 6 months out: order the stand from a builder, sign off on communication materials.
- 3 months out: brief the team, prepare Parcoursup decision-support materials (brochures, online demos, simulators).
- 6 weeks out: activate digital campaigns, book appointments via the official platform opening on 15 January.
- 2 weeks out: finalise the welcome teams (include student ambassadors and teachers available to answer questions).

### For the sixth-form or student visitor

- **Mandatory online registration** (one email per registrant, free).
- Check the exhibitor platform from **15 January 2026** to identify target schools and prepare a list of priority stands.
- Allow **at least half a day** for a focused itinerary, a full day to explore several pathways.
- Friday is the preferred slot for **supervised class visits**; Saturday and Sunday concentrate the families.
- Combine the visit with a L''Étudiant editorial talk session to shape your Parcoursup strategy before submitting choices.

## Policy & CSR initiatives

The Salon de l''Étudiant embraces a **public-service editorial mission**: fully free entry, structural partnerships with the Éducation nationale (**ONISEP**, **academic regions**) and with the regions, and targeted welcome of vulnerable audiences through the **Cordées de la réussite** scheme and means-tested grants.

The stated aim is to guarantee equal access to guidance information, whatever the social or geographical situation of pupils and their families. This "fully free + exhaustive coverage of public and private provision" positioning has historically been the hallmark of the L''Étudiant group and sets it apart from commercial trade shows targeting one school at a time.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
 { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
 { "key": "dates", "label": "Dates" },
 { "key": "lieu", "label": "Pavilions" },
 { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
 { "edition": "2026 (40th, upcoming)", "dates": "30 Jan - 1 Feb", "lieu": "5.2 and 5.3", "visiteurs": "35,000 to 45,000 estimated" },
