  { "edition": "2023", "lieu": "Poussay (88)", "visiteurs": "25,223", "exposants": "n/a" }
 ]}
 sources="salonherbe.com"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

Created in 1999 under the name Salon de l''Herbe, then broadened in 2015 to include forage (hence the current name), the trade show relies on a singular and hard-to-replicate format: open-field demonstration, over more than 30 hectares, paired with a forage showcase of more than 2 hectares. Its rotation between three livestock-farming regions (Centre, East, West) lets it capture a regional visitor base that renews from one year to the next, which explains the varying attendance depending on the site: around 25,000 visitors at Poussay (East), more than 31,000 at Villefranche-d''Allier (Centre) in 2025. The 2027 edition marks the return to the West, at Nouvoitou (35), where the previous edition (2024) drew 24,874 visitors.', seo_title_en='Salon de l''Herbe et des Fourrages 2027: Nouvoitou, dates and info', seo_description_en='Salon de l''Herbe et des Fourrages 2027 on 26-27 May at Nouvoitou (35). Open-field trade show for livestock farming and forage crops: equipment demonstrations, forage showcase. Organised by Profield Events.' WHERE slug='salon-herbe';
UPDATE salons SET description_en=NULL, editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- "A trade show dedicated to the nursing profession, for training, information and networking": Salon Infirmier brings together nurses, healthcare managers and students around continuing education, employment and care equipment.
- Organiser: Quinze Mai (a health-event designer, ISO 20121 certified by AFAQ), the show''s long-standing publisher since its creation.
- Upcoming edition: 40th edition in 2027 (2027 dates not yet announced), at [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles), Hall 7.1. The 39th edition took place on 25 and 26 March 2026.
- Audience announced by the organiser: more than 9,000 visitors and 130 exhibitors, with around 100 speaking slots and 200 speakers (figures as communicated, not OJS-audited).
- Integrated format: Salon Infirmier runs alongside People 4 Health (caregiver health) and Tech 4 Health (health technologies), both operated by the same organiser.
- Free admission for healthcare professionals and students upon registration: positioned around training, employment and professional debate rather than as a purely commercial trade show.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile
Salon Infirmier brings together exhibitors focused on the nursing profession: continuing education bodies (DPC, advanced practice), schools and IFSI nursing institutes, employers from the healthcare and medico-social sectors (hospitals, clinics, care homes, medical temping, healthcare recruitment agencies), manufacturers and distributors of medical devices and care equipment, software publishers and e-health solution providers, professional institutions and councils, as well as specialist press and media.

### Notable exhibitors
Among the exhibitors present at recent editions: AP-HP, Croix-Rouge française, Institut Curie, Gustave Roussy, MNH, Établissement français du sang, Ordre national des infirmiers, FNESI, ANCIM, Elsevier Masson, Asalée, Groupe Hospitalier Diaconesses Croix Saint-Simon and NéphroCare. The full list is published on the show''s official page: [Salon Infirmier exhibitors](https://www.saloninfirmier.fr/). Most exhibitors fall into training, healthcare employment, medical devices and e-health.

### Visitor quality
The organiser announces more than 9,000 visitors (figure as communicated, not OJS-audited). The attendees are overwhelmingly state-registered nurses, advanced practice nurses, healthcare managers and senior managers, nursing students and medico-social professionals. This is an audience that makes decisions on training and strongly influences the choice of care equipment within their establishments.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme
The programme is shaped by a scientific committee and combines plenary conferences, round tables and practical workshops. The organiser announces in the region of 100 speaking slots and around 200 speakers per edition. For the 39th edition (2026), the scientific committee notably included Alain Desbouchages (president of the Ordre National des Infirmiers), Peggy Alonso (president of the ANPDE), Katie Galois (advanced practice nurse, treasurer of the ANFIPA) and Julien Grizzetti (self-employed nurse, co-president of a CPTS in Meudon).

Each edition adopts a thematic thread: the 39th edition (2026) focused on "L''heure de la consultation infirmière : une réalité, plusieurs terrains d''exercice", tied to the legal recognition of the nursing consultation; the 38th edition (2025) had the theme "Infirmières et infirmiers citoyens : de l''engagement personnel au leadership inspirant ?". The detailed programme for the 2027 edition and its theme will be published on the official website.

### Prix Recherche Sciences Infirmières - Monique Rothan-Tondeur
Salon Infirmier awards the Prix Recherche Sciences Infirmières - Monique Rothan-Tondeur, in partnership with the Chaire de Recherche en Sciences Infirmières at Sorbonne-Paris Nord University. This award recognises the profession''s clinical and academic research: three nurses are honoured for their contribution to advancing knowledge. The 3rd edition was awarded during the show''s 39th edition (2026), with the call for applications closing on 28 January 2026. Media partner: infirmiers.com.

### Community & networks
The show draws on the organiser''s health editorial ecosystem and its media partners (professional nursing press, healthcare news sites, including infirmiers.com and EMPLOISoignant). An off-site day, with visits to innovative health sites, extends the two days of the exhibition (27 March for the 2026 edition, 28 March for the 2025 edition).

## On site: what you need to know

For access, parking and accommodation, see the venue page: [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles). The show is held in Hall 7.1. Admission is free for healthcare professionals and students, subject to prior registration and badge collection. Salon Infirmier shares the space with People 4 Health and Tech 4 Health: a single badge grants access to all of them. As the 2027 dates have not yet been published, check the official calendar before planning your visit.

## How much it costs to exhibit

The stand pricing schedule is not published online by the organiser: the cost of exhibiting is built on a quotation from Quinze Mai, depending on the surface area, location and visibility options.

<BudgetTable warning="Indicative sector ranges (2-day B2B trade show, Paris Expo Porte de Versailles). No public schedule is issued by the organiser: request a quotation from Quinze Mai." rows={[{ "poste": "Bare floor space rental (12 to 30 m2)", "low": "6 000 €", "high": "18 000 €" }, { "poste": "Stand fit-out & furniture", "low": "4 000 €", "high": "15 000 €" }, { "poste": "Staff, expenses, transport", "low": "3 000 €", "high": "9 000 €" }, { "poste": "Communication & collateral", "low": "2 000 €", "high": "8 000 €" }]} totals={[{ "label": "ESTIMATED TOTAL (indicative)", "low": "~15 000 €", "high": "~50 000 €" }]} footer="Agoris estimates for indicative purposes only, excluding sponsoring and speaking options. Actual rates to be confirmed with the organiser." />

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor
- 9 to 6 months before: commercial contact with Quinze Mai, choice of surface area and location (proximity to the plenary sessions and the employment area).
- 5 to 3 months before: stand booking, finalising the communication plan (programme presence, networks, visitor database), defining the training/employment offers to highlight.
- 2 months before: recruiting and briefing the team, preparing collateral, setting up qualified appointments.
- 1 month before: build-up logistics, deliveries, validation of exhibitor badges.

### For the decision-making visitor
Target in advance the conferences and workshops on the year''s thematic thread, identify the training bodies and employers present in the employment area, and take advantage of the free admission to send several members of the same care team. The off-site day deserves a separate registration if it is held again.

## Policy & CSR initiatives

The organiser Quinze Mai is ISO 20121 certified by AFAQ, the international standard for responsible event management. Salon Infirmier has its own Commitments page detailing the concrete measures in place: selective sorting and recovery of waste via specialist partners, reduced aisle carpeting, removal of paper printing (catalogues and plans replaced by a digital kiosk and on-screen signage), badges made from recycled plastic, promotion of public transport and accessibility for people with reduced mobility. The carbon impact is measured via CLEO, a tool dedicated to event carbon footprinting. Exhibitors are guided towards eco-designed and reusable stands, prioritised digital communication and limited giveaways. The host venue, Paris Expo Porte de Versailles, is operated by Viparis, itself ISO 20121 certified. Quinze Mai builds its approach around four pillars: Vigilance, Inclusion, Integrity, Transparency.

## How the show has evolved

<HistoryTable columns={[{ "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" }, { "key": "dates", "label": "Dates" }, { "key": "lieu", "label": "Venue" }, { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors", "align": "right" }, { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }]} rows={[{ "edition": "40th (2027, upcoming)", "dates": "Dates not announced", "lieu": "Paris Expo Porte de Versailles", "visiteurs": "n/a", "exposants": "n/a" }, { "edition": "39th (2026)", "dates": "25-26 March 2026", "lieu": "Paris Expo Porte de Versailles (Hall 7.1)", "visiteurs": "9,000+ (announced)", "exposants": "130+" }, { "edition": "38th (2025)", "dates": "26-28 March 2025", "lieu": "Paris Expo Porte de Versailles (Hall 7.1)", "visiteurs": "n/a", "exposants": "n/a" }]} sources="Official site saloninfirmier.fr, Quinze Mai (15-mai.com), Infirmiers.com." />

### Trajectory analysis
Salon Infirmier is one of the longest-standing events for the nursing profession in France: its 39th edition in 2026 confirms a longevity of nearly four decades, with the 40th edition in 2027 marking a major anniversary. The show has evolved towards a format structured around continuing education, employment and professional debate, now articulated with People 4 Health and Tech 4 Health under the umbrella of Quinze Mai. Recent editions anchor their programming to the profession''s regulatory developments (recognition of the nursing consultation, the rise of advanced practice), positioning the show as a sounding board for changes in the framework of nursing practice.', seo_title_en='Salon Infirmier 2027 Paris: dates, exhibitors, programme | Agoris', seo_description_en='Salon Infirmier 2027 (40th edition), Paris Expo Porte de Versailles: 9,000+ visitors, 130+ exhibitors, nursing training and employment. Organised by Quinze Mai. Free admission.' WHERE slug='salon-infirmier-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='A major tourism trade show open to the public and to industry professionals. Destinations, tour operators, cruises and responsible tourism.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- 49th edition of the Salon Mondial du Tourisme from **12 to 15 March 2026** at Paris Expo Porte de Versailles, **Pavilion 4**.
- **73,330 visitors** at the 2026 edition (official TourMag figure), stable compared with 2025, with **more than 200 exhibitors** and around **400 destinations** represented. 25% first-time participants.
- Organised by **Comexposium**, the leading French trade show organiser (created through the 2008 merger of Comexpo CCI Paris IDF and Exposium Unibail-Rodamco), owned by SIPAC (CCIR Paris IDF) and Crédit Agricole Assurances.
- Trade show led by **Christel Marzullo**, director of Comexposium''s Tourism Shows (Tourissima Lille, Mahana Lyon, Salon Mondial du Tourisme Paris, Destinations Nature), with 20 years in the group.
- France''s leading consumer tourism trade show, created in 1978 by the ASMT (Association du Salon Mondial du Tourisme), itself founded in 1973 by Adonet, the FFTST and Les Entreprises du Voyage.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

The trade show covers the entire consumer tourism value chain: French and foreign tourist offices, tour operators, cruise lines, airlines, eco-mobility players, accommodation providers, booking platforms and specialist media. More than 400 destinations are represented across the four days.

### Notable exhibitors

**French tourist offices**: Cœur du Var, Vichy Destination, Pays de Montbéliard, Bretagne, Région Sud, Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes.

**Foreign tourist offices**: Cuba, Sao Tomé, Turismo Burgos (Spain), cities of Asturias (Avilés, Gijón, Oviedo).

**Tour operators**: New Tour Voyage, Wisdom Travel / Erten Turizm.

**Cruise lines**: CroisiEurope, MSC Cruises and **Travel HX**, a new entrant in the expedition cruise segment (Antarctica, Arctic, Greenland, Galápagos).

**Rail eco-mobility**: Discovery Trains, Voyager en train, in line with the peak of interest among Île-de-France residents in low-carbon tourism (33% interested in rail travel, Comexposium 2026 statistics).

### Quality of the visitor base

A consumer visitor base with strong purchasing power, dominated by Île-de-France residents. Comexposium 2026 statistics:

- **97%** say they intend to travel in 2026
- **59%** of Parisians go abroad at least **twice a year** (trips of 3 days or more)
- **71%** take at least **2 weekend breaks per year in France**
- **33%** say they are interested in rail travel
- **21%** come to the trade show to discover new destinations
- Average travel budget of more than **€2,000 per person**

Top destinations sought in 2026: Europe, Asia, Africa. Within France: Bretagne, Région Sud, Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes.

## Activities and community

### World Dance and Music Festival

New for 2026: a festival dedicated to world dance and music, with **more than 20 troupes** programmed across the four days. An unprecedented format designed to give the trade show an experiential dimension, beyond the strictly exhibitor / visitor encounter.

### Content creators and travel influencers

Around **20 content creators** and specialist travel influencers take part in the 2026 edition (talks, signings, workshops). An audience lever for destinations looking to reach the 25-45 age group outside traditional media.

### Exhibitions and workshops

Three major exhibitions in 2026: travel photography, indigenous peoples, film destinations. A programme of **Asian cultural workshops** calibrated to the peak of interest among Île-de-France residents in Asia (28% of visitors are considering an Asian destination).

### Professional conferences

A day dedicated to tourism industry professionals on the Thursday, opening the trade show. Conference format covering market trends, CSR challenges and travellers'' new expectations.

### Responsible Tourism Awards

Third edition of the **Trophées Tourisme Responsable** (Responsible Tourism Awards) at the trade show. They reward tourism players who structure a measurable sustainable approach. Of note: the **Trophées Horizons ATD** (Acteurs du Tourisme Durable) are awarded off-site, in June, at the Auberge Yves Robert in the 18th arrondissement of Paris.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles) listing.

Specific to the trade show:

- **Pavilion location**: Pavilion 4.
- **2026 edition opening hours**: Thursday 12, Friday 13 and Saturday 14 March from **10 am to 7 pm continuously**, Sunday 15 March from **10 am to 6 pm**.
- **Visitor pricing**: **€10 online pre-sale, €12 on site**, free for under-18s. Free invitations available for download via the trade show''s partners.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor pricing not published to date. Sales contact via the dedicated form on the Comexposium site for the Tourism Shows. Figure not available publicly.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: make contact with the Comexposium Tourism Shows team, choose the setup (standalone stand, stand within a country / region village, co-exhibiting).
- 9 months out: apply for the **Trophées Tourisme Responsable** if relevant to position the destination or brand on the sustainability angle.
- 6 months out: finalise stand content and align with the other trade shows in the Comexposium Tourism network (Mahana Lyon, Tourissima Lille) to pool content.
- 3 months out: brief the sales teams, prepare consumer and content-creator communication kits.
- 1 month out: activate partner channels to distribute free invitations, targeting the upmarket Île-de-France audience.

### For the consumer visitor

- Buy the **€10 e-ticket** in pre-sale rather than €12 on site.
- Thursday: professional opening, a less crowded atmosphere, ideal for personalised appointments with tourist offices.
- Friday and Saturday: peak attendance, World Dance and Music Festival programme at its height.
- Sunday: closing at 6 pm, predominantly a family profile.
- Free entry up to age 18: a format suited to coming as a family.

## CSR policy and initiatives

Several structuring CSR measures at the 2026 edition:

- **Sustainable Initiatives Village** run by mN''Organisation, bringing together responsible tourism players.
- **Tourisme et Handicaps partnership**: roll-out of the label at the trade show, with the **MyEasy Access** app to make access easier for people with disabilities.
- **Partnership with Secours Populaire**: a scheme to fund holidays for children who would otherwise not get away, operated at the trade show.
- Editorial emphasis on low-carbon destinations (rail, soft mobility) and rail eco-mobility (Discovery Trains, Voyager en train) in response to the 33% of Île-de-France residents who say they are interested in rail travel.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
 { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
 { "key": "dates", "label": "Dates" },
 { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors", "align": "right" },
 { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
 { "edition": "49th, 2026", "dates": "12-15 March", "visiteurs": "73,330", "exposants": "200+" },
 { "edition": "48th, 2025", "dates": "March", "visiteurs": "~73,000", "exposants": "200+" }
 ]}
 sources="Comexposium, TourMag"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

The Salon Mondial du Tourisme has established itself over the long term as France''s leading consumer tourism event since 1978. The 2026 attendance (73,330 visitors) remains stable compared with 2025, a sign of a mature format that has found its balance between supply (200+ exhibitors, 400 destinations) and Île-de-France demand (97% intention to travel, average budget above €2,000). Three levers shape its recent evolution:

1. **Broadening of the experiential offer** with the 2026 launch of the World Dance and Music Festival and the growing role of content creators, in order to stay relevant to audiences who increasingly consume travel through social media.
2. **Scaling up of CSR** with the 3rd edition of the Trophées Tourisme Responsable, the Sustainable Initiatives Village, the Tourisme et Handicaps partnership and the Secours Populaire partnership, which anchor the trade show in a measurable sustainable approach.
3. **Integration into a national Comexposium Tourism network** (Mondial Tourisme Paris in March, Mahana Lyon, Tourissima Lille, Destinations Nature), all led by Christel Marzullo, which allows destinations to pool their presence across several visitor catchment areas throughout the year.

The trade show retains a unique positioning: entry-level consumer pricing (€10 in pre-sale, free up to age 18), a strong presence of institutional French and foreign tourist offices, and a four-day format that covers both a professional day (Thursday) and three consumer days including a full weekend.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='salon-mondial-du-tourisme-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Former Salon de la Photo, rebranded in 2026 to embrace video capture and content creation. 2025 figures at the Grande Halle de la Villette: 37,188 visitors, 114 exhibitors representing 167 brands.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- **2026 rebranding**: formerly the Salon de la Photo, the event becomes **Salon Photo & Vidéo**, announced on 27 February 2026 by Comexposium. Its editorial scope broadens to video capture and content creation.
- Dates: from **Thursday 8 to Sunday 11 October 2026** at the [Grande Halle de la Villette](/lieux/grande-halle-villette), open 10am-7pm. Four days for the general public.
- 2025 figures (9-12 October, Villette): **37,188 visitors** (+1% vs 36,823 in 2024), **114 exhibitors** representing **167 brands**, 1,000 sq m of photographic exhibitions. Modest growth confirmed after the move to Villette in 2022.
- Organiser: **Comexposium**. A strictly **B2C, general-public** format, distinct from Paris Photo (Grand Palais, November, a high-end photographic art fair). Admission **€13 full price**, **€7 concession**, free for under-12s, with numerous free invitation codes via the media partners.
- Expected exhibitors: Canon, Nikon, Sony, Fujifilm, Leica, OM System (formerly Olympus), Ricoh, Sigma, Tamron, Epson, Tetenal. The flagship **Les Zooms du Salon** competition (8 photographers selected via 8 media partners), the Public Prize and the Press Prize.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

A strictly B2C trade show: photo/video manufacturers, specialist opticians, accessory makers (tripods, filters, bags), photo printers and labs, retouching software publishers, schools and training providers, online storage and printing platforms, and the photographic press. The **Salon Photo & Vidéo 2026** brand introduces in 2026 a clear focus on video creation (content creators, photo/video hybrids, audio and stabilisation accessories).

### Notable exhibitors (2026)

- **Major manufacturers**: Canon, Nikon, Sony, Fujifilm, Leica, OM System (formerly Olympus), Ricoh.
- **Third-party optics**: Sigma, Tamron.
- **Printing and prints**: Epson, Tetenal.
- **Print partners**: **Picto** (prints offered to the Zooms du Salon winners, a long-standing partner).
- **Media partners** for the Zooms: Polka, Photo, Fisheye, Réponses Photo, Compétence Photo, Like Magazine, Phototrend, De l''air.

### Retention rate

A stable core of exhibitors (major manufacturers, third-party opticians, print firms) present at every edition since the return to Villette in 2022, reinforced each year by rotating accessory and software brands. The shift to 114 exhibitors for 167 brands in 2025 reflects a consolidation through shared stands (a single distributor may represent several brands).

### Quality of the audience

A **strictly B2C** audience: advanced amateur photographers, enthusiasts, content creators, photography and video students, families and curious newcomers. No professional buyer programme. The high density of hands-on equipment trials and the direct closeness to brands are the main engagement levers.

## Activities & community

### Les Zooms du Salon

A flagship competition created in **2010**, now central to the show''s editorial identity. Format: **8 photographers selected** by **8 media partners** (Polka, Photo, Fisheye, Réponses Photo, Compétence Photo, Like Magazine, Phototrend, De l''air). Two prizes awarded on site:

- **Public Prize**: voted live by the show''s visitors.
- **Press Prize**: awarded by a jury made up of the media partners.

The winners receive a print offered by **Picto**. 2025 winners: **Juliette Dupuis Carle** (Public Prize), the **Max & Louna duo** (Press Prize).

### CP+ Yokohama partnership

Since 2010 the show has maintained a partnership with **CP+ (Camera & Photo Imaging Show)** in Yokohama, the benchmark show of the Japanese photographic industry. The winning **Press Zoom** is notably exhibited as part of this exchange.

### Photographic exhibitions

A programme of **1,000 sq m of exhibitions** (2025 figure) built into the show''s floor plan: a projection of the Zooms, themed exhibitions led by the media partners and by the manufacturers (Canon, Nikon and Fujifilm regularly exhibit major photographic projects).

### Outdoor equipment trials

A unique advantage of the Villette site: an equipment trial zone **outdoors in the Parc de la Villette** (55 hectares), allowing real-world shooting (natural light, varied subjects, distances) impossible at the earlier Porte de Versailles editions. A defining selling point adopted by manufacturers since 2022.

### What''s new in 2026

The official **"Salon Photo & Vidéo" rebranding** confirmed on 27 February 2026. An editorial broadening towards video (content creators, hybrids, sound and stabilisation accessories), reflecting the convergence of photo and video in camera bodies and in expert amateur usage.

## On site: what you need to know

- **Venue**: [Grande Halle de la Villette](/lieux/grande-halle-villette), 211 avenue Jean Jaurès, 75019 Paris. Villette has been the show''s home since 2022 (NOT Porte de Versailles since that date).
- **2026 dates**: from Thursday 8 to Sunday 11 October 2026, open 10am-7pm.
- **Getting there**: Metro L5 (Porte de Pantin) a 3-minute walk from the Grande Halle, Metro L7 (Porte de la Villette) 8 minutes away. Tram T3b (Porte de Pantin and Ella Fitzgerald stops). Buses 75, 151. RER E (Pantin) 15 minutes away.
- **Parking**: Indigo Cité de la Musique car park beneath the park. Heavy congestion expected at the weekend (Saturday 10 and Sunday 11 October). Public transport is preferable.
- **Accommodation**: the 19th arrondissement (immediately adjacent), the 10th (Gare du Nord and Gare de l''Est) and the 20th (Belleville). Booking 2 to 3 months ahead is recommended, as an October weekend is already busy in Paris.
- **Visitor admission**:
  - **Full price**: €13.
  - **Concession**: €7 (students, under-18s, people with disabilities, groups of 10+, works councils).
  - **Free**: under-12s and a carer for visitors with reduced mobility.
  - **Free invitation codes**: numerous codes circulate via the media partners (Phototrend, 3DVF, Paris-Friendly). Check these sources before buying.
- **Equipment trials**: indoor areas (manufacturer stands) and **outdoor areas in the Parc de la Villette**, on presentation of an ID document.
- **Services**: cloakrooms, catering on site and within the Parc de la Villette, conference and demonstration spaces, and artist signings within the Zooms exhibitions.

## What it costs to exhibit

Comexposium does not publish a public rate card for the Salon Photo & Vidéo. Participation terms are provided on direct commercial request from the dedicated Comexposium team. The variables affecting cost are stand size, location (proximity to the main stage, exhibitions or entrance), the level of fit-out and additional services (signage visibility, activities, exhibition partnerships).

A typical format for a major manufacturer: large demonstration stands (100 to 300 sq m) with equipment-trial areas, talk stages and integrated exhibitions. A typical format for a third-party optician or software publisher: 20 to 50 sq m stands focused on product demonstration.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- T-10 months (December 2025): make contact with Comexposium, select the stand format and location.
- T-6 months (April 2026): sign the contract, settle the stand activities (demos, talks, workshops).
- T-4 months (June 2026): finalise the scenography, coordinate the outdoor equipment trials with the organisation, brief the stand team.
- T-2 months (August 2026): communication campaign, client invitations, scheduling meetings with influencers and content creators (the 2026 video focus).
- T-1 month (September 2026): final logistical adjustments, training for the welcome team (including a briefing on visual inclusion and signage).

### For the visitor

- Buy your ticket online or look for a **free invitation code** via Phototrend, 3DVF or Paris-Friendly before paying full price.
- Allow a full day to cover the 4 days of activities (1,000 sq m of exhibitions, manufacturer demonstrations, conferences, the Zooms du Salon).
- **Thursday 8 or Friday 9**: quieter days, ideal for in-depth equipment trials and conversations with exhibitors.
- **Saturday 10 and Sunday 11**: high attendance from families and enthusiasts, so plan tight slots for the popular demonstrations.
- Bring your own memory card to take away the images from the equipment trials.

## Policy & CSR initiatives

A structured CSR approach was launched by Comexposium in 2025 for the show, now consolidated for the 2026 edition:

- **No promotional goodies**: an end to handing out free, non-useful items, to reduce post-event waste.
- **Digital-only floor plan**: paper plans removed, with access via QR codes and a mobile app.
- **Fortnightly CSR committee** with exhibitors, to share best practice and set collective targets.
- **Responsible exhibitor guide**: a reference made available to exhibitors to limit the footprint of their stand.
- **Modular stands** reducing the use of single-use plastics and encouraging reuse between editions.
- **Circular economy**: refurbishing exhibited equipment, hiring rather than buying for certain items.

### Visual inclusion

The 2026 edition continues an inclusion focus begun in 2025:

- **Photographic exhibitions adapted for the visually impaired** in partnership with **Canon "Canon World Unseen"** and the **Fédération des Aveugles et Amblyopes de France**, including an "Images in relief" workshop.
- **Training of the welcome staff** by the **Souffleurs d''Images**, a service of the **Souffleurs de Sens** association (Groupe SOS Solidarités), to assist visually impaired and blind visitors.
- Adapted signage and a dedicated route on the show''s floor plan.

## The show''s evolution

