 ]}
 notes={["No editions in 2020 and 2021 (Covid).","Moved to the Grande Halle de la Villette in 2022 after 15 editions at Porte de Versailles.","2025 figures published by Comexposium: 37,188 visitors (+1% vs 2024), 114 exhibitors for 167 brands, 1,000 sq m of photographic exhibitions."]}
 sources="Comexposium, lavillette.com, Phototrend, 3DVF"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

Created in **2007** under the name Salon de la Photo (first edition 3-7 October 2007, Hall 4, Porte de Versailles), the show reached its historic peak in **2013 with 85,526 visitors**, driven by the democratisation of the digital SLR. The structural decline of the amateur photo market, competition from the smartphone and the maturity of the installed equipment base led to a gradual fall to 61,044 visitors in 2019, the last pre-Covid edition at Porte de Versailles. After the 2020-2021 interruption, the **move to the Grande Halle de la Villette in 2022** (30,000 visitors) marked a complete repositioning: a tighter format, a more upmarket venue, outdoor equipment trials in the park, and a committed CSR and inclusion approach. The trajectory is now one of **slow, controlled growth** (30,000 → 36,823 → 37,188 visitors between 2022 and 2025), with an ambitious 2026 target via the **Salon Photo & Vidéo** rebranding: broadening the visitor base to video content creators, a fast-growing segment that is strategic for manufacturers (hybrid camera bodies). The show embraces a **B2C, general-public** identity, differentiated from Paris Photo (an art fair at the Grand Palais in November), with a strong editorial DNA around the Zooms du Salon (competition, media partners, Picto, the CP+ Yokohama partnership) that makes it the benchmark annual gathering for advanced amateurs in France.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='salon-photo-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Trade show of solutions for founders and managers of micro-businesses and SMEs. Financing, management, marketing and tools for entrepreneurs.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- Salon SME, formerly Salon des micro-entreprises, is the annual gathering for the self-employed, freelancers, founders and managers of micro-businesses. It is organised by **en Personne expo** (chaired by Alain Bosetti), the company that also runs Silver Economy Expo and the Salon des services à la personne et de l''emploi à domicile.
- 2026 edition: Tuesday 13 and Wednesday 14 October 2026 at the [Palais des Congrès de Paris](/lieux/palais-des-congres-paris), level 3. Free admission with prior online registration.
- Announced format for 2026: 100 exhibitors, 500 entrepreneurship experts and 100 free-access conferences and workshops.
- Created in 1999 under the name Salon des micro-entreprises, renamed Salon SME in 2016 for its 18th edition (sources: economie.gouv.fr, lejournaldeleco.fr).
- A compact, content-driven trade show: the value lies in the conferences, masterclasses and meetings with experts more than in the exhibition floor space.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Banks and professional neobanks, financing and microcredit, insurance and social protection, management software, accounting and invoicing, AI solutions, telecoms and web hosting, accountancy and legal expertise, franchise networks, support and training bodies, and institutional players in business creation.

### Notable exhibitors

The exhibitor list is available on the official website: [salonsme.com/espace-exposants/exposants-secteurs.html](https://www.salonsme.com/espace-exposants/exposants-secteurs.html). Among the exhibitors at the 2025 edition: Bpifrance, CIC, Crédit Mutuel, Hello bank! Pro, MAIF, Agipi (AXA), Orange Pro, La Poste Pro, Indy, Tiime, EBP, SumUp, Hostinger, as well as the institutional players France Travail, Urssaf, Adie, France Active and France Num.

### Quality of attendees

- Attendees are project leaders and managers of micro-businesses with 0 to 20 employees: freelancers, independent consultants, slashers, solopreneurs, business founders and franchise candidates.
- The trade show draws on a vast market: more than one million businesses created per year in France since 2021, including 1,111,200 in 2024, a record (source: insee.fr).
- Franchise candidates can meet networks in active recruitment, complementing a specialist trade show such as [Franchise Expo Paris](/salons/franchise-expo-paris).

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

The 2026 edition announces **100 free-access conferences and workshops**, led by 500 entrepreneurship experts.

In 2025, the programme already featured around a hundred conferences and workshops spread across five rooms over two days (source: journaldunet.com): from idea to project, women''s entrepreneurship, starting a business after 50, freelancing, AI applied to small businesses, visibility on LinkedIn and social media, financing, choosing a franchise, B2B selling. Formats on offer: Masterclass, Freelance Talks, Speed Business Meetings and Meet-up.

### Awards and recognition

The trade show hosts the presentation of the **Trophées des Tribus**: in 2025, the trophies were awarded by the show''s partners (Alibaba.com, Be a Boss, CCI Paris Île-de-France, JPG becoming Mondoffice, Moze and Le Salon SME) as part of the Grand Rendez-vous des Tribus, which combines a conference, Speed Business Meetings and a networking aperitif.

### Community and networks

The organiser en Personne expo also runs a virtual events platform (en Personne Virtual) and the communications agency en Personne 360.

On the press side, the trade show maintains a press area with studies and infographics on entrepreneurship, and offers visitors free professional press content to download on site.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the listing for the [Palais des Congrès de Paris](/lieux/palais-des-congres-paris).

Specific to the trade show:

- **Opening hours:** 9 a.m. to 6 p.m. on both days.
- **Level:** the trade show occupies level 3 of the Palais des Congrès; allow around fifteen minutes between arriving at Porte Maillot and reaching the show entrance.
- **Visitor badge:** free with online registration, a personalised badge sent by email in September. No badge printing on site: you must download it before you come.
- **Cloakroom:** on level 3, with a charge (3 euros), for coats and helmets.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Stand prices are not published on the official website. Request the exhibitor pack from the organiser: contact@salonsme.com.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

A typical timeline observed for the 2025 edition, for guidance only:

- 12 to 6 months before: request the pack from en Personne expo and choose a location, as the number of stands is limited (around a hundred exhibitors).
- Early September: declaration of service providers, validation of fit-out plans and safety certificate.
- Mid-September: submission of signage files and completion of the extranet form to appear on the show''s printed materials.
- Late September: ordering of furniture.
- The day before opening: build-up.

### For the decision-maker visitor

- Free online registration at salonsme.com; a personalised badge received by email in September, to be downloaded before you come.
- One day is enough to walk the stands; allow both days to attend several masterclasses in a row and take part in the Speed Business Meetings.
- Check the conference schedule in advance: five rooms run in parallel.

## The trade show''s evolution

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
 { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
 { "key": "dates", "label": "Dates" },
 { "key": "reperes", "label": "Highlights" }
 ]}
 rows={[
 { "edition": "2026", "dates": "13-14 October 2026", "reperes": "100 exhibitors, 500 experts and 100 conferences announced" },
 { "edition": "2025", "dates": "13-14 October 2025", "reperes": "120 exhibitors, around a hundred conferences and workshops across 5 rooms" },
 { "edition": "2024", "dates": "23-24 September 2024", "reperes": "115 exhibitors, 500 experts, 100 conferences" },
 { "edition": "2020 (22nd)", "dates": "12-13 October 2020", "reperes": "100% online edition (Covid-19)" },
 { "edition": "2016 (18th)", "dates": "4-6 October 2016", "reperes": "the Salon des micro-entreprises becomes Salon SME" },
 { "edition": "1999", "dates": "September 1999", "reperes": "creation of the Salon des micro-entreprises" }
 ]}
 sources="salonsme.com, shine.fr, journaldunet.com, toute-la-franchise.com, entreprises.cci-paris-idf.fr, economie.gouv.fr"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

Created in 1999, ten years before the auto-entrepreneur scheme, Salon SME has accompanied the entire rise of the freelance economy and weathered Covid by switching 100% online in 2020 (22nd edition). The 2016 renaming, from Salon des micro-entreprises to Salon SME, clarified the target audience: the self-employed, founders and managers of micro-businesses with 0 to 20 employees. The current format remains deliberately compact: around a hundred exhibitors on a single level of the Palais des Congrès, with the centre of gravity firmly on content (500 experts, 100 conferences and workshops). For a provider of services to micro-businesses, the promise is direct contact with project leaders and young managers, not a mass-market trade show.', seo_title_en='Salon SME 2026 in Paris: dates, exhibitors and conferences', seo_description_en='Salon SME 2026, 13 and 14 October at the Palais des Congrès de Paris: exhibitors, conferences and experts for the self-employed, founders and managers of micro-businesses. Free admission with registration.' WHERE slug='salon-sme-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Tourism and travel trade show for Northern France. Destinations, holidays, cruises and local tourism.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- This listing corresponds to **Tourissima**, the tourism and outdoor activities trade show: the annual consumer travel event in the Hauts-de-France region, organised by **Comexposium** as part of the Les Salons du Tourisme network, alongside the [Salon Mondial du Tourisme](/salons/salon-mondial-du-tourisme-paris) and Destination Nature in Paris.
- 2027 edition: from Friday 29 to Sunday 31 January 2027 at [Lille Grand Palais](/lieux/lille-grand-palais), Hall Londres, from 10 am to 6 pm without interruption.
- 2026 results (37th edition): 18,570 visitors, up 5% on 2025, and 256 exhibitors (source: tourmag.com). Announced for 2027: around 250 professionals and more than 200 destinations.
- A consumer trade show geared towards holiday planning: destinations in France and Europe, outdoor, slow tourism, vanlife and cruises. Admission 8 € in advance, 10 € on the door, free for under-18s.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Tourist offices and destination marketing agencies (CRT, ADT), tour operators, specialist travel agencies, cruise lines, accommodation providers and players from the outdoor and vanlife scene (source: tourmag.com). A consumer audience: travellers from the Hauts-de-France region and neighbouring areas in the midst of planning their holidays.

### Notable exhibitors

Exhibitor catalogue on the official website: [salons-du-tourisme.com/fr-FR/lille/catalogue-exposants](https://www.salons-du-tourisme.com/fr-FR/lille/catalogue-exposants). In 2026, the trade show notably welcomed the Agence du Tourisme de la Corse and the Agence de Développement Touristique des Ardennes, also present in 2025 (sources: atc.corsica, pro.ardennes.com). The Village des Hauts-de-France brings together the regional destinations, their heritage and their gastronomy (source: lilleaddict.fr).

### Quality of the visitor base

- 18,570 visitors in 2026, up 5% on 2025 (source: tourmag.com)
- 2025 organiser report: 88% of visitors with a travel project, with an average budget above 3,000 €
- 2026 visitor survey: a desire for independence in planning, destinations in France (Brittany, Normandy, PACA) and Europe (Spain, Italy, Greece), a preference for short breaks and extended weekends (source: tourmag.com)

## Activities & community

### Activities and themed villages

Programme for the 2026 edition, as the 2027 programme has not yet been published: Village des Hauts-de-France, Espace Vanlife and Parcours Famille, built around the themes of Europe, outdoor and slow tourism, glamping, cruises and road trips (sources: zoomsurlille.fr, lilleaddict.fr). The stands come alive with music, dance and destination presentations (source: lilleaddict.fr).

### Community and networks

Tourissima opens Comexposium''s Salons du Tourisme season each year, which then continues in Paris at Paris Expo Porte de Versailles with the [Salon Mondial du Tourisme](/salons/salon-mondial-du-tourisme-paris) and Destination Nature, held jointly in mid-March. Mahana Lyon is on hold in 2026, with a revamped format announced for 2027 (source: tourmag.com).

The professional tourism press, led by TourMaG, covers every edition and publishes the trade show''s headline figures.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Lille Grand Palais](/lieux/lille-grand-palais) listing.

Specific to the trade show:

- **Hall:** Hall Londres, entrance on boulevard des Cités Unies.
- **Opening hours:** 10 am to 6 pm without interruption, from Friday to Sunday.
- **Ticket:** 8 € per person in advance online, 10 € on the door, free for under-18s. Free invitations are regularly circulated by exhibitors and media partners ahead of opening.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates are not published publicly: request a pack via [the Salons du Tourisme exhibitor area](https://www.salons-du-tourisme.com/fr-FR/espace-exposant).

## Planning your visit

### For the exhibitor

- Anticipate your stand request via the exhibitor area: the trade show takes place at the end of January and spaces in the themed villages (Hauts-de-France, Vanlife) are limited.
- Bear the network''s calendar in mind: Tourissima opens the season at the end of January, with the [Salon Mondial du Tourisme](/salons/salon-mondial-du-tourisme-paris) following in Paris in mid-March. Pooling stand and teams across the two events is common practice among exhibiting destinations.
- Target a project-driven visitor base: 88% of 2025 visitors had a travel project, with an average budget above 3,000 €. Plan a system for capturing leads and issuing quotes on the stand.
- Prepare a stand activity: visitors come looking for inspiration and experiences, not just brochures.

### For the visitor

- Buy your ticket in advance (8 € instead of 10 €), free for under-18s.
- Allow half a day to a full day depending on the number of destinations you are targeting: the trade show fits in a single hall.
- Prepare your list of destinations using the online exhibitor catalogue before your visit.
- With children, follow the Parcours Famille introduced in 2026.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
 { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
 { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors", "align": "right" },
 { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
 { "edition": "37th · 2026", "visiteurs": "18,570", "exposants": "256" },
 { "edition": "36th · 2025", "visiteurs": "17,640", "exposants": "223" },
 { "edition": "35th · 2024", "visiteurs": "16,800 (visits)", "exposants": "250" }
 ]}
 sources="tourmag.com, salons-du-tourisme.com (press releases 2024 and 2025 reports), zoomsurlille.fr"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

Attendance has risen steadily over three editions: 16,800 visits in 2024, 17,640 visitors in 2025, 18,570 in 2026, or roughly +5% per year. The number of exhibitors fluctuates between 210 (2023) and 256 (2026), with the peak reached at the latest edition. The "tourism and outdoor activities" positioning (outdoor, vanlife, slow tourism) broadens the trade show beyond the traditional travel agency and chimes with the trends highlighted in the 2026 visitor survey: independence, nature and short formats. Tourissima remains the major travel event for the Hauts-de-France public and the season opener for Comexposium''s Salons du Tourisme network.', seo_title_en='Tourissima Lille 2027: tourism and outdoor activities show', seo_description_en='Tourissima returns to Lille Grand Palais from 29 to 31 January 2027: more than 200 destinations, 2026 results (18,570 visitors), rates and exhibitor tips.' WHERE slug='salon-tourisme-voyages-lille';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='Trade show for the horticulture and plant sector. Horticulture, nurseries, landscaping, floral displays and green spaces.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- The Salon du Végétal is the leading biennial gathering for France''s horticulture and plant sector: ornamental production, nursery growing, landscaping, distribution and green-space design. It is organised by **Destination Angers** and co-built with VERDIR, the BHR and Valhor.
- 36th edition, from **15 to 17 September 2026** at the [Parc des Expositions d''Angers](/lieux/parc-expo-angers) (source: materiel-paysage.com for the edition numbering).
- 2024 results: **260 exhibitors**, 15% of them international, and **7,500** professional visitors (organiser''s closing report). For 2026, the organiser is announcing 280 exhibitors, 8,000 visitors and 60 expert sessions.
- Back in Angers since 2022 after three editions in Nantes, the trade show has adopted a biennial rhythm in even-numbered years, alternating with [Paysalia](/salons/paysalia-lyon) in Lyon in odd-numbered years (source: aiph.org).
- A structured eco-design approach, led by Destination Angers and certified to ISO 20121: [salonduvegetal.com/un-evenement-eco-concu](https://www.salonduvegetal.com/un-evenement-eco-concu/).

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Horticultural growers, nursery operators, suppliers of technical solutions and services, innovation developers, training bodies and institutional players: every link in the plant-sector chain is represented. In 2024, 15% of the 260 exhibitors came from abroad (source: aiph.org).

### Notable exhibitors

The list of 2026 exhibitors is on the official website: [salonduvegetal.com/liste-des-exposants](https://www.salonduvegetal.com/liste-des-exposants/). Exhibitors are spread across the general halls and dedicated areas: the Pôle Arbre(s) brings together the offering of nursery growers and tree professionals, while the Espace Jeunes Pousses showcases start-ups and young companies in the sector.

### Quality of the visitor base

- Free entry, but strictly reserved for plant-sector professionals, on an access badge
- Four visitor targets identified by the organiser: retailers and distributors (garden centres, florists, DIY/large retail chains, wholesalers, e-retailers), space designers and developers (landscapers, architects, hotels, golf courses, campsites), local authorities and public bodies, learners and prescribers
- Three signposted thematic visit routes ("affinity-based visit"), with the option of being guided

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

More than 60 conferences, forums and round tables at the 2024 edition (source: angers-developpement.com). Highlights announced for 2026: a major conference, "20 ans de Nature en ville", on the morning of Tuesday 15 September, marking the 20th anniversary of Plante & Cité, with Monique Barbut and Marc-André Selosse among the prospective guests; a highlight with the European landscape designers Cassian Schmidt and Bettina Jaugstetter, who will come to share their approaches and feedback; and the 19th Rencontres Nationales des Villes et Villages Fleuris, expected to bring together around 500 decision-makers (source: materiel-paysage.com).

The Soirée Pro brings exhibitors and visitors together alongside the trade show.

### Awards and prizes

The **Concours Innovert®**, of which Valhor is the official partner, rewards the sector''s innovations at each edition across four categories: "Innovation variétale", "Nouveautés commerciales végétales", "Produits & services pour la commercialisation et/ou l''usage des végétaux" and "Solutions (produits, services ou dispositifs) pour l''aménagement paysager". Entries close in mid-April (a maximum of two submissions per company), with the final jury in late June and the awards ceremony during the trade show; around fifty exhibitors entered the competition for 2026 (source: materiel-paysage.com). The winners are grouped together in the Espace Innovert®.

### Community and networks

The trade show is co-built with the sector''s professional bodies: VERDIR, the BHR (the show''s historical creator and owner of the brand, tied to the City of Angers by an agreement since late 2019, source: presse.angers.fr) and Valhor, the inter-trade organisation for horticulture, floristry and landscaping. The 2026 presidency is held by Marc-Henri Doyon (Ripaud Pépinières), who succeeds Thierry Browaeys (source: materiel-paysage.com).

Destination Angers organises [SIVAL](/salons/sival-angers), the trade show for specialised plant production, on the same site.

The show also runs an editorial platform throughout the year: in-depth articles, expert interviews, podcasts and replays freely available.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the listing for the [Parc des Expositions d''Angers](/lieux/parc-expo-angers).

Specific to the trade show:

- **Opening hours:** Tuesday 15 and Wednesday 16 September 9am-6pm, Thursday 17 September 9am-5pm.
- **Badge:** free entry, reserved for plant-sector professionals, on an access badge. The show''s newsletter announces the opening of visitor registration.
- **Key areas:** the Cœur Végétal (an immersive, scenographic space with a conference area), the Espace Innovert®, the Pôle Arbre(s), the Espace Jeunes Pousses and the Espace Emploi & Métiers.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Prices published by the organiser for 2026:

- **Green pack (turnkey):** a packaged 9 m² stand from €1,961 excl. VAT, including partitions, fascia sign, header, lighting, a 2 kW electricity meter, a furniture kit and 2 evening passes.
- **Grower rate:** from €111 excl. VAT per m² for a bare stand and €134 excl. VAT per m² for a fitted stand.
- **Bespoke offer:** from €116 excl. VAT per m² for a bare stand (minimum 18 m²) and €139 excl. VAT per m² for a fitted stand (minimum 9 m²).
- **Young growers rate:** a stand from €595 excl. VAT for companies less than 3 years old and young growers under 4 years old, grouped together in a dedicated area.
- **Pôle Arbre(s) and Emploi & Formation:** a stand from €595 excl. VAT for tree-sector players, associations, schools and employment bodies.
- **Referral offer:** €200 excl. VAT discount for a 2024 exhibitor who refers a new exhibitor.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: request your participation and the pricing brochure from Destination Angers'' sales department; weigh up the Green pack, a bare or fitted stand and the dedicated areas (Pôle Arbre(s), Jeunes Pousses).
- 5 months out: submit your Concours Innovert® entry before the mid-April deadline, a strong visibility lever at the show.
- 4 months out: activate the referral offer if you exhibited at the previous edition.
- 2 months out: check that your stand is listed in the thematic visit routes and prepare for the Soirée Pro.
- 3 weeks out: brief your team and book appointments with the buyers and prescribers you are targeting.

### For the decision-making visitor

- A free badge on online registration, reserved for plant-sector professionals; subscribe to the newsletter to be alerted when registration opens.
- Choose one of the three thematic routes to structure your visit according to your line of work.
- Target the conferences in the 2026 programme and the Innovert® awards ceremony in advance.
- Allow 1 to 2 days depending on the scope you want to cover (production, distribution, landscaping, local authorities).

## CSR policy & initiatives

The Salon du Végétal is run by Destination Angers, certified to ISO 20121, which sets out its approach on the [Un évènement éco conçu](https://www.salonduvegetal.com/un-evenement-eco-concu/) page. Published commitments, organised into five strands (waste, mobility, sector, communication, purchasing): permanent sorting signage across 10 waste streams, sorting of biowaste from source to skip, removal of plastic bottles in favour of glass, reduced carpeting in the aisles with verification of the recycling chain, collection and treatment of cigarette butts, and the pooling of facilities with other trade shows on the site.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
 { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
 { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Visitors", "align": "right" },
 { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
 { "edition": "2026 (36th)", "visiteurs": "8,000 expected", "exposants": "280 announced" },
 { "edition": "2024", "visiteurs": "7,500", "exposants": "260" }
 ]}
 sources="salonduvegetal.com, destination-angers.com (2024 closing release)"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

Created in 1981 under the name Salon Anjou Vert and renamed Salon du Végétal in 1985, the show was for a long time the major annual gathering for French horticulture, with a peak of close to 17,000 visitors in 2011 (source: Wikipedia). The move to Nantes from 2017 to 2019 did not win people over: in late 2019, the City of Angers and the BHR signed an agreement bringing the show back to Angers and entrusting its organisation to the SPL Destination Angers (source: presse.angers.fr). After a 2020 edition cancelled because of Covid, the relaunch took place in September 2022 in a tighter, now biennial format set in even-numbered years, alternating with [Paysalia](/salons/paysalia-lyon) in Lyon (source: aiph.org). The 2024 edition (260 exhibitors, 7,500 visitors) validated this repositioning as a sector show, more compact but better qualified, which the 2026 edition extends with its thematic routes and by hosting the Rencontres Nationales des Villes et Villages Fleuris.', seo_title_en='Salon du Végétal 2026 in Angers: dates, exhibitors, prices', seo_description_en='Salon du Végétal, from 15 to 17 September 2026 at the Parc des Expositions d''Angers: exhibitors from the plant sector, the Concours Innovert, stand prices, free badge for professionals.' WHERE slug='salon-vegetal-angers';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='48th edition at Porte de Versailles, Pavilion 3, exclusively reserved for winegrowers who grow, vinify, bottle and market their own production themselves. Previous edition results: 665 winegrowers present, of which 30% were women winegrowers.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- **48th edition** of the Salon des Vignerons Indépendants Paris Porte de Versailles, from **10 to 13 December 2026**, Pavilion 3. Not to be confused with the Champerret edition in March (33rd ed. from 19 to 22 March 2026), a separate fixture on the annual calendar.
- Organised by the **Confédération Nationale des Vignerons Indépendants de France (CNVIF)**, formerly the Confédération Nationale des Caves Particulières (CNCP), renamed "Vignerons Indépendants de France" in 2003. The confederation was founded in 1976 in Languedoc-Roussillon, and the trade show concept was created in 1978.
- **100% direct-sale audience**: exclusively winegrowers who tend their own vines, vinify themselves, bottle on the estate and market their own production. Cooperatives and merchants are excluded by charter. Private buyers, independent wine merchants, sommeliers and restaurateurs.
- Results of the previous edition (47th, 4 to 7 December 2025): **665 winegrowers** present (i.e. "almost 700" according to the organiser), with **30% women winegrowers** among the exhibitors.
- Entry fee: **€6 full price, €3 concession**. **Free downloadable invitation** on the official website and from partners (wine merchants, specialist blogs, media). **Complimentary INAO glass at the entrance** on presentation of a ticket or invitation.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

A single-sector trade show, 100% independent winegrowers. The charter is strict and is the distinctive feature of the trade show: no cooperatives, no merchants, no blended brands. Each exhibitor tends their own vines, vinifies their own grapes, bottles on their estate and markets their own production. The confederation has more than **6,000 winegrower** members across France, organised into a network of around **40 regional and departmental federations**.

### Notable exhibitors

The line-up rotates and is geographical: all the major French appellations are represented (Bordeaux, Burgundy, Languedoc-Roussillon, the Rhône Valley, the Loire, Alsace, Provence, the South-West, Champagne, Beaujolais, the Jura, Savoie, Corsica). The full list of exhibitors is published on the official website ahead of the trade show, organised by region.

### Quality of the visitor base

- Loyal private buyers: an urban, upper-bracket audience that builds a personal cellar each year at this trade show.
- Independent wine merchants sourcing directly, looking for estates outside the mass-distribution circuit.
- Sommeliers and restaurateurs, particularly from the Île-de-France region, seeking references for a short wine list.
- A knowledgeable audience: blind tasting and the INAO scoring grid are the norm, and technical exchange with the winegrower is part of the value proposition.

## Activities & community

### Concours des Vignerons Indépendants

The flagship competition of the sector, held ahead of the Paris trade show. 2026 edition: **3,700 samples** submitted, **1,031 medals** awarded (510 gold, 430 silver, 91 bronze). The competition''s distinctive feature: the jury is **made up exclusively of amateurs**, more than 1,000 tasters trained in the INAO method. Blind tasting, in short sessions, with jurors rotating. The medals are highlighted on the stands of the winners at the Paris PdV trade show.

### On-site activities

An educational tasting area, introductory tasting workshops, trails by grape variety and by appellation, meetings with women winegrowers (a deliberate focus at the 2025 edition, in keeping with the 30% female representation).

### Exhibitor and visitor services

Trolley hire to make buying bottles easier, a left-luggage area, home delivery (a paid option, via a carrier partner), an open-access educational tasting area. The whole set-up is designed to let visitors leave with their cellar for the year.

### National network 2026

Paris PdV closes a dense national calendar, timed to the buying season:

- Strasbourg, 31st ed., 13 to 16 February 2026
- Lyon, 6 to 8 March 2026
- Bordeaux, 27th ed., 13 to 15 March 2026 (Hall 3 Bordeaux Lac, 310 winegrowers, 20,000 visitors expected)
- Paris Champerret, 33rd ed., 19 to 22 March 2026
- Clermont-Ferrand, 11th ed., 16 to 18 October 2026
- Hamburg, 2nd ed., 23 to 25 October 2026 (European roll-out)
- Lyon Tony Garnier, 36th ed., 29 October to 1 November 2026
- Reims, 34th ed., 6 to 9 November 2026
- Lille, 12th ed., 13 to 16 November 2026
- **Paris Porte de Versailles, 48th ed., 10 to 13 December 2026**

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the listing for [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles).

Specific to the trade show:

- **Pavilion location:** Pavilion 3.
- **Dates:** 10 to 13 December 2026 (Thursday to Sunday).
- **Audience:** open to all every day, ticket or free invitation required.
- **INAO glass:** complimentary at the entrance on presentation of a ticket or invitation.
- **Buying services:** trolleys for hire, left-luggage area, home delivery option.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates not published: participation is via membership of the confederation and an application through the relevant regional or departmental federation. Figures not publicly available.

Visitor rates (official Paris PdV 2026 schedule):

| Category | Price |
|---|---|
| Full price | €6 |
| Concession | €3 |
| Free invitation | €0 (official website, partner wine merchants, specialist blogs and media) |

Complimentary INAO glass at the entrance. Partner wine merchants typically distribute invitations in November and early December.

## Planning your visit

### For the exhibiting winegrower

- Prior membership of the confederation via your regional or departmental federation (a network of around 40 federations).
- Apply for the Paris PdV trade show several months in advance; stand allocation is handled by the national federation.
- An identical stand for all exhibitors: this has been a **founding principle** since 1978, guaranteeing fairness and the "direct producer" positioning. No premium or two-tier stands.
- Prepare to take part in the Concours des Vignerons Indépendants beforehand: gold and silver are strong arguments to display on your stand.
- For estates starting out with direct sales at trade shows, favour the regional shows first (Strasbourg, Lyon, Bordeaux, Lille, Reims) before Paris PdV, which remains the most crowded fixture.

### For the buying visitor

- Download the free invitation from the official website or pick it up from a partner independent wine merchant before 10 December.
- Set a cellar budget: the average spend recorded by winegrowers ranges from €200 to several thousand euros over the course of the trade show.
- Hire a transport trolley as soon as you arrive if the aim is to build your annual cellar.
- Target 2 to 3 priority appellations in advance: the trade show covers the whole of France, and exhaustive tasting is impossible in a single day.
- Activate the home delivery option for heavy purchases, particularly if travelling by public transport.

## CSR policy and sector commitment

The independent winegrowers'' sector has a structured CSR positioning, documented by the Observatoire des Vignerons Indépendants: **32.4% organic farming** (+23% over 3 years), **15.1% under High Environmental Value level 3**, **3.6% in the process of certification**. Almost half of the members are therefore certified or in conversion under one of the two standards. The confederation publicly campaigns with the authorities for an HVE tax credit. At the Paris PdV trade show, certified estates are explicitly highlighted on the stands. The "short supply chain" value proposition (winegrower to consumer, with no intermediary) is consistent with a CSR reading of the value chain.

## How the trade show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
 { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
 { "key": "dates", "label": "Dates" },
 { "key": "exposants", "label": "Winegrowers", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
 { "edition": "48th, 2026", "dates": "10 to 13 December 2026", "exposants": "to come" },
 { "edition": "47th, 2025", "dates": "4 to 7 December 2025", "exposants": "665" },
 { "edition": "Concept created", "dates": "1978", "exposants": "identical stand for all principle" },
 { "edition": "Confederation founded", "dates": "1976", "exposants": "Languedoc-Roussillon" }
 ]}
 sources="Confédération Nationale des Vignerons Indépendants de France, Observatoire des Vignerons Indépendants, official trade show website"
/>

### Trajectory analysis

The Salon des Vignerons Indépendants Paris Porte de Versailles is the largest direct winegrower-to-consumer sales trade show in France. Its distinctiveness lies in the strict admission charter (production, vinification, bottling and marketing by the winegrower themselves), which sets it radically apart from the other Paris wine fixtures. The 2025 trajectory (665 winegrowers, 30% women winegrowers) confirms a mature format whose room for growth now lies in international expansion (the Hamburg roll-out since 2025) and in consolidating the national network of 9 French cities. The role of the Concours des Vignerons Indépendants, with its 100% amateur jury and its 3,700 annual samples, structures exhibitors'' commercial visibility throughout the year.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='salon-vignerons-independants-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='The trade show for healthcare and innovation. Hospital federation, healthcare facilities, social care and innovations.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- Paris Healthcare Week was rebranded **SantExpo** in 2019. It is France''s major annual gathering for healthcare and social care, organised by the **Fédération Hospitalière de France**, with **GL Events** as event operator.
- Next edition: 25 to 27 May 2027 at Paris Expo Porte de Versailles, Hall 1.
- 2026 review (figures published post-show on santexpo.com): 37,180 participants (around +6% vs 2025), 696 exhibitors, 749 speakers across 304 speaking slots, 156 accredited journalists.
- An umbrella trade show bringing together under a single identity several long-standing shows: **HopitalExpo** (hospital equipment, 1985), **GerontHandicapExpo** (social care, ageing, disability, 1987) and **HIT - Health-IT Expo** (digital health, 2007).
- A published, concrete CSR commitments page: [santexpo.com/content/rse](https://www.santexpo.com/content/rse).

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Medical equipment manufacturers, HIS publishers and digital health solutions, hospital furniture and architecture, mutual insurers and financing, public operators, professional federations. Strictly professional healthcare / social care visitors.

The long-standing [HopitalExpo](/salons/hopital-expo-paris) pavilion concentrates exhibitors focused on equipment and public hospital management.

### Notable exhibitors

The exhibitor list is on the official website: [santexpo.com](https://www.santexpo.com/). Companies that have exhibited at recent editions include: Philips Healthcare, Siemens Healthineers, GE Healthcare (equipment manufacturers and imaging); Dell Technologies, IndigoCare (healthcare IT infrastructure); Maincare-Docaposte, Enovacom (Orange group), Dedalus, ESII (HIS and digital health); MNH, AP-HP, Hospices Civils de Lyon, France Travail, Bpifrance (institutional bodies) (sources: santexpo.com/exposants, spreadly.app).

### Visitor quality

- Three distinct registration pathways (healthcare professional, institutional, industrial) ensure a qualified visitor base (source: santexpo.com/informations-pratiques)
- 37,180 participants in 2026, including 749 speakers and 156 accredited journalists
- Strong presence of facility CEOs, CME chairs, and public and private hospital management

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

**749 speakers** took the floor across **304 conferences and agoras** in 2026. The programme covers the sector''s major issues: mental health, hospitals facing an ageing population, excellence in research and care, digital health as a common good, environmental transformation, AI in healthcare, prevention, and hospital HR. Replays of the 2026 agoras are available online. The 2027 theme and programme have not yet been published (source: santexpo.com).

### Awards and prizes

**SantExpo Innovation Trophies** (a legacy of the HopitalExpo Trophies) awarded by the FHF Fund, with categories covering improving the caregiver experience, improving the patient experience, the operating theatre, environmental transformation, diagnostic support, women''s health, medicine in the age of AI, and autonomy (source: santexpo.com/espace-innovation-fond-fhf).

Other prizes awarded on site at recent editions: the **Environmental Transition in Healthcare Award**, the **Medical Attractiveness Award**, the **HR Impact Trophies** (in partnership with MGEN), and the Hospital Communication Award (sources: fhf.fr, miroirsocial.com). The 2027 categories and winners are to be confirmed as the show approaches.

### Community and networks

Institutional partners: FHF, FHP (Fédération de l''Hospitalisation Privée), FEHAP, ANAP, Agence du Numérique en Santé (ANS), Ministère du Travail, de la Santé, des Solidarités et des Familles, DGOS, CNAM, ADEME, MNH, Leem, Bpifrance (sources: fhf.fr, fhp.fr, fehap.fr, anap.fr).

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Paris Expo Porte de Versailles](/lieux/paris-expo-porte-de-versailles) listing.

Specific to the show:

- **Opening hours:** Tuesday and Wednesday 9 a.m.-6 p.m., Thursday 9 a.m.-5 p.m. at recent editions (to be confirmed for 2027 on santexpo.com).
- **Visitor badge:** free with prior online registration (named badge), reserved for healthcare and social care professionals. Registration opens in the spring preceding the show.
- **Map:** a single A5 map handed out per visitor (reduced paper footprint), with a mobile app and QR codes for detailed pathways.

## What it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates are not published: participation requests via the GL Events platform ([exposant.gl-events.com](https://exposant.gl-events.com)). Figures are not publicly available.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: get in touch with GL Events, choose your positioning among the HopitalExpo, GerontHandicapExpo and HIT pavilions.
- 9 months out: apply for the SantExpo Innovation Trophies (submit before the deadline, the final is heavily covered by the media).
- 6 months out: prepare your stand plan and demos, brief the team on the institutional partnerships to activate.
- 3 months out: leverage the e-health Village (ANS) for digital visibility.
- 6 weeks out: confirm appointments with facility CEOs, sales briefing.

### For the decision-maker visitor

- Free pre-registration on santexpo.com with proof of professional activity in healthcare / social care.
- Allow 1 to 2 days depending on the scope covered (hospital equipment, social care, digital health).
- Target conferences in advance: the programme is published several weeks before the show.
- Built-in job dating for hospital recruitment, open with a visitor badge.

## CSR policy & initiatives

SantExpo sets out its commitments on its [CSR](https://www.santexpo.com/content/rse) page. Published, concrete commitments: a shared responsibility model with exhibitors (2024 baseline of 1,110 m³ of waste, i.e. 333 tonnes, with the **RE''UP** partnership for reuse), partially reusable and PVC-free signage, carpet limited to key areas, dematerialisation of badges and maps (a single A5 map per visitor), lanyards and pouches reused from one edition to the next, and plastic-free catering compliant with the AGEC law with donations to **Le Chaînon Manquant**.

Accessibility and inclusion strand: needs gathered at registration, quiet rest areas, priority reception, adapted signage and trained staff. No ISO 20121 certification mentioned to date (source: santexpo.com/content/rse).

## How the show has evolved

<HistoryTable
 columns={[
 { "key": "edition", "label": "Edition" },
 { "key": "visiteurs", "label": "Participants", "align": "right" },
 { "key": "exposants", "label": "Exhibitors", "align": "right" }
 ]}
 rows={[
 { "edition": "2027 (upcoming)", "visiteurs": "n/a", "exposants": "n/a" },
 { "edition": "2026", "visiteurs": "37,180", "exposants": "696" },
 { "edition": "2025", "visiteurs": "35,150", "exposants": "695" },
 { "edition": "2024", "visiteurs": "30,000+", "exposants": "750" },
 { "edition": "2023", "visiteurs": "25,000+", "exposants": "~600" }
 ]}
 sources="santexpo.com, fhf.fr, lequotidiendumedecin.fr"
