### Trajectory analysis

The SIA remains the world''s leading agricultural consumer trade show with a unique format: 9 days open, 4 zones covering the entire sector, and a historic gauge of 600,000+ visitors in a full edition. **The 2026 edition marked a cyclical halt at 437,402 visitors**, a fall of 27.9% attributable to three cumulative and entirely exogenous factors: the absence of cattle linked to lumpy skin disease (the show''s core draw), an unfavourable alignment of France''s 3 school holiday zones in the same week, and wet weather across almost the entire show. **The 2027 edition plays a strategic rebound role**: the announced return of cattle, guest country Serbia succeeding Côte d''Ivoire, mascot Biguine the Martinican Brahman (re-entering after being prevented by LSD, against a political backdrop of Macron''s support for the farmer Prosper), the 133rd Concours Général Agricole, and above all a political dimension amplified by the presidential election year. A visit to the SIA has been an obligatory ritual for candidates to the Élysée since Jacques Chirac, structurally turning the 2027 edition into a national media echo chamber. The CENECA and Comexposium governance guarantees institutional continuity (CENECA created in 1963, first SIA edition in 1964, hence 63 editions in 2027), while the CSR programmes Animal Welfare, Agri Confiance, Demain la Terre and the opening up to new transitions (agroecology, carbon storage, agroforestry, short supply chains) anchor the show in the sector''s contemporary agenda.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='sia-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='The world''s largest air show. Civil and military aeronautics, space, defence and technological innovations.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- The Salon International de l''Aéronautique et de l''Espace (SIAE), known as the Paris Air Show or Le Bourget, marks its 56th edition from Monday 14 to Sunday 20 June 2027 at [Aéroport de Paris-Le Bourget](/lieux/paris-le-bourget), running on a biennial cycle in odd-numbered years (no 2026 edition).
- Split into two phases: 4 strictly trade days from Monday to Thursday, followed by 3 days open to the general public from Friday to Sunday, 8.30am to 6pm.
- The 2027 edition is framed around the **centenary of Charles Lindbergh''s transatlantic crossing** and his landing at Le Bourget in 1927, the official narrative thread chosen by the organisers.
- 2025 benchmark (55th edition, 16 to 22 June): **305,200 unique visitors** (vs 292,579 in 2023), 141,000 unique professionals of whom 38% were international, around 2,400 exhibitors from 48 countries, 80 official delegations (64 countries and 5 international organisations), 400+ delegations in total, 155 aircraft on display and 173 flying demonstrations.
- Organised by **SIAE SAS**, a subsidiary of **GIFAS** (Groupement des Industries Françaises Aéronautiques et Spatiales). The oldest air show in the world, created in 1909 at the Grand Palais under the name Exposition internationale de la locomotion aérienne by André Granet and Robert Esnault-Pelterie.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

A global B2B and B2G (government) trade show for civil and military aeronautics, space and defence. A unique blend of commercial showcasing, the signing of major contracts, state diplomacy and a public-facing showcase over the weekend. Complete coverage of the value chain: civil and military aircraft manufacturers, engine makers, tier-1 and tier-2 equipment suppliers, space integrators and launcher firms, missile makers, defence electronics specialists, drone and eVTOL companies, advanced air mobility start-ups, airport operators, MRO. The first 4 days are exclusively B2B, accessed via a named badge obtained by invitation or through the professional ticketing channel siae.fr.

### Notable exhibitors (2025, 2027 reference base)

**Civil and regional aircraft manufacturers**: Airbus (Toulouse), Boeing, Embraer, ATR, Dassault Aviation.

**Engine makers and equipment suppliers**: Safran, Rolls-Royce, GE Aerospace, Pratt & Whitney, Honeywell, Thales, Liebherr Aerospace.

**Defence and missiles**: MBDA, Lockheed Martin, Northrop Grumman, RTX (Raytheon), KNDS, Naval Group (cross-cutting presence), Turgis & Gaillard (Aarok drone, AirMaster S radar partnership with Thales).

**Space and launchers**: ArianeGroup, Thales Alenia Space, Airbus Defence and Space, European and American NewSpace players.

**Advanced air mobility and drones**: eVTOL start-ups, hybrid/electric propulsion, civil and military drones, autonomous systems integrators.

### National pavilions

- **United States pavilion**: around 450 companies grouped in Hall 3 under the aegis of AeroSociety (organiser of the American pavilion)
- Pavilions for **Germany, the United Kingdom, Italy, Japan, Brazil, India**, plus around thirty countries represented collectively
- **French SME and start-up regional pavilion** dedicated to home-grown innovation

### Visitor quality

141,000 unique professionals in 2025, of whom 38% were international, 80 official delegations (64 countries and 5 international organisations) and more than 400 delegations in total. A mix of very senior decision-makers: heads of state, defence ministers, military chiefs of staff, presidents and procurement directors of the major airlines, directors of national space programmes, specialist international press. **Inaugurated in 2025 by Prime Minister François Bayrou**, an opening tradition led by a senior representative of the French state and renewed at each edition.

## Programme & community

### Conference and innovation programme

**Paris Air Lab**, in its 4th edition in 2025, a space dedicated to disruptive innovation, start-ups and immersive talks. **Agora conferences** bringing together researchers, industry players, policymakers and astronauts around themed round tables. Seven structuring themes carried over into 2027:

1. Disruptive innovations
2. Sustainable air mobility (SAF, hydrogen, electric)
3. Defence and sovereignty
4. Civil and military drones
5. Space and exploration
6. Artificial intelligence and cybersecurity
7. Quantum technologies

### Flying displays

A daily programme of flying displays: military fighters, helicopters, aerobatics, civil and military widebodies. 2025 edition: **173 demonstrations** over the 7 days, **155 aircraft on display** on the ground and in the air. A grandstand is available as an add-on to the public ticket to watch the flights from dedicated seating.

### Announcements and order books 2025 (reference base)

The 2025 edition confirmed the momentum of global order books:

- **Airbus** dominant with around 248 firm orders and 156 options announced over the week. Standout moment: **Riyadh Air signs 25 firm A350-1000s** for around 9 billion dollars.
- **Embraer** signs with **SkyWest for 60 E175s** worth 3.6 billion dollars.
- **Boeing** kept a low profile owing to the Air India disaster on 12 June 2025 (4 days before the opening).
- **Thales** announced 48 **RAPIDFire** artillery systems (co-developed with KNDS) and a partnership with **Turgis & Gaillard** on the **Aarok** drone fitted with the AirMaster S radar.

The 2027 edition is expected to follow the same momentum, with a commercial agenda punctuated by the aircraft manufacturers'' daily announcements.

### Community & networks

Organiser: **SIAE SAS, a subsidiary of GIFAS** (Groupement des Industries Françaises Aéronautiques et Spatiales), the trade federation bringing together France''s aerospace industry. The show''s historic co-founders in 1909: **André Granet** and **Robert Esnault-Pelterie**. The show runs in biennial alternation with **Farnborough** (United Kingdom, even-numbered years) and then **ILA Berlin** or Munich, structuring the global aerospace calendar.

### 2027 specifics

Official narrative thread: the **centenary of Charles Lindbergh''s transatlantic crossing** (21 May 1927) and his landing at Le Bourget that same year. This historical anchor will be drawn upon in the Agora programming, Paris Air Lab and the scenography of the halls. The 7 themes from 2025 will be carried over. A CSR programme structured around decarbonising the sector (air transport accounts for 2.5% of global human-made CO2 emissions): sustainable aviation fuels (SAF), hydrogen, hybrid and electric propulsion, lightweighting of composite structures, AI applied to predictive maintenance.

## On site: what you need to know

- **Venue**: [Aéroport de Paris-Le Bourget](/lieux/paris-le-bourget), the show''s permanent site since 1953 (20th edition)
- **Transport access**: RER B to Le Bourget station, then a free shuttle to Porte L, located around 100 metres from the main entrance. Bus 133. Allow 35 to 45 minutes from central Paris by RER + shuttle.
- **Parking**: Visitor car parks on site, but heavy congestion midweek (trade days). Favour the RER on opening days.
- **Accommodation**: The Le Bourget area, Aulnay-sous-Bois, Roissy-CDG and the north-east of Paris (Saint-Denis, La Plaine, Pantin) as a priority. Book 6 months in advance, as the SIAE is the largest B2B event in June across the Île-de-France region, with more than 300,000 visitors over 7 days.
- **Opening hours & tickets 2027**:
  - 4 trade days (Monday 14 to Thursday 17 June) on a named badge, free (exhibitor invitation) or paid via the professional ticketing channel siae.fr
  - 3 public days (Friday 18 to Sunday 20 June) at €18 for a 1-day ticket
  - Grandstand (watching the flying displays from the seating): €12 extra
  - Free for under-7s, reduced group rates
  - Opening hours 8.30am to 6pm
- **Services**: Multi-point catering across the whole tarmac and the halls, international press room, VIP areas for official delegations, official app, interpreting services for delegations, cloakrooms, left-luggage.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 24 months out (summer 2025): make contact with SIAE SAS / GIFAS, hall or tarmac positioning, space negotiation
- 18 months out (January 2026): signing, choice of category (aircraft manufacturer, engine maker, equipment supplier, defence, space, drones, start-up)
- 12 months out (June 2026): validation of the scenographic concept, brief for the stand or pavilion contractor, aircraft logistics if static or flying presence
- 9 months out (September 2026): final plans, stand construction order, aircraft accreditation with the French authorities (DGAC)
- 6 months out (December 2026): commercial briefs, client delegation logistics, team accommodation (15 to 200 people depending on size)
- 3 months out (March 2027): client invitations, schedule of commercial announcements, press packs, spokesperson briefing
- 6 weeks out (May 2027): delivery of materials, final team briefing, coordination with the French military authorities for the flying displays

### For the decision-making visitor

- Register as soon as the 2027 professional ticketing opens (autumn 2026) on siae.fr: a free named badge by exhibitor invitation or paid via professional ticketing
- Favour Monday 14 or Tuesday 15 June for structuring commercial meetings (before the big announcements), and keep Wednesday for the Agora conferences and Paris Air Lab
- Prepare your meetings via the B2B matchmaking platform siae.fr in advance
- For international delegations: get in touch with SIAE protocol and the areas dedicated to partner countries
- Allow 2 to 3 full days to cover the entire display (civil, military, space, drones, start-up)
- For the general public: favour Friday 18 June (less busy than the weekend), and book the grandstand if you have a strong interest in the flying displays

## How the show has evolved

