The 20th edition of SIANE took place in 2025, confirming the trade show as the benchmark industrial event of the Grand Sud. The 2024 edition brought together 583 exhibitors and over 14,000 visitors, with a stated target of 700 exhibitors and 15,000 visitors for the 2025 anniversary. The trade show has managed to broaden its offering beyond subcontracting alone towards the challenges of transformation: artificial intelligence (the AI Village), decarbonisation and the industry of the future. The 21st edition, from 13 to 15 October 2026 at the MEETT, extends this momentum on a strong regional footing, supported by the Occitanie Region and the Toulouse industrial ecosystem. For industrial subcontractors, see also the regional series [SEPEM Industries Toulouse](/salons/sepem-toulouse) and the major national event [Global Industrie](/salons/global-industrie-lyon).', seo_title_en='SIANE 2026 Toulouse: trade show for industry partners of the Grand Sud', seo_description_en='SIANE 2026, 21st edition from 13 to 15 October at the MEETT in Toulouse. Subcontracting, machine tools, AI Village and the SIANE Trophies. Dates, exhibitors, practical info.' WHERE slug='siane-toulouse';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='The SIBCA (Low-Carbon Real Estate Trade Show) brings together all the key players committed to the financing, development, design, construction and renovation of low-carbon buildings, neighbourhoods and cities.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- SIBCA (Low-Carbon Real Estate Trade Show) is the flagship event for the decarbonisation of real estate, held at the Grand Palais in Paris, **under the high patronage of the Presidency of the French Republic**.
- 2026 edition: **12,500 professional visitors expected, 200 exhibitors, 300 speakers, 130 major debates and conferences, 400+ elected officials** (source: sibca.fr).
- Co-organised by the **Association BBCA** (Low-Carbon Building), confirmed on sibca.fr.
- Next edition: 1-3 September 2026, Grand Palais, Hall 1.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Players in the financing, development, design, construction and renovation of low-carbon buildings, neighbourhoods and cities.

### Notable exhibitors

List of exhibitors on the official website: [sibca.fr](https://www.sibca.fr/fr).

### Quality of attendance

- 70% senior executives, CSR directors, developers, buyers and investors - Strong presence of elected officials and journalists (400+ elected officials in 2025)

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

130 major debates and conferences per edition: new-build construction and renovation, decarbonisation at neighbourhood scale, in-use performance. "Biodiversity and Climate Adaptation" pavilion in 2025.

### Awards & accolades

BBCA Trophies, presented at the SIBCA: the Property Developers Awards and the Grand Prix BBCA, recognising labelled low-carbon real estate operations.

### Community & networks

Co-organised with the Association BBCA, which runs the Low-Carbon Building label and structures the decarbonised real estate community.

## On site: what you need to know

- **Venue:** Grand Palais, 7 avenue Winston Churchill, 75008 Paris.
- **Visitor badge:** prices not published. Talents Afternoon on Thursday 2pm-6pm free for students and jobseekers on proof of status (source: sibca.fr/participants).

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates not published: quotation on request via sibca.fr/exposants.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 9 months out: request a quotation from the organiser.
- 6 months out: stand plan, application for the BBCA Trophies.
- 3 months out: accreditations, accommodation.
- 6 weeks out: final brief, delivery of materials.

### For the decision-maker visitor

- Free Talent badge for students and jobseekers; paid Premium badge for full access.
- Allow 1 to 2 days: target the major debates and the Biodiversity pavilion in advance.

## Policy & CSR initiatives

The decarbonisation of real estate is the very purpose of the SIBCA, with the BBCA Trophies rewarding labelled low-carbon operations and a pavilion dedicated to biodiversity and climate adaptation.

## How the trade show has evolved

