### Trajectory analysis

Created in 2013, Tech''Elevage has established itself as the annual livestock innovation event for the greater West, France''s leading ruminant farming area. The 2025 edition, on the theme "Engagés pour une alimentation de qualité" (Committed to quality food), brought together more than 8,000 visitors and over 200 exhibitors. The 14th edition will be held from 24 to 26 November 2026 at the Parc Expo des Oudairies, consolidating its positioning on the cattle, sheep and goat sectors and on the challenges of agricultural transition and food sovereignty.', seo_title_en='Tech''Elevage 2026 La Roche-sur-Yon: greater West livestock trade show', seo_description_en='Tech''Elevage 2026: 14th edition from 24 to 26 November at the Parc Expo des Oudairies, La Roche-sur-Yon. The annual ruminant livestock innovation trade show (cattle, sheep, goats, equines). Over 200 exhibitors, 8,000+ visitors.' WHERE slug='tech-elevage-la-roche-sur-yon';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='National trade show for sheep-farming professionals. Technical demonstrations, ovine genetics and equipment for sheep farmers.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- Tech-Ovin is the national professional sheep trade show, held every two years in Bellac (Haute-Vienne) since 1999. Its official tagline is "The professional sheep trade show".
- It is run by the **APOSNO association** (Association Pour l''Organisation du Salon National Ovin), based in Bellac. There is no external event operator: the association handles the organisation in-house.
- Most recent edition held: the **14th edition**, on 3 and 4 September 2025, at the Rochettes site in Bellac, with more than 200 exhibitors announced.
- Next edition: the **15th edition** scheduled for September 2027 in Bellac. The precise dates have not yet been published on the official website.
- Predominantly professional, the trade show also welcomes the general public, with a strong concentration of sheep farmers, technicians and players from across the sector.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

The trade show brings together the entire commercial offering of the ovine sector: farming equipment manufacturers, handling and shearing equipment, genetics and selection, animal feed, health and reproduction, breed bodies, cooperatives and downstream players (meat, wool). Visitors are predominantly professional: sheep farmers, technicians, advisers and agriculture students, including around a thousand young people from agricultural colleges expected at recent editions.

### Notable exhibitors

The list of exhibitors is published on the official website: [techovin.fr](https://www.techovin.fr/). The 2025 edition brought together more than 200 exhibitors covering the entire ovine supply chain, from breeding farmers to equipment manufacturers.

### Quality of visitors

- A predominantly professional trade show, open to the general public, centred on the French ovine sector.
- A strong presence of farmers, selection bodies and cooperatives, with a pronounced training dimension (welcoming groups of students from agricultural colleges).
- More than 300 animals from around forty breeds presented at recent editions, supporting technical demonstrations and competitions.

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

At the 2019 edition, around sixty mini-conferences were offered over the two days, dedicated to the technical and economic challenges of the ovine sector. These are complemented by technical demonstrations (shearing, breed presentations, farming equipment, sheepdogs, wool work) that are the hallmark of the event. The detailed programme for each edition is put online on the official website as the trade show approaches.

### Awards and prizes

Several competitions and awards punctuate the trade show: the **"Berger Futé"** competition revived by APOSNO, the **French lamb meat cutting competition** (teams of apprentice butchers), breed competitions and innovation trophies. The National Ovine Breeding Stock Fair is traditionally held alongside the trade show (a separate site, Parc Charles Silvestre in Bellac), the day after the Tech-Ovin event days. Categories and winners are to be confirmed for the 2027 edition as the trade show approaches.

### Community and networks

Tech-Ovin is supported by the major institutions of the sector and by local authorities: FNO (Fédération nationale ovine), Interbev Ovins, APCA, IDELE (Institut de l''élevage), Races de France, as well as the Nouvelle-Aquitaine Region, the Haute-Vienne Department and the Town of Bellac.

Sheep farmers also attend other complementary national events such as the [Sommet de l''Élevage](/salons/sommet-elevage-clermont) in Clermont-Ferrand, [SPACE](/salons/space-rennes) in Rennes and [Tech-Élevage](/salons/tech-elevage-la-roche-sur-yon) in La Roche-sur-Yon.

## On site: what you need to know

The trade show is held outdoors at the Rochettes site, rue des Rochettes in Bellac (87300), Haute-Vienne.

- **Ticketing:** registration is done online via the HelloAsso platform, relayed from the official website.
- **Organiser contact:** APOSNO, 9 rue Chanzy, 87300 Bellac, tel. 05 55 60 27 72, contact@techovin.com.
- **Opening hours and map:** published on techovin.fr as each edition approaches.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: make contact with APOSNO to discuss positioning and book a pitch (outdoor site).
- 6 months out: prepare technical demonstrations and applications for competitions and innovation trophies.
- 3 months out: organise the logistics of transporting equipment and, where applicable, the animals being presented.

### For the visitor

- Register online via the HelloAsso ticketing relayed on techovin.fr.
- Allow one to two days depending on your scope (equipment, genetics, downstream sector) and identify the mini-conferences in advance.
- Outdoor trade show: bring clothing suited to early-September outdoor conditions.

## How the trade show has evolved

Founded in 1999, Tech-Ovin has established itself as the national event for the ovine sector, held every two years in Bellac. The 2019 edition marked the trade show''s 20th anniversary (20 years of existence). The 14th edition was held on 3 and 4 September 2025, with more than 200 exhibitors and over 300 animals from around forty breeds. The 15th edition is expected in September 2027 in Bellac, in keeping with this biennial rhythm. Attendance figures have not been the subject of an audited publication to date.', seo_title_en='Tech-Ovin 2027 Bellac: national sheep trade show', seo_description_en='Tech-Ovin 2027: the national professional sheep trade show in Bellac. 15th biennial edition, over 200 exhibitors from the ovine sector, demonstrations and competitions. Organised by APOSNO.' WHERE slug='tech-ovin-bellac';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='National agriculture festival. A Jeunes Agriculteurs event combining farming demonstrations, culture and live entertainment.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- **Les Terres de Jim 2026** runs from **Friday 11 to Sunday 13 September** at **Metz-Magny (Moselle, Grand Est)**, on a **100-hectare open-air site**, for the **12th edition** of Europe''s largest open-air farming festival.
- A **touring** event: Moselle hosts Les Terres de Jim for the second time, eleven years after the 2015 edition held on the former Frescaty air base. Moselle is the 2026 pilot department under the aegis of the **Jeunes Agriculteurs** union.
- The **Finale Nationale de Labour** (National Ploughing Final) remains the historic heart of the event: heir to the Jeunes Agriculteurs ploughing final (more than 65 years of heritage according to the union), it brought together **27 competitors** in 2025 and will be contested at Metz-Magny by reigning champion **Victor Duchaux**, the first Moselle native crowned French flat-ploughing champion (2025 edition, Vieux-Manoir).
- A **free, public-facing format**: more than 100 activities, livestock and crop sections, machinery demonstrations, helicopter and hot-air balloon flights, an educational farm, and evening concerts. **1,200 volunteers** mobilised, around **200** professional and agricultural exhibitors expected, with a target of **100,000+ visitors** across three days.
- **Friday 11 September dedicated to schools**: an educational day reserved for institutions (primary, secondary and sixth-form schools, specialist institutes) with a strand on discovering agricultural careers.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

The event brings together all of France''s agricultural sectors in a popular festival format: machinery exhibitors (tractors, combine harvesters, new and vintage equipment), the livestock sector (cattle, sheep, equine), the crop sector (arable farming, seeds), chambers of agriculture, cooperatives, unions, professional organisations (Crédit Agricole, Groupama, MSA), and agricultural education. Les Terres de Jim is not a classic B2B trade show: it is a showcase of French agriculture aimed at the general public, built around a flagship competition (the Finale Nationale de Labour) and a programme of activities.

### Notable exhibitors

Official 2026 partners announced on the organiser''s website: **TotalEnergies**, **Crédit Agricole**, **Groupama**, **Chambre d''agriculture de la Moselle**. Institutional partners: **Région Grand Est**, **Département de la Moselle**, **Eurométropole de Metz**, **Ville de Metz**. The **Qualité MOSL** brand (Moselle''s departmental quality mark) is highlighted to promote local sectors and terroirs. The full list of 2026 exhibitors will be available on the event''s official page.

### Visitor quality

A mixed profile: a majority of regional general public (Grand Est, Luxembourg, border areas of Belgium), families, and schools on the Friday, but also agricultural professionals, elected officials and specialist press (Réussir, La France Agricole, regional agricultural press such as La Moselle Agricole, Est Agricole, Le Paysan Vosgien). The 2025 edition in Seine-Maritime drew more than **70,000 visitors** across three days, attended notably by Édouard Philippe (the former Prime Minister visited on the Saturday). The 100,000+ visitor target set by the organisers remains a projection: actual figures vary with the weather and the appeal of the host department.

## Activities & community

### Conference and activities programme

A programme structured around several announced sections: an **animal section** (cattle, sheep, equine), a **crop section**, **motorised activities** (machinery demonstrations), **les Halles de Jim** (a gastronomy and terroir area), **youth activities** (educational farm, children''s workshops), and **helicopter and hot-air balloon flights**. At the 2025 edition in Seine-Maritime, flagship activities included tractor pulling, a maize maze, Garden Cross, Moiss'' Batt'' Cross, Auto-Foot and helicopter flights. Evening concerts are a tradition of the event (in 2025: Let Dzur, Collectif Métissé, Black M); the 2026 musical line-up will be announced by the organisers in the months before the event.

### Awards & accolades

**Finale Nationale de Labour**: the emblematic competition of Les Terres de Jim, a direct heir to the Jeunes Agriculteurs national ploughing final (more than 65 years of heritage according to the JA union, dating the creation of the JA ploughing contests to the 1950s). Two canonical categories: **flat ploughing** and **in-furrow ploughing**. In 2025 at Vieux-Manoir, 27 competitors took part. **Victor Duchaux** was crowned 2025 French flat-ploughing champion: he is the first Moselle native to win this title, which gives the event''s return to Moselle a strong symbolic dimension. Selection of the 2026 competitors goes through the regional ploughing finals, including the Grand Est final.

### Satellite events

**Schools day, Friday 11 September**: an opening partly reserved for educational institutions (primary, secondary and sixth-form schools, specialist institutes), with a strand on discovering careers and vocational guidance towards the agricultural sectors. **Festive evening, Saturday 12 September**: concerts open to the public. The three-day format combines an educational Friday, a festive Saturday and a competitive Sunday (the Finale Nationale de Labour as the highlight of the Sunday).

### Community & networks

The institutional social network of the **Jeunes Agriculteurs** (national organiser) and the **Jeunes Agriculteurs de la Moselle** (local organiser), with a strong presence on Facebook (the official Les Terres de Jim account), Instagram and YouTube. Cross-cutting mobilisation of the national JA network at every edition: all departments take part in recruiting competitors for the Finale Nationale de Labour.

Regular specialist press coverage: Réussir, La France Agricole, Pleinchamp, and the host department''s regional agricultural press. For 2026, Moselle regional press (Le Républicain Lorrain, Moselle TV, La Moselle Agricole, RCF Jerico Moselle) and institutional outlets (Moselle.fr, Région Grand Est) are active relays.

### What''s new in 2026

A return to **Moselle** eleven years after 2015 (Frescaty), with a twofold symbolic effect: Moselle is home to the reigning Finale Nationale de Labour champion, Victor Duchaux. The event takes place during a 2026 placed by the Département de la Moselle under the **"sign of agriculture"** (in the words of its president Patrick Weiten, Moselle.fr): preserving farming practices, developing local sectors, and the challenge of water resources. There is a stronger emphasis on the **Qualité MOSL** mark on local products exhibited and tasted on site.

## On site: what you need to know

- **Venue**: the open-air site of **Metz-Magny** (a district of Metz, Moselle, Grand Est region). A touring format: Les Terres de Jim moves to a different department each year, and the chosen site is an agricultural area of around 100 hectares laid out for the event (not an exhibition centre). The precise address and access plan are published by the organisers in the "practical information" section of the official website as the event approaches.
- **Access**: Metz is served by motorway (A4 Paris-Strasbourg, A31 Luxembourg-Nancy) and high-speed rail (Metz-Ville at 1h22 from Paris-Est, LGV Est line). The Eurométropole de Metz is a 2026 partner, and local shuttle services and dedicated car parks can be expected as the show approaches (to be confirmed on the official website).
- **Accommodation**: Metz and its conurbation (2-4 star hotels, rural guesthouses, campsites). Book early, as September is a busy month in Metz (start of the university year, festivals). The Luxembourg and German borders are nearby for international visitors.
- **Opening times**: Friday 11 September reserved for schools for part of the day, with Saturday 12 and Sunday 13 open to the general public all day. Precise times to be confirmed on the official website.
- **Free admission**: the event is traditionally **free for the general public** (a model funded by institutional and private partners). 2026 admission arrangements to be confirmed on the "practical information" page of the official website.
- **Services**: on-site catering (les Halles de Jim, food trucks, Qualité MOSL tasting area), educational farm, children''s areas, helicopter and hot-air balloon flights (by booking and at specific prices).

## Planning your visit

### For the exhibitor or partner

- 12 months out: make contact with the Jeunes Agriculteurs de la Moselle organising committee (national JA network) to join the exhibitor programme or a partnership
- 9 months out: confirm the terms of attendance (space, activity format, machinery demonstrations), book a pitch
- 6 months out: stand, demo and equipment logistics
- 3 months out: team briefing, regional press pack, coordination with JA volunteers
- 6 weeks out: final confirmation, team accreditations

### For the ploughing competitor

- Finale Nationale de Labour competitors qualify via the Jeunes Agriculteurs regional ploughing finals (notably the Grand Est final for ploughers in the area). The qualification calendar runs in the spring and summer before the event.
- Two categories: flat ploughing, in-furrow ploughing.

### For the general-public visitor

- Free entry (to be confirmed on the official website as the event approaches)
- Favour the Saturday for the full activities + concerts programme, and the Sunday for the Finale Nationale de Labour
- Friday is partly reserved for schools
- Bring outdoor footwear (a 100-hectare agricultural site) and dress for open-field weather
- Large-scale on-site parking (to be specified on the official website)

## Policy & CSR initiatives

Les Terres de Jim carries an implicit positioning that promotes French agricultural sectors, sustainable livestock farming and terroir products, with a strong educational dimension (a dedicated schools day, an educational farm). The 2026 edition highlights the Département de la Moselle''s **Qualité MOSL** mark, a departmental label for local and sustainable products, as well as Moselle agriculture within an "eat healthy and local" approach. The president of the Département de la Moselle, Patrick Weiten, has placed 2026 "under the sign of agriculture", with an announced focus on preserving farming practices, developing local sectors and managing water resources (Moselle.fr).

Beyond the institutional messaging, the Jeunes Agriculteurs does not set out a structured, public CSR policy on the official Les Terres de Jim page (no dedicated environmental commitment report, no Net Zero Carbon Events signature). The open-air, touring nature and volunteer mobilisation place the event within a logic of local proximity, but its footprint (visitor travel, machinery-demonstration energy, waste management) is not publicly documented.

## How the show has evolved

