### Trajectory analysis

Launched in 2014 alongside the Mondial de Labour in Bordeaux, Les Terres de Jim quickly established a stable formula: one host JA department per year, three days in September, the Finale Nationale de Labour as the competitive heart, a broad public-facing programme (activities, livestock, machinery, concerts) and a dedicated schools day. The touring model helps cover the country and mobilise the JA network locally, but it makes edition-to-edition comparison tricky: attendance depends heavily on the agricultural density of the host department, its tourist appeal and the weather.

The 2025 edition in Seine-Maritime drew a reported 70,000-plus visitors, a solid figure for a department where the event had never been held. The 2026 edition at Metz-Magny takes place in a territory that already knows the event (2015 at Frescaty) and is home to the 2025 Finale Nationale de Labour title-holder, Victor Duchaux. Potentially doubling attendance (the 100,000+ target) will rest in particular on the proximity of the Luxembourg and German borders, on the Duchaux ambassador effect and on the mobilisation of the Département and the Eurométropole.

The format remains typical of a touring, public-facing farming festival: no international B2B ambition, no classic "trade show" model, but a solid media and institutional showcase for the Jeunes Agriculteurs union and for the French agricultural sector.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='terres-de-jim';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='International textile and fashion trade show. Global textile sourcing for fashion and apparel professionals.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- Autumn edition of the **Texworld Apparel Sourcing Paris** cluster: **31 August to 2 September 2026** at Paris-Le Bourget, halls 2, 3 and 4. Co-located cluster **Texworld + Apparel Sourcing + Avantex** (Avantex present only at the September edition).
- Organiser: **Messe Frankfurt France**, a subsidiary of the German Messe Frankfurt group, which acquired the Texworld brand in December 2001 to make it its international textile/apparel sourcing hub in Paris.
- Most recent edition (58th edition, 2-4 February 2026): **1,100+ exhibitors from 33 countries**, including **591 at Texworld** (18 countries) and **500+ at Apparel Sourcing** (25 countries). Leading origins: China, Turkey, India, South Korea (under the KOFOTI banner), Taiwan.
- Spring-Summer 2027 trend book: **"Very Middle Ages"**, by artistic directors **Louis Gérin** and **Grégory Lamaud**. Articulated across 4 worlds: **Digital Lordship**, **Nuclear Sorcery**, **Speculative Crusade**, **Data Inquisition**.
- Strictly B2B trade show: **free named e-pass for professionals** with pre-registration, mobile QR code badge.
- Structuring programme: **Trend Forum**, **Agora** conferences, masterclasses, sessions on AI applied to fashion, materials circularity and natural materials.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Texworld Apparel Sourcing Paris is the benchmark European event for **Asian and Mediterranean textile and apparel sourcing**. The cluster brings together three co-located, complementary hubs at the September edition:

- **Texworld**: fashion fabrics (woven and weft, knit, denim, lace, silks, woollens), upstream suppliers in the value chain.
- **Apparel Sourcing**: garment-making and finished products (ready-to-wear for men, women and children, accessories, sportswear, lingerie, finished denim, uniforms).
- **Avantex** (September only): technical textiles, smart textiles, materials innovations, finishings and digital services for fashion.

Exhibitor profiles: spinners, weavers, knitters, OEM/ODM garment manufacturers, agents and sourcing offices, export federations (KOFOTI for South Korea, Indian, Chinese, Turkish and Pakistani national pavilions). Geographic distribution dominated by **China, Turkey, India, South Korea and Taiwan** origins, with growing momentum from **Bangladesh, Pakistan, Indonesia, Vietnam, Egypt and Morocco** pavilions on the Apparel Sourcing side.

### Benchmark figures (February 2026 edition)

- **1,100+ exhibitors** overall, **33 countries** represented.
- **591 Texworld exhibitors** from **18 countries**.
- **500+ Apparel Sourcing exhibitors** from **25 countries**.
- The September edition is traditionally larger thanks to the addition of the **Avantex** hub and the Spring-Summer 2027 pre-collection sourcing window.

### Quality of the visitor base

- Strictly professional visitor base: purchasing and sourcing departments of brands and retailers, design studios, manufacturers, importers, textile distributors, sales agents and ready-to-wear and home textile principals.
- **Named badge**, non-transferable, online pre-registration with proof of business activity.
- A structurally majority international visitor base: European buyers (France, Italy, Spain, Germany, United Kingdom, Benelux, Scandinavia), the Maghreb, Turkey, the Middle East and West Africa on entry-level and mid-range apparel textiles.

## Activities and community

### Trend Forum and the SS 2027 "Very Middle Ages" trend book

The editorial heart of the trade show, the **Trend Forum** gives scenographic form to the season''s trend book. For the September 2026 vintage, artistic directors **Louis Gérin** and **Grégory Lamaud** sign **"Very Middle Ages"**, a forward-looking Spring-Summer 2027 reading that blends a medieval imaginary with technological anticipation. The trend book is articulated across 4 worlds:

- **Digital Lordship**: heraldic and metallic codes, visual hierarchies reread through the lens of digital interfaces.
- **Nuclear Sorcery**: contemporary mysticism, alchemical materials, dark and ritual palettes.
- **Speculative Crusade**: exploration, reimagined martial functionality, technical and utilitarian textiles.
- **Data Inquisition**: the tension between transparency and opacity, sensor-readable materials, digital weft.

The Trend Forum acts as a compass for buyers and design studios. It feeds the selection of materials and garments presented across the Texworld, Apparel Sourcing and Avantex hubs.

### Agora conferences and masterclasses

A conference programme structured around the **Agora**, the central speaking space. Recurring themes: **AI applied to fashion** (generative design, trend forecasting, supply optimisation), **materials circularity** (recycled, bio-based, traceability), **natural materials** (cotton, linen, wool, silk, alternative fibres), **European regulation** (CSRD, AGEC, the future digital product passport). The **masterclasses** extend the Agora in a workshop format, more operational, aimed at purchasing and design teams.

### National pavilions

A historic feature of the trade show, the **national pavilions** structure geographic legibility. Regular presence of **KOFOTI** (Korea Federation of Textile Industries) for South Korea, and Chinese, Indian, Turkish and Pakistani institutional pavilions. On the Apparel Sourcing side, growing momentum from the **Bangladesh, Vietnam, Indonesia, Egypt, Morocco and Tunisia** pavilions.

### Texpertise Econogy programme

Texworld Apparel Sourcing Paris is part of the **Texpertise Econogy** programme run by Messe Frankfurt across all its textile and apparel trade shows. Four structuring features:

- **Econogy Hub**: a space dedicated to exhibitors committed to documented sustainability standards.
- **Econogy Talks**: sector conferences focused on the transition (materials, supply, regulation).
- **Econogy Tour**: themed guided routes for buyers.
- **Econogy Finder**: a listing tool for Econogy exhibitors on the trade show''s digital platform.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [Paris-Le Bourget](/lieux/paris-le-bourget) page.

Specific to the trade show:

- **Dates**: Monday 31 August to Wednesday 2 September 2026.
- **Halls**: 2, 3 and 4 of the Paris-Le Bourget Exhibition Centre.
- **Visitor badge**: **free named e-pass** with online pre-registration. Mobile QR code badge, scanned on entry.
- **Strictly B2B**, proof of business activity required at registration.

## How much it costs to exhibit

Exhibitor rates are not published publicly. Quotes on request from **Messe Frankfurt France** via the dedicated forms on texworld-paris.fr.messefrankfurt.com and apparelsourcing-paris.fr.messefrankfurt.com. Several formulas traditionally coexist at Messe Frankfurt:

- **Bare stand** (space-only rental, from a minimum sq m threshold per hub).
- **Turnkey fitted stand** (partitions, carpet, fascia, furniture, electricity).
- **National pavilions**: pricing negotiated via the sector federations (KOFOTI for South Korea, equivalents for China, India, Turkey and Pakistan).
- **Young and emerging brand packs** at Avantex for innovation and tech exhibitors.

No specific figure available to date.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: make contact with Messe Frankfurt France, choose your hub (Texworld materials, Apparel Sourcing garment-making, Avantex innovation).
- 9 months out: finalise the application, negotiate the national pavilion where applicable via the sector federation of origin.
- 6 months out: brief the stand contractor, apply for Econogy if you hold documented sustainability standards, align the SS 2027 collection calendar for consistency with the "Very Middle Ages" Trend Forum.
- 3 months out: team accreditations, scheduling of priority buyer meetings, trilingual sales materials (French, English, Mandarin for Asian exhibitors).
- 6 weeks out: specialist press activation (Journal du Textile, WWD, Sportswear International), pre-scheduled visit plan.

### For the decision-making visitor

- Early online pre-registration to obtain the free named e-pass (no on-site fee, but registration is mandatory with proof of business activity).
- 3-day trade show: **allow at least 1 full day** to cover the three hubs, **2 days recommended** for multi-segment purchasing profiles.
- Pace your visit around the **"Very Middle Ages" Trend Forum** (SS 2027 trend reading), then work through hub by hub: Texworld for materials, Apparel Sourcing for finished product, Avantex for innovation and technical textiles.
- Econogy buyers: use the **Econogy Finder** beforehand to identify committed exhibitors and plan a themed **Econogy Tour**.

## CSR policy and initiatives

The **Texpertise Econogy** programme structures the CSR line of all of Messe Frankfurt''s fashion and textile trade shows, including Texworld Apparel Sourcing Paris. Guiding principle: to highlight exhibitors committed to documented sustainability standards (recycled and bio-based materials, certifications such as GRS, GOTS, OEKO-TEX, supply chain traceability) and to steer buyers via dedicated routes.

The **Econogy Talks** conferences address the sector''s operational themes: **European regulation** (CSRD, AGEC, the future digital product passport), **fibre traceability**, **circular models** (post-consumer and post-industrial recycling), **alternative materials** (bio-based fibres, alternatives to conventional cotton). Strong consistency with the trade show''s sourcing positioning, in a context of regulatory tension and rising standards among European principals.

## How the trade show has evolved

