### Trajectory analysis

A trajectory of continuous growth since 2016, except for the COVID trough of 2020-2022. The 2025 edition marked a symbolic milestone with 180,000 visitors and 171 nationalities, placing VivaTech on a par with the world''s largest tech events in the B2B-startup segment. The 2026 anniversary edition goes all in on the step-change in scale: +30% floor space (Hall 7 across 3 levels is unprecedented at Porte de Versailles), 60+ country pavilions, the Oscars de la tech format, the Champs-Élysées takeover. The choice of Germany + India positions VivaTech on the axis of sovereign European AI against the American GAFAM. Publicis Groupe''s exit from the capital in August 2025 did not affect the model: Maurice Lévy remains a shareholder via Elvyr and co-pilots the project with LVMH/Les Echos.', seo_title_en=NULL, seo_description_en=NULL WHERE slug='vivatech';
UPDATE salons SET description_en=NULL, editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- Web Summit is Europe''s largest tech conference, founded in 2009 in Dublin by **Paddy Cosgrave, Daire Hickey and David Kelly**, and based in Lisbon since 2016. It is organised and operated by the company **Web Summit**, with no third-party co-organiser.
- Next edition: 9 to 12 November 2026 in Lisbon, at the [FIL Lisboa](/lieux/fil-lisboa) site (Lisbon Exhibition & Congress Centre) and the adjoining MEO Arena, in Parque das Nações.
- 2025 results (figures published post-event on websummit.com): 71,386 attendees from 157 countries, 1,857 investors (up 74% on the 1,066 of 2024), 2,725 startups from 108 countries, 869 speakers and 1,519 media representatives.
- A generalist technology and digital event: early-stage startups and scale-ups, investment funds, major technology groups, media and public decision-makers all meet around a format combining conferences and a startup exhibition zone.
- It is the Lisbon counterpart within the ecosystem of Europe''s major tech gatherings, in the same category as [VivaTech](/salons/vivatech) in Paris.

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

Startups in the seed and growth stages (the core of the exhibition zone), venture capital funds and investors, major technology groups, software publishers, innovation agencies and public operators, and specialist media. The visitor base is international and largely made up of decision-makers: 157 countries represented in 2025.

The startup presence is central to the event: 2,725 exhibiting startups in 2025, of which 40% were founded by women.

### Notable exhibitors

The exhibitor list and the programme are published on the official website: [websummit.com](https://websummit.com/). Recent editions have brought together leading names in global tech alongside thousands of young companies, as well as top-tier funds among the 1,857 investors present in 2025. The structure of the exhibition relies less on long-standing brands than on a significant annual turnover of the startup pool.

### Visitor quality

- 71,386 attendees in 2025, from 157 countries
- 1,857 investors present in 2025, up 74% on the 1,066 of 2024
- 2,725 exhibiting startups, of which 40% were founded by women
- 1,519 accredited media representatives

## Activities & community

### Conference programme

The heart of the trade show is its conference programme spread across several themed stages. **869 speakers** took to the stages in 2025. The dominant themes of recent editions cover artificial intelligence and machine learning, startup funding, consumer tech and the societal challenges of digital technology. The detailed programme is published on [websummit.com/schedule](https://websummit.com/schedule/). The 2026 theme and programme are to be confirmed as the event approaches.

### Community and networks

Web Summit relies on a matchmaking platform between attendees (with built-in messaging and connection requests) that structures networking across the four days.

The event enjoys dense international media coverage, with 1,519 publishers, executives, editors and journalists accredited in 2025. Web Summit also operates a network of international editions (Rio, Qatar, Vancouver), with the Lisbon edition remaining the original and most important one.

## On site: what you need to know

Access, parking and accommodation: see the [FIL Lisboa](/lieux/fil-lisboa) listing.

Specific to the trade show:

- **Venue:** the event spreads across the Lisbon Exhibition & Congress Centre (FIL) site and the adjoining MEO Arena, in Parque das Nações, in the east of Lisbon and served by the metro.
- **Badge:** paid access with prior online registration, several pass categories (from EUR 595 for the general pass, according to the rates published on websummit.com/tickets/). The terms and the opening of sales are published on [websummit.com](https://websummit.com/).
- **2026 dates:** from Monday 9 to Thursday 12 November 2026.

## Planning your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: monitor the opening of startup applications and dedicated programmes (PITCH, Masterclasses, Mentor Hours depending on stage).
- 9 months out: submit an application to the startup programme according to your stage of development.
- 6 months out: prepare your pitch, demos and appointment-booking strategy via the networking platform.
- 3 months out: activate your targeted investor network among the announced funds.
- 6 weeks out: finalise your appointment schedule and brief the team on the stages to target.

### For the decision-making visitor

- Paid online registration at websummit.com, with several pass levels depending on profile.
- Allow 2 to 4 days depending on your objectives (startup sourcing, tech intelligence, investor meetings).
- Target the conferences and stages in advance via the published programme.
- Make use of the matchmaking platform from the moment it opens to set up your appointments.

## How the trade show has evolved

