### Trajectory analysis

Attendance is rising steadily: +8% in 2024 to break the 20,500-visitor mark, then +2% in 2025 to 20,910 professionals. Internationalisation is the standout feature: near parity between French and foreign brands from 2024, then a majority of foreign brands in 2025 (168 out of 305).

The switch to the Workspace Paris name for the 2027 edition and the European business meetings programme launched in 2026 reflect Weyou Group''s stated ambition: to become "the largest annual trade show in the sector in Europe" (source: 2025 results release).', seo_title_en='Workspace Paris 2027 (formerly Workspace Expo): furniture, design and workplaces', seo_description_en='Workspace Paris (formerly Workspace Expo): more than 300 brands in furniture and workplace fit-out, from 16 to 18 March 2027 in Paris Porte de Versailles.' WHERE slug='workspace-expo-paris';
UPDATE salons SET description_en='The global civil nuclear industry trade show. The entire nuclear sector: construction, operation, decommissioning.', editorial_mdx_en='## Key takeaways

- World Nuclear Exhibition (WNE) is the leading global trade show for the civil nuclear sector, organised by RX France on behalf of [GIFEN](https://www.gifen.fr) (Groupement des Industriels Français de l''Énergie Nucléaire). Held on a **biennial** cycle (editions in 2014, 2016, 2018, then 2021, 2023, 2025 following the Covid postponement; aligned with odd years since 2021): the next edition is announced for **7-9 December 2027** at [Paris Nord Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte), halls 5A and 6. There is no WNE edition in 2026.
- The 2025 edition (4-6 November, halls 5A and 6) set a historic record with **1,070 exhibitors** (vs 780 in 2023) and **36,000 attendees** (vs 23,600 in 2023), a rise of more than 50% in attendance over two years: driven by the EPR2 relaunch, the rise of SMRs and the prominence of nuclear power in the European energy pact.
- **25 national pavilions** and more than **80 nationalities** represented in 2025: the most international event in the sector, with an exceptional institutional delegation (Rafael Mariano Grossi, Director General of the IAEA; Fatih Birol, Executive Director of the IEA; Sama Bilbao y León, Director General of the WNA; Roland Lescure, Minister for the Economy and Energy Sovereignty, France; Péter Szijjártó, Minister of Foreign Affairs, Hungary; Sharon S. Garin, Secretary of Energy, Philippines; Wojciech Wrochna, Ministry of Industry, Poland; Stephen Lecce, Minister of Energy and Mines, Ontario; Lassina Zerbo, President of the Rwanda Atomic Energy Board).
- **WNE Innovation Awards**: 110 entries in 2025 (+40% international participation, +15% candidate companies), 8 winners across 4 official categories. **WNE Startup Programme**: 11 startups from the 2023 cohort returned to exhibit at the France Pavilion in 2025.
- Two editorial themes confirmed for 2027: "**Nuclear Today and Tomorrow**" (existing fleet, EPR, SMR, AMR, safety, fuel cycle) and "**Beyond Electricity**" (medical, agriculture, propulsion, heat, hydrogen, desalination).

## Who exhibits

### Typical profile

The entire civil nuclear value chain: major contractors (EDF, Framatome, Orano, Westinghouse), tier-1 and tier-2 equipment manufacturers, engineering firms (Assystem, Egis, Tractebel), R&D and fusion (CEA, ITER, Fusion for Energy), SMR/AMR (NAAREA, Newcleo, Calogena, Jimmy, NuScale), deep tech startups, decommissioning services, instrumentation, nuclear robotics, nuclear medicine, low-carbon hydrogen. **45% international exhibitors** at the 2025 edition.

### Notable exhibitors

2025 Innovation Awards winners (major groups & mid-caps): **EDF**, **Westinghouse Electric**, **AIC** (Poland), **Framatome**. SME & startup winners: **CORE Energy Systems Limited**, **GraphiCore**, **Spaciotempo**, **Calogena**. Long-standing presence of the major French sectors (EDF, Framatome, Orano, Naval Group, CEA, Assystem) and of the international majors (Westinghouse, Hitachi, Mitsubishi Heavy Industries, KHNP, ROSATOM outside sanction periods). Full list on the [official exhibitor page](https://www.world-nuclear-exhibition.com/en-gb/wne-exhibitors.html).

### Visitor quality

Highly qualified institutional and industrial visitors: decision-makers from energy companies, nuclear safety authorities (ASN/ASNR in France, international equivalents), operators, engineering firms, investment funds, multilateral agencies (IAEA, OECD/NEA). In 2025: a massive ministerial and institutional presence (ministers from France, Hungary, the Philippines, Poland, the province of Ontario; President of the Rwanda Atomic Energy Board), 80+ nationalities, 25 national pavilions.

## Programme & community

### Conference programme

Programme structured around two overarching themes: "**Nuclear Today and Tomorrow**" (existing fleet, lifetime extension, EPR2, new reactors, SMR, AMR) and "**Beyond Electricity**" (nuclear medicine, food security, space exploration, low-carbon hydrogen, district and industrial heat, desalination). Opening plenaries were led in 2025 by **Rafael Mariano Grossi** (Director General of the IAEA), **Fatih Birol** (Executive Director of the IEA) and **Sama Bilbao y León** (Director General of the World Nuclear Association). 2025 ministerial session: Roland Lescure (France), Péter Szijjártó (Hungary), Sharon S. Garin (Philippines), Wojciech Wrochna (Poland), Stephen Lecce (Ontario). Technical sessions, country sessions and ministerial panels.

### Awards & accolades

**WNE Innovation Awards**: 4 official categories, verbatim wording from the 2025 edition (source: [official Awards page](https://awards.world-nuclear-exhibition.com/innovationawards2025/en/page/categories)):

1. "To boost Nuclear Installation Performance and LTO (Long Term Operation)"
2. "To contribute to more adaptive solutions to new challenges: need for pilotability, climate change, market instability or inefficiency…"
3. "To optimize construction costs and lead times"
4. "To innovate beyond electricity (medical, agriculture, propulsion, heat, hydrogen, desalination …)"

8 winners named in 2025 (4 major groups/mid-caps, 4 SMEs/startups). 110 international entries received, +40% international participation vs 2023.

### Satellite events

**WNE Startup Programme**: a support scheme for nuclear deep tech startups, integrated into the France Pavilion. Eleven startups from the 2023 cohort returned to exhibit in 2025 as mature companies. **Beyond Electricity Expo**: a space dedicated to the non-electrical uses of nuclear power (medicine, agriculture, space, hydrogen, heat, desalination). **9,000 business meetings** organised at the 2025 edition via the official matchmaking platform.

### Community & networks

Co-organised by GIFEN (institutional) and operated by RX France (formerly Reed Expositions, rebranded RX France in 2022). Media partner: World Nuclear News. Structuring industrial sponsorship led by the majors of the French nuclear sector (EDF, Framatome, Orano) and international players.

A network of satellite events during the week: GIFEN sector conferences, partner side events (IAEA, SFEN, Foratom now Nucleareurope), embassy receptions for the national pavilions.

### What''s new in 2027

The 2027 edition is announced for 7-9 December 2027, halls 5A and 6 at Paris Nord Villepinte. The detailed programme and list of national pavilions had not yet been published at the time of writing.

## On site: what you need to know

- **Venue**: [Paris Nord Villepinte](/lieux/paris-nord-villepinte), **halls 5A and 6** (configuration confirmed for the 2025 and 2027 editions).
- **Transport access**: RER B line, "Parc des Expositions" station, with a direct stop in front of the entrance hall. Direct link from Roissy CDG (3 stops on the RER B) and central Paris (Gare du Nord, ~25 minutes). In December, allow extra time for the plenary sessions (attendance concentrated in the early morning).
- **Accommodation**: Roissy CDG / Aéroville and the north-east of Paris are preferable (close to the RER B). December is peak conference season in Paris, so book 3 to 4 months in advance.
- **Opening hours & badges**: online pre-registration is mandatory. The 2025 edition was open from 9am to 6pm (days 1 and 2), closing at 5pm on day 3. 2027 hours to be confirmed.
- **Services**: multiple catering options on site, networking areas, plenary rooms, official pre-event and on-site matchmaking platform.

## Preparing your visit

### For the exhibitor

- 12 months out: get in touch with RX France or GIFEN, application file, choice between a direct stand, a national pavilion (Bpifrance / Business France for the France Pavilion) or a startup package.
- 9 months out: application for the **WNE Innovation Awards** (4 categories), application for the **WNE Startup Programme**.
- 6 months out: stand brief, targeting of priority contractors (EDF, Framatome, Orano, Westinghouse, KHNP, etc.), meeting requests on the official platform.
- 3 months out: team accreditation, accommodation in Roissy/Villepinte, technical brief. A B2B sector event: highly qualified visitors but long cycles, stand content geared towards technical proof (feedback, certifications, project references).
- 6 weeks out: scheduling of business meetings, sales briefing, coverage plan for the relevant country sessions.

### For the decision-maker visitor

- Free pre-registration is mandatory via the official website. Check the eligibility criteria (proof of professional involvement in the nuclear sector).
- Build your visit plan around the two editorial themes: "Nuclear Today and Tomorrow" for fleet and new-reactor issues, "Beyond Electricity" for non-electrical uses.
- Identify the national pavilions of interest in advance (25+ countries in 2025) and the corresponding country sessions.
- Activate the official matchmaking platform 4 to 6 weeks ahead to structure your meetings (9,000 meetings held in 2025).
- Book the opening plenaries (IAEA, IEA, ministers) as soon as registration opens, as capacity is limited.

## Policy & CSR initiatives

The official WNE website lists an "Our commitments" section dedicated to the event''s commitments, without detailed publication of impact indicators to date. The trade show is operated by RX France, a subsidiary of RELX, a group that has signed up to a Net Zero Carbon Events commitment across all its events. In terms of content, the "Beyond Electricity" theme structurally carries the environmental impact dimension of nuclear power (low-carbon hydrogen, desalination, decarbonised industrial heat, nuclear medicine), and the "Nuclear Today and Tomorrow" theme documents reactor lifetime extension as a major climate lever. The detailed impact report for the 2025 edition is not published on the website at the time of writing.

## How the show has evolved

